window.DEEP_STORIES["photography"] = {"slug":"photography","title":"Photography & Movies","emoji":"📸","tagline":"How people learned to catch light and make it move","intro":"Have you ever wondered how a picture gets trapped inside a camera? For most of human history, a moment you saw was gone forever unless someone painted it. Then, about 200 years ago, inventors in France and England discovered how to make light draw by itself. In these 20 parts you will travel from a tiny hole in a dark room to the camera in your pocket. You will meet Niépce, who waited hours for the sun to make the first surviving camera photograph; Daguerre and Talbot, who raced to share their secrets in 1839; George Eastman, who put cameras into ordinary hands; and the dreamers who made pictures move, talk, sing and turn into cartoons. You will learn the real science of lenses, silver crystals, frames and pixels, see how photos changed news, families and history, and face the tricky question of whether a picture can lie. Grab your popcorn. The lights are going down.","outline":[{"n":1,"title":"Light Through a Tiny Hole","when":"about 400s BCE – 1600s"},{"n":2,"title":"Silver That Darkens in the Sun","when":"1600s – 1820s"},{"n":3,"title":"Niépce and the View from the Window","when":"1816 – 1833"},{"n":4,"title":"Daguerre's Mirror with a Memory","when":"1822 – 1840s"},{"n":5,"title":"Talbot, Negatives and the Race of 1839","when":"1833 – 1850"},{"n":6,"title":"Inside the Camera: Lenses, Light and Silver","when":"The science (explained with 1800s examples)"},{"n":7,"title":"Glass Plates and a World on Camera","when":"1851 – 1880"},{"n":8,"title":"Freezing Motion","when":"1825 – 1888"},{"n":9,"title":"You Press the Button: Eastman and Kodak","when":"1878 – 1910s"},{"n":10,"title":"The Race to Make Pictures Move","when":"1888 – 1896"},{"n":11,"title":"How Movies Trick Your Eyes","when":"The science (explained with 1890s–1920s examples)"},{"n":12,"title":"Magic, Stories and the Silent Era","when":"1896 – 1927"},{"n":13,"title":"Painting with Light: Color","when":"1861 – 1950s"},{"n":14,"title":"The Talkies Arrive","when":"1890s – 1931"},{"n":15,"title":"Drawings That Move: Animation","when":"1892 – 1940"},{"n":16,"title":"Cameras for Everyone, Everywhere","when":"1913 – 1990s"},{"n":17,"title":"Truth, Tricks and Trouble","when":"1840 – today"},{"n":18,"title":"Screens at Home and Spectacles in Theaters","when":"1950 – 2000"},{"n":19,"title":"Pictures Made of Numbers","when":"1957 – 2012"},{"n":20,"title":"Every Pocket a Studio, and Tomorrow's Pictures","when":"1997 – today and the future"}],"people":[["Mozi (about 470 – about 391 BCE)","Chinese philosopher whose followers' writings describe the pinhole image"],["Ibn al-Haytham / Alhazen (about 965 – about 1040)","Arab scientist whose Book of Optics studied light with dark rooms"],["Giambattista della Porta (about 1535 – 1615)","Italian scholar who popularized the camera obscura in Natural Magic (1558)"],["Johann Heinrich Schulze (1687 – 1744)","German professor who showed light darkens silver salts"],["Thomas Wedgwood (1771 – 1805)","English experimenter who made unfixable silver images with Humphry Davy"],["Humphry Davy (1778 – 1829)","English chemist who co-published the 1802 paper with Wedgwood"],["Joseph Nicéphore Niépce (1765 – 1833)","French inventor who made the oldest surviving camera photograph"],["Louis Daguerre (1787 – 1851)","French artist and showman who created the daguerreotype"],["François Arago (1786 – 1853)","French scientist and politician who announced the daguerreotype in 1839"],["William Henry Fox Talbot (1800 – 1877)","English scientist who invented the negative-positive calotype process"],["John Herschel (1792 – 1871)","English astronomer who introduced hypo fixer and popularized photo terms"],["Hippolyte Bayard (1801 – 1887)","French photographer who invented a direct positive process and felt overlooked"],["Hercules Florence (1804 – 1879)","French-Brazilian inventor who experimented with photography in Brazil in the 1830s"],["Anna Atkins (1799 – 1871)","English botanist who made a book of cyanotype algae photographs"],["Robert Cornelius (1809 – 1893)","American who made one of the earliest photographic self-portraits"],["Frederick Scott Archer (1813 – 1857)","English sculptor who invented the wet collodion process"],["Roger Fenton (1819 – 1869)","British photographer of the Crimean War"],["Mathew Brady (about 1822 – 1896)","American portrait photographer whose teams photographed the Civil War"],["Alexander Gardner (1821 – 1882)","Scottish-American Civil War photographer"],["Nadar (Gaspard-Félix Tournachon, 1820 – 1910)","French portrait photographer and balloon photography pioneer"],["Julia Margaret Cameron (1815 – 1879)","British portrait photographer and artist"],["James Clerk Maxwell (1831 – 1879)","Scottish physicist who demonstrated three-color photography in 1861"],["Richard Leach Maddox (1816 – 1902)","English doctor who proposed the gelatin dry plate"],["Eadweard Muybridge (1830 – 1904)","English-American photographer of motion sequences"],["Leland Stanford (1824 – 1893)","American businessman who funded Muybridge's horse photographs"],["Étienne-Jules Marey (1830 – 1904)","French scientist who invented chronophotography"],["Joseph Plateau (1801 – 1883)","Belgian physicist who invented the phenakistiscope"],["Émile Reynaud (1844 – 1918)","French inventor of the praxinoscope and Théâtre Optique"],["George Eastman (1854 – 1932)","American founder of Kodak who made photography easy and cheap"],["Hannibal Goodwin (1822 – 1900)","American clergyman who patented celluloid photographic film"],["Louis Le Prince (1841 – disappeared 1890)","French inventor who filmed moving pictures in Leeds in 1888"],["Thomas Edison (1847 – 1931)","American inventor whose lab produced the Kinetograph and Kinetoscope"],["William Kennedy Laurie Dickson (1860 – 1935)","Edison's engineer who did much of the early film camera work"],["Max Skladanowsky (1863 – 1939)","German pioneer who, with brother Emil, projected films in Berlin in 1895"],["Auguste Lumière (1862 – 1954) and Louis Lumière (1864 – 1948)","French brothers behind the Cinématographe and Autochrome"],["Georges Méliès (1861 – 1938)","French magician and filmmaker famous for special effects"],["Alice Guy-Blaché (1873 – 1968)","French pioneering director and studio founder"],["Edwin S. Porter (1870 – 1941)","American director of The Great Train Robbery"],["D. W. Griffith (1875 – 1948)","American director whose techniques were influential but whose The Birth of a Nation was racist"],["Charlie Chaplin (1889 – 1977)","British comedian, actor and director of silent films"],["Buster Keaton (1895 – 1966)","American silent comedy star and director"],["Herbert Kalmus (1881 – 1963)","co-founder and leader of Technicolor"],["Leopold Mannes (1899 – 1964) and Leopold Godowsky Jr. (1900 – 1983)","musicians who co-invented Kodachrome"],["Lee de Forest (1873 – 1961)","American inventor who worked on sound-on-film (Phonofilm)"],["Al Jolson (1886 – 1950)","singer and star of The Jazz Singer"],["Émile Cohl (1857 – 1938)","French animator of Fantasmagorie"],["Winsor McCay (about 1867 – 1934)","American cartoonist who made Gertie the Dinosaur"],["Walt Disney (1901 – 1966)","American animation producer and studio founder"],["Ub Iwerks (1901 – 1971)","American animator who co-created Mickey Mouse"],["Lotte Reiniger (1899 – 1981)","German silhouette animator of The Adventures of Prince Achmed"],["Willis O'Brien (1886 – 1962)","American stop-motion pioneer behind King Kong"],["Oskar Barnack (1879 – 1936)","German engineer who designed the Leica"],["Harold Edgerton (1903 – 1990)","MIT engineer who pioneered high-speed strobe photography"],["Dorothea Lange (1895 – 1965)","American documentary photographer"],["Jacob Riis (1849 – 1914)","Danish-American journalist who photographed New York slums"],["Lewis Hine (1874 – 1940)","American photographer who documented child labor"],["Edwin Land (1909 – 1991)","American scientist who invented Polaroid instant photography"],["Bill Anders (1933 – 2024)","Apollo 8 astronaut who took Earthrise"],["Russell Kirsch (1929 – 2020)","American computer scientist who made one of the first digital images"],["Willard Boyle (1924 – 2011) and George E. Smith (born 1930)","Bell Labs scientists who invented the CCD"],["Bryce Bayer (1929 – 2012)","Kodak scientist who invented the Bayer color filter pattern"],["Steven Sasson (born 1950)","Kodak engineer who built an early digital camera prototype in 1975"],["Eric Fossum (born 1957)","engineer who led development of the CMOS active pixel image sensor"],["Philippe Kahn (born 1952)","inventor who shared one of the first instant phone photos in 1997"]],"glossary":[["camera obscura","a dark room or box where light through a small hole projects an upside-down image"],["lens","a curved piece of glass that bends light to make a sharp, bright image"],["aperture","the opening that controls how much light enters a camera"],["shutter","the part that opens and closes to let light in for a set time"],["exposure","the total amount of light that reaches the film or sensor"],["silver halide","light-sensitive crystals of silver combined with chlorine, bromine or iodine, used in film"],["latent image","an invisible picture formed by light that appears only after developing"],["developer","a chemical that turns the latent image into a visible one"],["fixer (hypo)","a chemical that removes unused light-sensitive silver so the picture stops changing"],["negative","an image with light and dark reversed, used to print many positive copies"],["daguerreotype","a one-of-a-kind photograph on a polished silver-coated copper plate"],["calotype","Talbot's paper negative process that allowed many prints"],["wet collodion","a process using glass plates coated with sticky collodion that had to stay wet"],["frames per second (fps)","how many still pictures (frames) are shown each second"],["sprocket holes","the small holes along film edges that let gears move it precisely"],["optical soundtrack","a strip beside movie frames that stores sound as patterns of light"],["additive color","making colors by mixing red, green and blue light"],["subtractive color","making colors with cyan, magenta and yellow dyes that absorb parts of white light"],["pixel","one tiny square of a digital picture that records a single color and brightness"],["image sensor (CCD/CMOS)","the chip in a digital camera that turns light into electrical signals"]],"parts":[{"n":1,"title":"Light Through a Tiny Hole","when":"about 400s BCE – 1600s","summary":"Long before anyone had a camera, people noticed something magical: if you make a room completely dark and poke a tiny hole in one wall, a picture of the outside world appears on the opposite wall, upside down! For over 2,000 years, thinkers in China, Greece, the Middle East and Europe played with this trick. There was just one problem: nobody could keep the picture.","sections":[{"heading":"A Room That Paints Itself","paragraphs":["Imagine it is a bright summer afternoon. You walk into a bedroom, pull thick curtains over the window, stuff towels into every crack and close the door. For a minute you see nothing at all. Then someone pokes a hole, about the size of a pencil, through a piece of cardboard taped over the window. As your eyes adjust, something wonderful happens. On the wall opposite the hole, a faint, glowing picture appears: the trees outside, the blue sky, a car driving down the street. Everything is in full color and everything is moving. But the sky is at the bottom, the trees hang down like icicles, and the car drives the wrong way.","You have just built a camera obscura. The name is Latin and simply means \"dark room\". It is the oldest ancestor of every camera that has ever existed, from the wooden boxes of the 1800s to the tiny lens in a smartphone. In fact, the word \"camera\" itself comes from it. When people later shortened \"camera obscura\" to just \"camera\", they were really saying \"room\".","Why does this happen? The secret is one simple rule: light travels in straight lines. Every point on a sunlit tree outside is bouncing light in all directions. Most of that light hits the wall of your house and never gets in. But a tiny bundle of rays from each point slips through the hole and keeps going in a perfectly straight line until it hits the opposite wall.","Now think about the very top of the tree. Light from up there has to travel downward at a slant to get through the hole, so it keeps going downward and lands low on the wall. Light from the bottom of the trunk travels upward through the hole and lands high on the wall. The rays cross over each other right at the hole, like an X. That is why the picture is upside down. The same crossing happens from side to side, so the left side of the scene ends up on the right side of the wall. The image is flipped both top-to-bottom and left-to-right, which is the same as turning it halfway round, like spinning a photo on a table by 180 degrees.","The size of the hole matters a lot. A big hole lets in light from each point of the scene through a wide gap, so every point becomes a blurry blob that overlaps its neighbors. The picture is bright but fuzzy. A tiny hole lets in only a thin pencil of light from each point, so the picture is sharp but very dim. Make the hole far too small and a new problem appears: light waves start to spread out as they squeeze through, a wave effect called diffraction, and the picture gets blurry again. Builders of pinhole cameras today use rules of thumb, such as keeping the hole no wider than about one hundredth of the distance to the screen, to find a happy middle.","The distance matters too. Move the screen closer to the hole and the picture shrinks and grows brighter. Move it farther away and the picture grows bigger but dimmer, because the same amount of light is being spread over a larger area. These trade-offs between brightness, sharpness and size would puzzle and fascinate people for more than two thousand years. They still matter to every photographer today."]},{"heading":"Clues from Ancient China and Greece","paragraphs":["Nobody knows who first noticed a pinhole picture. It probably happened many times, all over the world, by accident. Sunlight slipping through a gap in a tent, a crack in a shutter or a hole in a roof would have painted a dim, upside-down scene on a dark wall. The earliest written descriptions we have come from two very different places at about the same time: China and Greece, roughly 2,400 years ago.","In China, a philosopher named Mozi lived from about 470 to about 391 BCE. He taught that people should care for everyone equally, avoid wasteful luxury and oppose wars of conquest. His followers, the Mohists, were also skilled craftsmen and engineers. They were famous experts at defending cities against sieges, and they were curious about geometry, mechanics and light. The collection of writings linked to Mozi and his school, put together over many years, contains short, puzzle-like statements about how light behaves.","One of these passages describes an image formed when light passes through a small opening into a dark place. The writer explains that the picture is upside down, and gives a clever reason. Light coming from a person's feet travels upward and passes the hole low, so it ends up forming the top of the image, while light from the head ends up at the bottom. In other words, the Mohist writer understood that the rays cross at the opening. The text calls the place where the image forms by a word that has been translated as a \"collecting point\" or \"treasure house\". Scholars still argue about exactly what that word meant, and one later Chinese scholar suggested it might be an old copying mistake for \"screen\".","Around the same time, in ancient Greece, the great thinker Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE) and his students were collecting questions about nature in a work known as the Problems. Not all of it was written by Aristotle himself, so historians often say \"Aristotle or his followers\". One puzzle in it asks something you can test yourself: why does sunlight shining through a square hole, or through the gaps in woven wicker, make a round spot of light on the ground instead of a square one? And why, during an eclipse of the Sun, do those spots turn into little crescents, like bites taken out of a cookie?","You may have seen this yourself. During a partial solar eclipse, the shade under a leafy tree fills with hundreds of tiny glowing crescents. Each gap between the leaves acts like a pinhole and projects its own little picture of the Sun. When the Moon covers part of the Sun, every one of those pictures becomes a crescent. Today, space agencies such as NASA suggest a safe way to see this: hold a kitchen colander or a card with a pinhole in it, stand with your back to the Sun, and look at the shadow on the ground. Never look at the Sun itself, through a pinhole or any other way, without proper eclipse protection.","The Greek writer did not fully solve the puzzle. He talked about cones of light and suggested that the corners of the square hole were too weak to show. But the important thing is that he noticed it, wrote it down and asked why. That habit of asking why is where science begins."]},{"heading":"Ibn al-Haytham and the Candles in the Dark","paragraphs":["The person who did more than anyone else in the ancient and medieval world to explain the camera obscura was born in about 965 CE in Basra, a busy trading city in what is now Iraq. His full name was Abu Ali al-Hasan ibn al-Haytham. Later European scholars, who found his name hard to pronounce, called him Alhazen.","Ibn al-Haytham lived during what is often called the Islamic Golden Age, when scholars in cities like Baghdad, Basra and Cairo translated, studied and improved on Greek, Persian and Indian learning. He became known as a gifted mathematician. Later he moved to Cairo, the capital of the Fatimid caliphs in Egypt. Old stories say that he boasted he could control the floods of the Nile, then realized the plan was impossible and was afraid of the powerful and unpredictable caliph al-Hakim. A famous tale claims he pretended to be mad to escape punishment and was kept shut up in his house until the caliph died in 1021. Historians treat this as a legend, but it is true that he spent many years in Cairo writing, and he lived there until his death around 1040.","His greatest work was the Book of Optics, in Arabic Kitab al-Manazir, a huge study in seven volumes written roughly between 1011 and 1021. Before him, many thinkers believed that our eyes send out invisible rays that touch the things we look at, a bit like feeling objects with a long, invisible finger. Ibn al-Haytham argued the opposite: light comes from objects into the eye. That is how we actually see, and modern science agrees with him.","What made his work special was the way he tested ideas. He did not just argue. He set up experiments, and the dark room was one of his favorite tools. In one famous experiment, he placed several candles in different spots outside a small opening that led into a darkened space with a pale wall. Inside, each candle made its own separate spot of light on the wall. Each spot sat exactly opposite its candle, in a straight line through the opening. When he covered one candle, only its matching spot vanished. The others stayed. This showed two important things: light travels in straight lines, and the light from different sources passes through the same small hole without getting mixed up.","Ibn al-Haytham also used a dark chamber to study the Sun during a partial eclipse, watching its crescent shape projected through a small hole. In a separate treatise, On the Shape of the Eclipse, he worked out mathematically how the size and shape of the opening change the image. Some historians call this the first truly experimental study of the camera obscura.","His Book of Optics was translated into Latin, probably in the late 1100s or early 1200s, with the title De aspectibus. In 1572 a printed edition appeared in Europe. It became one of the most important science books in the medieval and Renaissance world. Scholars such as Roger Bacon in England, and centuries later Johannes Kepler, built on his ideas. Some writers call Ibn al-Haytham the father of modern optics. Other historians warn that he should not be seen as a lone genius, since he built on earlier Greek and Arabic scholars. Both points are fair: he stood on the shoulders of others, but he climbed a long way higher.","Meanwhile, in China, the idea had not been forgotten. In 1088 the scientist and statesman Shen Kuo wrote about the pinhole image in his Dream Pool Essays. He compared the way the image flips to an oar moving in a rowlock: when the handle goes up, the blade goes down, because the oar pivots on a fixed point. The hole works like that pivot. It is a lovely comparison, and it shows that people on opposite sides of the world kept rediscovering the same simple truth about light."]},{"heading":"Renaissance Notebooks and Better Holes","paragraphs":["Jump forward to Italy around the year 1500. Cities like Florence, Milan and Venice were bursting with artists, engineers and scholars, all hungry to understand how the world really looked. Painters were learning the rules of perspective, the geometry that makes a flat picture seem to have depth. To them, a device that drew the world in perfect perspective all by itself was very exciting.","Leonardo da Vinci, the painter of the Mona Lisa and a tireless note-taker, filled his notebooks with thoughts about the camera obscura. In pages from around 1502, now part of a giant collection called the Codex Atlanticus, he gave a very clear description: when sunlit buildings or people are seen through a small round hole into a very dark room, their images appear on a white paper or wall inside, smaller and upside down. He drew around 270 diagrams about it. Leonardo also compared the dark room to the human eye, which was a brilliant insight, even though he got one detail wrong. He thought something inside the eye must flip the image back the right way up. In fact, the picture on the back of your eye really is upside down, and your brain sorts it out.","It is important not to say Leonardo invented the camera obscura. As you have seen, it had been known for many centuries before him. And his notes stayed private. They were not published until 1797, almost 300 years after he wrote them, so they did not spread the idea in his own time.","Others did spread it. In 1545 a Dutch scholar named Gemma Frisius published a book that includes the oldest known printed picture of a camera obscura. The woodcut shows a room with a hole in one wall and the crescent Sun projected on the far wall, because Frisius had used the device to watch a solar eclipse on 24 January 1544. Astronomers loved the dark room for exactly this reason. Looking straight at the Sun can badly damage your eyes, but looking at its projected image on a wall is safe.","Next came an upgrade that changed everything: the lens. A convex lens is a piece of glass that bulges outward in the middle, like a magnifying glass. When light passes through it, the glass bends the rays inward so that light from each point of the scene comes back together at a single point. That means you can use a big opening, which lets in plenty of light, and still get a sharp image, as long as the screen is at the right distance. In 1550 the Italian doctor, mathematician and gambler Gerolamo Cardano described putting a glass disc, probably a convex lens, into the hole of a camera obscura. Suddenly the picture was much brighter.","A few years later the Venetian nobleman Daniele Barbaro, in a book on perspective published in the late 1560s (often dated 1568), described a camera obscura with a lens and gave a smart tip. If you cover part of the lens to make the opening smaller, the picture becomes sharper. Barbaro had discovered something every photographer still uses: the aperture, an adjustable opening that controls how much light gets in and how sharp the picture looks. Barbaro also recommended the device to artists, explaining that they could trace the outlines of buildings and get the perspective perfectly right. With a lens and a smaller opening, the camera obscura had quietly gained two of the three main parts of a modern camera. The third part, something to record the picture, was still missing."]},{"heading":"Della Porta's Book of Wonders","paragraphs":["If the camera obscura had a publicity agent in the 1500s, it was Giambattista della Porta. Born around 1535 near Naples into a noble family, della Porta grew up in a household so full of learning that it was said to be like an academy. He wrote plays, studied plants, codes, mathematics and magnets, and was endlessly curious about what he called the secrets of nature.","In 1558, when he was only in his early twenties, he published Magia Naturalis, which means Natural Magic. It was a bit like a giant book of science tricks and experiments mixed with old beliefs about alchemy and astrology. The book was hugely popular and was translated into several languages. In 1589 he released a much bigger version, expanded to twenty books. In it, he described the camera obscura in detail and explained how adding a convex lens made the image clearer, and how it could help someone who could not draw well to trace a scene.","Della Porta did not invent the camera obscura either, but his book made it famous across Europe. He also thought of using it as a show. In the 1589 edition he described entertainments in which guests sat in a darkened room while, outside in the sunshine, actors performed on a little stage. Through the lens, the guests saw hunting scenes, banquets, battles and plays glowing on a white sheet, complete with tiny trees, animals and people. He wrote that his friends could hardly believe it was an optical trick. In a way, this was a kind of live cinema, more than 300 years before real movies.","Della Porta's interest in strange and secret things sometimes got him into trouble. He founded a group of curious thinkers that studied nature, but it was shut down after its members were suspected of dabbling in magic, and he was questioned by the Inquisition, the Church court of the time. His books were banned for several years in the 1590s. Still, he remained respected, and in 1610 he joined the Accademia dei Lincei, the Academy of the Lynxes, one of the first scientific societies in the world, which the astronomer Galileo also joined."]},{"heading":"Kepler, Tents and Boxes","paragraphs":["The name \"camera obscura\" was first used in print by the German astronomer Johannes Kepler. In 1604 he published a book on optics, written as an add-on to the work of the medieval optics writer Witelo, and in it he used the term camera obscura. Kepler wanted to understand exactly how pinhole images form because astronomers used them to measure the Sun and Moon during eclipses, and the measurements did not always make sense. In July 1600, in the Austrian town of Graz, he had set up a pinhole device to observe a partial eclipse of the Sun. To work out the geometry, he even modeled the light rays with threads stretched from a book through an opening, tracing the paths one by one.","Kepler also explained how the eye works, building on Ibn al-Haytham's ideas. He showed that the lens of the eye focuses an upside-down image onto the retina at the back. In other words, your eye is a tiny camera obscura with a lens. In 1607 he spotted a dark speck on the projected Sun and thought it was the planet Mercury passing in front. It was almost certainly a sunspot. By about 1620 he was carrying a portable tent camera obscura with a lens, turning around inside it to draw the countryside.","Tents were just the beginning. Inventors realized you did not need a whole room, only a dark box. In 1676 the German mathematician Johann Christoph Sturm described a portable box with a mirror tilted at 45 degrees inside. The mirror bounced the image upward onto a sheet of oiled paper on top of the box, so the artist could see it the right way up and trace it. In 1685 a German churchman and scholar named Johann Zahn published a book describing several small box cameras, including handheld designs with a lens at the front and a mirror inside. Looked at today, Zahn's box designs look astonishingly like the wooden cameras of the 1800s. In 1694 the English scientist Robert Hooke showed the Royal Society a cone-shaped version that fitted over the user's head and shoulders.","By the 1700s, portable camera obscuras were sold as drawing aids to artists, architects and wealthy travelers. English painters such as Paul Sandby and Joshua Reynolds are known to have used them. Some boxes folded up like books. Others were built into sedan chairs or small huts in gardens and parks.","Did great painters secretly use the camera obscura to make their pictures so lifelike? This is one of art history's longest arguments. The Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer, who painted Girl with a Pearl Earring in the 1660s, is the most famous example. Some researchers think the soft glowing highlights and the exact perspective in his paintings show he used one. Others strongly disagree, and there is no written record proving it. Historians still debate it, so it is fairest to say he might have, not that he definitely did."]},{"heading":"The Picture That Would Not Stay","paragraphs":["By the 1600s and 1700s, the camera obscura was everywhere: in astronomers' observatories, in artists' studios, in scholars' cabinets of curiosities and in the gardens of the rich. People understood its optics very well. They knew light travels in straight lines. They had lenses to brighten the image, small openings to sharpen it and mirrors to flip it the right way up. It could show colors, movement and perfect perspective far better than any human hand.","And yet every picture it made was a ghost. The moment a cloud covered the Sun, the image dimmed. When evening came, it faded to nothing. If the artist stepped away from the box, the scene on the paper did not wait. The picture was made of light alone, and light does not stay still. The only way to keep it was to trace it by hand with a pencil or pen, which took skill, patience and many hours, and which could never capture every leaf and brick and ripple.","Think about how strange that is. For more than 2,000 years, since the Mohist writers and Aristotle's followers, people had known how to make a perfect, living picture of the world appear on a wall. Generations of scientists studied it, artists traced it and showmen used it to amaze audiences. But not a single one of those images survived. Everything people saw in a camera obscura before the 1800s is gone forever.","The missing piece was not optical. It was chemical. Somebody needed to find a material that would change when light fell on it, so that bright parts of the picture became one shade and dark parts another, and then stop changing so the picture would last. Nobody in Leonardo's, della Porta's or Kepler's time even knew that such materials existed.","So imagine an artist in the 1700s, bent over a wooden camera obscura in a sunny garden, carefully tracing the outline of a church tower while the trees around it shimmer in the breeze on the glass. Her pencil can never keep up with the light. She must have wondered, just as you might now: what if the picture could draw itself, and stay?"]}],"deepDive":"Why does a pinhole image get blurrier when the hole is made too small? Two effects fight each other. With a large hole, each point of the scene projects a disc of light roughly the size of the hole, so geometric blur grows with hole diameter. With a very small hole, light behaves as a wave and spreads out by diffraction, and that blur grows as the hole shrinks. The sharpest image comes from balancing the two. In 1857 the mathematician Joseph Petzval proposed an optimum diameter of about the square root of twice the screen distance times the wavelength of light; Lord Rayleigh in 1891 suggested a hole roughly 40 percent larger. For a pinhole box 10 centimeters deep and green light, the answer is only about a third of a millimeter, which is why pinhole images are so dim and why early dark rooms were used mainly for bright subjects like the Sun. Adding a convex lens breaks this trade-off: a lens collects light over a wide area and brings it back to a focus, so the image can be both bright and sharp, though only objects at one distance are perfectly sharp at a time. Historians also debate credit. The Mohist and Aristotelian texts are short and difficult, and scholars disagree about how well their authors understood what they described. Ibn al-Haytham is widely seen as the first to treat the camera obscura experimentally and mathematically, though some historians, such as G. J. Toomer, caution against crediting him with the whole modern scientific method. Leonardo's descriptions are vivid but were unpublished until 1797, so their influence was small at first. Finally, the long-running Vermeer debate, revived in the 2000s by the artist David Hockney and the physicist Charles Falco, who argued that many old masters used optical aids, remains unresolved. Their critics point out that skilled painters could achieve the same effects by eye, and that period documents describing such use are scarce.","funFact":"The word \"camera\" simply means \"room\" in Latin, so every time you say you are taking a picture with your camera, you are really talking about a tiny dark room.","check":{"q":"Why is the picture in a camera obscura upside down?","a":"Because light travels in straight lines, so rays from the top and bottom of the scene cross at the hole","wrong":["Because the wall acts like a mirror and reflects the scene","Because the glass lens always turns pictures over","Because light from the sky is heavier and falls downward"]},"sources":[{"title":"Camera obscura","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camera_obscura"},{"title":"Ibn al-Haytham","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ibn_al-Haytham"},{"title":"Pinhole camera","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinhole_camera"},{"title":"Giambattista della Porta","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giambattista_della_Porta"},{"title":"Mozi","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mozi"},{"title":"Eclipse Safety","publisher":"NASA Science","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/safety/"},{"title":"History of photography","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_photography"},{"title":"Hockney–Falco thesis","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hockney%E2%80%93Falco_thesis"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/01-1.jpg","alt":"Gemma Frisius's 1545 woodcut: a room-sized camera obscura projects the eclipsed Sun of January 1544 onto its wall.","file":"1545 gemma frisius - camera obscura - 121704 1 1.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1545_gemma_frisius_-_camera_obscura_-_121704_1_1.png","author":"Gemma Frisius","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/01-2.jpg","alt":"A wooden portable box camera obscura made by W. & S. Jones of London around 1800-1820, National Museum of Scotland.","file":"Portable box camera Obscura, W&S Jones, London, UK, 1800-1820. National Museum of Scotland, Edinburgh.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portable_box_camera_Obscura,_W%26S_Jones,_London,_UK,_1800-1820._National_Museum_of_Scotland,_Edinburgh.jpg","author":"Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg)","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/01-3.jpg","alt":"The eyes and optic nerves drawn in an old Arabic copy of Ibn al-Haytham's Book of Optics (Kitab al-Manazir).","file":"Alhazen1652.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alhazen1652.png","author":"Copy of the Kitab al-Manazir (MS Fatih 3212, vol. 1, fol. 81b, Süleimaniye…","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3912},{"n":2,"title":"Silver That Darkens in the Sun","when":"1600s – 1820s","summary":"Some chemicals made with silver turn dark when sunlight touches them. In the 1700s and early 1800s, curious scientists used this trick to make the first light-drawn shadow pictures of words, leaves and lace. But the pictures kept darkening until they turned completely black, and nobody could figure out how to make them stop.","sections":[{"heading":"A Bottle That Wrote in the Sunshine","paragraphs":["Picture a university laboratory in Germany around the year 1717. Glass flasks and jars crowd the shelves, a fire crackles in a furnace, and the air smells sharply of acid. A professor named Johann Heinrich Schulze is mixing chalk with nitric acid, a strong acid that can dissolve metals. Without realizing it, he is using acid that has some silver dissolved in it. He sets the cloudy mixture down near a sunny window. When he comes back, he notices something odd. The side of the bottle facing the window has turned a deep dark red-purple, while the side facing the room is still pale.","Schulze, born in 1687, was a busy scholar who taught medicine and studied ancient languages at the universities of Altdorf and later Halle. He was the kind of person who could not leave a mystery alone. Was the darkening caused by the heat of the Sun or by its light? Plenty of chemicals change when they get warm, so heat seemed like a reasonable guess.","To find out, he did what good scientists do: he tested one thing at a time. He warmed a sample of the mixture by a fire, out of the sunlight. It did not darken. So heat alone was not the cause. Then he tried something clever and playful. He cut words and letters out of paper to make stencils, and he stuck them onto the outside of a bottle of the mixture. He placed the bottle in direct sunlight. Where light shone through the cut-out letters, the liquid darkened. Where the paper blocked the light, it stayed pale. When he peeled the stencils away, the words appeared written inside the bottle in dark letters.","It was the first time anyone had used light on purpose to make a lasting-looking pattern on a light-sensitive chemical. It was not quite a photograph, but it was something close: writing done by the Sun. Of course, it did not last. If he shook the bottle, the dark parts mixed with the pale parts and the letters disappeared. If he left the bottle in the light, the whole mixture darkened and the words vanished into the dark.","Schulze gave his light-sensitive mixture the name Scotophorus, meaning \"darkness-bringer\", in contrast to phosphorus, which means \"light-bringer\". He seems to have thought it might be useful for testing whether a mineral contained silver. He did not think of using it to make pictures. He described his experiments in a paper published in 1719. Many books give the date as 1727, but that was a reprint, and the experiments themselves were done around 1717.","Schulze was not the very first person to notice that silver compounds change in light. Back in 1614 an Italian-born chemist named Angelo Sala had noticed that powdered silver nitrate turned black in the Sun, and in 1694 the scientist Wilhelm Homberg described how light darkened certain chemicals. But Schulze was the first to prove carefully that light, and not heat, was responsible. Because of this, some German writers count him as an early inventor of photography. That is a stretch, but he certainly found one of the key ingredients."]},{"heading":"A Novel, a Prism and the Color Violet","paragraphs":["Before scientists worked out how to save a picture made by light, a storyteller imagined it. In 1760 a French writer named Charles-François Tiphaigne de la Roche published a strange fantasy novel called Giphantie. In it, a traveler visits a magical land where spirits have learned to coat a canvas with a sticky substance that captures the image of whatever is in front of it, like a mirror, and then dries so the picture stays forever, perfect in every detail. The author did not think of light-sensitive chemicals; he imagined the substance trapped the image because it was gooey. But he described the dream perfectly: a mirror that remembers.","Meanwhile, real science was creeping closer. Carl Wilhelm Scheele was born in 1742 in Stralsund, a town on the Baltic coast that then belonged to Sweden. At about fourteen he became an apprentice in a pharmacy, a shop where medicines were mixed by hand, and he spent much of his life working as a pharmacist in small Swedish towns, doing brilliant chemistry in his spare time. He discovered oxygen independently of other scientists, and he helped identify chlorine, barium, molybdenum and tungsten. He was elected to the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences in 1775.","In 1777 Scheele published a famous book on air and fire. In it he described experiments with silver chloride, a white powder that forms when silver meets chlorine. He knew it darkened in the light, and he wanted to know what was really going on. First, he looked at the dark material. He found that if he poured ammonia over the darkened powder, the unchanged white silver chloride dissolved away, but tiny black particles were left behind. Those particles were metallic silver. So light was breaking silver chloride apart, setting free specks of pure silver, which look black when they are that small.","Without knowing it, Scheele had also found a way to fix a light picture. Ammonia could wash away the silver chloride that light had not touched, leaving only the dark silver behind. If someone had put the two ideas together, photography might have arrived decades earlier. But nobody did. Early picture-makers either did not know about Scheele's result or did not realize what it meant.","Scheele's second discovery was about color. He used a glass prism, a triangular block of glass that splits sunlight into a rainbow, called a spectrum. He laid a piece of paper coated with silver chloride so that the rainbow fell across it. The paper did not darken evenly. Under the violet end of the rainbow it turned dark quickly, while under the red end it hardly changed at all. Violet light was far more powerful at causing the chemical change than red light.","Today we know why. Light comes in tiny packets of energy, and violet and blue light carry more energy per packet than red light. Only packets with enough energy can knock the silver chloride apart. This discovery had a very practical result much later. Photographers learned that they could work with light-sensitive paper and film under a dim red lamp without spoiling it. That is why old-fashioned darkrooms glow red. A few years later, in 1801, the German scientist Johann Wilhelm Ritter used the same kind of silver chloride test and found that the paper darkened even beyond the violet end of the rainbow, where the eye sees nothing. He had discovered invisible ultraviolet light. Silver, it turned out, could detect things human eyes could not.","Scheele died in 1786, at only 43. Doctors said the cause was mercury poisoning, most likely from years of handling dangerous chemicals. He never made a picture. But he had explained what light does to silver and had stumbled on a way to stop it, two clues that later inventors would need."]},{"heading":"Tom Wedgwood's Shadow Pictures","paragraphs":["The first person we know of who seriously tried to use light-sensitive silver to make pictures was a young Englishman named Thomas Wedgwood, usually called Tom. He was born in 1771, the son of Josiah Wedgwood, the famous potter whose factory made elegant plates, vases and cups that are still collected today. The Wedgwood family was rich, clever and interested in science. They were friends with many of the leading thinkers of the age, and Tom's sister Susannah later became the mother of Charles Darwin, which made Tom one of Darwin's uncles.","Tom Wedgwood was bright but often ill, and he never had a regular job. Instead he spent his time and his inheritance on ideas. He was a generous supporter of writers; in 1798 he and his brother gave the poet Samuel Taylor Coleridge an allowance of money so he could write instead of becoming a preacher. Tom's own passion was the question of how we see. Growing up around a pottery business, he would have known that artists were constantly copying images onto ceramics, and that the camera obscura was used as a drawing aid. Could light do the copying itself?","Around 1800 he began experimenting. He soaked sheets of white paper and white leather in silver nitrate, a silver compound that dissolves in water. Then he laid objects on top, such as leaves, the wings of insects or pieces of lace, and put them in the sunshine. Where the object blocked the light, the paper stayed white. Where the Sun shone straight on the paper, it darkened. When he lifted the object off, there was a white silhouette on a dark background: a shadow picture.","He found that white leather worked even better than paper, darkening faster. He also tried copying pictures painted on glass. Light passed through the clear parts of the glass painting and darkened the paper underneath, while the painted parts blocked it, so the paper recorded a reversed copy. This is called contact printing, and photographers used the same basic idea for more than a hundred years.","Notice something important about these pictures. Light areas in the original came out dark, and dark areas came out light. Wedgwood's shadow pictures were what we would now call negatives. That was not a big problem for a silhouette of a leaf, but it would matter a great deal later.","Wedgwood's biggest dream was to capture the image inside a camera obscura. He tried, and failed. The picture formed by the camera's lens was far dimmer than direct sunlight, and his silver nitrate paper was not sensitive enough. After a long time in the camera, he could get nothing at all, or only the faintest trace. Bright sunlight acting on paper for a few minutes was enough for a shadow picture, but a camera image was perhaps hundreds of times weaker."]},{"heading":"Davy's Report: Pictures to Be Seen by Candlelight","paragraphs":["Tom Wedgwood shared his experiments with a rising young scientist named Humphry Davy. Davy, born in 1778 in Penzance in Cornwall, was the son of a woodcarver and had started out as an apprentice to a surgeon and apothecary. He became famous very young for experiments with nitrous oxide, the gas nicknamed laughing gas, which he breathed himself to test its effects, and he noticed it might one day help with pain during surgery.","In 1801 Davy moved to London to work at the Royal Institution, a new organization set up to bring science to the public. He was a sensation. His lectures were full of flashes, bangs and dramatic demonstrations, and crowds of fashionable Londoners, including many women, who were shut out of universities at the time, packed the lecture theater to hear him. By 1802, aged just 23, he had become the Institution's full lecturer in chemistry.","In June 1802 Davy published a short paper in the very first issue of the Journals of the Royal Institution. Its long, old-fashioned title described it as an account of a method of copying paintings on glass, and of making profiles, by the agency of light upon nitrate of silver, invented by T. Wedgwood, with observations by H. Davy. A profile here meant a silhouette of a person's face, a popular kind of portrait at the time. This paper is often counted as the first published account of making images with light-sensitive chemicals.","The paper explained Wedgwood's methods and added some of Davy's own experiments. Davy found that silver chloride was more sensitive than silver nitrate. He also tried using a solar microscope, a device that used sunlight and a lens to project an enlarged image of something tiny, such as an insect, onto a screen. He managed to record these magnified images on prepared paper, though only when the paper was placed very close to the lens where the light was strongest. Some historians credit this as the first idea of photographic enlargement.","But the paper was also an honest report of failure. The camera obscura image, it admitted, was too faint to record in any reasonable time. And the pictures they did make could not be kept. Once a shadow picture was made, it had to be stored in the dark. It could be looked at only briefly, by the light of a candle or an oil lamp, which was too weak and too red to darken the silver much. Bring it out into daylight, even for a few minutes, and the white parts slowly darkened too, until the whole picture disappeared into an even brown-black.","Davy and Wedgwood tried washing the pictures and coating them with varnish, but nothing worked. Davy, busy with other research, moved on. He went on to discover the elements sodium and potassium using electricity, and later invented a safety lamp for coal miners. Tom Wedgwood, whose health was getting worse, died in 1805 at the age of 34. Neither man knew that the chemist Scheele had already found, in ammonia, a way to wash the unchanged silver salt away.","Did any of Wedgwood's pictures survive? For many years, people hoped so. In 2008 a faint photograph of a leaf was offered at auction, attributed to Wedgwood and described as possibly the oldest photograph in the world. The sale was withdrawn after doubts were raised, and later research by the photography historian Larry Schaaf suggested it was probably made around 1839 by a woman named Sarah Anne Bright. As far as anyone knows, all of Wedgwood's pictures faded away, just as he and Davy predicted."]},{"heading":"The Fixing Problem","paragraphs":["Wedgwood and Davy's paper made one problem crystal clear. Photography historians call it the fixing problem, and it is worth understanding, because it held up the invention of photography for decades.","Here is the trouble in simple terms. Imagine a sheet of paper coated all over with a light-sensitive silver salt. You place a leaf on it in the sunshine. Where light falls, the silver salt breaks down and leaves dark specks of silver. Under the leaf, the salt is untouched and stays white. So far, so good. You have a picture.","But the white areas are not really finished. They are still covered with fresh, unused silver salt, just waiting for light. The moment you take the paper out to look at it, daylight starts working on those white areas too. Slowly they darken, catching up with the rest of the paper. Soon there is no difference between the parts that were under the leaf and the parts that were not. The picture has not faded in the usual sense. It has drowned in darkness.","To fix the picture, you need to get rid of every bit of unused silver salt after the picture is made, while keeping the dark silver specks that form the image. Then there is nothing left that can react to light, and the picture stops changing. It becomes permanent. This is what photographers later called fixing, and the chemical that does it is called a fixer. You will meet the most famous fixer, nicknamed hypo, later in this story.","Why was this so hard? Silver salts like silver nitrate dissolve easily in water, but silver chloride does not. Ordinary washing removed some of the chemical, but not enough. Early experimenters needed a liquid that would dissolve the unused silver salt without also dissolving or damaging the tiny silver grains of the picture. They also needed to know what to look for, and the chemistry of the time was still young. Scheele's ammonia trick and, later, ordinary strong salt water would turn out to work partly, by making the leftover silver salt much less sensitive or carrying it away. But in 1802 nobody connected these clues.","There was a second problem, too: sensitivity. Paper soaked in silver nitrate needed bright direct sunlight to change quickly. The image inside a camera obscura is far dimmer, because only the light passing through the small lens opening reaches it. Inventors needed both a more sensitive material and a way to fix it. It would take two more decades before anyone managed the first, and nearly four before the whole puzzle was solved."]},{"heading":"Machines for Drawing","paragraphs":["While chemists struggled with silver, ordinary people still needed pictures. In the early 1800s there were no photographs in newspapers, no snapshots of family members and no pictures of faraway places except those drawn by hand. Travelers who wanted to remember a mountain or a ruin had to sketch it. Scientists who wanted to record a fossil or a flower under the microscope had to draw it. Anyone who could not draw well was stuck, and good artists were expensive.","Inventors therefore kept improving drawing aids. One of the most popular was the camera lucida, patented in 1806 by the English chemist and physicist William Hyde Wollaston. Wollaston, who also discovered the metals palladium and rhodium, made up the name to contrast with the camera obscura. Camera lucida means \"lit room\", because it worked in ordinary daylight.","Despite its name, the camera lucida is not a camera at all and does not project an image. It is a small glass prism with four carefully angled faces, mounted on a stand above a sheet of paper. The artist looks down past the edge of the prism. Half of the eye sees the scene in front, bounced through the prism by two reflections so that it is the right way up, and the other half sees the paper. The brain blends the two views, so the landscape seems to float on the paper like a ghostly overlay. The artist can then trace the main outlines with a pencil.","The camera lucida was light, small enough to carry on a journey and needed no darkened room. Artists, travelers and especially scientists used it. Naturalists and microscope users kept using versions of it to draw specimens well into the 1900s. But it was tricky to use. If you moved your head even slightly, the image slid across the paper. Some people found the double view confusing, and the device did not give you any shading or detail. It only helped you draw. One frustrated user of a camera lucida would play a very important part in the story of photography, as you will see.","Other gadgets competed too. There were physionotrace machines that helped trace the profile of a sitter's face and engrave small portraits. Silhouette artists cut profiles from black paper in minutes. Engravers and lithographers, who printed pictures from carved metal plates or specially treated stones, made copies of paintings for books and prints. Each of these methods showed how strong the demand was for accurate, affordable pictures. Whoever could make light draw a picture by itself, and keep it, would find an eager world waiting."]},{"heading":"An Inventor in Burgundy","paragraphs":["Now let us travel to the rolling countryside of Burgundy in eastern France, to meet the man who would finally make light leave a lasting mark. Joseph Niépce was born on 7 March 1765 in the town of Chalon-sur-Saône, where his father was a wealthy lawyer. While studying at a religious college in Angers, he took the name Nicéphore, which comes from Greek and means something like \"bringer of victory\".","His early life was full of adventure and upheaval. France was shaken by the Revolution, which began in 1789. Niépce joined the French revolutionary army in 1792 and served as an officer, but poor health forced him to leave. He then worked as a local administrator in the district of Nice, by the Mediterranean. In 1795 he resigned to devote himself to inventing with his older brother Claude, and by 1801 the brothers had returned to the family estate near Chalon, where they also grew beets and made sugar.","The Niépce brothers' first big invention was not a camera but an engine. They called it the Pyréolophore, from Greek words meaning roughly \"fire\", \"wind\" and \"carrier\". It was one of the earliest internal combustion engines, meaning the fuel burned inside the engine itself rather than in a separate boiler, as in a steam engine. A bellows blew air into a chamber, a small dose of fine, burnable powder was dropped in and lit by a glowing fuse, and the sudden puff of burning gas pushed water out of a pipe at the back of a boat, shoving the boat forward. At first the fuel was lycopodium powder, made from the dusty spores of a plant called clubmoss, which flashes into flame. Because it was expensive, the brothers switched to finely crushed coal dust mixed with a little resin.","In 1807 the engine pushed a boat weighing about 900 kilograms upstream on the River Saône at Chalon, with about a dozen bursts every minute. The brothers had presented their work to French scientists the year before, and on 20 July 1807 they received a ten-year patent signed by Napoleon Bonaparte. It was an astonishing machine for its time. But it never became a business. Claude eventually moved to England to promote the engine, and over the following years he spent much of the family fortune on failed schemes. Nicéphore, back in France, turned more and more to a different dream.","That dream grew partly from lithography, a new printing method invented in Germany in the 1790s, in which an image drawn with greasy crayon on a flat stone could be inked and printed many times. Lithography became a craze in France in the 1810s. Niépce was excited by it, but he could not draw well. So he wondered: could light draw the image onto the stone or plate for him?","By about 1816, working at his country house at Saint-Loup-de-Varennes near Chalon, Niépce was building small camera obscuras and putting paper coated with silver chloride inside them. Letters he wrote to his family that year describe his results. He did manage to capture views from his workroom window on paper. In one letter he describes pointing the camera at a birdhouse with the windows open and getting a faint image of the window frames. He called these camera pictures \"points de vue\", or viewpoints. It was a huge step: unlike Wedgwood, he had recorded an actual camera image.","But Niépce ran into the same wall as Wedgwood and Davy. His pictures were negatives: the bright sky came out dark and the dark buildings came out pale. He tried to think of ways to reverse them. Worse, he still could not fix them. When he brought the paper out to look at it, the unused silver chloride kept darkening, and the faint pictures slowly sank into darkness. None of these early paper images survives.","Many people would have given up. Niépce instead decided the problem might be the silver itself. Silver salts darkened in light, which was why his pictures came out as negatives, and they kept on darkening, which was why they would not last. What if he used a material that behaved the opposite way? He began testing all sorts of substances that react to sunlight. And he became more and more interested in one of the strangest: a sticky black tar, a natural asphalt dug from the ground near the Dead Sea, that did not darken in the Sun at all. Instead, where sunlight touched it, it slowly hardened."]}],"deepDive":"What happens chemically when silver salts darken? Silver chloride (AgCl) and silver nitrate (AgNO3) contain silver as positively charged ions. When a packet of light with enough energy strikes a silver chloride crystal, it frees an electron, which can join a silver ion and turn it into a neutral silver atom. Clusters of such atoms are so tiny that they scatter and absorb light, making the material look grey, purple or black rather than shiny. Scheele's 1777 prism experiment showed this reaction depends on color: violet light is effective and red light almost useless, which we now explain by photon energy, since violet photons carry nearly twice the energy of deep red ones. In 1801 Johann Wilhelm Ritter used the same darkening to detect invisible ultraviolet rays beyond the violet end of the spectrum. Historians argue about how close each pioneer came. Schulze is honored in Germany as a forerunner, but he never tried to form an image of a scene. Scheele effectively discovered both the action of light on silver and a partial fixer, ammonia, yet did not link them to picture-making. Wedgwood and Davy clearly aimed at pictures and published in 1802, so some call Wedgwood the first photographer, but none of his images survives and the 2008 leaf photogram once credited to him is now thought to date from about 1839. Niépce's 1816 silver chloride camera images were probably the first true camera photographs, but they were unfixed negatives that have long since vanished. There is also a dating debate: the Schulze experiment is often given as 1727, but the original publication was 1719, describing work from about 1717. Finally, note the size of the sensitivity problem. Direct summer sunlight is very roughly a hundred thousand lux, while the image on a camera obscura screen might be hundreds or thousands of times dimmer, which is why shadow pictures worked in minutes and camera pictures barely worked at all.","funFact":"Tom Wedgwood, who made some of the first pictures with light-sensitive silver around 1800, was the uncle of Charles Darwin: his sister Susannah was Darwin's mother.","check":{"q":"What was the big 'fixing problem' that stopped Wedgwood and Davy from keeping their pictures?","a":"The unused silver salts kept reacting to light, so the whole picture slowly darkened","wrong":["The paper caught fire in bright sunlight","The silver evaporated into the air overnight","The pictures could only be made at night by moonlight"]},"sources":[{"title":"Johann Heinrich Schulze","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann_Heinrich_Schulze"},{"title":"History of photography","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_photography"},{"title":"Thomas Wedgwood (photographer)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Wedgwood_(photographer)"},{"title":"Humphry Davy","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humphry_Davy"},{"title":"Camera lucida","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camera_lucida"},{"title":"Nicéphore Niépce","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nic%C3%A9phore_Ni%C3%A9pce"},{"title":"Pyréolophore","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyr%C3%A9olophore"},{"title":"The Niépce Heliograph","publisher":"Harry Ransom Center, University of Texas at Austin","url":"https://www.hrc.utexas.edu/niepce-heliograph/"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/02-1.jpg","alt":"Painted portrait of the French inventor Joseph Nicéphore Niépce by Léonard-François Berger, made after his death.","file":"Posthumous portrait of Nicéphore Niépce.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Posthumous_portrait_of_Nic%C3%A9phore_Ni%C3%A9pce.jpg","author":"Léonard François Berger","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/02-2.jpg","alt":"Old engraving of an artist using a camera lucida prism on a stand to trace a small statue onto paper.","file":"Camera Lucida in use drawing small figurine.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Camera_Lucida_in_use_drawing_small_figurine.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/02-3.jpg","alt":"Chemist Humphry Davy painted by Sir Thomas Lawrence, with his miners' safety lamp on the table beside him.","file":"Sir Humphry Davy, Bt by Sir Thomas Lawrence.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Humphry_Davy,_Bt_by_Sir_Thomas_Lawrence.jpg","author":"Thomas Lawrence","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4171},{"n":3,"title":"Niépce and the View from the Window","when":"1816 – 1833","summary":"A patient French inventor named Niépce coated a metal plate with sticky black tar and pointed his camera out of an upstairs window. The sun shone on it for hours, maybe even days. When he washed the plate, a ghostly view of rooftops and trees appeared, and it is still the oldest camera photograph in the world.","sections":[{"heading":"A Gentleman Tinkerer in Burgundy","paragraphs":["Imagine a big stone country house in Burgundy, in eastern France, surrounded by fields, barns and a courtyard with a pear tree. The house is called Le Gras, and it stands near the little village of Saint-Loup-de-Varennes, a few kilometres outside the town of Chalon-sur-Saône. In the 1820s, an upstairs room of this house was not a bedroom or a library. It was a workshop full of bottles, metal plates, lenses and wooden boxes, and it belonged to a man who could not stop experimenting.","His name was Joseph Nicéphore Niépce (say it 'nee-EPS'). He was born in Chalon-sur-Saône on 7 March 1765, the son of a wealthy lawyer. He had been a student, a soldier and a local official during the stormy years of the French Revolution and the wars that followed. Around 1801 he and his older brother Claude came home to the family estates and became what people then called gentlemen-farmers. They grew beets, helped make sugar, and spent a great deal of their time and money on inventions.","The two brothers dreamed big. As you saw in the last part, they built the Pyréolophore, one of the earliest internal combustion engines, which pushed a boat along a river by burning fuel in little puffs. They also experimented with ways to make dyes and with other machines. Claude eventually moved to England to try to sell their engine there, while Nicéphore stayed in Burgundy and turned more and more of his attention to a different puzzle: how to make pictures with light.","Part of his interest came from a craze sweeping France at the time called lithography, a new way of printing pictures from a flat slab of limestone. Artists drew on the stone with greasy crayon, then the stone was wetted and inked so that the ink stuck only to the greasy lines. Niépce loved the idea of printing pictures, but he was not a skilled artist. If only, he thought, light itself could draw the picture onto the plate for him.","By about 1816 he was already pointing small camera obscuras out of his windows. He called these views 'points de vue', which means 'points of view'. With paper soaked in silver chloride he managed to catch faint images, but they came out as negatives, with light and dark reversed, and he could not stop them from darkening further in daylight. Like Wedgwood and Davy before him, he had run straight into the fixing problem. So he went looking for a completely different kind of light-sensitive material."]},{"heading":"Sun Writing with Sticky Tar","paragraphs":["The material Niépce settled on sounds like something from a road crew, not an art studio. It was bitumen of Judea, a natural asphalt: a thick, dark, tar-like substance that seeps out of the ground in a few places in the world, including around the Dead Sea. (Judea was the old name for part of that region, which is how the bitumen got its name.) People had used it for thousands of years to waterproof boats and baskets. Printmakers in Niépce's time also used it, because acid could not eat through it, which made it useful for etching designs into metal.","Bitumen has a strange and wonderful property. When it sits in bright light for a long time, it slowly hardens. Chemists today explain that the light causes its long molecules to link together into a tougher network. Hardened bitumen no longer dissolves easily in certain oils. Bitumen that has stayed in the dark, on the other hand, still dissolves.","That was all Niépce needed. Here is his recipe, step by step. First, he dissolved powdered bitumen in oil of lavender, the same sweet-smelling oil used in perfumes. Second, he spread a very thin coat of this mixture over a carefully polished plate and let it dry. Third, he put the plate into a camera and pointed it at a scene, or he laid a printed picture right on top of the plate and set it in the sun. Fourth, after a long time in the light, he washed the plate with a mixture of lavender oil and white petroleum (a light, clear oil). Wherever light had struck strongly, the hardened bitumen stayed stuck to the plate. Wherever it had been dark, the soft bitumen washed away, leaving bare metal.","The result was a picture made of two things side by side: pale patches of hardened bitumen and darker patches of shiny bare metal. Because the bright parts of the scene became the pale bitumen, the image was a positive, not a negative, when viewed at the right angle. Niépce named his process héliographie, or heliography, from the Greek words 'helios' (sun) and 'graphein' (to write or draw). Heliography means 'sun writing'.","Niépce tried his bitumen on all sorts of surfaces: glass, copper, silver-coated copper, zinc, pewter and even limestone. On glass, he could look through the picture as if it were a window. On metal, he hoped that the bitumen could act as a mask for acid, so that the bare areas could be etched into grooves, inked and printed again and again like an engraving. In other words, he wasn't only trying to make a single picture. He was trying to invent a printing machine powered by the sun."]},{"heading":"Copying Pictures Without an Artist","paragraphs":["Before Niépce tackled the harder task of photographing the real world, he practised on something easier: copying existing pictures. Engravings were printed on fairly thin paper. If he soaked an engraving in oil, the paper became partly see-through. He could then lay it directly on a bitumen-coated plate, black lines down, and leave it in the sun. Light passed through the blank paper and hardened the bitumen beneath, but the black lines blocked the light, so the bitumen under them stayed soft and washed away. This method, with the original pressed right against the plate and no lens at all, is called contact printing.","In 1822 Niépce made what he and many historians regard as the first permanent image made by light: a heliographic copy of an engraved portrait of Pope Pius VII on glass. Sadly, it no longer exists. He later tried to make printed copies from it, and the plate was destroyed in the process. For an inventor this was the normal price of progress: the best evidence was often used up by the next experiment.","Others survive. From around 1825 there are heliographs of old engravings, including one showing a man leading a horse. A famous one from 1826 copies an engraving of Cardinal Georges d'Amboise, a French churchman of the 1500s. From about 1825 Niépce worked with a Paris engraver named Augustin François Lemaître, who helped etch plates and made ink prints on paper from them, showing that sun-made images could indeed be turned into printing plates.","Altogether, a small number of Niépce's heliographic plates and prints from the later 1820s survive in museums and libraries today. They look faint and brownish, a bit like old pencil rubbings. But they prove something astonishing. For the first time in history, a picture had been copied by light and chemistry alone, without a human hand drawing a single line.","There was a big difference, though, between copying an engraving and capturing the world outside. An engraving pressed against a plate in full sunshine sends a lot of light onto the bitumen. A camera obscura, by contrast, gathers only a little light from a scene and spreads it over the plate as a dim image. Bitumen was very slow to react even in direct sun. Making it record a dim camera image would test Niépce's patience like nothing before."]},{"heading":"The View from the Upstairs Window","paragraphs":["Sometime around 1826 or 1827, probably in the summer of 1827, Niépce climbed to his upstairs workroom at Le Gras and set up a camera obscura beside an open window. Inside the camera he placed a polished sheet of pewter, a grey metal made mostly of tin mixed with a little lead, coated with his thin layer of bitumen. The plate measured about 16.2 by 20.2 centimetres, roughly the size of a small notebook. Then he pointed the lens out at the view he saw every day: the rooftops and walls of the farm buildings around his courtyard, with trees and open sky beyond.","Then he waited. And waited. Nobody living today knows exactly how long that plate sat in the camera, but it was certainly many hours, and quite possibly several days. When Niépce finally took it out and washed it with his lavender oil and white petroleum, a picture remained. It was blurry, grainy and strange, but it was there: on the left, part of the upstairs of the house; a tall pigeon house or loft; a slanting barn roof in the middle; the tops of trees; and on the right, another wing of the building. It was the world outside his window, drawn by the sun itself.","Today this plate is known as View from the Window at Le Gras, and it is the oldest surviving photograph taken with a camera. Niépce had very likely made earlier camera views, and he had made heliographic copies of engravings before it, but every older camera image has been lost. This one, by a mixture of luck and care, is still with us almost two hundred years later.","If you look at a good reproduction, you might at first see only a jumble of grey shapes, a bit like looking at clouds. The trick is to find the roofline. Once your eye spots the triangle of the barn roof in the middle, the rest of the scene snaps into place, and you are suddenly standing in a French farmhouse two centuries ago, looking out of the same window as Niépce.","The real plate is even harder to see than the copies. It is a mirror-like sheet of metal with a very thin, pale layer on top, so the picture only appears when light hits it at just the right angle. Tilt it a little and it vanishes into a shiny blank. Niépce himself was modest about his results, treating them as only a first, uncertain step in a completely new direction. He was right on both counts: the step was uncertain, and the direction was completely new."]},{"heading":"Sunshine on Both Sides","paragraphs":["How long did the exposure really take? For many years, books gave a neat answer: about eight hours. That number came from a clever clue inside the picture itself. Look closely and the buildings seem to be lit by the sun on both their left and right sides at once. In real life, of course, the sun moves across the sky from east to west during the day. In the morning it lights one side of a building; by afternoon it lights the other. A picture showing sunshine on both sides must have been exposed through much of a day, while the sun swung across the sky.","More recent research suggests that eight hours may have been far too short. Researchers who carefully recreated Niépce's bitumen process, using his materials and methods, found that a camera image like this probably needed several days of light to form. If that is right, the plate may have sat in the camera through many sunrises and sunsets, catching the sun from many angles. This is why careful historians now say the exposure lasted 'hours, possibly days', rather than giving one exact number.","Either way, the long exposure explains why there are no people, animals or carts in the picture. Anything that moved simply did not stay in one place long enough to leave a mark. A cat strolling across the courtyard, a farm worker pushing a wheelbarrow, birds landing on the roof: all of them would have been far too quick. Only the buildings, which did not move for days, were recorded. For a photograph of the real world to include people, the exposure time would have to shrink from hours to minutes, and that would take another inventor and another chemistry.","It is worth pausing to imagine how this felt. Niépce had no light meter, no instructions and no one to ask. Every experiment meant coating a plate, waiting a long time, washing it and hoping. Most attempts failed. Some plates showed nothing at all; others came out as a fog. He worked like this for years, writing long letters to his brother Claude in England about every small success and setback. The View from the Window is not the result of one lucky afternoon. It is the reward for about ten years of stubborn trial and error."]},{"heading":"A Disappointing Trip to England","paragraphs":["In 1827, Niépce received worrying news: his brother Claude was seriously ill in England. Claude had been living near London for years, trying to find investors for their engine and spending much of the family fortune in the process. Nicéphore and his wife Agnès set out to visit him. On the way, they stopped in Paris, and there Niépce met a famous showman and painter named Louis Daguerre, who had heard about his experiments and was eager to talk. Keep that name in mind; it will matter a great deal.","When Niépce reached England in September 1827, he found Claude in poor health and, sadly, not entirely clear in his mind. Claude's grand hopes for the engine had come to nothing. While staying near London, Niépce met Francis Bauer, a talented botanical artist who painted plants for the royal gardens at Kew. Bauer looked at the heliographs Niépce had brought, including the View from the Window, and was impressed. He encouraged Niépce to present his discovery to the Royal Society of London, the most famous scientific society in Britain.","Niépce wrote a short paper, which he called a notice on heliography, and offered it to the Society. But there was a snag. Niépce did not want to explain his full method. He hoped one day to earn money from it, and he feared that if he gave away every detail, someone else would profit instead. The Royal Society had a rule against considering secret processes: if you wanted scientists to take your discovery seriously, you had to tell them how it worked so they could test it. Because Niépce kept the key steps to himself, the Society would not consider his paper.","Disappointed, Niépce returned to France in early 1828. Before he left, he gave his paper and several of his heliographs, including the View from the Window, to Francis Bauer. Claude died in England in 1828, destitute after spending so much on the brothers' inventions. It was a sad journey in every way. Yet by leaving his plates with Bauer, Niépce accidentally did something enormously important: he made sure that his oldest camera image would be kept safe in England, far from the experiments in his workshop that destroyed so many of his other pictures."]},{"heading":"Two Inventors Join Forces","paragraphs":["Back at Le Gras, Niépce knew his bitumen process had a serious weakness: it was simply too slow. A picture that needed hours or days of sunshine could never capture people, animals or busy streets. He needed better lenses, more sensitive materials, and money. Daguerre, the showman he had met in Paris, was well connected and full of energy, and he was also experimenting with light-sensitive chemicals. Daguerre had reportedly learned about Niépce through the Chevalier family, well-known Paris opticians who sold lenses to both men.","In December 1829 the two men signed a formal partnership agreement. Niépce, as the inventor of heliography, would share his secret process. Daguerre would contribute his own ideas, skill and energy, and help improve the method and make it known. Any profits would be shared. For the next few years, they wrote to each other regularly, reportedly sometimes using code numbers instead of chemical names so that nosy postal clerks or rivals could not steal their ideas.","Together they tried many new materials. One process they developed, around 1832, used the sticky residue left after distilling lavender oil, dissolved in alcohol, as the light-sensitive layer. They called it the physautotype. Like the later daguerreotype, a physautotype was a one-of-a-kind picture that looked positive or negative depending on how the light reflected off it. It was faster than the old bitumen method, but still far too slow for portraits.","Meanwhile both men also experimented with silver-coated plates. Letters between the partners from about 1831 and 1832 show that Daguerre was especially interested in polished silver treated with iodine fumes, which formed a thin light-sensitive layer. This would turn out to be the winning path, but neither man knew it yet."]},{"heading":"Lost, Found and Kept Safe in Texas","paragraphs":["On 5 July 1833, Nicéphore Niépce suffered a stroke at Le Gras and died, aged 68. He had spent so much of the family fortune on inventions that he died nearly penniless. The local town council paid for his grave in the little cemetery of Saint-Loup-de-Varennes, close to the house where he had made his sun pictures. He was buried without knowing that, just six years later, photography would be announced to the world and celebrated in newspapers across Europe and America. His son, Isidore Niépce, took his father's place in the partnership with Daguerre.","And what happened to the View from the Window? After Francis Bauer died in 1840, Niépce's plates were sold and passed from one British owner to another. In 1839, while Bauer was still alive, they had been shown at the Royal Society, as the excitement about new photographic inventions grew. After that, the oldest camera photograph in the world slowly faded from memory. By the early 1900s it had been put away, and for decades almost nobody knew where it was.","Then, in 1952, two photography historians, Helmut Gernsheim and his wife Alison, tracked it down after a long search. Helmut was a German-born photographer and collector living in Britain, and he was building one of the greatest collections of early photographs ever assembled. When he finally held the pewter plate, he realised he was looking at the beginning of photography itself.","Showing it to the world was harder than finding it. The image was so faint that ordinary photographs of it came out as a confusing grey smear. The Gernsheims asked an expert at the Kodak Research Laboratory in England to make a copy, and it took great effort to capture even part of what the eye could see on the plate. Helmut then retouched one copy heavily by hand, painting in details to make the scene clearer. For many years, until the late 1970s, that retouched version was the only one he allowed to be published, so many older books show a picture that is part Niépce and part Gernsheim.","In 1963, the University of Texas at Austin bought most of the Gernsheim Collection, thanks to Harry Ransom, a university leader who wanted to build a world-class library and research centre. Niépce's plate travelled across the Atlantic with thousands of other early photographs. Today it is kept at the Harry Ransom Center in Austin, where it is normally on display in the main lobby.","Looking after it is serious science. Between 2002 and 2003, experts from the Getty Conservation Institute in California studied the plate closely. They confirmed that the image really is made of bitumen and that the plate is pewter: mostly tin with lead, plus tiny traces of iron, copper and nickel. They also noticed small bumps that had formed near three of its corners, which catch the light and make the picture even harder to see. To protect it, the Getty team designed a special sealed display case filled with an inert gas instead of air, so there is no oxygen to slowly corrode the metal. Sensors keep watch on the conditions inside all the time.","So if you ever visit Austin, you can stand in front of a small dark frame and see, very faintly, the same rooftops that Niépce saw from his window almost two hundred years ago. It is one of the most precious objects in the history of science, and it fits in a frame smaller than a school notebook.","Niépce never became famous in his lifetime, and for many years after his death his partner's name was far better known. Today, though, historians agree that the very first surviving photograph from a camera is his, and his home in Burgundy has become a place of pilgrimage for photographers. The town of Chalon-sur-Saône, where he was born, has a museum of photography named after him.","But the story did not stop at Le Gras. When Niépce died in 1833, his partner was left alone in Paris with a pile of letters, notes, recipes and half-finished experiments. Daguerre was not a patient country gentleman. He was a theatre man, used to dazzling crowds. Holding Niépce's notes in his workshop, he made up his mind: pictures that took hours were no use to anyone. He would find a way to make the sun draw in minutes."]}],"deepDive":"Why was Niépce's camera image so slow, and why do historians argue about its exposure time? Bitumen of Judea is a complex natural mixture of large carbon-based molecules. Light, especially ultraviolet and blue light, slowly causes these molecules to cross-link into a tougher, less soluble network. That is a direct chemical change: every bit of hardening must be caused by light energy actually arriving at that spot. There is no amplification. The daguerreotype and later silver processes, by contrast, depend on a latent image. A few photons create tiny specks of silver, and then a developer multiplies the effect enormously. That difference in chemistry explains why the bitumen process needed hours or days, while Daguerre's needed minutes. The traditional figure of about eight hours came from the observation that sunlight seems to strike both sides of the buildings. In the 1990s and 2000s, researchers who reconstructed the process, notably the French researcher Jean-Louis Marignier, concluded that a camera image of this kind would need several days. That would also explain the double lighting, since the sun would light each side many times over. Because nobody recorded the exposure, the honest answer is 'hours, possibly days'. The date is also debated: Gernsheim first dated the plate to 1826, while later study of Niépce's letters points to the summer of 1827. Another debate concerns credit. Niépce's 1822 contact copy of an engraving may have been the first permanent image made by light, but it was destroyed, so the Le Gras plate holds the title of oldest surviving camera photograph. Historians also note that the plate's visibility depends heavily on lighting: the image consists of thin bitumen on reflective metal, so it can look positive, negative or invisible depending on viewing angle. Modern scientific study, including work by the Getty Conservation Institute and analysis by the Louvre's laboratory in 2007 using ion beams, has examined slow oxidation of the metal, which is why the plate now lives in an oxygen-free case. Finally, Niépce's secrecy shaped history. By refusing to publish his method in 1827, he lost the chance for early recognition, and the public announcement of photography in 1839 came under Daguerre's name instead.","funFact":"Niépce's printing plates were coated with the same kind of natural asphalt that ancient peoples used to waterproof boats, and he dissolved it in lavender oil, so his workshop probably smelled like a perfume shop mixed with a road crew.","check":{"q":"What material did Niépce use on the Le Gras plate that hardened where sunlight hit it?","a":"Bitumen of Judea, a natural asphalt","wrong":["Silver iodide","Melted beeswax","Egg white mixed with salt"]},"sources":[{"title":"View from the Window at Le Gras","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/View_from_the_Window_at_Le_Gras"},{"title":"The Niépce Heliograph","publisher":"Harry Ransom Center, University of Texas at Austin","url":"https://www.hrc.utexas.edu/niepce-heliograph/"},{"title":"Nicéphore Niépce","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nic%C3%A9phore_Ni%C3%A9pce"},{"title":"Heliography","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heliography"},{"title":"Louis Daguerre","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Daguerre"},{"title":"Physautotype","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physautotype"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/03-1.jpg","alt":"Niépce's View from the Window at Le Gras (about 1826-27), the oldest surviving camera photograph, in an enhanced 1950s copy.","file":"View from the Window at Le Gras, Joseph Nicéphore Niépce.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_the_Window_at_Le_Gras,_Joseph_Nic%C3%A9phore_Ni%C3%A9pce.jpg","author":"Joseph Nicéphore Niépce","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/03-2.jpg","alt":"Joseph Nicéphore Niépce in a printed portrait by Dujardin, copied from Léonard-François Berger's painting.","file":"Joseph Nicéphore Niépce..jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Nic%C3%A9phore_Ni%C3%A9pce..jpg","author":"Héliogravure von Dujardin, nach einem Gemälde von Léonard-François Berger…","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/03-3.jpg","alt":"Old photo of Niépce's framed metal plate copying a portrait of Cardinal d'Amboise, labelled his first heliographic result.","file":"Le cardinal d'Amboise. 1er résultat héliographique de N. Niépce. 1824. Zinc gravé - reproduction - btv1b108489114.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_cardinal_d%27Amboise._1er_r%C3%A9sultat_h%C3%A9liographique_de_N._Ni%C3%A9pce._1824._Zinc_grav%C3%A9_-_reproduction_-_btv1b108489114.jpg","author":"Bibliothèque nationale de France","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3836},{"n":4,"title":"Daguerre's Mirror with a Memory","when":"1822 – 1840s","summary":"Louis Daguerre was a Paris showman who painted giant scenes that seemed to come alive with light. After his partner Niépce died, he found a way to make photographs on shiny silver plates in minutes instead of hours. In 1839 France gave his invention to the whole world, and soon everyone wanted their portrait taken.","sections":[{"heading":"The Showman of the Diorama","paragraphs":["Picture yourself in Paris in the 1820s, buying a ticket to the most talked-about show in town. You walk down a dark corridor and step into a round, dim room with a few hundred other people. Ahead of you, through a great opening about seven metres wide and six metres tall, you see a mountain valley in Switzerland. As you watch, the light slowly changes. Clouds seem to drift, the sky darkens, and the valley sinks into evening. Then, with a low rumble, the whole room you are standing in turns slowly on a giant turntable, and a second scene swings into view: a ruined chapel, perhaps, with moonlight falling through its broken windows.","This was the Diorama, and the man behind it was Louis-Jacques-Mandé Daguerre. Daguerre was born on 18 November 1787 in Cormeilles-en-Parisis, a village near Paris. As a young man he trained in architecture and theatre design, and he worked as an assistant to Pierre Prévost, a painter of panoramas, the enormous 360-degree paintings that surrounded viewers in a circular building. Daguerre became a celebrated designer of stage scenery for Paris theatres, famous for clever lighting effects.","In 1821 he teamed up with another painter, Charles-Marie Bouton, and in 1822 they opened the Diorama in Paris. The trick lay in how the pictures were painted. Each scene was painted on huge sheets of linen, and some areas were painted so that they were partly see-through. Light could shine onto the front of the painting or through it from behind, controlled by skylights, shutters, screens and coloured blinds. By slowly changing which light was used, the operators could make day turn into night, or make a lamp glow in a window that had seemed dark. Each scene lasted about 10 to 15 minutes, and up to about 350 people at a time stood or sat on the turning floor.","Audiences were amazed, and many people believed they were looking at a real landscape. One of Daguerre's scenes, the Interior of Roslin Chapel in Scotland, was shown in Paris in the 1820s, and The Times newspaper in London later called it 'perfectly magical'. The Diorama was so popular that a second one opened in London's Regent's Park in 1823, and others followed in Berlin and elsewhere. At its height, Daguerre's Paris Diorama is said to have earned about 200,000 francs a year.","Notice what Daguerre's whole career was about: realistic images, light, and wowing a crowd. He spent his days thinking about how light falls on things and how to fool the eye into seeing reality. It is no wonder that, like many painters of his time, he used a camera obscura to help with his drawings, and wished its glowing image could somehow be kept. That wish is what led him to Niépce, and to the partnership you read about in the last part."]},{"heading":"Alone in the Workshop: The Hidden Picture","paragraphs":["When Niépce died in 1833, Daguerre carried on, working with Niépce's son Isidore as his new partner on paper, but mostly experimenting on his own. He focused on silver. He took a sheet of copper coated with a thin layer of silver and polished it until it shone like a mirror. Then he held the plate over iodine, a chemical whose purple vapour reacts with silver to form a pale yellow coating of silver iodide, a light-sensitive substance.","Silver iodide darkens in light, but slowly. If Daguerre waited until a visible picture appeared in his camera, it still took far too long. The great breakthrough, probably made around 1835, was the discovery of the latent image. 'Latent' means hidden. Daguerre found that after a much shorter exposure, when the plate still looked completely blank, light had already left an invisible trace in the silver iodide. If he then treated the plate with the right chemical, that hidden picture could be brought out, or developed, into a visible one.","The chemical he used was mercury, the silvery liquid metal once used in thermometers. When a plate was held over a dish of gently heated mercury, the mercury vapour settled on the places where light had struck and formed tiny, bright, whitish grains there. The more light a spot had received, the more mercury gathered on it. Slowly, as if by magic, a sharp, detailed picture appeared on the blank silver.","There is a famous story about how Daguerre found this out. According to the tale, he put an under-exposed plate away in a cupboard full of chemicals, and when he took it out a few days later, a picture had mysteriously appeared. He then removed the chemicals one by one until he realised that the culprit was mercury from a broken thermometer. It is a lovely story, but it is a legend. Modern historians have found no reliable evidence for it and think it was invented later to make the discovery sound more dramatic. Discoveries in chemistry usually come from long, patient experiments, and Daguerre's almost certainly did too.","There was still one problem: the plate had to be made permanent. Unused silver iodide would keep darkening in light and slowly ruin the picture. In 1837 Daguerre found that he could stop this by washing the plate in a hot, strong solution of ordinary table salt, which dissolved away most of the remaining light-sensitive silver. That year he made a still life of plaster casts, a wicker-covered bottle and other objects in a corner of his studio, which is one of the oldest surviving daguerreotypes. He named his new process the daguerreotype, after himself, and began looking for a way to make money from it."]},{"heading":"How to Make a Daguerreotype, Step by Step","paragraphs":["Making a daguerreotype was a little like following a difficult cooking recipe in which several of the ingredients were dangerous. Here is how a photographer in the early 1840s would have done it.","Step one: polish the plate. The photographer took a copper plate coated with silver and buffed it again and again with soft cloths and fine powders such as rottenstone and jeweller's rouge, until the silver was a flawless mirror. Any scratch or fingerprint would show up in the final picture. Some photographers also wiped the plate with a little weak nitric acid to clean it.","Step two: make it sensitive. In a dim room, the plate was placed face down over a box containing iodine crystals. The iodine vapour turned the silver surface a golden yellow colour. After 1840, photographers learned to add a second fuming with bromine or chlorine, which made the plate much more sensitive to light and greatly cut exposure times.","Step three: expose it. The plate, protected in a light-tight holder, was slipped into the camera. The photographer removed the lens cap, counted the seconds or minutes, and put the cap back. In 1839 a sunny outdoor view needed several minutes. Later, with better plates and lenses, a portrait in a bright studio could take just a few seconds to under a minute.","Step four: develop it. In the dark, the plate was placed at an angle in a box above a small dish of mercury, which was warmed by a spirit lamp underneath. Over several minutes, the mercury vapour brought out the hidden image. Through a small yellow window in the box, the photographer could watch the picture appear.","Step five: fix it. Daguerre first used hot salt water, but within a year or so most photographers switched to a chemical nicknamed 'hypo' (sodium thiosulfate), which dissolved the leftover silver iodide far better. You will meet the scientist behind hypo in the next part. Step six: protect it. From 1840, many photographers followed the advice of the French physicist Hippolyte Fizeau and gently heated the plate with a gold chloride solution, which made the image richer and tougher. Finally, because the surface could be smudged by the lightest touch, the plate was sealed behind glass and put in a small hinged case, often lined with velvet."]},{"heading":"Free to the World","paragraphs":["Daguerre had a wonderful invention, but he had a business problem. If he sold the method to one customer, that customer could simply tell everybody else, and the secret would be gone. He tried to sell shares in the process by subscription, but not enough people signed up. So he turned to one of the most famous scientists in France for help.","François Arago was director of the Paris Observatory, a leading physicist and astronomer, permanent secretary of the French Academy of Sciences, and also a member of the French parliament, the Chamber of Deputies. Daguerre showed him the process under a promise of secrecy, and Arago was thrilled. He saw at once how useful it could be for science: for copying ancient inscriptions, for astronomy, for making accurate maps and drawings. He decided France should buy the invention and give it away.","On 7 January 1839, Arago stood before the members of the French Academy of Sciences and told them about Daguerre's discovery. He described what the pictures looked like and how amazingly detailed they were, but he did not reveal the method. News spread quickly. Within days, newspapers across Europe were reporting that a Frenchman could make nature draw its own portrait in a few minutes. Many people could hardly believe it.","Then came the politics. Arago and others persuaded the French government to make a deal. Daguerre would receive a pension of 6,000 francs a year for the rest of his life, and Isidore Niépce, representing his father's share, would receive 4,000 francs a year. Part of Daguerre's larger pension is said to have been a reward for also revealing how he made his Diorama effects. In return, the full process would be published for anyone to use. The law was passed during the summer of 1839.","On 19 August 1839, at a crowded joint meeting of the Academy of Sciences and the Academy of Fine Arts in Paris, Arago explained the full method in detail. The French government declared it a gift 'free to the world'. Crowds gathered outside the building, eager for news. Within hours, opticians' shops in Paris were reportedly selling out of lenses and chemicals as people rushed to try it themselves.","There was one curious exception to 'free to the world'. A few days before the August announcement, Daguerre's agent, a man named Miles Berry, quietly took out a patent in England. That meant people in England and Wales had to pay a licence fee to make daguerreotypes, while photographers almost everywhere else could use it freely. It was a reminder that even a gift to the world could come with a little fine print."]},{"heading":"Daguerreotypomania!","paragraphs":["Daguerre quickly published a manual explaining his process, with drawings of the equipment. It was translated into many languages and reprinted again and again within months. In Paris, the firm of Alphonse Giroux began selling a complete official daguerreotype outfit: a big wooden sliding box camera with a lens, plus boxes for coating and developing, bottles of chemicals and everything else needed. It was heavy and expensive, but it is often called the first camera made for sale to the public.","Paris went wild. People carried cameras to bridges and boulevards and pointed them at famous buildings. A French cartoonist, Théodore Maurisset, drew a famous comic picture called 'La Daguerréotypomanie', meaning 'Daguerreotype mania', showing crowds rushing to buy cameras, people queuing for portraits and even gallows for sale to engravers, who joked that they would soon be out of work. The joke had a point. Painters of miniature portraits were indeed among those who lost business as photography spread.","The craze leapt across the Atlantic almost immediately. One of the first Americans to hear about it was Samuel Morse, the painter and inventor who later became famous for the telegraph and Morse code. Morse was in Paris in early 1839 and visited Daguerre, who showed him his pictures. Morse wrote home in amazement, and his letter was published in New York newspapers. Around the same time, disaster struck: on 8 March 1839 a fire broke out at the Diorama, destroying the theatre and many of his paintings. Luckily, much of his photographic equipment and notes were saved.","By the end of 1839, people were experimenting with daguerreotypes in Britain, the United States, Germany, Russia and beyond. Scientists used them to record the Moon, microscopic specimens and, in 1851, a total solar eclipse. Travellers carried them to Egypt to photograph the pyramids and ancient monuments. A new way of seeing the world had arrived."]},{"heading":"The First People Caught on a Plate","paragraphs":["One of the most famous of Daguerre's own pictures was taken from a window of his studio near the Diorama, looking down on one of the busiest streets in Paris, the Boulevard du Temple. It was made around eight o'clock one spring morning, usually dated to 1838. The street would have been full of carriages, carts, horses and walkers. But in the picture, the boulevard looks strangely empty, like a ghost town.","Why? Because the exposure lasted several minutes. Every carriage that rolled past and every person who strolled by moved too fast to leave a clear image. They passed through the scene like ghosts and vanished. Only things that stayed still were recorded: the buildings, the trees, the pavement.","But look down at the lower left corner of the picture. On the pavement, there is a small dark figure of a man standing with one leg raised, resting his foot on something. He seems to be having his boots shined, and a second, blurrier figure near him may be the bootblack doing the polishing. The customer stood still long enough, while his boots were being cleaned, to become part of history. This tiny figure is often called the first photograph of a living person. (Some researchers have wondered whether the bootblack is really a water pump, and there may be other faint figures, but the customer is clearly a person.)","Daguerre made at least three views of the boulevard that day, in the morning, at noon and in the evening. In October 1839, two of them were set into a frame and presented as a gift to King Ludwig I of Bavaria. The plates later went to a museum in Munich, where they were damaged over the years, especially during a failed restoration attempt in the 1970s. Luckily, the American photography historian Beaumont Newhall had photographed them in the 1930s, so good copies survive. The man with his shined boots never knew he had become the first famous unknown person in photography.","In Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, a young man named Robert Cornelius heard about the daguerreotype in 1839. He worked in his father's company, which made lamps and polished metal fittings, so he knew a lot about silver plating and chemistry. Around October 1839, he set up a homemade camera outside the family shop, removed the lens cap, ran into the frame and sat very still for a long time, possibly as long as ten to fifteen minutes. Then he ran back and covered the lens.","The result is one of the most charming pictures in history. Cornelius, aged about 30, appears slightly off-centre, with his arms crossed and his hair messy, staring at the camera with a mixture of curiosity and seriousness. On the back, he wrote: 'The first light Picture ever taken. 1839.' It may not have been absolutely the first photograph of a person, as you have just seen, but it is one of the earliest photographic portraits of a human being that still exists, and since he took it of himself, it is often called the world's first selfie.","Cornelius did not stop there. Working with a Philadelphia chemist named Paul Beck Goddard, who found ways to make plates more sensitive, he cut portrait exposures to under a minute. In May 1840 he opened one of the earliest photographic portrait studios in the world. A few months earlier, in New York, Alexander Wolcott and John Johnson had opened a studio using a camera with a concave mirror instead of a lens, which gathered more light. Within a couple of years, Cornelius returned to the family lamp business, which earned more money, but his self-portrait made him famous long after his death."]},{"heading":"Sit Still! Life in an Early Portrait Studio","paragraphs":["Everyone wanted their portrait taken. Before 1839, only fairly well-off people could afford to have a painter make their likeness. A daguerreotype cost money too, but far less than an oil painting, and it was fantastically exact. Soon photographers were opening studios in nearly every city, often on the top floor of a building with large glass windows or skylights, because they needed as much daylight as possible.","In the first years, sitting for a portrait could be hard work. Early lenses were dim, and exposures might last a minute or more. Sitters were told to stay perfectly still. Many studios used a posing stand, a hidden iron brace with a clamp that held the back of the head steady, a little like the neck rest on a dentist's chair. Others had sitters lean on tables or chairs. Parents of small children sometimes hid under a cloth behind the child, holding them steady, a trick that produced what are today called 'hidden mother' photographs.","Technology improved quickly. In 1840, the Hungarian-born mathematician Joseph Petzval designed a new portrait lens that let in many times more light than Daguerre's original lens; the Austrian firm Voigtländer began making it and a compact metal camera to go with it. Together with the extra bromine or chlorine fuming of the plates, the new lenses cut portrait exposures to well under a minute in good light, and sometimes to just a few seconds. That is one reason why the old idea that people in early photos never smiled only because exposures were long is too simple. Holding a smile for a few seconds is easy. Many people looked serious because a portrait was a formal occasion, and because the traditions of painted portraits favoured calm, dignified faces.","The daguerreotype business grew enormous, especially in the United States. By the early 1850s, Americans were making an estimated three million daguerreotypes a year. Famous studios in New York and Boston photographed presidents, writers and ordinary families alike. For the first time, a farmer's family could have an exact image of their grandmother, a soldier could carry a picture of his sweetheart, and grieving parents could keep a picture of a child who had died."]},{"heading":"A Mirror That Remembers","paragraphs":["If you hold a real daguerreotype in your hand, it feels like holding a tiny, precious mirror. Look at it straight on and you might see only your own reflection. Tilt it slightly against a dark background and the picture leaps out, sharp and silvery, with astonishing detail. Daguerreotypes are so detailed that you can sometimes read shop signs across a street or count the threads in a lace collar with a magnifying glass. An American writer, Oliver Wendell Holmes, later described photography as 'the mirror with a memory', and the phrase fits the daguerreotype perfectly.","But the daguerreotype also had real drawbacks. Each one was unique: the picture was made directly on the plate, and there was no negative from which to print copies. If you wanted two portraits, you had to sit twice, or someone had to make a daguerreotype of the daguerreotype. The image was also usually reversed left to right, just like a reflection, so a man's coat buttons might appear on the wrong side, and the writing on a sign would appear backwards unless the photographer used a mirror or prism on the camera to flip it.","The surface was fragile too. A finger could wipe the image away, and the silver tarnished if exposed to air. That is why daguerreotypes were kept in small hinged cases made of wood covered with leather or paper, lined with velvet or silk, and sealed behind a glass cover. Later, from the mid-1850s, decorated cases were moulded from a tough early plastic made of shellac and sawdust, called Union cases.","There was a hidden danger, as well. Mercury vapour is poisonous to breathe, and the developing boxes released it into small, poorly ventilated darkrooms. People at the time knew mercury could be harmful, but few took proper precautions, and some daguerreotypists suffered from shaking, headaches and other symptoms of mercury poisoning. Today, museums and modern artists who still make daguerreotypes use careful safety equipment.","By the mid-1850s, cheaper photographs on glass and paper had largely replaced the daguerreotype, as you will see in later parts. Yet for about fifteen years, Daguerre's shining plates were how the world first saw itself in photographs. Daguerre himself retired to the village of Bry-sur-Marne near Paris, where he died on 10 July 1851. His name is one of 72 names of French scientists and engineers engraved around the Eiffel Tower.","There is one more twist in this story. When the news from Paris arrived in England in January 1839, it reached a quiet country house called Lacock Abbey. There, a gentleman scientist named William Henry Fox Talbot had been secretly making pictures with light for years, on paper and by a completely different method. Reading about Daguerre's triumph, Talbot felt a jolt of alarm. Had someone else just beaten him to the greatest invention of his life?"]}],"deepDive":"What actually happens on a daguerreotype plate? Fuming polished silver with iodine forms a very thin layer of silver iodide, and adding bromine or chlorine creates mixed silver halides that are far more sensitive. When light strikes these crystals, it frees electrons that gather at small defects and reduce a few silver ions to metallic silver atoms. A cluster of just a handful of atoms is enough to form a latent image site. In development, mercury vapour condenses selectively on these sites and forms a silver-mercury amalgam, which builds up into tiny particles a fraction of a micrometre across. These particles scatter light and look white, while the bare polished silver between them reflects like a mirror and looks dark when it reflects a dark surrounding. That is why a daguerreotype flips between positive and negative depending on the viewing angle. Fizeau's gold toning of 1840 coats the particles with gold, strengthening them and warming the tone. The latent image principle was the decisive leap. A direct process like Niépce's bitumen needs light to do all of the chemical work; a developed process lets light do only a tiny amount, which chemistry then multiplies millions of times. This turned exposures of hours into minutes, and later, with brighter lenses such as Petzval's portrait lens of 1840, which had an aperture of about f/3.6 instead of around f/14 or slower, into seconds. Because each f-stop halves or doubles the light, that change in lens alone meant roughly fifteen times more light. Historians debate several points. The broken-thermometer story is widely regarded as a later legend, and letters from 1831–1832 suggest the idea of the iodized silver plate came from Daguerre rather than Niépce, while the debt to Niépce's partnership and earlier work is also clear. Credit for the 'first photograph of a person' depends on definitions: the Boulevard du Temple figure is the earliest verified, though a daguerreotype portrait possibly from 1837 and Bayard's lost self-portraits are sometimes mentioned. Finally, the French state's purchase is often celebrated as an early example of open technology, though the English patent shows the gift was not quite universal.","funFact":"Daguerre's Paris street scene of the Boulevard du Temple shows a busy road as almost empty, because every moving carriage and walker vanished during the minutes-long exposure, leaving only a man who stood still to have his boots shined.","check":{"q":"Which substance did Daguerre use to develop the hidden (latent) image on his silver plates?","a":"Heated mercury vapour","wrong":["Lavender oil","Melted bitumen","Lemon juice"]},"sources":[{"title":"Louis Daguerre","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Daguerre"},{"title":"Daguerreotype","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daguerreotype"},{"title":"Boulevard du Temple (photograph)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boulevard_du_Temple_(photograph)"},{"title":"Robert Cornelius","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Cornelius"},{"title":"Diorama","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diorama"},{"title":"François Arago","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7ois_Arago"},{"title":"Louis Daguerre (French)","publisher":"Wikipédia","url":"https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Daguerre"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/04-1.jpg","alt":"Daguerre's 1838 Boulevard du Temple: the busy street looks empty, but a man having his boots shined appears at lower left.","file":"Boulevard du Temple by Daguerre.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boulevard_du_Temple_by_Daguerre.jpg","author":"Louis Daguerre","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/04-2.jpg","alt":"Robert Cornelius's 1839 daguerreotype self-portrait, arms crossed, one of the earliest photographs of a person.","file":"RobertCornelius.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RobertCornelius.jpg","author":"Robert Cornelius","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/04-3.jpg","alt":"An original 1839 Giroux daguerreotype camera, a wooden box with a brass lens and Daguerre's signed label, in Barcelona.","file":"Cámara para obtener vistas al daguerrotipo, original del año 1839, conservada en Barcelona, España, Spain.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%C3%A1mara_para_obtener_vistas_al_daguerrotipo,_original_del_a%C3%B1o_1839,_conservada_en_Barcelona,_Espa%C3%B1a,_Spain.jpg","author":"CARLOS TEIXIDOR CADENAS","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3881},{"n":5,"title":"Talbot, Negatives and the Race of 1839","when":"1833 – 1850","summary":"An English gentleman who could not draw found a way to let sunlight draw for him on paper. His trick was a backwards picture called a negative, which could print copy after copy. Meanwhile, inventors in France and Brazil were racing to the same idea.","sections":[{"heading":"A Bad Artist on a Beautiful Lake","paragraphs":["Picture a sunny October day in 1833 on the shores of Lake Como in northern Italy. Mountains rise straight out of blue water, villas glow pink and yellow along the edge, and a 33-year-old Englishman is sitting with a sketchbook, getting more and more annoyed. His name is William Henry Fox Talbot, and he is trying to draw the view. He is failing.","Talbot was not lazy, and he was certainly not stupid. Born on 11 February 1800 at Melbury House in Dorset, he had studied at Trinity College, Cambridge, finishing in 1821 among the top mathematics students of his year. He wrote papers on light, coloured flames and mathematics, and in 1831 he was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society, Britain's most famous club of scientists. His family home was Lacock Abbey in Wiltshire, a rambling stone house that had begun life in 1232 as a nunnery. He could read ancient languages, study plants and do hard sums. What he could not do was draw.","To help, he was using a camera lucida, the drawing aid you met in Part 2. It is a small glass prism on a stick. When you look down through it, you seem to see the scene and your paper at the same time, as if the view were floating on the page, so you can trace it. In theory, anyone can then draw like a professional. In practice, the moment Talbot moved his eye, the ghostly image slid around, and when he took his eye away he found, as he later wrote, only a few sad, wandering pencil lines on the paper.","He remembered that years before he had tried the older trick, the camera obscura, which throws a bright image onto paper inside a box. That had not gone well either. But the memory of those lovely little pictures sitting on the paper set him thinking. Talbot later described the moment in his own words in a short history he published in 1844. He was, he said, \"amusing myself on the lovely shores of the Lake of Como, in Italy\" when an idea struck him: what if the natural images of the camera could be made to print themselves on paper and stay there?","It was the same dream Thomas Wedgwood and Humphry Davy had chased in 1802, and the same one Niépce and Daguerre were quietly working on in France. Talbot did not know how far the French had got. He only knew that light changes silver, and that he was a good enough chemist to find out how."]},{"heading":"Photogenic Drawing: Salt, Silver and Sunlight","paragraphs":["Talbot returned to England in January 1834 and began experimenting at Lacock. His recipe sounds almost like cooking. First he soaked a sheet of good writing paper in a weak solution of ordinary table salt, which is sodium chloride, and let it dry. Then he brushed one side with silver nitrate dissolved in water. Where the two chemicals met inside the fibres of the paper, they made silver chloride, a light-sensitive silver halide. (Remember the glossary word: a silver halide is silver joined to chlorine, bromine or iodine.)","Now he laid things on top of the paper: a leaf, a feather, a piece of lace, and pressed them flat under glass. He set the sandwich out in the sun. Over minutes, the uncovered paper darkened to a deep purple-brown, while the paper under the leaf stayed pale. When he lifted the leaf off, there was its outline in white, every vein showing, on a dark background. These were contact prints, made by touching an object to the paper, and Talbot later called the whole technique photogenic drawing, which means a drawing produced by light.","He made a surprising discovery along the way. You might guess that more salt would make the paper more sensitive. Instead he found the opposite. As he put it, \"a lesser quantity of salt produced a greater effect.\" The trick was to leave a little extra silver nitrate in the paper compared with the salt. By brushing on more silver, drying, and brushing again, he made paper sensitive enough to catch the dim image inside a camera.","That still left the old problem from Part 2. Unused silver salt in the white parts of a picture would keep darkening whenever it saw daylight, until everything turned the same dull colour. Talbot found a partial fix. He soaked finished pictures in a strong solution of salt, or in potassium iodide, which made the leftover silver much less sensitive. He called this stabilizing rather than truly fixing. It worked well enough that some of his 1830s pictures still survive, though many have faded and yellowed.","Camera pictures were much harder than contact prints. Inside a camera, the light reaching the paper is weak, and in 1834 Talbot's exposures of buildings were too faint to show detail. So he built small wooden cameras with short lenses, which made tiny but brighter images, and he scattered several around the grounds at Lacock to work at once. With paper given repeated washes of salt and silver, he wrote, a bright day could produce a picture in about ten minutes."]},{"heading":"The Window Smaller Than a Stamp","paragraphs":["In August 1835, Talbot pointed one of his little cameras at a tall window in the south gallery of Lacock Abbey. It is an oriel window, a kind of bay window that sticks out from the wall, divided by stone bars and filled with a lattice of small diamond-shaped glass panes. Bright daylight came through the glass and the dark stone frame stood out sharply against it. That strong contrast made it perfect for a slow, weak process.","The result is now the oldest known surviving camera negative in the world. It is astonishingly small. The image itself measures only about 35 by 29 millimetres, roughly the size of a large postage stamp. Look closely and you can see the stone bars of the window and the crisscross pattern of the panes. But something is strange about it: the window glass, which was the brightest thing in the scene, looks dark, and the shadowy stone looks pale.","That is what a negative is. Light darkened the silver, so the brightest parts of the scene became the darkest parts of the picture. A bright sky turns black and a dark coat turns white. At first this seems like a failure. Talbot realised it was actually a gift. If you put a negative on top of a fresh sheet of sensitive paper and shine light through it, the dark parts of the negative block the light and the pale parts let it through. The new print comes out reversed a second time, which means the right way round. And because the negative itself does not get used up, you can do it again and again.","This idea, which later historians call the negative-positive process, is the most important thing in this part. It is how almost all film photography worked for about 150 years. Every school photo printed on paper from film, every holiday snapshot handed out in envelopes in the 1980s, comes from this little stamp-sized window.","The famous negative is now in the collection of the Science Museum Group in Britain and is cared for at the National Science and Media Museum in Bradford. It is kept mostly in the dark, because even today light can damage it. Lacock Abbey itself belongs to the National Trust, and the Fox Talbot Museum there tells his story. Harry Potter fans may recognise the cloisters of Lacock, too: scenes from several of the films were shot there.","Then Talbot did something that, in hindsight, seems almost unbelievable. He put photography aside. He was busy with mathematics, optics, plants and the running of a large country estate, and he seems to have felt there was no hurry. He did not publish anything about his pictures. For more than three years, the oldest camera negative in the world sat quietly among his papers."]},{"heading":"January 1839: The Race Begins","paragraphs":["On 7 January 1839, as you saw in Part 4, François Arago stood up at the French Academy of Sciences in Paris and announced that Louis Daguerre had found a way to fix the images of the camera. Arago did not explain how the process worked; he only described the astonishing results. Within days the news was in newspapers across Europe.","Imagine Talbot reading it at Lacock. He had been making camera pictures since 1835, and now a Frenchman was about to get all the credit. Worse, he could not tell from the reports whether Daguerre's method was the same as his. If it was, the world might think Talbot had copied it. He moved fast.","On 25 January 1839, less than three weeks after Arago's speech, some of Talbot's photogenic drawings went on show at the Royal Institution in London, the famous science hall where the great experimenter Michael Faraday gave lectures. Within about a fortnight, Talbot sent a paper to the Royal Society explaining his work, and a few weeks later he gave the full details of how to make sensitive paper and how to stabilize the images. Unlike Daguerre, who was still keeping his recipe secret while the French government decided how to reward him, Talbot told everyone exactly what he did.","Then the two methods were finally compared, and they turned out to be completely different. Daguerre's picture was a single, mirror-like image on a polished silver plate. Talbot's was a negative on paper. Daguerre's was far sharper and more detailed; people gasped at the tiny letters you could read on signs with a magnifying glass. Talbot's paper images were soft and fuzzy, because the light spread out through the fibres of the paper. But Talbot's could be copied.","There was one more twist in the race. France gave Daguerre's process to the world free on 19 August 1839. But a few days earlier, Daguerre's agent had taken out a patent on it in England and Wales. So the free gift was not quite free everywhere: in England, you were supposed to pay for a licence to make daguerreotypes. Patents, as we will see, became a big part of Talbot's story too."]},{"heading":"Sir John Herschel: The Fixer and the Word-Maker","paragraphs":["Talbot's most helpful friend in all this was Sir John Herschel, one of the most famous scientists in Britain. Herschel was an astronomer, the son of William Herschel who had discovered the planet Uranus. From 1834 to 1838 he had lived at Feldhausen near Cape Town in southern Africa, mapping the stars of the southern sky with a telescope about 21 feet long. He was back in England, full of energy, just as the photography news broke.","Herschel had an important secret weapon, though he had not realised it was a weapon. Twenty years earlier, in 1819, he had studied a chemical then called hyposulphite of soda, and found that it dissolves silver salts that water cannot touch. Today chemists call it sodium thiosulfate, but photographers still call it hypo for short.","When Herschel heard about the new pictures in early 1839, he started experimenting himself within days. He quickly saw that hypo was exactly the answer to the fixing problem. Wash a finished picture in hypo, and it dissolves away all the unused silver salt, the silver that light has not touched yet. With nothing sensitive left in the white areas, the picture can sit in sunlight without darkening. Herschel generously told both Talbot and Daguerre. Talbot was using hypo for most of his prints by the early 1840s, and Daguerre's process adopted it as well. Hypo, or its close cousin ammonium thiosulfate, is still the standard fixer in darkrooms today, nearly two centuries later.","Herschel also gave the new art its name. Talbot said photogenic drawing; Daguerre said daguerreotype. In 1839 Herschel began using the word photography, from Greek words meaning light and writing, and he was the first to use the words negative and positive for the two kinds of photographic image. He talked about them in papers read to the Royal Society in March 1839 and January 1840. These words were so useful that they spread across the world. Every time you say photo, you are using a word Herschel helped make popular.","Herschel kept inventing. On 9 September 1839 he made an image on glass of the wooden frame of his father's giant 40-foot telescope, one of the earliest photographs on glass. In 1842 he invented the cyanotype, a completely different kind of photograph that uses iron instead of silver. Paper is coated with two iron chemicals, ferric ammonium citrate and potassium ferricyanide. Where sunlight hits, they react to form Prussian blue, the same deep blue used in paints. You simply wash the paper in plain water, and the unexposed chemicals rinse away, leaving a white picture on a brilliant blue background. The cyanotype later became the blueprint, used for copying engineering and architects' drawings until about the 1940s. That is why we still call a detailed plan a blueprint."]},{"heading":"Forgotten Rivals: Bayard in Paris, Florence in Brazil","paragraphs":["The race of 1839 had more than two runners. In Paris, a quiet civil servant named Hippolyte Bayard, who worked at the Ministry of Finance, had been experimenting on his own. By March 1839 he had invented a way to make a positive picture directly on paper, inside the camera, with no negative needed. His pictures were soft and gentle, like Talbot's, but each one came out the right way round.","In the summer of 1839, Bayard put about thirty of his photographs on display at a charity exhibition in Paris. Many history books give the date as 24 June 1839 and call it the world's first public exhibition of photographs, though some French sources say the show actually opened in July. Either way, Bayard was showing real photographs on paper to the public before the full daguerreotype method had been revealed.","Here is where his luck ran out. François Arago, the powerful scientist who was promoting Daguerre, persuaded Bayard to wait before announcing his process. Arago was Daguerre's friend and wanted the daguerreotype to be France's great invention. The government gave Bayard 600 francs to buy better equipment. Daguerre and Isidore Niépce, by comparison, received lifetime pensions totalling 10,000 francs a year. When Bayard finally described his method to the Academy of Sciences in February 1840, the world was already talking about daguerreotypes.","Bayard answered with one of the cleverest protests in history. In October 1840 he made a photograph of himself slumped against a wall, eyes closed, shirtless, with a cloth over his lap, posed as if he were a drowned body. On the back he wrote a mocking note, pretending to be someone describing the corpse. It said, roughly, that this was the inventor Bayard, who had drowned himself in despair because the government had given so much to Daguerre and nothing to him. Of course, Bayard was perfectly alive and took the photo himself. Self Portrait as a Drowned Man is now often called the first staged or faked photograph, a reminder, as Part 17 will show, that photographs could tell stories that were not true almost from the very beginning. Bayard went on to help found the French Photographic Society and to photograph historic French buildings for a government survey in 1851.","Meanwhile, thousands of kilometres away in Brazil, another forgotten inventor had been at work even earlier. Hercules Florence was born in Nice in 1804, sailed to Rio de Janeiro in 1824, and worked as an artist on a Russian-sponsored scientific expedition into the Amazon from 1826 to 1829. He settled in the small town of Campinas, where he invented his own printing method, which he called polygraphia. Wanting a cheap way to print labels and documents, he began experimenting with silver nitrate on paper around 1832 or 1833, with help from a local pharmacist, Joaquim Corrêa de Mello.","Florence's notes use a word meaning photography, photographia or photographie, in the early 1830s, years before Herschel made the word famous. He could not fully fix his images, and when news of the daguerreotype reached Brazil in 1839 he gave up the project. Because he lived far from Europe's scientific societies and published little, almost nobody knew about him until historians, especially the Brazilian scholar Boris Kossoy, rediscovered his notebooks in the 1970s. Today a Brazilian institute, the Instituto Moreira Salles, holds an 1833 image by Florence that it describes as the oldest photographic record in the Americas. Florence, by the way, also invented a way to write down animal sounds as music, and he had 20 children."]},{"heading":"The Calotype: A Beautiful Picture in a Minute","paragraphs":["Talbot's paper pictures had one big weakness: they were slow. In the camera, photogenic drawing paper needed an hour or more in bright light, because the silver had to darken all by itself until the picture was visible. Daguerre had already solved that problem with the latent image, an invisible picture that a developer brings out. On 23 September 1840, Talbot found his own way to do the same thing on paper.","His new paper was coated with silver iodide. Just before use, he brushed it with a mixture he called gallo-nitrate of silver, made with gallic acid, a chemical that comes from oak galls (the round growths wasps cause on oak trees), plus silver nitrate. After a short exposure, the paper looked completely blank. But when he brushed on more gallo-nitrate and warmed the paper gently, a picture slowly bloomed out of nothing, like invisible ink appearing over a candle. The light had left a hidden mark, and the chemistry made it visible.","Instead of an hour, an exposure in bright sunshine now took about a minute or two. That was short enough for portraits, as long as the sitters were patient. Talbot named the process the calotype, from the Greek word kalos, meaning beautiful. Some people called it the talbotype in his honour.","The paper negative was then fixed, often waxed to make it more see-through, and laid on salted paper in the sun to print as many positive copies as anyone wanted. The prints were usually warm brown or reddish. Because the light passed through paper fibres twice, once in the negative and once in printing, calotypes had a soft, slightly grainy look, more like a charcoal drawing than a mirror. Artists loved that. People who wanted crisp detail preferred daguerreotypes.","Talbot patented the calotype in England in February 1841. If you wanted to use it, you needed a licence. Licences were expensive: one historian notes that Talbot took about 30 percent of a licensed studio's takings. In August 1841 Henry Collen became the first licensed professional calotype photographer.","English patents did not apply in Scotland, and there the calotype flourished. In Edinburgh, the painter David Octavius Hill and the young photographer Robert Adamson made around 3,000 photographs from calotype negatives between 1843 and 1848, including fishing families, churchmen and city scenes. In 1845 they photographed Peter Jones, also called Kahkewaquonaby, an Ojibwe minister visiting Scotland. That calotype is one of the oldest surviving photographs of a Native North American person. Their work showed that the soft paper picture could be real art."]},{"heading":"Seaweed in Blue and the Pencil of Nature","paragraphs":["One of the first people to see what photography could do for science was a woman named Anna Atkins. Born in Tonbridge, Kent, in 1799, she lost her mother as a baby and grew up close to her father, John George Children, a chemist and natural scientist at the British Museum. Both her father and her husband were friends of Talbot, so Atkins heard about photography almost as soon as it existed. She was also a skilled botanist who collected seaweeds and other algae.","Drawing seaweed accurately is maddening. The fronds are lacy, wavy and branching in every direction. Atkins realised that Herschel's brand-new cyanotype could do the job perfectly. She laid each dried specimen directly on cyanotype paper, set it in the sun, then rinsed the sheet in water. Out came a white silhouette of the seaweed, every delicate branch in place, floating on deep blue like an underwater ghost.","In October 1843 she published the first part of Photographs of British Algae: Cyanotype Impressions. Even the text pages were made the same way, from her own handwriting printed as cyanotypes. It is often called the first book illustrated with photographs. Atkins kept adding parts for about ten years, and complete copies hold hundreds of plates; the Royal Society's copy has 389, and one at the Horniman Museum in London has 443. Only 17 copies are known to survive, so they are fantastically valuable: one sold at auction in 2004 for 229,250 pounds. Later, with her friend Anne Dixon, she made more cyanotype albums, including one of ferns in 1853.","Talbot, meanwhile, wanted to show the world what his negatives could do. In 1844 he began publishing The Pencil of Nature, the title meaning that light itself was doing the drawing. It came out in six parts between 1844 and 1846, with 24 real photographic prints pasted in by hand, and it is usually called the first commercially published book illustrated with photographs. (Atkins's book came first but was privately made for friends and scientists, not sold in shops.) Each plate came with a short essay explaining what photography might be good for: copying old documents, recording the china on your shelves in case a thief stole it, preserving buildings, or making art. The Open Door shows a broom leaning in a sunlit stone doorway, and The Haystack shows a ladder against a huge stack of hay; both look almost like paintings.","To print so many photographs, Talbot's assistant set up a workshop in Reading in 1844, sometimes called the Reading Establishment, where rows of printing frames sat out in the sun. It was a bold idea, a photograph factory, but it lost money. Many prints from the period faded, because the chemistry of fixing and washing was still poorly understood. The Pencil of Nature did not sell well either, and Talbot stopped after six parts, although he had planned more. About 40 nearly complete copies survive.","Talbot's patents also slowed things down. Many photographers felt he was trying to control the whole art, and in 1854 he took a photographer named Martin Laroche to court. The jury agreed Talbot's patent was valid, but decided that Laroche, who used the newer glass-plate method you will meet in Part 7, was not breaking it. Talbot did not renew his patent after that. By then the race had a clear winner, and in a surprising way, it was both inventors. Daguerreotypes were sharper and dominated the 1840s, but they were one of a kind. Talbot's idea of a negative that could make endless copies won in the long run, and it ruled photography until the age of digital cameras.","Yet nobody in 1850 could fully explain what was actually going on inside the silver. How does a beam of light leave an invisible mark? Why does a lens make a sharper picture than a pinhole? What does a developer really do? To find out, the next part opens up the camera and looks inside."]}],"deepDive":"Who invented photography? Historians today mostly refuse to name one person, and the race of 1839 shows why. Niépce made the oldest surviving camera photograph around 1826 to 1827. Talbot made camera negatives by 1835. Florence was writing about his own experiments in Brazil around 1833 to 1834. Bayard made direct positives by early 1839. Daguerre's process was the first to be announced to the world and the first to become a practical, widely used method. Each person solved a different part of the problem, and several worked without knowing about the others. This pattern, called multiple or simultaneous invention, happens often in the history of technology, because many people are building on the same earlier discoveries, in this case Schulze's work on silver salts and centuries of camera obscura experience.\n\nThe chemistry of the calotype is also worth a closer look. Talbot's photogenic drawing was a printing-out process: the light itself had to make enough metallic silver to see, which needs a lot of light. The calotype was a developing-out process: the light only had to make a tiny invisible change, and the gallic acid developer did the rest, multiplying the effect enormously. That is why exposures fell from about an hour to a minute or two. Every film camera of the next 150 years used developing-out, not printing-out.\n\nThere is a real debate about Talbot's patents. Some historians argue that his licence fees and lawsuits slowed the spread of photography in England in the 1840s and early 1850s, pointing out that the calotype flourished in Scotland and France, where his English patent did not apply. Others note that Talbot spent a great deal of his own money on research and on The Pencil of Nature, and that patents were normal for inventors of his day. Daguerre, by contrast, was paid a state pension, so he could afford to let France give his process away, except in England, where his agent took out a patent.\n\nFinally, words matter. Herschel's terms photography, negative and positive were so clear that they replaced everyone's own inventions, which is one reason he is sometimes called a hidden founder of photography even though he never claimed to have invented it.","funFact":"Anna Atkins made even the text pages of her famous seaweed book as cyanotypes of her own handwriting, so the whole book, words and pictures, was printed by sunlight.","check":{"q":"What was the big advantage of Talbot's paper negatives over Daguerre's plates?","a":"Many positive prints could be made from one negative","wrong":["They were much sharper","They showed full natural colour","They needed no light at all to make"]},"sources":[{"title":"Henry Fox Talbot","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Fox_Talbot"},{"title":"Windows From Inside South Gallery, Lacock Abbey","publisher":"Science Museum Group Collection","url":"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co17291/windows-from-inside-south-gallery-lacock-abbey"},{"title":"The Pencil of Nature, by H. Fox Talbot","publisher":"Project Gutenberg","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/33447/pg33447-images.html"},{"title":"Calotype","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calotype"},{"title":"John Herschel","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Herschel"},{"title":"Hippolyte Bayard","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hippolyte_Bayard"},{"title":"Hercules Florence","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hercules_Florence"},{"title":"Anna Atkins","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Atkins"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/05-1.jpg","alt":"Talbot's August 1835 paper negative of the latticed window at Lacock Abbey, with his handwritten note beside it.","file":"Talbot, latticed window.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Talbot,_latticed_window.png","author":"William Henry Fox Talbot","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/05-2.jpg","alt":"A blue cyanotype of the seaweed Cystoseira ericoides from Anna Atkins's book Photographs of British Algae.","file":"Anna Atkins Cystoseira ericoides.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anna_Atkins_Cystoseira_ericoides.jpg","author":"Anna Atkins","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/05-3.jpg","alt":"Photography pioneer Hippolyte Bayard's 1847 self-portrait among flowerpots and tools in a garden, a salt print.","file":"Bayard Self-Portrait in the Garden.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayard_Self-Portrait_in_the_Garden.jpg","author":"Hippolyte Bayard","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4204},{"n":6,"title":"Inside the Camera: Lenses, Light and Silver","when":"The science (explained with 1800s examples)","summary":"A camera is really just a dark box with a hole, a glass lens and something that remembers light. In this part we open the box and see how glass bends light, how silver crystals catch it, and how chemicals turn an invisible picture into a real one.","sections":[{"heading":"A Dark Box, a Hole and a Memory","paragraphs":["By 1840, people had cameras, chemicals and pictures, but not many of them really understood what was happening inside. So let's take one apart. Imagine an early camera from the 1840s sitting on a wooden table: a box made of polished wood, a brass tube sticking out of the front, and a slot at the back where you slide in a plate or a sheet of paper. It looks simple, and in a way it is. Every camera ever made, from Talbot's tiny wooden boxes to the phone in your pocket, has the same three jobs.","Job one is to make an image: to gather light from the scene and arrange it into a sharp picture on a flat surface. Job two is to control how much light arrives, because too little gives a dark, murky picture and too much burns everything to white. Job three is to remember the light, which is where the silver chemistry comes in. In a phone, the memory is an electronic chip, which you will meet in Part 19. In the 1800s, it was always silver.","Start with the simplest camera of all, the camera obscura from Part 1, which is just a dark box with a small hole. Light travels in straight lines. A ray from the top of a tree passes through the hole and keeps going downwards to the bottom of the back wall. A ray from the bottom of the tree goes upwards to the top. That is why the picture comes out upside down and flipped left to right.","A pinhole has a problem, though. Each point in the scene sends a whole little cone of light through the hole, and on the back wall that cone makes a small blurry spot instead of a perfect dot. If you make the hole smaller, the spots get smaller and the picture gets sharper. But a smaller hole also lets in less light, so the picture gets dimmer. Go too small and something strange happens: light waves start to spread out as they squeeze through the tiny gap, an effect called diffraction, and the picture becomes blurry again.","Scientists have worked out the best size. For a box 50 millimetres deep, about the length of your little finger, the ideal pinhole is only about a quarter of a millimetre across, roughly the width of three human hairs. That gives a reasonably sharp picture, but a very faint one. A pinhole camera loaded with modern film can need anything from a few seconds to several hours to take a picture. For the chemicals of the 1830s, which were far less sensitive, a pinhole was hopeless. Inventors needed something that could be both bright and sharp at the same time. They needed glass."]},{"heading":"Bending Light: How a Lens Focuses","paragraphs":["Put a straw in a glass of water and look at it from the side. The straw seems to break where it enters the water. Nothing has happened to the straw; the light coming from it has changed direction as it crossed from water into air. This bending of light when it passes from one transparent material into another is called refraction. Light travels a little slower in glass or water than in air, and when a beam hits the surface at an angle, one side of it slows down first, which swings the whole beam round, a bit like a shopping trolley veering when one wheel runs onto grass.","A lens uses refraction on purpose. A convex lens is thicker in the middle than at the edges, like a lentil (in fact, the word lens comes from the Latin for lentil). Light hitting the outer parts of the lens meets the curved glass at a steep angle and is bent strongly towards the middle. Light passing through the centre goes almost straight. The result is that all the rays from one point of the scene, which spread out on their way to the camera, are bent back together so that they meet at a single point behind the lens. That meeting point is the focus.","This is the big difference from a pinhole. A pinhole lets through only a thin pencil of light from each point. A lens can be much wider, collecting a fat cone of light from each point and squeezing it all into one sharp dot. So a lens gives you both brightness and sharpness, which a pinhole never can.","The distance behind the lens where light from a very faraway object, such as a mountain or the Moon, comes into focus is called the focal length. Here is the important part. Closer objects focus a little farther behind the lens. Scientists describe this with a neat equation, the thin lens equation: one divided by the focal length equals one divided by the object's distance plus one divided by the image's distance. Try it with a lens of 50 millimetres focal length. A mountain far away focuses about 50 millimetres behind the lens. A friend standing one metre away focuses at about 52.6 millimetres. A flower 20 centimetres away focuses about 67 millimetres back.","That is why you focus a camera by moving the lens. Old cameras of the 1840s often had a box that slid inside another box, or a folding leather bellows, so the photographer could move the lens in and out while looking at the image on a ground glass screen at the back, a sheet of glass roughened so the picture shows up on it. Photographers draped a black cloth over their heads to block stray light and see the dim, upside-down picture clearly. When it looked sharp, they slid in the sensitive plate instead.","Lenses were nothing new. The Nimrud lens, a polished piece of rock crystal from ancient Assyria, is nearly 3,000 years old (around the 8th or 7th century BCE), although nobody is sure what it was used for. Glass reading stones, laid on a page to magnify the words, were used from about the 11th century, and spectacles appeared in northern Italy in the late 1200s. But simple lenses have a flaw: glass bends blue light a bit more than red light, so the colours focus in slightly different places and pictures get coloured, blurry fringes. This is called chromatic aberration. Around 1730 (accounts say 1729 or 1733) an Englishman named Chester Moore Hall found that by gluing together two lenses made of different kinds of glass, he could cancel most of the problem. These achromatic lenses, meaning without colour, were what made the cameras of 1839 possible."]},{"heading":"The Aperture: How Wide Is the Window?","paragraphs":["The aperture is the opening that lets light through the lens. You have one in each eye: the pupil, the black circle in the middle of your iris. In bright sunshine it shrinks to a tiny dot; in a dark room it opens wide to catch every bit of light it can. Try it with a mirror: close your eyes for a few seconds, open them, and watch your pupils get smaller.","Early cameras controlled the aperture with a set of metal plates, each with a different-sized hole, that slid or dropped into a slot in the lens tube. They were called stops, because they stopped some of the light. Later cameras used an iris diaphragm, a ring of thin overlapping metal blades that can open or close smoothly, like the pupil of an eye.","Photographers describe the aperture with a number called the f-number. It is simply the focal length divided by the width of the opening. A lens with a focal length of 100 millimetres and an opening 50 millimetres wide is f/2. Close the opening to 25 millimetres and it becomes f/4. The confusing part is that a bigger f-number means a smaller hole and less light.","Lenses are marked with a standard series of f-numbers: f/1.4, f/2, f/2.8, f/4, f/5.6, f/8, f/11, f/16, f/22. Each step is called a stop, and each one lets in half as much light as the one before. Why such odd numbers? Because the amount of light depends on the area of the hole, not its width. To halve the area of a circle, you shrink its width by about 0.7, which is one divided by the square root of two. Multiply 1.4 by 1.4 and you get about 2; multiply 2 by 1.4 and you get about 2.8, and so on. So going from f/2 to f/4 is two stops, which means one quarter of the light.","Now you can see why a pinhole is so dim. The best pinhole for a 50-millimetre box works out to about f/190. A good portrait lens at f/2 lets in roughly 9,000 times more light. Your own eye works at about f/2 in the dark and about f/8 in bright light.","This is exactly how the first great lens breakthrough sped up photography. Daguerre's original camera of 1839 used a fairly slow lens. In 1840, a mathematics professor in Vienna named Joseph Petzval used careful calculations to design a new portrait lens, made by the optical firm of Voigtländer. It worked at about f/3.6 and gathered roughly 22 times as much light as Daguerre's lens. Combined with more sensitive plates, it helped cut portrait exposures to under a minute in good conditions, which is why daguerreotype studios could suddenly photograph ordinary people.","The aperture changes something else too: how much of the picture is sharp from front to back, called the depth of field. With a wide aperture like f/2, only things at one distance are sharp and the background melts into a soft blur, which is lovely for portraits. With a narrow aperture like f/16, nearly everything from a flower at your feet to the distant hills can be sharp. Landscape photographers in the 1800s often used tiny apertures for this reason, even though it made their exposures longer."]},{"heading":"The Shutter and the Art of Exposure","paragraphs":["The aperture decides how wide the door is. The shutter decides how long the door stays open. Together they control the exposure, the total amount of light that reaches the plate or film.","In the 1840s, the shutter was usually the photographer's hand. Exposures took many seconds or even minutes, so the photographer simply pulled the brass cap off the front of the lens, counted, perhaps using a pocket watch, and put the cap back on. Nothing faster was needed, because nothing faster was possible. Only after plates became much more sensitive, in the 1870s and 1880s, did inventors need mechanical shutters that could open and close in a fraction of a second. One early example was a rapid shutter made by A. A. Pearson of Leeds in 1875.","Modern shutters come in two main types. A leaf shutter sits inside the lens and is made of thin metal blades that spring open and snap shut. A focal-plane shutter sits just in front of the film or sensor and uses two curtains that race across it, leaving a slit of light between them. Shutter speeds are marked as fractions of a second: 1/30, 1/60, 1/125, 1/250, 1/500. Each step roughly halves the time, which means it halves the light, just like one f-stop.","This is the clever part. Because both the aperture and the shutter work in halves and doubles, a photographer can trade one for the other. Suppose a picture is correctly exposed at f/8 for 1/125 of a second. You could open up one stop to f/5.6, which doubles the light, and use 1/250 of a second, which halves the time. The exposure is exactly the same. Open up again to f/4 and use 1/500. Same exposure again. So why choose one over another? A fast shutter freezes a running dog. A small aperture keeps the whole landscape sharp. Every exposure is a choice.","The third thing that matters is how bright the scene is. In bright sunlight there may be many thousands of times more light than in a candlelit room, which is why early studios had huge glass windows and skylights, often on the top floor, and why photographers worked mostly in the middle of the day. Some even used mirrors and reflectors to bounce extra sunlight onto a sitter's face.","And the fourth is how sensitive the plate or film is, which today we call film speed, or ISO. A faster film needs less light. To understand why some silver is more sensitive than other silver, we have to shrink down and look at the crystals themselves."]},{"heading":"Silver Crystals and the Invisible Picture","paragraphs":["Take a piece of old black-and-white film and look at it under a powerful microscope. The light-sensitive layer, called the emulsion, is not a smooth coating at all. It is a layer of gelatin, the same kind of stuff that makes jelly wobble, packed with billions of tiny crystals. These are grains of silver halide, usually silver bromide, sometimes mixed with silver chloride or silver iodide. Each grain is far too small to see with your eyes, typically around a thousandth of a millimetre across. The gelatin holds the grains in place but lets the processing chemicals soak in and out.","Each crystal is a neat, repeating pattern of two kinds of particles: silver ions, which are silver atoms that have lost an electron, and bromide ions, which are bromine atoms that have gained one. As long as nothing disturbs them, they sit happily in their pattern. Now let a bit of light arrive.","Light comes in tiny packets of energy called photons. When a photon hits a silver bromide crystal, it can knock an electron loose. That free electron wanders through the crystal until it gets stuck at a weak spot, a little flaw or a speck of impurity on the crystal, called a sensitivity speck. There it meets one of the silver ions that also roam around inside the crystal and joins with it. A silver ion plus an electron makes an ordinary silver atom. Two British physicists, Ronald Gurney and Nevill Mott, explained this process in 1938, long after photography was invented; for the first hundred years, photographers used it without really knowing why it worked.","One silver atom is not enough. It can easily fall apart again. But if more photons arrive, more atoms gather at the same spot, and once a cluster has grown to about four silver atoms, it is stable. Those few atoms are the latent image, the invisible picture from the glossary. You could look at the film all day with an ordinary microscope and see nothing. Yet the information is there, a pattern of grains with tiny silver clusters where light fell and grains without them where it did not.","The latent image is surprisingly tough. It can last for months or longer. In 1897 the Swedish explorer S. A. Andrée and two companions set off to fly over the Arctic in a hydrogen balloon and never returned. When their last camp was found in 1930, their exposed film was developed, about 33 years after the pictures were taken, and some images still appeared.","Grain size matters, too. Bigger crystals catch more photons, so they reach that magic four-atom cluster with less light, which makes the film faster. But big crystals also make the picture look grainy, like sand. Fine-grained film shows more detail but needs more light. Photographers have always had to choose between speed and sharpness."]},{"heading":"In the Darkroom: Developer, Stop, Fixer, Wash","paragraphs":["Now for the magic trick. Step into a darkroom: a room with no windows, a sink, trays of liquid, and a dim red or orange safelight glowing in the corner. Ordinary black-and-white printing paper is hardly sensitive to red light, so you can see what you are doing without spoiling it.","Step one is the developer. This is a chemical that can turn silver halide into pure metallic silver. The clever part is that it works far, far faster on crystals that carry a latent image. Those four or so silver atoms act like a seed: once the developer starts at that spot, it converts the whole crystal, billions of silver atoms, into a tiny black clump of metallic silver. That makes the developer a chemical amplifier, multiplying the effect of light by up to billions of times. Grains that saw no light stay untouched, at least for a while. Common developing chemicals include metol and hydroquinone, mixed with an alkaline chemical to make them work. Early photographers had their own: Daguerre used mercury fumes, and Talbot used gallic acid from oak galls.","Timing matters. If you leave film in the developer too long, it eventually starts turning even the unexposed grains black, and the whole picture fogs over grey. So after a few minutes you move on to step two, the stop bath, a weak acid, often similar to dilute vinegar, that halts the developer almost instantly.","Step three is the fixer, the hypo that Herschel discovered. The developed areas are now metallic silver, which light cannot change any more. But the unexposed areas are still full of light-sensitive silver halide, and if you carried the film into daylight now, it would slowly darken all over. The fixer, sodium thiosulfate or a close relative, latches onto those leftover silver halide grains and turns them into a substance that dissolves in water. After fixing, nothing light-sensitive remains, and the picture is safe in daylight.","Step four is washing, and it is more important than it sounds. If fixer chemicals stay in the film or paper, over the years they can stain the picture yellow or brown and eat away the silver. That is exactly what happened to many early prints, including some of Talbot's, which faded because nobody yet knew how carefully they had to be washed. Museums today recommend thorough fixing and washing for any picture meant to last a century. Finally, the film is hung up to dry, often with clothes pegs on a line.","So what is a black-and-white photograph really made of? Countless microscopic clumps of pure silver metal, embedded in gelatin. Where there are many clumps, the picture looks black; where there are few, it looks grey; where there are none, you see the clear film or white paper underneath."]},{"heading":"Flipping the World: Negatives and Positives","paragraphs":["Think about what that means for a picture of a sunny garden. The bright sky sent lots of light to the film, so it is full of black silver. A dark green hedge sent little light, so it is mostly clear. A white cat is black; a black cat is clear. That is the negative, with every tone reversed.","To get a normal picture, you do the whole process a second time, on paper. In a contact print, you lay the negative directly on top of a sheet of light-sensitive paper, press it flat under glass, and shine light through. Where the negative is dark, as in the sky, little light reaches the paper, so the paper stays white. Where the negative is clear, as in the hedge, lots of light gets through and the paper turns dark. After developing and fixing the paper, the tones are flipped back to normal. Two reversals make a positive, just as two minus signs in maths make a plus.","This is exactly what Talbot did with his calotypes, laying waxed paper negatives on salted paper in the sunshine. A contact print is always the same size as the negative, so if you want a bigger picture you need a bigger negative. That is why some Victorian photographers lugged around enormous cameras with plates as large as a newspaper page.","Later, photographers used an enlarger, which is really a camera working backwards. A lamp shines through the negative, and a lens projects a magnified image of it down onto a sheet of paper on a baseboard. Move the head up and the picture gets bigger. With an enlarger, a tiny negative can become a poster, which is what made small cameras practical in the 20th century.","Some processes skip the negative altogether. A daguerreotype is a direct positive: the image formed on the silver plate is the final picture, which is why every daguerreotype is one of a kind. Bayard's paper photographs of 1839 were direct positives too. Colour negatives, which came much later, reverse the colours as well as the brightness, so a blue sky looks orange-brown and red lips look greenish. But the principle is still Talbot's: make one negative, then print it as often as you like."]},{"heading":"Try It Yourself: Pinholes and Sun Prints","paragraphs":["You do not need a darkroom to see this science in action. Here are two safe experiments to try with an adult.","First, a pinhole viewer. Take a long cardboard box, such as a cereal box, or a tube. Cut a square hole at one end and tape a piece of aluminium foil over it. Prick a single small, neat hole in the foil with a pin. At the other end, tape a sheet of white paper inside to act as a screen, and cut a viewing hole in the side near that end. Point the pinhole at a brightly lit window or a sunny street, put your eye to the viewing hole and cover your head with a dark cloth. You will see a faint, upside-down, colourful image of the world on the paper. Try making a second, bigger hole and compare: brighter, but blurrier. You have just rediscovered the camera obscura from Part 1. The same viewer lets you watch a solar eclipse safely, because you look at the projected image on the paper, never at the Sun itself. Never look at the Sun directly, through the pinhole or through any lens, binoculars or camera.","Second, a sun print. Toy shops, science shops and craft stores sell sun print paper, which is usually cyanotype paper, the same blue process Herschel invented in 1842 and Anna Atkins used for her seaweed. It is pre-coated, so you do not handle any chemicals yourself. Indoors, away from bright light, arrange flat objects on the paper: fern leaves, keys, lace, paper cut-outs. Cover with a sheet of clear plastic or glass from a picture frame to hold them flat. Carry it into bright sunshine for a few minutes, until the paper fades to a pale colour. Then rinse it in a bowl of plain water for a minute or so and let it dry. Your objects appear as crisp white shapes on deep blue.","Notice what you have made. The parts that saw sunlight turned dark blue; the parts that were covered stayed white. It is a negative, just like Talbot's leaves. Rinsing with water does the job of fixing, washing away the chemicals that did not react, so the picture will no longer change in the light. Even with no special paper at all, you can see light at work: leave a sheet of dark construction paper in a sunny window for a week with a few coins on it, and you may find pale paper with dark coin shapes where the light faded the dye.","So now we know the whole secret: a lens to make a bright, sharp image, an aperture and a shutter to control the light, silver crystals to catch a hidden picture, and chemistry to bring it out and fix it. But in the 1840s, the material holding those crystals was still the weak point. Paper was cheap and could make copies, but its fibres made every picture fuzzy. Metal daguerreotype plates were beautifully sharp but could never be copied. What if you could have both? What if you coated the silver onto something perfectly clear and smooth, something like glass?"]}],"deepDive":"The numbers behind exposure are all about doubling and halving, which is why photographers count in stops. One stop is a factor of two in light. The f-number series goes up by about 1.41, the square root of two, because light depends on the area of the aperture, which grows with the square of its width. A lens at f/2 therefore delivers four times the light of the same lens at f/4. Shutter speeds, film speeds and even lighting are all measured in the same factor-of-two steps, so a photographer can swap one for another without doing any real arithmetic.\n\nThe pinhole shows the physics of light most clearly. For a pinhole of diameter d at distance f from the screen, a bigger hole blurs the image by simple geometry, while a smaller hole blurs it by diffraction. The best balance falls at a diameter proportional to the square root of f times the wavelength of light; the mathematician Joseph Petzval, designer of the 1840 portrait lens, worked out a version of this in 1857 and Lord Rayleigh another in 1891. For a 50-millimetre pinhole camera the optimum is about 0.26 millimetres, giving an f-number near 190, roughly 13 stops slower than a portrait lens at f/2.\n\nThe silver chemistry was not properly explained until 1938, when Ronald Gurney and Nevill Mott proposed that light frees electrons in a silver halide crystal, that these electrons gather at sensitivity specks, and that mobile silver ions join them to build up atoms of metallic silver. Later work, notably by J. W. Mitchell, showed that the positive holes left behind also matter. Experiments suggest a stable latent image needs a cluster of at least about four silver atoms. Development then converts the whole grain, billions of atoms, giving an amplification of up to several billion times. This enormous built-in amplifier is why developing-out processes such as the calotype and later film were so much faster than printing-out processes that waited for light alone to darken the silver.\n\nThere is one more surprising fact: in very heavy overexposure, film can show an image even without developing, the old printing-out effect returning. Experimenters have shown that 35mm film given an enormous overexposure, millions of times more light than normal, can show a visible picture straight out of the camera, with no developer at all.","funFact":"When the lost 1897 Arctic balloon expedition of S. A. Andrée was found in 1930, its exposed film was developed about 33 years later, and pictures still appeared.","check":{"q":"In a darkroom, what does the fixer do?","a":"It removes the unexposed silver halide so the picture stops changing in light","wrong":["It makes the hidden picture appear for the first time","It adds colour to a black-and-white image","It bends light to focus the picture"]},"sources":[{"title":"F-number","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-number"},{"title":"Pinhole camera","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinhole_camera"},{"title":"Lens","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lens"},{"title":"Petzval lens","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petzval_lens"},{"title":"Latent image","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latent_image"},{"title":"Andrée's Arctic balloon expedition","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9e%27s_Arctic_balloon_expedition"},{"title":"Photographic fixer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photographic_fixer"},{"title":"Cyanotype","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanotype"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/06-1.jpg","alt":"Diagram of a convex lens: rays from the focal point come out parallel, and parallel rays meet at the focal point.","file":"Biconvex lens-behaviour of light rays from focal point.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biconvex_lens-behaviour_of_light_rays_from_focal_point.png","author":"Kvr.lohith","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/06-2.jpg","alt":"A shiny brass Voigtländer Petzval lens from about 1840, the fast lens that made portraits quicker to take.","file":"Petzval-Objektiv-Voigtlaender.1840.P1000366.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petzval-Objektiv-Voigtlaender.1840.P1000366.jpg","author":"Bautsch","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/06-3.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white printing darkroom with dark walls, sinks and developing trays.","file":"Svartvit mörkrum.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svartvit_m%C3%B6rkrum.jpg","author":"Rolf Svensson","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4301},{"n":7,"title":"Glass Plates and a World on Camera","when":"1851 – 1880","summary":"In 1851 photographers started making pictures on sheets of glass coated with a sticky goo that had to stay wet. Suddenly photos were sharp, cheap and quick, and cameras went everywhere: to battlefields, up in balloons and deep into the mountains of California.","sections":[{"heading":"A Sticky Liquid That Changed Everything","paragraphs":["By 1850, photography had a split personality. The daguerreotype was dazzlingly sharp, but every picture was a one-off on a silver mirror that could never be copied. Talbot's calotype could make many prints from one paper negative, but the fibres of the paper made everything look slightly fuzzy, as if seen through a fog. Photographers wanted the best of both: the sharpness of a mirror and the copying power of a negative. The answer turned out to be a material that was already lying around in every home: glass.","Glass is clear, smooth and flat, which makes it a perfect base for a negative. The problem was getting light-sensitive silver to stick to it. Silver salts will not cling to bare glass any more than paint sticks to a greasy window. You need a thin, clear 'glue' to hold them in place. Several inventors had tried egg white. Then an English sculptor and chemist named Frederick Scott Archer tried something stranger.","Collodion is a syrupy liquid made by dissolving guncotton (cotton treated with nitric and sulfuric acids) in a mixture of ether and alcohol. Doctors used it as a kind of liquid bandage, because when it is painted onto skin the ether and alcohol evaporate and leave a tough, transparent film. Archer had been experimenting since about 1848, and in March 1851 he published his method in a magazine called The Chemist. It became known as the wet collodion process, or simply the wet plate.","Here is what a photographer actually had to do, step by step. First, clean a sheet of glass until it squeaks. Second, pour a pool of collodion, mixed with a little iodide or bromide salt, onto the middle of the plate and tilt it carefully so the syrup flows to every corner in an even layer, then pour the extra back into the bottle. Third, in a darkened room, lower the sticky plate into a bath of silver nitrate solution for a few minutes. The silver joins with the iodide and bromide to form light-sensitive silver halide crystals trapped inside the collodion skin. Fourth, slide the plate, still dripping, into a light-tight holder, rush to the camera and take the picture. Fifth, hurry back to the dark and pour developer over the plate so the hidden image appears. Finally, fix it, wash it and varnish it to protect it.","The catch is in the name: wet. The plate only stayed sensitive while the collodion was moist. Once it dried, the silver bath crystallised, the chemicals stopped working properly and the picture was ruined. From the moment the plate was coated to the moment it was developed, a photographer usually had only about 10 to 15 minutes. On a hot, dry day it could be even less. Every single photograph was a small race against the clock.","Why put up with such a fuss? Because the results were spectacular. A glass negative was crystal clear, so prints made from it were razor sharp, with fine detail in faces, leaves and brickwork. It could be printed again and again, usually onto paper coated with egg white and silver called albumen paper. And it was fast. In good daylight an exposure took just a few seconds, compared with half a minute or more for many earlier methods. For portraits, that was the difference between a stiff, frozen expression and something closer to a natural face.","Most generous of all, Archer chose not to patent his invention. Anyone could use it for free. Within a few years the wet plate had swept across the world and, by the end of the 1860s, it had almost completely replaced the daguerreotype. Sadly, Archer earned very little from his gift. He died in London in 1857, still in his early forties, in poor health and poor circumstances. Fellow photographers raised money for his family, and a small government pension later helped support his children.","The wet plate had one more odd habit that you can still see in old photographs. Its silver crystals reacted strongly to blue and ultraviolet light but hardly at all to red, orange or yellow, and only weakly to green. So a blue sky, full of blue light, turned completely black on the negative and printed as blank white. Clouds vanished. Red lips and rosy cheeks, which send back little blue light, came out dark, and a ripe tomato could look like a shiny black ball."]},{"heading":"Pictures for Pennies: Ambrotypes, Tintypes and Cartes de Visite","paragraphs":["The wet plate did not only make better negatives. With a clever twist, it could also make cheap pictures that needed no printing at all. If a collodion negative on glass is slightly underexposed and then laid against something black, a strange thing happens. The thin, silvery parts of the image reflect light and look pale, while the clear glass shows the black backing and looks dark. Your eye reads the negative as a positive picture.","This was the ambrotype. James Ambrose Cutting of Boston took out several patents on versions of it in 1854, and the name, from Greek words meaning something like 'immortal impression', may have been invented in the Philadelphia studio of a daguerreotypist named Marcus Aurelius Root. Photographers backed the glass with black velvet, black varnish or dark red glass, then set it in a little folding case lined with velvet, just like a daguerreotype. Ambrotypes were much cheaper than daguerreotypes and did not have that confusing mirror shine, so by the late 1850s they had become more popular.","Then came something even cheaper and tougher. Instead of glass, why not use a thin sheet of iron painted black? The image would sit on the dark surface and appear as a positive, just like an ambrotype, but it would not shatter if dropped. Adolphe-Alexandre Martin described the idea in Paris in 1853, and Hamilton Smith patented it in the United States in 1856. It went by several names, including melainotype and ferrotype, but the one that stuck was tintype, even though there was no tin in it at all.","Tintypes were the fast food of photography. Because there was no drying and no printing, a customer could sit for a picture and walk away with it a few minutes later. Travelling photographers set up tents at fairs, beaches and carnivals. Soldiers in the American Civil War tucked tintypes of themselves into letters home, and carried pictures of sweethearts and children in their pockets, where a glass picture would have cracked. Sizes ranged from tiny 'gem' portraits small enough for a locket up to large plates, and tintypes stayed popular as a seaside and fairground novelty well into the 1900s. Some artists still make them today.","Meanwhile in Paris, a photographer called André-Adolphe-Eugène Disdéri had a money-saving idea. In 1854 he patented a way to take several small portraits on one glass negative, by using a camera with multiple lenses or a back that slid across between exposures. One plate and one printing gave a whole sheet of little pictures, which were cut apart and glued onto stiff cards about the size of a modern business card, roughly 6 by 10 centimetres. They were called cartes de visite, French for 'visiting cards'.","For a few years the idea was only moderately successful. Then in 1859 Disdéri published small portraits of Emperor Napoleon III, and suddenly everyone wanted one. He charged 20 francs for a dozen pictures, at a time when one large portrait print could cost 50 to 100 francs. The craze, nicknamed 'cardomania', spread across Europe and America in the 1860s. People swapped cards with friends and visitors, and collected cards of queens, generals, actors and authors, which photographers sold in shop windows. In the United States alone, an estimated 300 to 400 million cartes de visite were sold each year during the 1860s.","To hold all these cards, families bought thick photograph albums with cut-out windows on each page. The album sat in the front parlour, and showing it to guests became part of a visit. Many historians compare it to an early form of social media: a public display of who you knew, who you admired and how you wanted to look. For the first time in history, an ordinary clerk or shopkeeper could own pictures of grandparents, cousins and children, as well as the face of the queen."]},{"heading":"A Darkroom on Wheels: Roger Fenton in the Crimea","paragraphs":["The wet plate created a puzzle for any photographer who wanted to leave the studio. If every plate had to be coated, exposed and developed within a quarter of an hour, then the darkroom had to come too. Landscape photographers loaded mules, carts and boats with tents, glass, bottles of chemicals, water tanks, heavy wooden cameras and tripods. In the field, a photographer would crawl inside a light-tight tent or wagon, coat a plate by the dim glow of yellow or orange glass, run out to the camera, then rush back in to develop it.","The most famous early example was a British photographer named Roger Fenton. In 1854 Britain, France and the Ottoman Empire went to war with Russia on the Crimean peninsula, on the northern shore of the Black Sea. Newspaper reports about the suffering of British soldiers in the cold and mud had shocked people at home. Encouraged by Prince Albert and the government's Secretary of State for War, and paid for by the print publisher Thomas Agnew and Sons, Fenton set out to photograph the war.","He sailed in February 1855 and landed at the harbour of Balaklava on 8 March. With him came an assistant, Marcus Sparling, a servant named William, and a large horse-drawn van, said to have once belonged to a wine merchant, rebuilt as a travelling darkroom. Around it were piled hundreds of glass plates and chests of chemicals.","Working conditions were awful. As spring turned into summer, the heat inside the dark van became stifling, so hot that the collodion sometimes dried almost as soon as it was poured. Dust stuck to the sticky plates. Fenton fell and broke several ribs, and later caught cholera. He was also deeply upset by the misery he saw around him.","Fenton could not photograph actual fighting. His exposures took several seconds and his equipment was enormous, so anything moving fast simply turned into a blur, and setting up near the battle lines would have been both impossible and deadly. Instead he photographed what could stand still: officers and soldiers posing in groups, camps and cooking huts, the crowded harbour, and the bare, rocky landscape. He also chose not to photograph dead or wounded soldiers, partly because his pictures were meant to be sold to the public and partly, perhaps, to avoid upsetting families at home.","His most famous image shows a stony road winding through an empty valley, scattered with cannonballs. Agnew exhibited it in London in September 1855 with the title The Valley of the Shadow of Death, borrowed from the Bible. There are actually two versions, one with cannonballs on the road and one without, and for many years people argued about whether Fenton moved the cannonballs to make the scene more dramatic. In 2007 the filmmaker Errol Morris studied the shadows and pebbles carefully and concluded that the empty-road picture was taken first. That means someone probably placed the cannonballs on the road for the second shot. Even the first great war photograph may have been arranged.","Fenton stayed in the Crimea until 22 June 1855 and came home with more than 350 usable large negatives. An exhibition of over 300 prints toured London and other British cities, and he showed them to Queen Victoria, Prince Albert and the French Emperor Napoleon III. Sales were disappointing, but the idea was now planted: a camera could follow an army and show people at home what war looked like, at least when it was standing still."]},{"heading":"Brady's Teams and the American Civil War","paragraphs":["Six years after Fenton came home, a much bigger and bloodier war broke out across the Atlantic. The American Civil War (1861–1865) was the first major war to be photographed on a large scale, and the name most connected with it is Mathew Brady. Brady was already America's most famous portrait photographer. He had opened a studio in New York in 1844 and another in Washington, D.C., in 1849, and he had photographed presidents, senators and celebrities. On the day in 1860 when Abraham Lincoln gave an important speech at the Cooper Union in New York, he also sat for a portrait in Brady's studio.","When the war began, Brady decided to create a complete photographic record of it, at his own expense. But here is something many people do not realise: Brady himself took very few of the war photographs. His eyesight had been getting worse since the 1850s, and he worked more like a director or publisher. He hired and equipped teams of photographers, around twenty of them, each with a travelling darkroom wagon, and sent them to follow the armies. Among them were Alexander Gardner, a Scottish-born photographer who had managed Brady's Washington studio, Timothy O'Sullivan, who had started out as a teenage apprentice in Brady's studio, and George Barnard.","The pictures were published under the label 'Brady' even when someone else had taken them, which was normal business practice then but frustrated the men in the field. By 1863 Gardner had left to open his own studio in Washington, and O'Sullivan and others went to work with him.","The war wagons became a familiar sight. Soldiers, seeing the strange black-covered carts with chemical smells drifting out, sometimes called them 'What-is-it? wagons'. Inside, photographers worked in heat, dust and danger. Like Fenton, they could not capture battles while they were happening. Exposures still took seconds, and gunsmoke, galloping horses and running men would have been nothing but blurs. What they could photograph was what came before and after: camps, cannons, bridges, ruined towns, generals in front of their tents, and the battlefield once the fighting had stopped."]},{"heading":"The Dead of Antietam","paragraphs":["On 17 September 1862, near Antietam Creek and the small town of Sharpsburg, Maryland, Union and Confederate armies fought the bloodiest single day in American history. About 23,000 soldiers were killed, wounded, captured or missing in a single day. Within a day or two, Alexander Gardner and his assistant James Gibson arrived with their wagon and cameras. Over the next few days they photographed the battlefield while many of the fallen soldiers had not yet been buried.","This was something completely new. According to historians, it was the first time an American battlefield had been photographed before the dead were taken away. Many of Gardner's pictures were made as stereographs, pairs of photographs that look three-dimensional in a special viewer, which made the scenes feel even more real and close.","In October 1862, Brady put the photographs on display at his gallery on Broadway in New York, under the title The Dead of Antietam. Until then, most civilians in Northern cities knew the war through newspaper reports, printed lists of names, drawings in magazines, and letters from soldiers. Paintings and engravings of battles usually showed brave charges, flags and heroic poses. Now crowds stood in a city gallery looking quietly at real photographs of a road, a fence, a field, and the soldiers who had died there.","Visitors were deeply moved. Some leaned in with magnifying glasses to study the faces, afraid they might recognise someone they knew. The New York Times wrote that Brady had done something to bring home to the public 'the terrible reality and earnestness of war', and added that if he had not brought the bodies and laid them in people's dooryards and along their streets, he had 'done something very like it'. A photograph, people realised, could make faraway events feel close in a way that words could not.","For Brady, the war ended in money trouble. He had spent more than 100,000 dollars making over 10,000 plates, expecting the government to buy the collection. It did not, at least not for years, and he went bankrupt. Congress finally paid him 25,000 dollars in 1875, but he died in poverty in 1896. Today the Library of Congress and the National Archives hold thousands of these glass negatives, and they remain one of the most important visual records of the war."]},{"heading":"Above the Rooftops: Nadar and the First Aerial Photographs","paragraphs":["While some photographers headed to war, a Parisian with flaming red hair headed straight up. His real name was Gaspard-Félix Tournachon, but everyone knew him as Nadar. He began as a caricaturist, drawing funny pictures of famous people for humour magazines, and he moved into photography in the 1850s. By 1860 he had a grand studio at 35 Boulevard des Capucines in Paris, with his name written in giant letters across the front.","Nadar's portraits were very different from the stiff, fussy pictures of many studios. He used soft natural daylight from big windows, a plain background, and simple poses, and he chatted with his sitters to bring out their personalities. His camera recorded many of the most famous people in France: the novelist Victor Hugo, the poet Charles Baudelaire, the composer Franz Liszt and the actress Sarah Bernhardt.","Nadar also loved balloons. In 1858 he rose above Paris in a tethered balloon and made what are considered the first aerial photographs. Picture the problem: he had to coat, expose and develop wet plates inside a swaying wicker basket, with his darkroom crammed in alongside him, while the gas from the balloon could spoil his chemicals. Those very first photographs no longer survive. Later, he built a huge balloon called Le Géant ('The Giant') in 1863, and he experimented with taking photographs underground in the Paris catacombs using artificial electric light. During the siege of Paris in 1870–71, he helped organise balloon flights that carried letters out of the surrounded city, often described as the world's first airmail service.","The oldest aerial photograph that still exists was taken on the other side of the Atlantic. On 13 October 1860, the photographer James Wallace Black and the balloonist Samuel Archer King went up over Boston in a balloon called the Queen of the Air and photographed the city from several hundred metres up (accounts give heights from about 370 to 630 metres). The picture shows a crowded patchwork of rooftops, church spires, streets and ships' masts. Black gave it a poetic title: Boston, as the Eagle and the Wild Goose See It. For the first time, people could see their own town the way only birds ever had."]},{"heading":"Art, Fame and 3D: Julia Margaret Cameron and the Stereoscope","paragraphs":["Was photography an art, or just a machine for copying? Victorians argued about this endlessly, and one woman answered with her camera. Julia Margaret Cameron was born in Calcutta, India, in 1815, and lived much of her later life at Freshwater on the Isle of Wight, in a house called Dimbola. Around Christmas 1863, when she was about 48 years old, her daughter and son-in-law gave her a camera. It changed her life.","Cameron turned her coal house into a darkroom and a glass-roofed chicken house into a studio, which she called her 'glass house'. Then she persuaded, and sometimes practically ordered, family members, servants, neighbours and famous visitors to sit for her. Her neighbour was the poet Alfred Tennyson. Other sitters included the scientists Charles Darwin and John Herschel, the very man who had helped invent photographic fixing, and the young actress Ellen Terry.","Her pictures looked unlike anyone else's. She came in very close, filling the frame with a face. She used dramatic light from one side, dark backgrounds and long exposures, and she often let the image go slightly out of focus on purpose. Some of her plates show smudges, scratches and swirls in the collodion. Many critics hated this. One 1865 review complained that her blurry effects came at the expense of all the other qualities of a good photograph. But Cameron was not trying to make a perfect record. She wanted to capture something of a person's character and mood, the way a painter might. In about twelve years she made roughly 900 photographs, and today she is seen as one of the great artists of early photography.","Meanwhile, in many middle-class homes, people were enjoying photography as a kind of magic show. The stereoscope used a simple trick of human vision. Because your two eyes are a few centimetres apart, each sees the world from a slightly different angle, and your brain combines the two views to judge depth. In 1838 the scientist Charles Wheatstone showed that if each eye is shown a separate drawing made from a slightly different viewpoint, the brain fuses them into a single solid-looking scene.","Photography made this easy. A stereo camera with two lenses, side by side, took two pictures at once, which were mounted next to each other on a card. In 1849 David Brewster suggested a viewer using lenses, small enough to hold in your hands. When Queen Victoria admired stereoscopes at the Great Exhibition in London in 1851, a craze began. Later, in 1861, the American writer and doctor Oliver Wendell Holmes designed a cheap, simple viewer with two lenses, a wooden hood and a stand for the card. He deliberately did not patent it, and versions of it were made for about a century.","Families could now sit by the fire and 'visit' the pyramids of Egypt, the streets of Paris, Niagara Falls or the battlefields of the Civil War in startling depth. Companies sold boxed sets of stereo cards like we might buy box sets of shows. It was, in a way, the virtual reality of the Victorian age."]},{"heading":"Mountains That Persuaded a Nation","paragraphs":["The wet plate also travelled into wild places, and some of those pictures helped protect the land itself. Carleton Watkins was a photographer in San Francisco who, in July 1861, made a journey into Yosemite Valley in the Sierra Nevada mountains of California. Most Americans in the East had never seen it, and many found written descriptions of granite cliffs and giant waterfalls hard to believe.","Watkins carried an enormous camera that used 'mammoth plates', glass negatives roughly 46 by 56 centimetres (about 18 by 22 inches). With no easy way to enlarge prints, a big picture needed a big negative, and each huge sheet had to be coated and developed on the spot. Along with the glass, he brought a stereo camera, chemicals and a darkroom tent, carried on pack animals over rough trails.","The results were breathtaking: sheer walls of El Capitan, Yosemite Falls dropping down the cliffs, giant sequoia trees and calm rivers reflecting the mountains like mirrors. Watkins became famous for these reflection pictures. His prints were shown in New York and Washington, and California's senator John Conness made sure members of Congress saw them.","In 1864, in the middle of the Civil War, Congress passed and President Abraham Lincoln signed the Yosemite Grant, which set aside Yosemite Valley and the Mariposa Grove of giant sequoias to be protected 'for public use, resort and recreation'. It was the first time the US government set aside land especially to protect its natural beauty, and it paved the way for the national parks that followed. Many historians believe Watkins's photographs helped persuade lawmakers that the place was worth saving.","Watkins himself had a sad ending. Rivals copied his pictures without credit, which the law did not prevent at the time; he lost his business through debts; and much of his work was destroyed in the San Francisco earthquake and fire of 1906. He died in 1916 in a state hospital. But the valley he photographed is still protected today.","By 1880 the camera had been carried into wars, up into the sky, onto mountaintops and into millions of parlour albums. Yet one thing still defeated every photographer on Earth. Anything that moved quickly, a running child, a flying bird, the legs of a galloping horse, came out as a ghostly smear. The dream was clear: to freeze a split second so perfectly that you could see what the human eye was too slow to notice. Could anyone ever photograph a horse at full gallop without a blur?"]}],"deepDive":"Why did the wet collodion process dominate photography for about thirty years despite its awkwardness? The answer lies in a trade-off between sensitivity, sharpness and reproducibility. A calotype paper negative scattered light through its fibres, softening every print. A daguerreotype was sharp but unique. Collodion on glass offered a smooth, transparent, grainless base, so an albumen print made by contact (laying the negative directly on paper in sunlight) could reproduce extremely fine detail. Its sensitivity was also much higher, partly because the silver halide was developed while the plate was still wet and chemically active. Exposures fell from minutes to a few seconds in daylight, which made the carte de visite trade, mass-produced stereographs and fieldwork possible. The drawbacks were serious. Ether and alcohol fumes were unpleasant and flammable, and some photographers suffered from the chemicals, including the poisonous potassium cyanide sometimes used as a fixer. The emulsion responded mainly to blue and ultraviolet light (it was 'colour-blind' to red and only weakly sensitive to green), so skies printed white and red objects dark. Historians also debate how 'truthful' these images were. Fenton's two versions of The Valley of the Shadow of Death, and evidence that a body was moved for a Gardner photograph at Gettysburg in 1863, show that staging began almost as soon as war photography itself. Another debate is about credit. Brady's name appears on thousands of images actually made by Gardner, O'Sullivan, Barnard and others, so modern archives try to identify the true photographer whenever possible. The influence of photography on policy is also discussed cautiously: Watkins's pictures were circulated in Congress before the 1864 Yosemite Grant, but the law also came from lobbying by Californians who wanted the valley protected, so photographs were one powerful argument among several, not the only cause. Finally, the economics matter. Archer gave his process away freely, while Talbot had tried to enforce patents on paper negatives. The open, unpatented wet plate spread rapidly, a useful early lesson in how sharing an invention can speed up its adoption.","funFact":"The oldest surviving aerial photograph, taken over Boston from a balloon in 1860, was titled Boston, as the Eagle and the Wild Goose See It.","check":{"q":"Why did wet collodion photographers have to carry a darkroom with them?","a":"The plate had to be coated, exposed and developed in about 10–15 minutes, before it dried","wrong":["Because glass plates could only be bought on the battlefield","Because daylight would melt the glass plates","Because the law required photographers to develop pictures outdoors"]},"sources":[{"title":"Collodion process","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collodion_process"},{"title":"Frederick Scott Archer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_Scott_Archer"},{"title":"Roger Fenton","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Fenton"},{"title":"Mathew Brady","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathew_Brady"},{"title":"Aerial photography","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerial_photography"},{"title":"Carte de visite","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carte_de_visite"},{"title":"Julia Margaret Cameron","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_Margaret_Cameron"},{"title":"Carleton Watkins","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carleton_Watkins"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/07-1.jpg","alt":"Roger Fenton's \"Photographic Van\" in the Crimea, 1855, with his assistant Marcus Sparling on the seat.","file":"Roger Fenton's waggon.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roger_Fenton%27s_waggon.jpg","author":"Roger Fenton (1819–1869)","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/07-2.jpg","alt":"Boston seen from a balloon on 13 October 1860 by James Wallace Black, the oldest surviving aerial photograph.","file":"Boston, as the Eagle and the Wild Goose See It.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boston,_as_the_Eagle_and_the_Wild_Goose_See_It.jpg","author":"James Wallace Black","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/07-3.jpg","alt":"Carleton Watkins's photograph of the giant granite cliff El Capitan rising above the river in Yosemite Valley.","file":"El Capitan, Yosemite MET DP142935.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Capitan,_Yosemite_MET_DP142935.jpg","author":"Carleton Watkins","license":"CC0"}],"words":4342},{"n":8,"title":"Freezing Motion","when":"1825 – 1888","summary":"Spinning toys showed that quick pictures in a row can look like they are moving. Then faster photo plates let a photographer named Muybridge catch a galloping horse in mid-air with a row of cameras. His pictures proved that horses really fly, all four feet off the ground!","sections":[{"heading":"Toys That Fooled the Eye","paragraphs":["Long before anyone could photograph motion, people were playing with toys that made motion out of nothing. They were sold in toy shops and opticians' shops in the 1820s and 1830s, and they turned living rooms into tiny laboratories of the human brain. Each one used the same surprising discovery: show the eye a series of slightly different pictures fast enough, and you will see something move that is really standing still.","The simplest was the thaumatrope, a name that roughly means 'wonder turner'. It was just a small cardboard disc with a picture on each side and a piece of string tied to the left and right edges. On one side you might see a bird, and on the other an empty cage. Twirl the strings between your fingers so the disc spins fast, and the bird appears to sit inside the cage. The first commercial sets went on sale in London in April 1825, sold by W. Phillips in boxes of 12 or 18 discs. The London doctor John Ayrton Paris is usually given credit for the toy, and he described it in a children's science book in 1827. But according to the mathematician Charles Babbage, the idea began with a friendly challenge: the astronomer John Herschel asked Babbage how he could see both sides of a coin at once, and the answer was to spin it on a table. A geologist friend, William Henry Fitton, then made a card version with a parrot on one side and a cage on the other.","The thaumatrope blended two pictures, but it could not tell a story. In December 1832, a Belgian physicist named Joseph Plateau came up with a toy that really animated a sequence. Almost at the same moment, an Austrian mathematician, Simon Stampfer, invented nearly the same thing in Vienna. Plateau's version became known as the phenakistiscope, from Greek words meaning something like 'deceiving viewer'. Plateau and Stampfer had both been inspired by experiments with spinning wheels by the English scientist Michael Faraday.","Here is how it worked. Around the edge of a cardboard disc were narrow slits. Between the slits and the centre was a ring of drawings, perhaps a juggler, a dancer or a pumping blacksmith, each one a little further through the movement than the last. You held the disc up to a mirror, with the pictures facing the glass, spun it, and peeked through the slits as they whirled past your eye. Each slit gave you a split-second glimpse of one drawing reflected in the mirror before the card blocked your view again. Glimpse, dark, glimpse, dark, and the drawings seemed to come alive and move smoothly round and round. Narrow slits made the pictures sharper, but darker.","Publishers in London, Paris, Vienna and elsewhere began selling sets of discs in 1833. There was only one problem: just one person at a time could peek through the slits. Still, the phenakistiscope was the first widely popular device to show a smooth illusion of motion, and every film and cartoon you have ever watched relies on the same idea of quick separate pictures."]},{"heading":"Wheels of Life and Rings of Mirrors","paragraphs":["In January 1834 a British mathematician, William George Horner, from Bristol, published a clever variation. Instead of a flat disc, he imagined a hollow drum with slits cut in its sides and pictures arranged on a strip inside. He called it the daedaleum, after Daedalus, the inventor of Greek myth. For some reason Horner's plans to sell it stalled, and for decades the idea mostly slept.","Then, in the 1860s, a Brown University student in America named William Ensign Lincoln improved the design so that the slits sat above the picture strip, and the strips could be swapped easily. The toy company Milton Bradley began selling it in 1866, and a US patent followed in April 1867. Lincoln gave it the name that stuck: zoetrope, from Greek words for 'life' and 'turning', a translation of its nickname, the 'wheel of life'. A whole family could gather round the spinning drum, each person looking through a different slit, and watch horses jumping, acrobats tumbling or a man endlessly being bitten by a dog. Paper strips could be bought by the dozen, so it was like owning a cinema with a very short programme.","The slits had a downside, though. They let in only a sliver of light, so the moving pictures looked dim, and they slightly squashed the image. In 1877 a French science teacher and inventor named Émile Reynaud solved this beautifully. In his praxinoscope (roughly 'action viewer'), the slits were gone. Instead, a ring of small flat mirrors stood around the centre of the drum, one mirror for each picture on the strip. As the drum turned, you looked at the mirrors, and each one showed you a picture at just the right instant. The images were brighter, clearer and less distorted than in a zoetrope.","Reynaud kept improving his idea. In 1879 he made a version hidden in a box with a little theatrical stage set, so the animated figure appeared to move in front of scenery. Around 1880 he showed a projecting version, which went on sale from 1882, and in 1888 he patented the Théâtre Optique, which projected long painted picture strips onto a screen for an audience. That story belongs to the history of animation. For now, notice what all these toys had in common: every picture was drawn by hand. Nobody yet knew exactly what a real horse or a real bird did in a fraction of a second, so artists could only guess."]},{"heading":"Dry Plates: Photography Gets Faster","paragraphs":["To photograph a real moving creature, photographers needed something the wet collodion plate could not give them: speed. A wet plate needed a few seconds of exposure in daylight, and it had to be prepared on the spot. Some tricks could make it work faster in very bright sun, but it was still awkward and messy.","The breakthrough came from an English doctor named Richard Leach Maddox. He was a keen photomicrographer, someone who takes photographs through a microscope, and he worked with wet plates constantly. The ether fumes from the collodion were making him unwell, so he went looking for a different substance to hold the silver crystals. He chose gelatin, the clear protein that makes jelly wobble, made by boiling animal skin and bones.","On 8 September 1871, the British Journal of Photography published his article, 'An Experiment with Gelatino-Bromide'. He described mixing silver nitrate and a bromide salt into warm gelatin so that tiny crystals of silver bromide formed inside it, then spreading this 'emulsion' onto glass and letting it dry. Maddox's own plates were slow and not very reliable, and he freely admitted that more work was needed. Like Archer, he did not patent the idea and gave it to the world.","Others quickly took up the challenge. The most important discovery came from Charles Harper Bennett in 1878: if the emulsion was kept warm for hours or days before coating, a process called ripening, the silver bromide crystals grew larger and much more sensitive. Suddenly, gelatin plates were not slower than wet plates but many times faster. Exposures could be shortened to a fraction of a second, short enough to be called 'instantaneous'. That speed also meant cameras needed a proper shutter, a fast-moving flap or blade, because simply taking off and replacing a lens cap by hand was now far too slow.","Just as important, the new plates were dry. They could be manufactured in a factory, packed in boxes, stored for months and developed whenever convenient, days or even weeks after exposure. By the late 1870s companies in Britain and the United States were selling ready-made dry plates. Photographers no longer had to haul a darkroom tent up a mountain or across a battlefield. They could simply take a box of plates and a camera, and leave the chemistry for later. One of the people who saw the business opportunity was a young man in New York State who would soon build a plate-coating machine. We will meet him at the end of this part."]},{"heading":"A Photographer and a Railroad Millionaire","paragraphs":["Our next hero was a man who kept reinventing himself, even his own name. He was born Edward James Muggeridge in 1830 in Kingston upon Thames, a town near London. As a young man he moved to America and worked as a bookseller in San Francisco. In 1860 he was badly hurt when a stagecoach crashed near the Texas border, and people who knew him later said his personality seemed changed afterwards. He went back to England to recover, learned photography, and returned to California calling himself Muybridge, a spelling he believed was closer to the old Anglo-Saxon form of his name. Later he even began spelling his first name Eadweard.","Muybridge became a celebrated landscape photographer. In 1867 he photographed Yosemite Valley, and his views of waterfalls and cliffs were sold widely, including as stereo cards. He photographed the coast, lighthouses, the new railroads and the city of San Francisco. His life also had a very dark chapter: in 1874 he shot and killed a man he believed was involved with his wife. At his trial in 1875 the jury acquitted him, finding the killing 'justifiable' under the ideas of that time, though historians today see the case as a troubling one.","The man who changed Muybridge's career was Leland Stanford, a former governor of California who had become enormously rich building the Central Pacific Railroad. Stanford owned a farm at Palo Alto, south of San Francisco, where he bred and trained racehorses, especially trotters, which pull a light two-wheeled cart called a sulky. Stanford was fascinated by the science of how horses move, because he wanted to train them to go faster.","There was a question that horse experts had argued about for a very long time. When a horse trots or gallops, is there ever a moment when all four of its hooves are off the ground at once? The legs move too fast for the human eye to be sure. Painters had their own answer: for centuries they had shown galloping horses in a 'flying gallop', with front legs stretched forward and back legs stretched behind, like a rocking horse. A popular story claims Stanford made a 25,000-dollar bet on the question, but that tale is a legend; historians have found no good evidence for it. What is clear is that Stanford wanted proof, and he hired Muybridge to get it.","Muybridge first tried in 1872 or 1873, photographing Stanford's famous trotter Occident with a single camera. The image was so blurry and underexposed that it showed little more than a smudged outline, and it was never properly published. Stanford's money, newer and faster plates and an ingenious plan would make the next attempt a world-famous success."]},{"heading":"Twelve Cameras and a Flying Horse","paragraphs":["By 1878, Muybridge had built an amazing outdoor laboratory beside the track at Stanford's farm. Along one side of the track stood a long shed holding a row of 12 large cameras, each pointing across the track. On the other side stood a tall white wall, marked with numbered vertical lines, so that the position of the horse's legs could be measured against them. The white background bounced back as much sunlight as possible, which helped with the very short exposures.","Each camera had a fast shutter, worked by springs or rubber bands, which could snap open and shut in a tiny fraction of a second. Muybridge then needed a way for the horse to press the shutters itself, one after another, exactly as it passed each camera. His answer was to stretch threads or wires across the track. For a trotter pulling a sulky, the metal wheel of the cart passed over the wires and completed an electric circuit, which fired the shutters with electromagnets. For a horse galloping with a rider, threads across the track were snapped by the horse's body as it ran. Either way, one camera after another fired in quick succession, like a ripple running down a line of dominoes.","On 15 June 1878, Muybridge staged a demonstration and invited newspaper reporters to watch. A horse thundered down the track, the wires and threads were tripped, and the 12 shutters clicked in a fraction of a second. Muybridge developed the plates on the spot, while the reporters waited. Within minutes, the witnesses could see a sequence of silhouettes, each one frozen at a different instant of a single stride. The most famous sequence from these June trials, usually dated 15 or 19 June, shows a mare called Sallie Gardner, ridden by a jockey listed as G. Domm.","Think about what this meant. No human being had ever been able to look at a galloping horse frozen in time. Now anyone could hold a card in their hands and study, frame by frame, what a horse's legs were really doing. Muybridge's photographs were published as a series of cards called The Horse in Motion, and drawings based on them soon appeared in magazines such as Scientific American.","The answer to the old question was yes: there is a moment when all four hooves leave the ground at once. But the photographs held a bigger surprise. The flying moment did not look anything like the famous paintings. The horse was not stretched out in a graceful leap with its legs pointing forwards and backwards. Instead, at the moment it was airborne, its legs were tucked up together underneath its belly, bunched like a coiled spring. The 'flying gallop' that artists had painted for centuries was simply wrong.","Many people refused to believe it at first. The poses looked clumsy, almost ugly, and some critics said the camera must be lying. But the same results came again and again, and soon painters began to change how they drew horses. Some artists studied Muybridge's photographs carefully to get anatomy right, while others complained that a frozen instant was not how the eye actually experiences motion. This debate, between what a camera records and what we perceive, has continued ever since.","Muybridge did not stop at one horse. In 1879 he expanded the setup to 24 cameras, and he photographed horses walking, trotting, galloping and jumping, as well as dogs, deer, oxen and people running and leaping. Fame soon followed. He travelled to Europe and gave lectures in Paris and in London, including at the Royal Institution in 1882. That same year, however, Stanford published a book called The Horse in Motion, written by his friend Dr J. D. B. Stillman, which used Muybridge's pictures but gave him hardly any credit. Muybridge was furious, and the two men fell out."]},{"heading":"The Zoopraxiscope and Animal Locomotion","paragraphs":["Muybridge wanted audiences to see his sequences move, not just study them frame by frame. So in 1879 he designed a projector he called the zoopraxiscope, and it was ready by early 1880. It was a kind of magic lantern, an older projector that shone a powerful light through a glass picture and a lens onto a screen, combined with the spinning disc of a phenakistiscope.","The pictures were painted around the edge of a glass disc about 16 inches (roughly 40 centimetres) across. They were based on his photographs, mostly as silhouettes painted by an artist whose name is not known. A slotted metal disc acted as a shutter, so the audience saw each picture only for an instant. Turn the handle, and on the screen a horse galloped, a man ran or a pair of dancers whirled around, larger than life. Because the images were stretched slightly by the spinning, the painted figures were drawn a little longer than in the photographs to make them look natural on the screen.","Muybridge used the zoopraxiscope in his lectures from 1880 to 1895, and audiences in America and Europe watched in amazement. It is often counted as one of the first machines to project moving pictures based on photographs, and it probably helped inspire later inventors, including Thomas Edison's team. The zoopraxiscope and most of its discs are still kept today at the Kingston Museum, in the English town where Muybridge was born.","His biggest project came next. From 1883 to 1886, with support from the University of Pennsylvania in Philadelphia, Muybridge photographed movement on an enormous scale. Using the new, fast gelatin dry plates and banks of cameras, he recorded men and women walking, running, jumping, climbing stairs, carrying buckets and throwing balls, as well as horses, elephants, tigers, camels, birds and many other animals, some of them borrowed from the Philadelphia Zoo. People were usually photographed in front of a background grid, so their movements could be measured.","In 1887 he published the results as Animal Locomotion: 781 large plates containing around 20,000 separate photographs. Artists, doctors, scientists and engineers have used it ever since. It also changed art itself. Painters such as Marcel Duchamp, decades later, were inspired by the idea of a body broken up into a sequence of moments. Even today, animators study Muybridge's sequences to learn how a body really moves."]},{"heading":"Marey's Photographic Gun","paragraphs":["Across the Atlantic, a French scientist was chasing the same dream from a different direction. Étienne-Jules Marey was a physiologist, a scientist who studies how living bodies work. He started with the heart and blood, inventing instruments that drew wavy lines on paper to record a person's pulse. Then he turned to movement: how people walk, how horses trot and, most of all, how birds fly. When he saw Muybridge's horse photographs, he realised that photography could become a scientific measuring tool.","Muybridge's method used many cameras, each taking a picture from a slightly different spot. Marey wanted a single camera, with one viewpoint, that could take a rapid series of pictures in a row. In 1882 he built a remarkable device shaped like a rifle, called the chronophotographic gun ('chrono' means time). It had a wooden stock you held against your shoulder and a long barrel containing the lens. Instead of bullets, there was a round drum holding a light-sensitive plate.","When Marey aimed at a flying bird and pulled the trigger, clockwork turned the plate in steps, and a shutter flicked open for each step. The gun could take about 12 pictures every second, each with an exposure as short as about 1/700 of a second. He could follow a seagull or a pelican across the sky, just like a hunter aiming a shotgun, but instead of hurting the bird he brought home a circle of tiny photographs showing its wings rising and falling. Marey's idea was itself inspired by an earlier invention, the 'photographic revolver' that the astronomer Jules Janssen had built in 1874 to record the planet Venus passing in front of the Sun.","Marey soon moved on to even better methods. He built cameras that recorded many positions of a moving subject on a single fixed plate, so a runner dressed in black against a black background, with white stripes along the arms and legs, became a beautiful pattern of lines showing the whole motion at once. By about 1889 he was making cameras that used long strips of flexible film instead of glass. He studied falling cats, jumping athletes, swimming fish and the swirling smoke trails of air, which helped lead to early wind tunnels for studying flight.","Marey was not trying to make entertainment. He wanted to measure nature. But his single-camera series of pictures, taken rapidly through one lens onto a moving strip, is exactly what a movie camera does. His work helped scientists studying motion and inspired the inventors, in France and elsewhere, who soon built the first true motion-picture cameras."]},{"heading":"A Light in a Rochester Kitchen","paragraphs":["By 1888 the pieces of a revolution were lying on the table. Toys had shown that a series of quick pictures could fool the brain into seeing movement. Muybridge had shown that a camera could freeze a galloping horse. Marey had shown that a single camera could take a rapid series of pictures. And thanks to Maddox and Bennett, photographic plates were dry, fast and could be made in a factory. What was still missing was something flexible and cheap enough for ordinary people, and long enough to hold hundreds of pictures in a row.","Meanwhile, in Rochester, New York, a young bank clerk had been spending his evenings doing something unusual. After a full day of adding up numbers at the bank, he came home and experimented late into the night in his mother's kitchen, mixing gelatin emulsions and coating glass plates, sometimes falling asleep in his clothes. He had first bought a camera for a holiday trip and had been shocked by how heavy, messy and complicated photography was. He was convinced that it could be made simple enough for anyone. His name was George Eastman."]}],"deepDive":"Why do the toys in this part work? For much of the 1800s the standard answer was 'persistence of vision': the idea that an image lingers on the eye for a fraction of a second, so pictures blend together. Psychologists now think this explanation is too simple. Lingering images would produce blurry overlaps, not convincing motion. Instead, the brain actively interprets a series of slightly different, briefly flashed images as one object moving, an effect called apparent motion (studied by Max Wertheimer and others in the early 1900s). The slits in a phenakistiscope or zoetrope matter because they act as a shutter, cutting each picture into a short flash and preventing smearing. Muybridge's achievement also depended on chemistry and engineering. Ripened gelatin emulsions were dramatically more sensitive than wet collodion, and a bright white backdrop reflected as much light as possible, so even very short exposures (often quoted as around 1/1000 to 1/2000 of a second for his shutters, though exact figures are debated) could record a silhouette. The photographs are mainly outlines; fine detail was limited, and some published versions were retouched, a point that critics raised at the time. Historians also discuss credit. Muybridge's 1878 results were genuine and groundbreaking, but the electrical triggering system relied on engineers from Stanford's Central Pacific Railroad, and Stanford's 1882 book barely mentioned Muybridge, damaging his reputation in Britain for a time. Muybridge's sequences are not quite 'movies': each frame comes from a different camera at a slightly different viewpoint, and the intervals depend on the horse's speed rather than a steady clock. Marey's approach, with one lens and evenly timed exposures, is closer in principle to cinema, which is why many historians see the two men as complementary: Muybridge proved that instants could be captured, Marey showed how to record them systematically. Their work also launched biomechanics, the scientific study of movement, used today in sports science, medicine and robotics. Finally, the famous 25,000-dollar wager is a good example of how legends attach themselves to inventions; Stanford's interest in improving his racehorses is a much better documented motive.","funFact":"Muybridge's zoopraxiscope and most of its painted glass discs are kept at the Kingston Museum in Kingston upon Thames, the English town where he was born and died.","check":{"q":"What did Muybridge's 1878 photographs show about a galloping horse?","a":"All four hooves leave the ground at once, with the legs tucked under the body","wrong":["A galloping horse always keeps one hoof on the ground","All four legs stretch out forwards and backwards, like in old paintings","The horse's back legs leave the ground but the front legs never do"]},"sources":[{"title":"The Horse in Motion (Sallie Gardner at a Gallop)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sallie_Gardner_at_a_Gallop"},{"title":"Eadweard Muybridge","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eadweard_Muybridge"},{"title":"Zoopraxiscope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zoopraxiscope"},{"title":"Chronophotographic gun","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chronophotographic_gun"},{"title":"Richard Leach Maddox","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Leach_Maddox"},{"title":"Phenakistiscope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phenakistiscope"},{"title":"Zoetrope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zoetrope"},{"title":"Thaumatrope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thaumatrope"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/08-1.jpg","alt":"Muybridge's 1878 card \"The Horse in Motion\": twelve pictures of the mare Sallie Gardner galloping.","file":"The Horse in Motion high res.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Horse_in_Motion_high_res.jpg","author":"Eadweard Muybridge","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/08-2.jpg","alt":"A phenakistiscope disc from about 1830 with slits around the edge, leaping cats and nodding donkeys.","file":"Optical Toy, Phenakistiscope Disc with Cats and Donkey, ca. 1830 (CH 18607981).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Optical_Toy,_Phenakistiscope_Disc_with_Cats_and_Donkey,_ca._1830_(CH_18607981).jpg","author":"Unknown artist","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/08-3.jpg","alt":"Engraving of Marey's 1882 photographic gun: the rifle-shaped camera, its spinning shutter and its round plate box.","file":"Fusil photographique Marey2.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fusil_photographique_Marey2.png","author":"Louis Poyet","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3804},{"n":9,"title":"You Press the Button: Eastman and Kodak","when":"1878 – 1910s","summary":"A young bank clerk named George Eastman mixed photo chemicals in his mom's kitchen at night. He went on to invent a little box camera called the Kodak that anyone could use: you just pressed a button and mailed it back. Soon even kids had one-dollar Brownie cameras and families everywhere were filling photo albums.","sections":[{"heading":"A Bank Clerk with a Kitchen Laboratory","paragraphs":["Picture a tall, serious young man walking home through the streets of Rochester, New York, in the late 1870s. By day he sits at a desk in a savings bank, adding columns of numbers in neat handwriting. By night he does something much stranger. He shuts himself in his mother's kitchen, mixes warm gelatin with silver salts, pours the gooey mixture over squares of glass and sets them to dry in the dark. Sometimes he works so late that he falls asleep beside the kitchen stove, still in his clothes. His name is George Eastman, and he is about to change who gets to take photographs.","Eastman was born on 12 July 1854 in the small village of Waterville, New York. When he was about six, his family moved to the growing city of Rochester, where his father had started a business school. Then disaster struck: his father died in 1862, leaving his mother, Maria, to raise George and his two older sisters with very little money. One of his sisters, Katie, died when George was fifteen. To help pay the bills, George left school in his early teens and went to work, first as an office boy and then as a junior clerk at an insurance firm and later at the Rochester Savings Bank. He never went to college. Almost everything he knew about chemistry and machines he taught himself.","Photography came into his life almost by accident. In 1877, when he was 23, Eastman planned a holiday to the island of Santo Domingo in the Caribbean, and someone suggested he bring back pictures. He bought a full outfit of photographic gear and paid about five dollars for lessons in how to use it. In the end he never made the trip, but he had caught the photography bug.","What shocked him was how much equipment one picture needed. This was the age of the wet collodion process that you met in Part 7. A photographer heading outdoors had to haul a big wooden camera, a heavy tripod, a stack of glass plates, bottles of chemicals, a water jug and a tent to use as a darkroom, because each plate had to be coated, exposed and developed while it was still wet. Eastman later joked that the whole kit was a load for a pack horse. He began to wonder whether all of this could be made simpler.","The answer was arriving from England. As you saw in Part 8, Richard Leach Maddox had suggested in 1871 that photographers coat glass with gelatin containing silver bromide instead of wet collodion. Once dry, these gelatin dry plates kept their sensitivity for weeks or months. Eastman read about them in British photography magazines, copied the recipes, and started experimenting in that kitchen. If plates could be made ahead of time, he realized, then someone could make them in a factory, by the thousands, and sell them to photographers. That someone might as well be him."]},{"heading":"Dry Plates, a Buggy-Whip Partner and a Hard Lesson","paragraphs":["Coating glass by hand is slow and messy. Pour too much emulsion and it runs thick at one edge; pour too little and bare patches appear. In 1879 Eastman designed a machine that spread an even coat of emulsion across glass plates, and he travelled to London, then the centre of the photographic business, to patent it. He patented it in the United States as well. He was still working at the bank, using his savings to rent space and buy chemicals.","In April 1880 he began making dry plates to sell. Business was good enough that he needed a partner with money. He found one in Henry A. Strong, a Rochester businessman whose family made buggy whips and who had lodged in the Eastman family home. On 1 January 1881 the two men formed the Eastman Dry Plate Company, with Strong as president and Eastman as treasurer, though Eastman was the one who really ran things. Later that year he finally quit the bank to work on photography full time.","Then came the lesson that shaped his whole career. Not long after the company got going, photographers began complaining that Eastman plates were coming out fogged and weak. Some of the gelatin he had bought had a problem that ruined the emulsion, and plates sitting on dealers' shelves had lost their sensitivity. Eastman could have blamed the customers. Instead he recalled the faulty plates and replaced them, even though it nearly wiped out his young company. He then spent weeks running test after test until he could make good plates again. Afterward he said that the firm's reputation mattered more than any single sale.","This experience taught Eastman two things he never forgot. First, a factory product had to be reliable every single time, so he became fussy about testing and quality control. Second, he did not want to depend only on selling one fragile, heavy product to professional photographers. He started to dream of something lighter than glass, something that could be rolled up."]},{"heading":"Goodbye, Glass: The First Roll Film","paragraphs":["Glass has real problems as a home for photographs. It is heavy; a box of plates for a day's shooting could weigh several kilograms. It breaks. And each plate holds only one picture, so you must swap plates in the dark or carry a separate holder for every shot. A flexible material that could be wound on a spool, like thread on a reel, would let a photographer carry dozens of pictures in a pocket-sized roll.","Eastman's first try used paper. In 1884 and 1885 he and a camera maker named William H. Walker designed a roll holder, a small box with two spools that could be fitted to the back of an ordinary plate camera. The patent for this Roll Holder for Photographic Films was issued in May 1885. Inside it was a long ribbon of paper coated with light-sensitive emulsion. After each picture, you turned a key to wind fresh film into place. The company called its paper film American Film.","Paper, though, has fibres and grain. When light shone through a paper negative to make a print, the texture of the paper showed up as a blotchy pattern, just as with Talbot's calotypes in Part 5. Eastman's clever fix was stripping film: the emulsion was coated on paper with a soluble layer in between, and after developing, the emulsion was peeled off the paper and transferred onto a clear sheet of gelatin. It worked, but it was fiddly enough that skilled darkroom workers struggled with it.","Professional photographers mostly shrugged. They were used to plates and saw little reason to change. That disappointment pushed Eastman toward a bold idea. Perhaps the real market was not the few thousand professionals at all, but millions of ordinary people who had never taken a picture in their lives because it seemed too hard. To reach them, he would have to sell not just film but a whole system: a camera so simple that anyone could use it, and a service that would do all the hard chemistry for them."]},{"heading":"The Kodak: You Press the Button, We Do the Rest","paragraphs":["In 1888 Eastman launched the result, a small wooden box covered in black leather, about 17 centimetres long, 10 centimetres tall and 8.5 centimetres wide. It was called simply the Kodak. There were no knobs for focus and no settings to puzzle over. The lens, with a focal length of about 57 millimetres, was fixed in place so that everything from a few feet away to the far distance looked reasonably sharp. It did not even have a proper viewfinder; you aimed it using lines marked on the top of the box.","Taking a picture was a three-step dance. You pulled a string on top to set the shutter, a rotating cylinder inside the box. You pressed a button to take the picture. Then you turned a key to wind the film forward for the next one. The camera came already loaded with a roll long enough for 100 exposures, and each picture came out round, about 2.5 inches (roughly 6.5 centimetres) across. The round shape matched the circle of sharp light the simple lens could produce, so nothing in the corners would look blurry.","The truly new part came after the last picture. Instead of opening the camera in a darkroom, the owner mailed the whole camera back to the Eastman factory in Rochester with $10. Workers there removed the film, developed it, printed the pictures, loaded a fresh 100-exposure roll and sent everything back: camera, prints and negatives. The camera itself cost $25. That was a lot of money in 1888, about two weeks or more of wages for many workers, so the first Kodak owners tended to be fairly well-off. But for the first time, you did not need any chemistry knowledge at all.","Eastman summed it up in one of the most famous advertising slogans ever written: “You press the button, we do the rest.” He was a brilliant marketer. His advertisements appeared in popular magazines, not just photography journals, and they showed ordinary people, especially young women, happily snapping pictures. Later, a long-running character called the Kodak Girl, a fashionable young woman with a camera, appeared in Kodak advertisements and posters, sending the message that photography was easy, modern and fun rather than a messy job for men in stained aprons.","And then there was the name. Eastman invented the word Kodak himself, and it was trademarked in 1888. It does not come from any language or have a hidden meaning. Working with his mother, he played with letter tiles, hunting for a word that was short, easy to say in any language and unlike any other name. He was especially fond of the letter K, which he called a “strong, incisive sort of letter,” and he liked that his new word both started and ended with one. Other companies made cameras that used roll film, but few had a name that was so easy to remember. In everyday speech, people soon called almost any small camera a kodak.","Success came quickly. In 1889 the company offered a bigger Kodak No. 2 that took larger round pictures, and a whole family of Kodak models followed in the 1890s, including folding cameras that tucked into a coat pocket. Eastman reorganized his business as the Eastman Company in 1889, and in 1892 it became the Eastman Kodak Company. In 1890 work began on a huge new factory site outside Rochester that would become Kodak Park, eventually one of the largest industrial sites in the United States."]},{"heading":"Clear Film and a Clergyman's Patent","paragraphs":["Paper-backed film had always been a stopgap. What Eastman really wanted was a film base that was thin, tough, flexible and as clear as glass, so that light could shine straight through the negative with no paper texture in the way. The material he needed was a kind of early plastic called celluloid, made from nitrocellulose (cotton or wood fibres treated with nitric acid) softened with camphor. Celluloid was already being used to make combs, collars and imitation ivory billiard balls. Could it be poured into sheets thin enough for film?","In 1886 Eastman hired a young chemist named Henry Reichenbach to find out. Reichenbach experimented with dissolving nitrocellulose in different solvents, spreading the syrupy liquid in a thin layer, letting it dry and then coating it with emulsion. In 1889 the company patented its transparent nitrocellulose roll film and began selling it. Soon all Kodak cameras used the new film, and photographers no longer had to peel emulsion off paper. A few years later, in the early 1890s, Kodak added film rolls with a light-proof paper backing that could be loaded into a camera in daylight, so owners could change film themselves instead of mailing the whole camera to Rochester.","There was a twist. Across the state line in Newark, New Jersey, lived a clergyman named Hannibal Goodwin, the rector of the House of Prayer Church from 1867. Goodwin wanted better pictures to project for Bible lessons and Sunday school talks, because glass lantern slides were heavy and breakable. Working in his attic, he developed his own way of making a clear, flexible film from nitrocellulose, and on 2 May 1887 he filed a patent application for it, about two years before Eastman's company filed for its own.","Goodwin's application crawled through the patent office for more than eleven years, partly because examiners found his descriptions confusing and sent it back again and again. It was finally granted on 13 September 1898 as US patent 610,861. Goodwin hoped to manufacture film himself, but he died in 1900 before he could. The patent passed to new owners and ended up in the hands of the camera and film maker Ansco, a Kodak rival, which sued Eastman Kodak for using Goodwin's invention without permission.","The court battle dragged on for years. In 1913 a federal court ruled that Kodak had infringed Goodwin's patent, and after appeals, in March 1914, Kodak agreed to pay about $5 million to settle the case, a gigantic sum at the time. Accounts differ on exactly who received what, but Goodwin's family and the companies that had backed his patent finally shared in the money. Historians still argue about who deserves the most credit for celluloid roll film: Goodwin filed first, but Reichenbach and Eastman turned clear film into a reliable product made by the mile.","Celluloid roll film turned out to be important for a reason nobody had planned. A long, clear, flexible strip could hold not just 100 separate snapshots but thousands of pictures taken one after another, very quickly. Inventors trying to make moving pictures, from Thomas Edison's laboratory in New Jersey to workshops in Europe, would soon be buying Eastman's film by the roll. The same base material, cut into strips and punched with holes, became motion picture film."]},{"heading":"The Brownie: A One-Dollar Camera for Kids","paragraphs":["By the end of the 1890s, Eastman wanted to reach an even bigger crowd: children. In February 1900 Kodak launched the Brownie, a simple box camera made of cardboard covered in imitation leather, about 13 centimetres long. It was designed by Frank A. Brownell, Kodak's chief camera designer, and it cost just $1. In Britain it sold for five shillings. A roll of film for it was cheap too, and so was developing, which is how Kodak really made its money: sell the camera for very little, then earn profits from the film people bought again and again.","The Brownie took square pictures 2¼ inches (about 5.7 centimetres) on each side, using a new film size called No. 117. You loaded it yourself, pointed it at your subject and worked the shutter. Its name came from the Brownies, mischievous little elf-like characters invented by the Canadian writer and artist Palmer Cox, whose books and cartoons were hugely popular with children. Kodak put the Brownies on boxes and in advertisements, so children would think of the camera as their own.","How could a camera be so cheap? The Brownie stripped photography down to its basics, the same ones you met in Part 6. At the front sat a single simple lens with a fixed focus, so anything more than a few steps away looked sharp enough. Behind it, a spring-loaded shutter flicked open for a small fraction of a second when you worked the shutter release, fast enough to catch a smiling friend in daylight but not fast enough for dim rooms. There was no exposure dial and no focusing ring. The back of the box came off so you could slip in a roll of film, and a winding key on the side moved it forward after each shot, with light-proof paper wound along with the film to keep it safe. The design was so basic that factory workers could assemble Brownies quickly from inexpensive parts, which kept the price at one dollar.","The plan worked brilliantly. Kodak shipped more than 150,000 Brownies in the first year of production. In 1901 came the No. 2 Brownie, which took larger rectangular pictures for $2. Over the decades Kodak sold Brownie cameras in dozens of models, from a special Boy Scout edition in the 1930s to the Brownie Starflash of 1957, Kodak's first camera with a built-in flash. The very last Brownie, a cartridge model made in Brazil, appeared in 1986, more than 85 years after the first.","The Brownie also changed who took pictures. Children who had never been allowed to touch expensive equipment could now photograph their pets, friends and siblings. Many famous photographers later said their first camera was a Brownie. More importantly, millions of ordinary families, not just wealthy ones, could now afford to make their own pictures."]},{"heading":"The Snapshot Revolution","paragraphs":["The word snapshot is older than the Kodak. In 1860 the astronomer John Herschel, whom you met in Part 5, wrote about the possibility of taking a photograph quickly, as if by a snap-shot, the name hunters used for a quick shot fired without careful aim. With fast dry plates and roll film, that possibility became real. A snapshot came to mean a quick, casual photo taken by an ordinary person, usually with a cheap camera and with no aim except to remember something.","Think about what photographs had been before. Since the 1840s, most people had gone to a photographer's studio, put on their best clothes, and sat stiffly in front of a painted backdrop. A portrait was a special event, maybe once a year or once in a lifetime. With a Kodak or a Brownie, the camera came to you. People photographed picnics, beach trips, snowball fights, new babies, birthday cakes, first cars, family dogs and silly faces. Holidays now came home in envelopes of prints.","These pictures went into a new kind of family treasure: the photo album. Families glued snapshots onto black paper pages, wrote names and dates in white ink underneath, and pulled the albums out whenever relatives visited. For the first time, ordinary people kept their own visual history instead of relying on a professional to decide how they should look. Historians today love these albums, because they show how everyday people really lived, dressed and played, details that rarely appeared in official records or paintings.","Not every photographer cheered. Some professionals and serious amateurs sniffed that snapshooters were careless, wasting film on crooked, blurry pictures of nothing in particular. Others worried that cheap cameras would put studio photographers out of business. In fact, many studios survived for decades by offering things a box camera could not, such as careful lighting, large prints and formal wedding portraits. Meanwhile, photographic clubs and magazines for amateurs multiplied, and some snapshot takers grew into skilled photographers who entered competitions and exhibitions. Photography had split into two worlds that still exist today: the quick, personal snapshot and the carefully planned picture made as art or as a job.","Snapshots also travelled. Families separated by migration could mail pictures across oceans, so grandparents in Europe or Asia could see grandchildren they might never meet. Soldiers carried photos of loved ones in their pockets. And as cameras grew smaller, people began taking them everywhere. Kodak's 1898 Folding Pocket Kodak, with its fold-out bellows, really could fit in a large coat pocket. Much later, advertisers would invent the phrase “a Kodak moment” for a happy event worth photographing, showing how completely Eastman's company had become linked with memories."]},{"heading":"The Right to Be Let Alone","paragraphs":["Not everyone was delighted. Before small cameras, it was almost impossible to photograph someone without their knowledge: the subject had to sit still in a studio, often paying for the privilege. Now a stranger with a box under their arm could point it at you on a beach, at a funeral or through a window, and you might never know. Newspapers in the 1890s grumbled about camera fiends who snapped people without asking.","At the same time, newspapers were changing. Cheap, mass-circulation papers competed for readers with gossip about wealthy and famous people: their parties, weddings and quarrels. New printing methods were starting to make it possible to print photographs and drawings based on them in large numbers. Put candid cameras and gossip columns together, and private life suddenly felt exposed.","Two young Boston lawyers decided the law needed to catch up. On 15 December 1890, Samuel D. Warren and Louis D. Brandeis published an article called “The Right to Privacy” in the Harvard Law Review. They argued that recent inventions, specifically naming instantaneous photographs, along with the newspaper business, had invaded the sacred privacy of home and family life. They said every person had what an earlier judge had called “the right to be let alone,” and that courts should protect people from having their private lives and images published without consent.","The article is often described as one of the most influential law review articles ever written. Brandeis, who is thought to have written most of it, later became a Justice of the US Supreme Court in 1916 and kept defending privacy for the rest of his career. Laws and court decisions in the United States and elsewhere slowly built a right to privacy, including rules against using someone's picture in an advertisement without permission. The questions Warren and Brandeis raised in 1890 are still with us today, every time someone wonders whether it is okay to post a photo of a friend online.","By 1900 George Eastman had done what he set out to do: he had turned photography from a difficult craft into something anyone could do with the press of a button. Along the way he had given the world a long, clear, flexible strip of celluloid film. Photographers saw it as a better way to carry 100 snapshots. But a handful of inventors on both sides of the Atlantic looked at that same strip and saw something else entirely. It was exactly what they needed to capture not one moment, but thousands of moments in a row, fast enough to bring a whole scene back to life."]}],"deepDive":"Historians often describe Eastman's real invention not as a single gadget but as a business system. Before 1888, photography was a craft: the photographer owned the chemistry, the darkroom and the skill. The Kodak split the job in two. The customer only aimed and pressed; a factory did everything else. This idea, which business writers sometimes call the razor-and-blades model, priced the camera low and earned steady profits from film and processing. The Brownie at $1 pushed it to the limit, and Kodak's enormous film sales paid for research, advertising and factories such as Kodak Park. The approach was so successful that for most of the twentieth century Kodak dominated film sales in the United States, which helps explain why the company later struggled when digital pictures made film unnecessary (see Part 19).\n\nThe celluloid film story is a classic patent puzzle. Hannibal Goodwin filed his application on 2 May 1887; Eastman's company, with chemist Henry Reichenbach, filed later but turned transparent nitrocellulose film into a mass-produced product in 1889. Goodwin's patent was not granted until 13 September 1898 because of repeated revisions. Courts eventually sided with the Goodwin patent's owners, and Kodak paid about $5 million in 1914. Sources even disagree about details such as exactly which company was named in the suit, which is a useful reminder that 'firsts' in invention history are often decided by lawyers years later, not by a single moment in a lab.\n\nNitrocellulose had a dark side. It is chemically related to guncotton, an explosive, and it burns fiercely once lit. Kodak introduced slower-burning cellulose acetate 'safety film' in 1908, but nitrate stayed standard for professional 35mm movie film until 1951, leading to many fires and lost films (see Parts 11, 12 and 17).\n\nFinally, the Kodak changed what photographs were for. Studio portraits had been formal and posed; snapshots were casual, personal and abundant. Cultural historians argue that snapshots shaped how families remembered themselves, often recording happy moments and leaving out hard ones. Warren and Brandeis's 1890 article shows that the privacy worries of the camera age began almost as soon as cameras became small and fast.","funFact":"The very first Kodak camera made round pictures, about 2.5 inches across, and each roll held enough film for 100 of them.","check":{"q":"What did owners of the first Kodak camera (1888) do when all 100 pictures were taken?","a":"Mailed the whole camera back to the factory, which developed the pictures and reloaded it","wrong":["Developed the film themselves in a tent darkroom","Took the glass plates to a local portrait studio","Threw the camera away and bought a new one"]},"sources":[{"title":"George Eastman","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Eastman"},{"title":"Kodak","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kodak"},{"title":"The Kodak box camera (c. 1888)","publisher":"Science Museum Group Collection","url":"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co15731/the-kodak-box-camera"},{"title":"George Eastman: history","publisher":"Kodak","url":"https://www.kodak.com/en/company/page/george-eastman-history"},{"title":"Hannibal Goodwin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hannibal_Goodwin"},{"title":"Brownie (camera)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownie_(camera)"},{"title":"The Right to Privacy (article)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Right_to_Privacy_(article)"},{"title":"Photographic film","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photographic_film"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/09-1.jpg","alt":"George Eastman's 1888 patent drawing for the Kodak box camera, showing the outside and the film rolls inside.","file":"George Eastman patent no 388,850.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Eastman_patent_no_388,850.png","author":"George Eastman","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/09-2.jpg","alt":"George Eastman, founder of Kodak, in a portrait by the Paris photographer Paul Nadar.","file":"George Eastman founder of Eastman Kodak Company.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Eastman_founder_of_Eastman_Kodak_Company.jpg","author":"Paul Nadar","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/09-3.jpg","alt":"An original Kodak Brownie box camera with its clip-on viewfinder. It went on sale in 1900 for one dollar.","file":"Brownie camera avec son viseur.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brownie_camera_avec_son_viseur.jpg","author":"Deveycx","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":3999},{"n":10,"title":"The Race to Make Pictures Move","when":"1888 – 1896","summary":"In 1888 a Frenchman in England filmed his family walking in a garden for about two seconds, then mysteriously vanished. Soon Thomas Edison's helpers built a peephole movie machine, and in Paris the Lumière brothers showed films on a big screen to a paying crowd. Lots of inventors in different countries raced to make pictures move, and together they invented the movies.","sections":[{"heading":"Two Seconds in a Leeds Garden","paragraphs":["It is a cool Sunday in October 1888, in the garden of a large house called Oakwood Grange in Roundhay, a leafy part of Leeds in the north of England. Four people are walking on the lawn in a slightly odd way, turning in circles, coats flapping, as if someone has asked them to keep moving. Facing them, mounted on a sturdy wooden stand, is a strange box with a crank. Behind it stands a tall Frenchman named Louis Le Prince. When he turns the handle, a strip of film inside jerks past the lens again and again. He is not taking one photograph. He is taking a whole series of them, one after another.","The result, now called Roundhay Garden Scene, was filmed on 14 October 1888. It lasts only about two seconds. Yet it is generally considered the oldest surviving motion picture. The people in it were Le Prince's son Adolphe, his father-in-law Joseph Whitley, his mother-in-law Sarah Whitley, and a family friend, Annie Hartley. Sadly, Sarah Whitley died just ten days later, so these few seconds of her walking in the garden are an unexpected memorial.","Louis Le Prince was born in Metz, in eastern France, in 1841. He studied painting and chemistry and learned about photography as a young man. In 1866 he moved to Leeds to work for a brass-founding company run by his friend John Whitley, whose sister Elizabeth he married. The couple later ran a school of applied art, and they became known for a method of fixing colour photographs onto metal and pottery. Le Prince also spent several years in the United States, where he became fascinated by the idea of capturing motion.","His first design, patented in 1888, was a machine with 16 lenses, each taking a picture in turn. It was clumsy, because each lens saw the scene from a slightly different position, making the picture wobble. So he built a camera with a single lens that moved a strip of sensitized film in jerks, stopping it briefly for each exposure. For Roundhay Garden Scene he used Eastman paper-based film, the kind you met in Part 9. Adolphe later said the camera ran at 12 frames per second, though modern experts who have studied the surviving frames think it may have been closer to 7.","That same autumn Le Prince filmed traffic crossing Leeds Bridge, with horse-drawn carriages and people on foot moving across the frame, and he filmed Adolphe playing an accordion. The original paper negatives did not survive. In the 1930s, the Science Museum in London made copies on glass of 20 frames of Roundhay Garden Scene, and those copies are how we can still watch it today. Le Prince's surviving films and equipment are now looked after by the National Science and Media Museum in Bradford, not far from Leeds."]},{"heading":"The Man Who Vanished","paragraphs":["By 1890 Le Prince was ready to show his invention to the world. His wife and children had moved to New York, and he planned to join them to exhibit his work in the United States. First he visited his brother in Dijon, in eastern France. On 16 September 1890 he boarded a train from Dijon heading toward Paris, where friends were expecting him. He never arrived. Neither he nor his luggage was ever found, and no one has ever been able to prove what happened to him. French authorities eventually declared him dead in 1897.","Because Le Prince disappeared just before he might have become famous, people have been arguing about his fate ever since. Some have suggested that rivals who wanted his invention out of the way were involved, and his family suspected Thomas Edison's side because of the patent battles that later followed. Others have wondered whether he chose to disappear because of money troubles, or whether he took his own life. In 2003 researchers found a photograph in the Paris police archives of a drowned man from 1890 who looked somewhat like Le Prince, but others have argued that the man was too short to be him. None of these theories has been proved. The honest answer is that the mystery is unsolved.","His son Adolphe later testified in an American court case about who had first invented motion picture cameras, hoping to win recognition for his father, but the court did not accept Le Prince's work as evidence in the way the family hoped. Adolphe himself died young a few years later. For decades Le Prince was mostly forgotten outside Leeds, and the credit for inventing cinema went to bigger names. Only in the twentieth century did historians rediscover how early and how clever his work had been. Leeds now proudly honours him as a local hero, and a plaque on Leeds Bridge remembers the films he made there."]},{"heading":"Edison's Idea and Dickson's Hands","paragraphs":["Across the Atlantic, in the busy laboratory of the world's most famous inventor, a similar dream was taking shape. In October 1888 Thomas Edison filed a caveat (a kind of early notice to the patent office announcing an idea he was working on) describing a machine that would “do for the Eye what the phonograph does for the Ear.” Edison had invented the phonograph in 1877, so it seemed natural to him that pictures, like sounds, might be recorded and played back. In March 1889 he gave the machine a name: the Kinetoscope, from Greek words meaning “motion” and “to watch.”","Edison was busy with many projects, from electric lighting to iron-ore mining, so he handed the job to one of his most talented assistants, William Kennedy Laurie Dickson. Dickson was born in 1860 in Brittany, France, to a Scottish father and an American mother. As a young man he wrote to Edison asking for a job, was turned down, came to America anyway and was finally hired at Edison's Menlo Park laboratory in 1883. He was a skilled photographer, an engineer and an artist, exactly the mix of talents the project needed. Historians agree that Dickson and his assistants did most of the hands-on experimental work on Edison's motion pictures between 1889 and 1892, while Edison took most of the public credit.","The first experiments copied the phonograph a little too closely. Dickson and his team tried placing rows of tiny photographs in a spiral around a cylinder, which turned while a viewer looked at the pictures through a microscope-like eyepiece. Around 1889 the team made some test films this way, including a short clip known as Monkeyshines, in which a lab worker in a white costume moved around clumsily. The pictures were tiny, blurry and hard to light. A cylinder simply could not hold enough images big enough to be clear.","The breakthrough was switching to a long strip of Eastman's new transparent celluloid film. Edison had travelled to Paris for the great Exposition of 1889, where he met Étienne-Jules Marey, whose chronophotography you met in Part 8 and who was already capturing motion on strips. Back in New Jersey, the team turned to the strip approach. On 20 May 1891, about 150 members of the National Federation of Women's Clubs visiting Edison's laboratory peered into a prototype Kinetoscope and saw a short film of Dickson himself bowing, smiling and taking off his hat. That film, Dickson Greeting, is one of the earliest motion pictures made in America."]},{"heading":"The Kinetograph, the Kinetoscope and 35mm Film","paragraphs":["Edison's system had two machines. The camera was the Kinetograph, a heavy box driven by an electric motor, so heavy that it mostly stayed in one place. Inside, a toothed wheel called a sprocket gripped the film, pulled one section into position behind the lens, held it still while a shutter let in light, then pulled the next section into place. It did this about 40 times a second, much faster than the 16 or so frames per second that later became common for silent films.","The film strip itself was the team's most lasting contribution. Dickson settled on a strip 1⅜ inches wide, almost exactly 35 millimetres, cut from Eastman film. Along each edge of each frame ran four small rectangular holes, the sprocket holes, so the gears could move the film with precision. Each frame was about one inch wide and three-quarters of an inch tall. This exact format, 35mm film with four perforations per frame on each side, became the world standard for motion pictures and remained the main professional film format for more than a century. If you ever see a reel of old Hollywood film, it is almost certainly a direct descendant of Dickson's strip.","Why such an odd width? Nobody is completely sure. A popular story says Dickson's team simply slit 70-millimetre Eastman rolls down the middle, but film historians say that tale does not hold up. Whatever the reason, nobody was thinking about creating a world standard; they were solving the problem in front of them. But because Edison's films spread around the world, and because other inventors wanted their machines to show Edison films too, many chose the same width and hole pattern. A practical shortcut in a New Jersey workshop became the shape of cinema for generations.","The viewer was the Kinetoscope, a wooden cabinet about as tall as a person's chest. Inside, a loop of film roughly 50 feet long wound endlessly around a series of spools. An electric lamp shone up through the film, and a spinning wheel with a narrow slit let the viewer glimpse each frame for a split second as it passed. You bent over and looked down through an eyepiece on top, like peeking into a box of secrets. A film lasted around 20 seconds, then looped back to the start. Only one person at a time could watch.","The completed system was shown publicly at the Brooklyn Institute of Arts and Sciences on 9 May 1893. The star film was Blacksmith Scene, in which three men, really Edison employees playing blacksmiths, hammer on an anvil and pass around a bottle. Edison did not pay to protect the Kinetoscope with patents in most other countries, a decision historians still argue about. That allowed others, such as the British instrument maker Robert W. Paul, to build copies and then their own film cameras."]},{"heading":"The Black Maria and the First Kinetoscope Parlor","paragraphs":["Films need light, lots of it. Early film was not very sensitive, and electric lights of the time were too weak to light a scene well. So in December 1892 workers at Edison's laboratory complex in West Orange, New Jersey, began building the world's first film studio, finished in 1893. It was a long, narrow wooden shed covered in black tar paper, with a section of roof that could be swung open to let in sunshine. The entire building sat on a circular track, a turntable, so it could be rotated during the day to follow the sun across the sky. Inside, walls painted black made the performers stand out brightly against a dark background.","Dickson and his colleagues nicknamed the building the Black Maria, after the slang name for police wagons of the time, because both were black, cramped and stuffy. Performers of every kind came to West Orange to be filmed: dancers, acrobats, boxers, comedians, trained animals and strongmen. One of the most famous early films made there is Fred Ott's Sneeze, from early 1894, in which an Edison employee takes a pinch of snuff and sneezes for the camera. The studio closed in 1901, when Edison moved film production to a new glass-roofed studio in New York City, and the old building was torn down in 1903. A replica was built on the site in 1954, and the National Park Service reopened it to visitors in April 2024.","Being filmed in the Black Maria was a strange experience. Performers had to squeeze their act into a small patch of floor in front of the camera, which could not move, and finish it in well under a minute, because that was all the film a single take could hold. In 1894 the famous strongman Eugen Sandow flexed his muscles there, and the sharpshooter Annie Oakley fired her rifle at glass balls. Dancers twirled long skirts, two cats were filmed in a tiny boxing ring, and real boxers fought rounds shortened to fit the film. These little films were not stories. They were moving snapshots of acts that people might otherwise pay to see in a theatre.","With a studio full of films, it was time to make money. On 14 April 1894 the Holland Brothers opened the first commercial Kinetoscope parlor at 1155 Broadway in New York City. It had ten Kinetoscope machines arranged in two rows of five, each showing a different film. For 25 cents a customer could watch every machine in one row, or for 50 cents, both rows. That was not cheap, but crowds lined up anyway. Within months, Kinetoscope parlors opened in Chicago, San Francisco, London and other cities, and Edison's company sold hundreds of machines.","The excitement did not last. Once people had seen the novelty of a 20-second film through a peephole, many did not come back, and the second year's profits fell by more than 95 percent. Edison tried to revive interest in 1895 with the Kinetophone, a Kinetoscope with a phonograph inside that played music through ear tubes, but the sound was not truly synchronized with the pictures and only about 45 were made. Edison believed the money was in selling machines to parlors, one viewer at a time. Other inventors were already betting on a different idea: throwing the moving picture onto a big screen so a whole room of people could watch together."]},{"heading":"Projectors Appear in Many Countries","paragraphs":["Projecting moving pictures sounds simple, but it was harder than it looks. A projector needed a bright light, a strong mechanism to move the film in jerks without tearing it, and a way to avoid melting or setting fire to the film with the heat of the lamp. During 1894 and 1895, inventors in the United States, Britain, France and Germany were all racing to solve these problems, often without knowing about each other.","In the United States, Charles Francis Jenkins showed a filmed motion picture to an audience in his hometown of Richmond, Indiana, in June 1894. In New York, the Latham family, with Dickson secretly advising them while he still worked for Edison, built a projector called the Eidoloscope and on 20 May 1895 showed a film of a boxing match to a paying audience, one of the earliest commercial film projections anywhere. The Lathams also developed a clever trick, a slack loop of film above and below the gate, still known as the Latham loop, that kept long films from snapping as they were jerked along.","In Berlin, two brothers named Max and Emil Skladanowsky built a projector called the Bioscop. Their solution to flicker was unusual: the Bioscop had two lenses and two strips of film, and it showed frames from each strip alternately, so one picture was always appearing while the other strip moved. The film was 44.5 millimetres wide and had no sprocket holes like Edison's. On 1 November 1895 they presented the Bioscop at the Wintergarten, a grand music hall in Berlin, as part of a three-hour variety show. Their program lasted about 15 minutes and included eight very short films, played over and over, such as acrobats, a folk dance and a boxing kangaroo.","In Britain, Robert W. Paul and the photographer Birt Acres made a camera in 1895 and filmed scenes such as waves crashing on the shore. Paul then designed his own projector, which he showed in London early in 1896. Each of these inventors could claim to be among the first to project films. That is exactly why historians say that no single person invented the movies: the technology grew in many places at once, with each team borrowing ideas, making mistakes and sometimes racing to patent offices."]},{"heading":"The Lumière Brothers and the Grand Café","paragraphs":["The inventors who usually get the most attention for this moment were two brothers from Lyon, in France: Auguste Lumière, born in 1862, and Louis Lumière, born in 1864. Their father, Antoine, was a painter and photographer who started a business making photographic plates. Louis, while still a teenager, improved the recipe for dry plates, and the Lumière factory in the Monplaisir district of Lyon grew into one of the biggest makers of photographic plates in Europe. The family had money, chemistry skills and machine workshops, a perfect combination for building a film machine.","Antoine had seen Edison's Kinetoscope in Paris in 1894 and urged his sons to do better. The brothers, with Louis leading the technical work, designed a machine they called the Cinématographe, borrowing a name first used by another French inventor, Léon Bouly, in 1892. They patented it on 13 February 1895. It was ingenious: a single hand-cranked wooden box that worked as a camera, then, with a few changes, as a printer to copy the film, and then, with a lamp behind it, as a projector. A small claw-like mechanism driven by a cam pulled the film down one frame at a time, using one round sprocket hole on each side of each frame. It weighed only about 7 kilograms, small enough to carry anywhere, which meant operators could film real life in streets, stations and gardens rather than in a studio.","The Cinématographe ran at about 15 to 16 frames per second, much slower than Edison's camera, which saved film. A typical roll was about 17 metres long, enough for roughly 50 seconds. The brothers' most famous early film, Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory, shows dozens of employees, many of them women in long skirts and hats, pouring out of the factory gates in Lyon, followed by a man on a bicycle and a dog. Three versions were filmed in 1895. One was shown privately on 22 March 1895 in Paris to about 200 members of a society for encouraging French industry, who were astonished.","Then came the famous evening. On 28 December 1895 the Lumières held a paid public screening in the Salon Indien, a basement room of the Grand Café on the Boulevard des Capucines in Paris. Only a small audience, a few dozen people, came to that first show; tickets reportedly cost one franc. The program had ten short films, each less than a minute long, and the whole show was over in well under half an hour. It included workers leaving the factory, blacksmiths at work, a baby being fed breakfast, people swimming in the sea and a little comedy in which a boy steps on a gardener's hose, the gardener gets sprayed in the face and then chases the boy. That comedy, later known as L'Arroseur arrosé, is often called the first fiction film comedy.","Word spread quickly, and soon queues stretched down the boulevard. In 1896 Lumière operators carried Cinématographes around the world, filming and screening in London, New York, Buenos Aires, Egypt and many other places, and bringing home pictures of distant cities. Many people in many countries saw their very first moving pictures thanks to a Lumière operator. Curiously, the brothers themselves are said to have called cinema “an invention without any future,” and they had mostly stopped making films by 1905 to concentrate on other projects, including the Autochrome colour plates you will meet in Part 13.","You may have heard that when the Lumières showed their film of a steam train pulling into the station at La Ciotat, the audience screamed and ran away from the screen. That story is a legend. The train film was not even on the program of 28 December 1895, and historians have found no reports written at the time describing a panic. Some viewers certainly found the approaching train startling and thrilling, but there is little evidence that anyone fled."]},{"heading":"Edison Catches Up: The Vitascope","paragraphs":["Back in the United States, Edison's company had fallen behind on projection, so it bought its way back in. In 1895 two young inventors, Thomas Armat and Charles Francis Jenkins, modified Jenkins's projector, the Phantoscope, and demonstrated it at the Cotton States and International Exposition in Atlanta. The partners soon quarrelled, each claiming to be the true inventor. Armat sold the rights to his version to the businessmen Norman Raff and Frank Gammon, who sold Edison's Kinetoscopes. They made a deal with Edison: his company would manufacture the projector, and it would be marketed under Edison's famous name as the Vitascope.","On 23 April 1896 the Vitascope had its first theatrical showing at Koster and Bial's Music Hall in New York City, as part of a vaudeville show. Huge images of dancers, a comic boxing scene and waves breaking on a beach, filmed in England by Robert Paul's partner Birt Acres, flickered onto a screen while a band played. Newspapers raved, and the waves especially impressed the audience. Within a few months, Vitascopes were touring many American cities. But only about 20 films were available, and competition from the Lumière Cinématographe and other projectors was fierce. By late 1896 Edison's company had introduced its own projector, the Projectoscope, and stopped selling the Vitascope.","So who invented the movies? Le Prince filmed the first surviving moving pictures in Leeds. Dickson and the Edison team created the 35mm film strip and the first film studio. The Lathams and Jenkins showed early projections in America, the Skladanowskys in Germany, Paul and Acres in Britain, and the Lumières turned the Cinématographe into a worldwide sensation. Many inventors in several countries contributed, and none of them could have done it without Eastman's celluloid film. There is still one question left, and it is a strange one. Every one of these films is just a row of still photographs. So why does your brain see a man bowing, a train rolling and waves crashing, instead of a stack of frozen pictures?"]}],"deepDive":"Ask 'who invented cinema?' and the answer depends on what you count as cinema. If it means recording a sequence of photographs on a strip with a single camera, Louis Le Prince's Roundhay Garden Scene of 14 October 1888 is the oldest surviving example. If it means a practical system of camera, film format and viewer, the Edison laboratory's Kinetograph and Kinetoscope, built largely by W. K. L. Dickson between 1889 and 1893, have the strongest claim. If it means projected films for a paying audience, the Lathams' Eidoloscope (20 May 1895, New York) and the Skladanowskys' Bioscop (1 November 1895, Berlin) both came before the Lumières' famous show of 28 December 1895. French tradition and many textbooks still celebrate the Lumière date, because the Cinématographe was portable, reliable and quickly spread worldwide, and because its programme looks like a real film show.\n\nTechnical numbers reveal very different design choices. Edison's camera ran at about 40 frames per second, using lots of film per second of action, while the Lumières recommended about 15 to 16 frames per second; at 16 frames per second a 17-metre roll gave roughly 800 frames, about 50 seconds. Edison's 35mm film had four rectangular perforations on each side of every frame, while the Lumière film had a single round hole per side. Edison's format won, partly because his films circulated so widely, and 35mm with four perforations per frame remained the main professional film standard for more than a century.\n\nCredit was shaped by business and law. Edison put his name on Dickson's work and on the Armat–Jenkins projector he sold as the Vitascope. Armat and Jenkins quarrelled for years over who invented their machine. Edison's company later fought a long series of patent lawsuits to control American filmmaking, and Le Prince's family tried, unsuccessfully, to use those courts to win recognition for him. His disappearance in 1890 remains unexplained, and claims that he was murdered are speculation, not established fact. Modern historians, such as those who have studied Dickson's papers, have worked to restore credit to the people who did the hands-on engineering.","funFact":"The oldest surviving motion picture, Roundhay Garden Scene from 1888, lasts only about two seconds, and the original film is lost; it survives because the Science Museum in London copied 20 of its frames onto glass in the 1930s.","check":{"q":"Where and when did the Lumière brothers hold their famous first paid public film screening?","a":"At the Grand Café in Paris on 28 December 1895","wrong":["At the Black Maria in New Jersey in 1893","At the Wintergarten in Berlin on 1 November 1895","At Koster and Bial's Music Hall in New York in April 1896"]},"sources":[{"title":"Louis Le Prince","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Le_Prince"},{"title":"Roundhay Garden Scene","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roundhay_Garden_Scene"},{"title":"William Kennedy Dickson","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Kennedy_Dickson"},{"title":"Kinetoscope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinetoscope"},{"title":"Edison's Black Maria","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edison%27s_Black_Maria"},{"title":"Max Skladanowsky","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Skladanowsky"},{"title":"Cinématographe","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cin%C3%A9matographe"},{"title":"Vitascope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitascope"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/10-1.jpg","alt":"A frame from Louis Le Prince's Roundhay Garden Scene (1888): four people walking in a garden in Leeds.","file":"LouisLePrinceFirstFilmEver RoundhayGardenScene.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LouisLePrinceFirstFilmEver_RoundhayGardenScene.png","author":"Louis Le Prince","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/10-2.jpg","alt":"An Edison Kinetoscope with its side opened, showing the long film loop running over rows of spools.","file":"Kinetoscope vue intérieur.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kinetoscope_vue_int%C3%A9rieur.jpg","author":"William K L Dickson","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/10-3.jpg","alt":"Lumière Cinématographe no. 101 (1896), a wooden box camera set up for filming, at the Institut Lumière in Lyon.","file":"Cinématographe Lumière numéro 101 équipé pour la prise de vue (1896) - Institut Lumière (Lyon) - 3.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cin%C3%A9matographe_Lumi%C3%A8re_num%C3%A9ro_101_%C3%A9quip%C3%A9_pour_la_prise_de_vue_(1896)_-_Institut_Lumi%C3%A8re_(Lyon)_-_3.JPG","author":"Benoît Prieur","license":"CC0"}],"words":3974},{"n":11,"title":"How Movies Trick Your Eyes","when":"The science (explained with 1890s–1920s examples)","summary":"A movie is really thousands of still photos shown very fast, and your clever brain fills in the movement between them. Inside the projector, a little claw or a cross-shaped wheel jerks the film forward and stops it again 24 times every second, while a spinning shutter hides each jump. Early film was so bright and beautiful that it was also dangerously easy to set on fire!","sections":[{"heading":"A Ribbon of Still Pictures","paragraphs":["Hold a strip of old movie film up to a window and you will discover the biggest secret in the history of cinema: nothing on it moves. Not one thing. What you see instead is a long ribbon of tiny photographs, one after another, like a comic strip with no speech bubbles. In the first picture a man's hand is lifted. In the next it is lifted a tiny bit higher. In the next, a tiny bit higher still. Each little picture is called a frame, and a movie is nothing more than thousands of frames shown one after another very quickly.","The speed at which those frames are shown is measured in frames per second, usually written fps. If a film runs at 24 frames per second, then every single second of the story you watch is really 24 separate still photographs. That sounds like a lot until you start multiplying. One minute is 60 seconds, so one minute of film needs 24 x 60 = 1,440 frames. An hour needs 86,400. A normal 90-minute movie, the length of a typical family film, needs about 129,600 individual pictures. Somebody, or some machine, had to photograph every one of them, and the projector has to show every one of them in exactly the right order.","Standard cinema film is 35 millimetres wide, roughly the width of a postage stamp, with small square sprocket holes punched down both edges. On the common 35mm format each frame sits between four pairs of those holes, and 16 frames fit into one foot of film (a foot is about 30 centimetres). So at 24 frames per second the film races through the projector at 90 feet every minute, which is about 27 metres, or roughly the length of a tennis court every minute. A 90-minute feature therefore needs a ribbon of film about 8,100 feet long, close to 2.5 kilometres.","The very first movie machines used much shorter strips. Thomas Edison's Kinetoscope, the peephole cabinet you met in the last part, held a loop of about 50 feet of film. At 16 frames per foot that is roughly 800 frames. Because the Kinetoscope's camera usually ran at about 40 frames per second, those 800 frames lasted only about 20 seconds. Imagine paying a coin, pressing your eyes to a wooden box, and watching a strongman flex or a cat box for twenty seconds. To people in 1894, that was a marvel.","You can build your own tiny movie without any film at all. Draw a stick figure in the corner of each page of a small notebook, raising its arm a little more on each page, then flip the pages fast with your thumb. The figure waves. This is a flip book, and it works on exactly the same principle as a cinema, only with paper instead of a projector. But it leaves a puzzle: why does a stack of still drawings look like one thing moving? The answer is not in the paper. It is inside your head."]},{"heading":"Your Brain, the Hidden Projector","paragraphs":["For more than a hundred years, books and teachers explained movies with two words: persistence of vision. The idea was that every image you see stays printed on the retina, the light-sensitive layer at the back of your eye, for a fraction of a second after it disappears. If the next picture arrives before the old one fades, the two blend together and you see smooth motion. It sounds neat, and it has a long history. In 1768 a French scientist named Patrick d'Arcy whirled a glowing coal around on a string and measured that it had to spin about once every 0.13 seconds to look like an unbroken ring of fire. In December 1824 the English doctor Peter Mark Roget (the same Roget who later wrote a famous thesaurus) gave a talk to the Royal Society about why the spokes of a rolling wheel look curved when you see them through the gaps of a fence, and he blamed lingering images on the retina.","Belgian physicist Joseph Plateau read Roget's work, and in late 1832 he built the phenakistiscope, the spinning disc with slots that you met in Part 8. Persistence of vision became the standard explanation for all such toys, and later for cinema. But even in the 1800s some scientists were doubtful. The Austrian Simon Stampfer, who invented his own spinning discs in 1833, thought the gaps between images mattered more than any lingering. In 1868 the English physiologist William Benjamin Carpenter called the effect 'rather a mental than a retinal phenomenon', meaning it happened more in the mind than in the eye.","Modern scientists agree with Carpenter. Think about what would really happen if every picture simply stayed stuck on your retina. A ball flying across the screen would leave a smeary trail of balls behind it, like a long exposure photograph, and every film would look like a blurry mess of ghosts. That is not what we see. Persistence of vision does explain one thing well: why we do not notice the moments of darkness between frames, a matter we will return to with the shutter. But it does not explain why we see movement.","The better explanation is called apparent motion. In 1875 the Austrian scientist Sigmund Exner showed that if two small electric sparks flash one after the other a short distance apart, people see a single spark jumping from one place to the other, even though nothing actually moved. In 1912 the psychologist Max Wertheimer ran careful experiments in Frankfurt using a tachistoscope, a device that flashes images for exactly measured instants. When two lines were flashed too slowly, people saw two separate lines. When they were flashed at just the right speed, people saw one line moving smoothly from the first spot to the second. Wertheimer called this the optimal motion; today it is usually called beta movement. When the flashes were even faster, people reported something stranger: a sense of pure movement, like a shadowy something whooshing across, without a clear object. He named that the phi phenomenon.","The key lesson is this: your brain is not a camera that simply records. It is a detective that is constantly guessing what is going on in the world. When it sees a hand in one place, then a moment later sees an almost identical hand just a little higher, it concludes, sensibly, that the hand rose. It fills in the in-between movement that was never actually shown. Scientists today separate long-range apparent motion, like Wertheimer's flashing lines far apart, from short-range apparent motion, where each frame differs only slightly from the one before, as in a film. Special motion-detecting nerve cells in the visual parts of the brain respond to these small, rapid changes as if they were real movement. So when you watch a movie, the real projector is in the cinema, but the motion is made by a second, hidden projector: your own brain.","How many pictures per second does the brain need? From about 16 frames per second upward, most people see fairly smooth movement; much slower than that, it starts to look jerky, like a slideshow. Because film was expensive, many early filmmakers aimed for about the slowest speed that still felt like motion."]},{"heading":"Stop, Go, Stop, Go","paragraphs":["Now for the machines. If you pulled film past a lens in a smooth, steady slide, every frame would be moving while it was shown, and the picture on the screen would be a smudge. So a movie projector, and a movie camera too, must do something that sounds almost impossible: it must yank the film forward one frame, stop it dead, hold it perfectly still while light shines through, then yank it forward again. At 24 frames per second, that stop-and-go happens 24 times every second, for hours, without tearing the film. Engineers call this intermittent movement, from a word meaning starting and stopping.","Interestingly, Edison's Kinetoscope cheated. Its film moved continuously, pulled steadily around a loop of rollers by an electric motor. To stop the picture from blurring, a spinning wheel with a tiny slit let light reach the film for only a split second as each frame passed. The flash was so brief that each frame looked frozen. This worked for one person peering into a small box, but it wasted most of the light, far too much for throwing a big, bright picture on a wall for a whole audience. Projectors needed true stop-and-go motion.","One solution is the claw. Picture a small metal hook that pokes into the sprocket holes at the edge of the film, pulls the strip down exactly one frame, then slips out, rises back up, and pokes in again. The Lumière brothers' Cinématographe of 1895 used a triangular cam, a rotating part shaped a bit like a rounded triangle, to drive its pins in this up-and-down, in-and-out pattern. The whole machine weighed only about 7 kilograms (16 pounds), was cranked by hand, and could work as a camera, a printer and a projector. Claws like this are still used in movie cameras and in smaller film formats such as 8mm and 16mm projectors.","The other famous solution came from the world of watches. It is called the Geneva drive, after the Swiss city famous for watchmaking, or the Maltese cross, because the slotted wheel at its heart looks a little like the cross on the flag of Malta. Here is how it works. One wheel turns smoothly and steadily, and it carries a single pin. Next to it sits the cross-shaped wheel with four slots. Once in every turn, the pin slides into one slot and pushes the cross a quarter of a turn, 90 degrees. Then the pin slides out, and a curved locking disc on the first wheel fits against the cross and holds it perfectly still until the pin comes round again. So steady spinning goes in, and jerk, stop, jerk, stop comes out.","In a 35mm projector, the Maltese cross turns a special intermittent sprocket, a toothed drum whose teeth fit into the sprocket holes. Each quarter-turn pulls the film down exactly four perforations, which is exactly one frame. Because the cross always stops in exactly the same position, each frame lands in the same spot in the gate, the window where light shines through, so the picture does not jiggle. Projectors made by Oskar Messter in Germany and Robert William Paul in England were among the first to use the Geneva drive, in 1896. A few earlier machines used a different idea, a beater mechanism, in which a spinning arm thumped the film down a frame at a time; Georges Demenÿ, who had worked with Étienne-Jules Marey, devised it in 1893.","There is one more clever trick hidden in every film projector: the loops. The reels feeding and collecting the film turn smoothly, but the film in the gate jerks. If the strip were pulled tight between them, the jerking would rip it. So the film runs in a small slack loop just above the gate and another just below, like the extra slack in a garden hose. The loops soak up the difference between steady motion and stop-and-go motion. The idea is often called the Latham loop, after the American film pioneer Woodville Latham, whose team, including William Kennedy Laurie Dickson and Eugene Lauste, developed it around 1895."]},{"heading":"The Spinning Shutter and the War on Flicker","paragraphs":["Stop-and-go motion solves the blur problem but creates a new one. While the film is being pulled down to the next frame, the picture on the screen would slide downwards in a streak, a smear projectionists called travel ghost. To hide it, every projector has a shutter: a spinning metal disc with blades, placed in the beam of light. Each time the film moves, a blade swings across and blocks the light. Each time the film stops, the open space between blades lets light through. So the screen is not lit all the time at all. It is really flashing on and off, dark while the film moves, bright while it stands still.","Here is the surprising part. In a typical film show, the screen is dark for a large share of every second. You have sat in cinemas staring at darkness for a big fraction of the film and never once noticed. This is where the old idea of persistence of vision is genuinely useful: when flashes of light come fast enough, your visual system blends them into one steady brightness. The speed at which this happens is called the flicker fusion threshold. Below it, you see a flicker, a shaky, pulsing light that can give people headaches. Above it, the light looks perfectly steady. For moving pictures in a dark room, the threshold is usually taken to be somewhere around 48 to 60 flashes per second, though it rises when the light is very bright, and many people notice flicker more easily out of the corner of the eye.","Early films had a real flicker problem. If a film ran at 16 frames per second and the shutter blinked once per frame, the screen flashed only 16 times a second, well below the threshold. Audiences saw the picture pulsing. This is one reason people in English-speaking countries nicknamed movies the flicks, and why going to the cinema is still sometimes called going to the flicks.","Thomas Edison had his own opinion. He claimed that 46 frames per second was the minimum for the eye, warning that 'anything less will strain the eye'. But filming 46 frames every second would have used film at a terrifying rate. Engineers found a much cheaper answer: instead of adding more frames, flash each frame more than once. A shutter with two blades blocks the light once while the film moves and once more while the film is standing still. A three-blade shutter does it twice more. So at 16 frames per second, a three-blade shutter gives 48 flashes per second. At 24 frames per second, a two-blade shutter shows each frame twice and the screen flashes 48 times a second, while a three-blade shutter flashes it 72 times a second. The audience sees no flicker, and the studio pays for only 24 frames.","The light itself was a battle too. Early projectors borrowed limelight and electric bulbs from magic lantern shows; from the early 1900s until the late 1960s most cinemas used a carbon arc lamp, in which an electric spark jumps between two carbon rods, which the projectionist had to keep adjusting as they burned away. All that light carried enormous heat, concentrated onto a frame of film smaller than your thumbnail. If the film ever stops in the gate while the lamp is on, the frame can scorch or melt in a fraction of a second. Projectors were fitted with a douser, a metal flap that automatically drops in front of the beam when the film stops. As you will see later in this part, with early film, a hot lamp and a stuck frame could mean far more than a melted picture."]},{"heading":"How Fast Is a Silent Film?","paragraphs":["Today a film is shot and shown at exactly 24 frames per second, every time, everywhere. Early filmmakers had no such rule. Most cameras before the late 1920s were cranked by hand. The camera operator turned a handle on the side of the box, and every turn pulled a set number of frames past the lens; on many cameras of the time one full turn moved about eight frames, so a steady two turns per second gave about 16 frames per second. Good operators learned to crank with a steady rhythm.","In practice, silent films were shot at a wide range of speeds, from about 12 up to about 40 frames per second. The Lumière brothers recommended around 15 or 16 frames per second for their Cinématographe. Over the years camera operators gradually cranked faster, because faster rates looked smoother and less flickery, and by the mid-to-late 1920s many films were being shot at around 20 to 26 frames per second. Historians have found that the number 16, often quoted as 'silent speed', was never a true standard.","Hand cranking was also an art. Operators sometimes cranked slower than normal on purpose, a trick called undercranking. When the film was then projected at the usual speed, the action looked faster than life, which made chases, car crashes and comedy fights more exciting. Cranking faster than normal did the opposite, giving slow motion. Projectionists played with speed too, and some cinemas ran films faster than they were shot to squeeze in an extra show each evening.","This explains a mystery you may have noticed. Why do people in very old films often scurry about in a jerky, speeded-up way, like wind-up toys? Usually it is not because people moved strangely back then. It is because a film shot at, say, 16 or 18 frames per second is being shown at a modern 24. Every second of real life now flashes past in two-thirds of a second, so everyone walks too fast and moves in little jerks. When film archives restore silent films carefully and show them at the right speed, the people suddenly move naturally, and the films look graceful rather than comical.","The arrival of sound, which you will read about in Part 14, ended the freedom. Your eyes do not mind much if a picture runs a little fast or slow, but your ears certainly do. A recording played too fast sounds squeaky and high, like a chipmunk; played too slowly it sounds deep and groaning. Sound and picture had to stay locked together at one steady speed. Between about 1926 and 1930 the industry settled on 24 frames per second for 35mm sound film. Many theatres had been running silent films somewhere around 22 to 26 frames per second, so 24 was a sensible compromise: fast enough for good sound quality, not so fast that film costs exploded. With a two-blade shutter, 24 frames also gave 48 flashes a second, safely above the flicker threshold. That number, chosen almost a century ago, is still the standard speed for most cinema today."]},{"heading":"Beautiful, Bright and Dangerous: Nitrate Film","paragraphs":["The film itself hid a danger. From 1889, when Eastman's company began selling it, until around 1950, nearly all professional movie film was made on a base of cellulose nitrate, often just called nitrate. Cellulose is the tough material in plant fibres such as cotton; treating it with nitric acid turns it into nitrocellulose. Mixed with camphor, it becomes celluloid, a clear, strong, flexible plastic. Nitrate film was wonderful to look at. Projectionists and film historians still praise its deep blacks and silvery, glowing brightness. But nitrocellulose is a close chemical cousin of guncotton, an explosive used in the 1800s.","Nitrate film burns fiercely and fast. Worse, it carries enough oxygen inside its own molecules to keep burning without air. A roll of burning nitrate can keep burning even when smothered or pushed under water, and it gives off thick, poisonous smoke. A nitrate fire can spread through a reel in seconds. In a projection booth, the main danger was the gate: if the film jammed or slowed while the fierce lamp was shining through it, the frame could catch fire, and the flame could race up the strip to the reels. Running film too slowly was risky for the same reason, which is one reason projectionists were warned against running nitrate prints at speeds as slow as 16 frames per second.","The danger was learned the hard way. On 4 May 1897, in Paris, a charity fair called the Bazar de la Charité was showing films with the new Cinématographe, as one of its most popular attractions. The projector's lamp, which used ether, caught fire, and flames spread through the wooden hall with its cloth decorations. About 126 people died in the blaze and the rush to escape, many of them well-known women from wealthy families who had been running the stalls. The disaster shocked France and made many people think of cinema itself as dangerous for years afterwards.","Because of fires like this, many countries passed laws about how films could be shown. Projection booths became small fireproof rooms, separate from the audience, often lined with asbestos (now known to be harmful, so no longer used). Their small windows had metal shutters that dropped shut automatically in a fire, and reels were enclosed in metal boxes above and below the projector. That is why the projector at a classic cinema was always tucked away in a booth at the back, with only a beam of light reaching the room.","Nitrate is also its own enemy even when no one strikes a match. As it ages, it slowly gives off nitric acid, which attacks the film further. Over decades a reel can turn sticky, then bubbly, then into a brown, dangerous powder that can catch fire on its own if stored somewhere warm. Fires in film storage vaults destroyed whole studio collections, including at Fox in 1937 and at MGM in 1965.","The cure was safety film, made of cellulose acetate, which burns only slowly, like paper rather than gunpowder. Safety film for amateur use came early in the 1900s, but for a long time it was not tough or clear enough for professional 35mm prints. In 1948 Kodak introduced a strong new base called cellulose triacetate, and the industry switched over. Kodak stopped making 35mm nitrate motion-picture film in 1951. Safety film was printed with the word SAFETY along its edge, so anyone could tell it apart. Today, a few specially equipped cinemas, such as the Dryden Theatre at the George Eastman Museum in Rochester, New York, can still project original nitrate prints, under strict fire rules, so audiences can see that famous silvery glow."]},{"heading":"A Train, a Wave and a Magician","paragraphs":["Put all of this together, the frames, the brain's apparent motion, the stop-and-go claw or Maltese cross, the flashing shutter, the hand crank and the dangerous, beautiful nitrate, and you have the complete magic trick of the early cinema. Now imagine being one of the first people ever to see it work.","One of the most famous early Lumière films is L'Arrivée d'un train en gare de La Ciotat, about 50 seconds long, shot in one continuous view at a station in the south of France. A steam train grows from a dot in the distance to fill the frame, puffing straight towards the camera, and passengers climb on and off. It was not part of the first paid show on 28 December 1895; it was shown publicly in early 1896. A popular story claims that audiences screamed and ran from their seats, terrified that the train would burst out of the screen. Historians have searched old newspapers for reports of such a panic and found none. It is best treated as a legend. Still, people really were startled and thrilled. The Russian writer Maxim Gorky, after watching Lumière films in 1896, described feeling that the train was rushing straight at him and wrote of a strange, grey kingdom of shadows.","When Edison's Vitascope premiered in New York on 23 April 1896, one of the films that most delighted the crowd was not a story at all. It was Rough Sea at Dover, filmed in 1895 by the British pioneers Birt Acres and Robert W. Paul: waves crashing and foaming against a pier. For many early audiences, ordinary moving things, waves, smoke, leaves stirring in the wind, were the real wonder.","But one man in Paris saw something more. He had watched the Lumière invention at a demonstration in December 1895 and had immediately tried, and failed, to buy one. He was not a scientist or a factory owner. He was a stage magician who ran his own theatre of illusions. Everyone else saw a machine for recording the real world. He looked at the same stop-and-go strip of frames and realised something thrilling: if you could stop the camera, change things, and start again, the film would not care. Movies could show things that never happened at all."]}],"deepDive":"Why do we see motion in a film? Textbooks long said 'persistence of vision', but perception scientists largely abandoned that answer decades ago. The real situation has two separate parts. First, the brain must not notice the darkness between frames. That is a question of flicker fusion: if light pulses arrive quickly enough, the visual system averages them into a steady brightness. The Talbot-Plateau law describes that average, and the fusion frequency rises with brightness, which is why a big bright cinema screen needs about 48 flashes per second or more, while a dim scene can get away with fewer. Second, the brain must see the changes between frames as movement. That is apparent motion, studied by Sigmund Exner in 1875 and Max Wertheimer in 1912. Today researchers distinguish long-range apparent motion (two objects flashed some distance apart, as in Wertheimer's beta movement) from short-range apparent motion, where tiny frame-to-frame shifts are picked up by motion-sensitive neurons much as real motion would be. Film depends mostly on the short-range kind, so even beta movement and phi are not the full explanation. Film theorists Joseph and Barbara Anderson argued in 1993 that clinging to 'persistence of vision' matters philosophically: it treats the viewer as a passive recording surface, while the apparent-motion view treats the viewer as an active builder of what is seen. The engineering numbers are a story of compromise. Edison wanted about 46 frames per second; studios wanted cheap film; the multi-blade shutter let 16 or 24 frames do the work of 48 or 72 flashes. When sound arrived, 24 frames per second was chosen because theatres were already near that speed and the optical soundtrack needed a steady, reasonably fast rate. Modern experiments with 48 frames per second and higher show the trade-off is partly about taste: many viewers find high frame rates smoother but describe them as looking 'like video', because a century of cinema has trained audiences to link 24 frames, with its slight motion blur, to the feeling of a movie. Other animals live with different numbers: one 2014 survey found flicker fusion values from about 6.7 per second in the cane toad to about 400 in a fire beetle, so a cinema might look like a slideshow to some creatures and like a strobe light to others.","funFact":"In a cinema showing film at 24 frames per second, the screen is completely dark for a large part of every second, and almost nobody in the audience ever notices.","check":{"q":"Why does a film projector's shutter have two or three blades instead of one?","a":"To flash each frame two or three times so the screen flickers too fast for people to notice","wrong":["To make the film run backwards between frames","To cool the lamp so the film cannot burn","To add color to black-and-white film"]},"sources":[{"title":"Persistence of vision","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persistence_of_vision"},{"title":"Beta movement","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beta_movement"},{"title":"Geneva drive","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geneva_drive"},{"title":"Movie projector","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Movie_projector"},{"title":"Frame rate","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frame_rate"},{"title":"Flicker fusion threshold","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flicker_fusion_threshold"},{"title":"Nitrocellulose (nitrate film)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrate_film"},{"title":"L'Arrivée d'un train en gare de La Ciotat","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%27Arriv%C3%A9e_d%27un_train_en_gare_de_La_Ciotat"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/11-1.jpg","alt":"A strip of 35mm film held up to the light, with a row of sprocket holes along both edges.","file":"Ektachrome100ReversalFilmP3.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ektachrome100ReversalFilmP3.jpg","author":"Wiley.andrew.r","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/11-2.jpg","alt":"A Geneva drive: the pin on the blue wheel turns the red four-slot cross one quarter step at a time.","file":"Geneva drive four-position animation.gif","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geneva_drive_four-position_animation.gif","author":"MichaelFrey","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/11-3.jpg","alt":"A museum display of the Lumière brothers' Cinématographe: the wooden camera box, a projection lens stand and a poster.","file":"Le cinématographe des Frères Lumière.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_cin%C3%A9matographe_des_Fr%C3%A8res_Lumi%C3%A8re.JPG","author":"Linili","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4390},{"n":12,"title":"Magic, Stories and the Silent Era","when":"1896 – 1927","summary":"A Paris magician named Georges Méliès discovered he could stop his camera and make people vanish, then sent a rocket crashing into the Moon's eye. Alice Guy filmed a fairy finding babies in a cabbage patch, and soon whole cities were packing into five-cent theaters. Silent films were never really silent: a piano or a mighty organ played along in the dark!","sections":[{"heading":"The Magician Who Wanted a Camera","paragraphs":["Paris, 27 December 1895. In a room at the Grand Café, the Lumière brothers are giving a private demonstration of their Cinématographe, the night before their first paid public show. Among the invited guests sits a 34-year-old man with a neat beard and a showman's sparkle in his eyes. His name is Georges Méliès. When the flickering grey pictures of workers and street scenes appear on the screen, he is thunderstruck. Afterwards he goes straight to the Lumières' father, Antoine, and offers to buy a machine. According to the usual account he offered 10,000 francs, a huge sum. The answer was no. The Lumières wanted to control their invention themselves.","Méliès was born in Paris on 8 December 1861, the son of a wealthy boot manufacturer. His family expected him to join the shoe business, but he loved drawing, puppets and above all stage magic. While in London as a young man he visited the Egyptian Hall, a famous home of conjuring shows, and was hooked. In 1888 he used his share of the family fortune to buy the Théâtre Robert-Houdin in Paris, a small magic theatre founded by the great French magician Jean-Eugène Robert-Houdin. There Méliès performed illusions, built mechanical gadgets and designed elaborate stage tricks in which people vanished, floated or lost their heads.","Refused by the Lumières, Méliès did not give up. In early 1896 he travelled to London and bought a projector called the Animatograph from the British inventor Robert W. Paul. Back in Paris he and his mechanics rebuilt it to work as a camera too. By April 1896 his theatre was showing films between the magic acts, and soon he was shooting his own. Later that year he founded a company with the bold name Star Film, whose trademark was a star, and in September 1896 he began building one of the first film studios in the world, in the garden of his family property at Montreuil, just outside Paris.","The Montreuil studio looked like a giant greenhouse. Its walls and roof were made of glass, because film of the time needed enormous amounts of light, and electric lighting strong enough was not yet practical. Inside was a stage like a theatre's, with trapdoors, pulleys and painted backdrops. Méliès painted most of the scenery himself, usually in shades of grey, because the film of the time was black and white and real colours often photographed in strange ways. He was producer, writer, designer, director, actor and special-effects expert all at once."]},{"heading":"A Bus That Turned into a Hearse","paragraphs":["Méliès liked to tell a story about how he discovered his greatest trick, and it is a wonderful tale, though we only have his own word for it. One day, he said, he was filming traffic in a Paris street when his camera jammed. It took him about a minute to fix it and start cranking again. When he developed the film and projected it, he saw something astonishing. During that minute, the street had changed. A horse-drawn bus seemed to transform, in an instant, into a hearse, a carriage for carrying coffins, and men seemed to turn into women. Whether or not it happened just like that, it makes a good legend about the birth of the special effect.","The trick that the story describes is called the stop trick, or substitution. It works because of exactly what you learned in the last part: film is a row of still frames. Stop the camera, change something in front of it, keep everything else perfectly in place, and start the camera again. When the film is projected, the change happens between one frame and the next, in one twenty-fourth of a second or less, and it looks like magic. A magician can make a lady vanish on stage only by using hidden trapdoors and mirrors. With a camera, Méliès could make her disappear by simply stopping, asking her to walk away, and starting again. To make the join seamless, he would often cut the film at the exact frame and glue the pieces together, an early form of the careful editing we now call a substitution splice.","Méliès went much further. He discovered multiple exposure: he would film a scene with part of the lens covered in black, wind the film back, uncover that part, cover the rest and film again. In The One-Man Band (1900) he played seven musicians at the same time, all of them him. In The Man with the Rubber Head (1901) his own head swells to an enormous size; he achieved this by moving himself towards the camera on a sliding cart and combining the shot with another. He used dissolves, in which one scene slowly fades into another, and he made objects appear, vanish and transform. Many of his films were sold in colour versions that were hand-painted, frame by frame, by teams of women working with tiny brushes and dyes, in a workshop he hired for the job.","Between 1896 and 1913 Méliès made more than 500 films. Most were only a minute or a few minutes long, but they were packed with devils, fairies, skeletons, mermaids, exploding characters and transformations. People sometimes call him the father of special effects, and though others also invented film tricks, no one in those years used them with such wild imagination."]},{"heading":"A Rocket in the Moon's Eye","paragraphs":["In 1902 Méliès made the film that is still his most famous: Le Voyage dans la Lune, or A Trip to the Moon. It took about three months to make and cost about 10,000 francs, a fortune for a film at the time. The story borrows ideas from Jules Verne's novels From the Earth to the Moon and Around the Moon, and perhaps from H. G. Wells's The First Men in the Moon, which had just come out. A club of bearded astronomers in robes and pointed hats, led by Professor Barbenfouillis, played by Méliès himself, decides to fly to the Moon. Workers build a giant cannon on the rooftops. The astronomers climb into a bullet-shaped capsule and are fired into space.","Then comes the most famous image in early cinema. The Moon, shown as a round face in the sky, grows larger and larger as the capsule approaches, and the rocket lands with a splat right in its eye. On the Moon the explorers watch the Earth rise, sleep under the stars, and wander through a cave of giant mushrooms. They meet the Selenites, Moon creatures with insect-like bodies, played by acrobats from the Folies Bergère theatre in Paris. When a scientist bonks a Selenite with his umbrella, it explodes in a puff of smoke, another stop trick. The explorers escape, tumble back to Earth, splash into the sea, and return home as heroes.","A Trip to the Moon was a hit all over Europe and America. Sadly for Méliès, much of the money went to others. Copying a film was easy, and copyright laws had not caught up with the new invention. Companies in the United States, including Thomas Edison's company and others run by Siegmund Lubin and William Selig, made copies of Méliès's films and sold them without paying him. This piracy was one reason Méliès sent his brother Gaston to open an American branch of Star Film in New York in 1903, and why he began stamping his star trademark into the scenery of his films.","For about a century, people believed the hand-coloured version of A Trip to the Moon had vanished forever. Then in 1993 a coloured print turned up at the Filmoteca de Catalunya, the film archive of Catalonia in Spain. It was in terrible condition; the nitrate had decayed so badly that the reels were almost a solid lump. Over many years, restorers led by the French company Lobster Films carefully separated and copied fragments, frame by frame, then used digital tools to rebuild missing parts. The work cost about 1 million US dollars. In 2011 the restored colour film was shown at the Cannes Film Festival, 109 years after it was made, with a new soundtrack by the French band Air. That same year, Martin Scorsese's film Hugo, based on a novel by Brian Selznick, introduced Méliès and his Moon to a whole new generation of children."]},{"heading":"Alice Guy and the Cabbage Fairy","paragraphs":["While Méliès was building his glass studio, another French pioneer was quietly changing what films could be. Alice Guy was born in 1873 near Paris and grew up partly in Chile, where her father ran bookshops. As a young woman she learned typing and shorthand, then new skills for women entering offices, and in 1894 she became the secretary to Léon Gaumont at a company that sold photographic equipment. Gaumont's company soon began making its own film cameras and projectors.","In 1895 Alice Guy went with Gaumont to a Lumière demonstration. The Lumière films, like most early films, mostly showed real life: workers leaving a factory, a train arriving, a baby eating breakfast. Guy thought films could do more. They could tell invented stories, like little plays. She asked Gaumont whether she could make some films herself, as long as it did not interfere with her secretarial work. He agreed, reportedly thinking it was a harmless hobby.","Her first film was La Fée aux Choux, The Cabbage Fairy, made in 1896. It was about a minute long and drew on an old French folk tale in which babies are found growing in cabbage patches, much like the English story that babies are brought by storks. Historians still argue about the details, because the original 1896 version is lost. Alice Guy herself said she remade the story at least twice, and versions from 1900 and 1902 survive, showing a fairy in a white dress lifting real babies out of a garden of cabbages. Whatever the exact date, she is widely credited as one of the very first people, and the first woman, to direct fiction films: films with a planned story, actors and sets.","Guy soon became head of film production at Gaumont, a job she held until 1906. She wrote, directed and supervised hundreds of films, including comedies, fairy tales, dance films and a long Life of Christ in 1906 with hundreds of extras. She also experimented with Gaumont's Chronophone, a system that played sound recordings on a phonograph alongside the film, making some of the earliest synchronized music films, more than twenty years before the talkies."]},{"heading":"Train Robbers and Nickel Theatres","paragraphs":["In 1907 Alice Guy married Herbert Blaché, a British cameraman who worked for Gaumont, and the couple moved to the United States. In 1910 Alice Guy-Blaché co-founded her own company, Solax, and in 1912 she built a modern studio in Fort Lee, New Jersey, across the river from New York City, which was then the busiest filmmaking town in America. On the studio wall, it is said, hung a big sign with her advice to actors: Be Natural. Her Solax film A Fool and His Money (1912) is thought to be the first film with an all-African-American cast. Over her career she made hundreds of films; estimates range from several hundred to around a thousand, though many are lost. After her marriage ended she returned to France in 1922 and never directed again. She was largely forgotten for decades, received France's Legion of Honour in 1958, and died in New Jersey in 1968. A 2018 documentary, Be Natural, helped bring her story back to light.","Meanwhile American filmmakers were learning to tell exciting stories. In November 1903 Edwin S. Porter, a camera operator and director working for the Edison Manufacturing Company, made The Great Train Robbery. It runs about 12 minutes, long for the time, and follows a gang of outlaws who hold up a railway station, rob a steam train and its passengers, flee on horseback, and are finally caught by a posse. Some scenes were shot in Edison's New York studio, but others were filmed outdoors in New Jersey, including along a real railway line, which made the action feel thrillingly real.","Porter's film is famous for its storytelling. He cut between different places, showing the robbers escaping while, elsewhere, the telegraph operator is found tied up and the townspeople gather, so the audience understood that events were happening at the same time. This technique is called cross-cutting. The film also ends with a shock: a medium close-up of the outlaw leader, played by Justus D. Barnes, pointing his revolver straight at the camera and firing at the audience. The shot has nothing to do with the plot, and cinemas could show it at the start or the end. Some prints were hand-coloured so that gunshot smoke and clothing glowed with colour. It was not the first Western or the first story film, as people sometimes claim, but it was one of the most popular films of its decade.","Where did people see films like this? At first, in vaudeville theatres, fairgrounds and travelling shows. Then on 19 June 1905, in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, two businessmen named Harry Davis and John P. Harris opened a small storefront theatre on Smithfield Street dedicated to moving pictures. They called it the Nickelodeon, from the nickel, the five-cent coin it cost to get in, plus odeon, an old Greek word for a theatre. The Great Train Robbery was among the films audiences flocked to see.","The idea spread like wildfire. Nickelodeons were usually converted shops, narrow and long, with fewer than 200 hard wooden chairs, a piano near the screen and a projector at the back. Programs of short films ran over and over all day, so people could drop in whenever they liked. For working families, and for immigrants who were still learning English, five cents bought an affordable trip into another world. By 1908 there were about 8,000 nickelodeons in the United States, and by 1910 as many as 26 million Americans were going to the movies every week."]},{"heading":"Westward to Hollywood, and a Film That Did Harm","paragraphs":["In the early 1900s most American films were made around New York and New Jersey. But in 1908 Thomas Edison and other big companies formed the Motion Picture Patents Company, which used its patents on cameras and projectors to try to control who could make films. Independent filmmakers who did not pay could be sued, or have their cameras seized. Southern California offered an escape, as well as many other attractions: bright sunshine almost all year round for filming outdoors, cheap land, and an amazing variety of scenery within a day's journey, including oceans, mountains, deserts, forests and farms.","In early 1910 the director D. W. Griffith brought a film crew west from New York and shot In Old California in the little farming suburb of Hollywood, often called the first film made there. Studios soon followed. By the late 1910s Hollywood had become the movie capital of America, and before long the name Hollywood stood for the American film industry itself.","Griffith became one of the most influential directors of the silent era. Working at the Biograph company from 1908, he directed hundreds of short films and developed a smooth style of storytelling: moving between close-ups that showed actors' faces and wide shots that showed huge scenes, using cross-cutting to build suspense, as when a heroine in danger is shown in one shot and the rescuers racing to save her in the next, and asking actors to perform in a subtle, natural way instead of waving their arms like stage actors. He did not invent all these techniques, but he used them with great skill.","In 1915 Griffith released The Birth of a Nation, a long epic about the American Civil War and the period afterwards, which ran around three hours in some versions. It had enormous battle scenes, a full orchestral score, and was a gigantic commercial success, one of the highest-earning films of the entire silent era. It was also deeply racist. Based on a novel called The Clansman, it presented the Ku Klux Klan, a violent white-supremacist group, as heroes, and portrayed Black Americans, many of them played by white actors in blackface makeup, as dangerous and foolish. The National Association for the Advancement of Colored People (NAACP) organised protests, and the film was banned in some cities and states. Historians agree that it helped inspire the revival of the Ku Klux Klan later in 1915. Today it is studied both for its filmmaking techniques and as a painful example of how powerful moving pictures can spread harmful lies."]},{"heading":"Stars, Title Cards and a Piano in the Dark","paragraphs":["As films grew longer, audiences began to love particular actors, and the film star was born. Charlie Chaplin, a young English music-hall comedian, made his first film in America in 1914 and that same year created the Little Tramp, a gentle wanderer with a bowler hat, a cane, baggy trousers, oversized shoes and a tiny moustache. Through films such as The Kid (1921) and The Gold Rush (1925), in which the starving Tramp boils and eats his own boot, Chaplin became one of the most famous people on Earth. Buster Keaton, nicknamed the Great Stone Face because he almost never smiled on screen, performed astonishing stunts himself. In The General (1926) he rides a speeding steam locomotive, and in Steamboat Bill, Jr. (1928) the whole front wall of a house falls on him, and he survives because he is standing exactly where an open window lands.","Mary Pickford, born in Canada, was nicknamed America's Sweetheart and was one of the most popular and highest-paid stars in the world. She was also a sharp businesswoman. On 5 February 1919 she founded the company United Artists with Chaplin, the swashbuckling star Douglas Fairbanks, and D. W. Griffith, so the artists could control and profit from their own films. A rival studio boss reportedly joked that 'the inmates are taking over the asylum'.","Silent films had no recorded voices, so how did viewers follow the story? Mostly through acting, faces and gestures, but also through intertitles, printed cards inserted between scenes. Some gave information, such as 'Next morning', while others showed what a character was saying. Writing them became a separate job, and good title writers could make audiences laugh or cry with a few words. Films could even be sold abroad simply by translating the cards. In Japan, a live narrator called a benshi stood beside the screen and explained the story, voiced the characters and added commentary, and the most famous benshi were stars in their own right.","The most common mistake people make about silent films is that they were watched in silence. Almost every show had live music. A small nickelodeon might have a single pianist who watched the screen and changed the tune for chases, love scenes and villains. Bigger cinemas installed theatre organs, such as the famous Wurlitzer, that could imitate a whole orchestra plus sound effects like bells, horns and thunder. The grandest picture palaces of the 1920s had full orchestras. Studios sent out cue sheets suggesting what music to play for each scene, and big films got their own scores. Many films were also tinted with coloured dyes: blue for night, amber for lamplight, red for fire, green for something eerie. By around 1920, an estimated 80 to 90 percent of films had some colour of this kind."]},{"heading":"The Lost Films","paragraphs":["Here is a sad truth: most of these films are gone. In 2013 the Library of Congress in Washington, D.C., published a study by the film historian David Pierce. He counted 10,919 American silent feature films released between 1912 and 1929 and then searched archives and collections around the world to find which survived. Only 1,575, about 14 percent, exist complete in their original American 35mm form. Another 11 percent survive complete in other forms, such as foreign versions or smaller-gauge copies. About 5 percent survive only in pieces. The remaining 70 percent are believed to be completely lost. Put another way, about 75 percent of the creative record of American silent features either no longer exists or survives only in incomplete form.","Why? Partly because of nitrate, the dangerous film you met in the last part. As it ages it decays into sticky goo and then powder, and vault fires destroyed huge collections, such as the 1937 fire at a Fox storage site and the 1965 fire at MGM that destroyed the last known copy of a famous horror film, London After Midnight (1927). But the biggest reason was simply that nobody thought old films mattered. When sound arrived, silent films seemed worthless overnight. Studios threw prints away, or melted them down to recover the silver in the film. During the First World War, the French army even confiscated many of Méliès's prints and melted them to make material for the war effort. Ruined by debt and changing fashions, Méliès had stopped making films by 1913, and in despair he is said to have destroyed his own stored negatives.","Méliès's story had a gentler ending. For years he ran a small toy and sweet stall at the Montparnasse railway station in Paris, with his second wife, the actress Jehanne d'Alcy, who had appeared in many of his films. In the late 1920s journalists and film fans rediscovered him, a gala of his surviving films was held in Paris in 1929, and in 1931 he was made a Knight of the Legion of Honour, with the medal presented by none other than Louis Lumière. He died in 1938.","Lost films still turn up today. Prints have been found in attics, in foreign archives where American films had been sent decades earlier, and in strange places. In 2008 a nearly complete copy of Fritz Lang's German science-fiction epic Metropolis (1927), with scenes missing for about 80 years, was found in a museum in Buenos Aires, Argentina. Every discovery is like opening a time capsule. Each one also reminds us of the colours that were often missing from these silver-grey films. Audiences in the 1910s and 1920s watched heroes and villains in a world of black, white and tinted shades, and many longed for the day they could see the true red of a rose and the deep blue of the sky on the screen."]}],"deepDive":"The silent era is full of disputed 'firsts', and historians have become cautious about them. Alice Guy-Blaché is often called the first director of a fiction film, but the Lumières' L'Arroseur arrosé (1895), a staged joke about a gardener soaked by his own hose, was also a planned little story, so the fairer claim is that Guy was among the very first to make fiction films and the first woman to direct. Dating her La Fée aux Choux is hard because the 1896 original is lost and later remakes were long confused with it. D. W. Griffith was once praised as the man who 'invented' film grammar; scholars now point out that cross-cutting, close-ups and location shooting appeared earlier, in films by Porter, by British filmmakers such as G. A. Smith and James Williamson, and by others, and that Griffith's real achievement was combining and refining them. The Birth of a Nation raises a different kind of question: how to study a film that was technically important and enormously popular while also spreading racist propaganda that helped bring back the Ku Klux Klan. Most historians today discuss both facts together, and the NAACP's 1915 campaign against it is now seen as an early landmark in protests about media representation. Statistics about lost films are also debated. The Library of Congress's 2013 report by David Pierce was the first systematic count for American silent features: of 10,919 titles, 14 percent survive complete in original 35mm, 11 percent complete in other versions, 5 percent incomplete, and 70 percent completely lost. The often-repeated older claim that half of all American films made before 1950 are lost had, as the report itself notes, gained its authority mainly through repetition; sound films of the 1930s and 1940s survived much better because studios had a reason to copy them for television. Finally, Méliès's 'jammed camera' story is best treated as his own colourful origin myth: substitution tricks were also being explored by others, including in the Edison film The Execution of Mary Stuart (1895), which used a stop to swap an actor for a dummy before a beheading.","funFact":"When the hand-coloured print of A Trip to the Moon was found in 1993, its nitrate film had decayed so badly that restorers needed many years, and about a million dollars, to rescue it, and it was finally shown at Cannes in 2011.","check":{"q":"What did a ticket to an early nickelodeon theater cost?","a":"Five cents (a nickel)","wrong":["One dollar","Twenty-five cents","It was always free"]},"sources":[{"title":"Georges Méliès","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georges_M%C3%A9li%C3%A8s"},{"title":"A Trip to the Moon","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Trip_to_the_Moon"},{"title":"Alice Guy-Blaché","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alice_Guy-Blach%C3%A9"},{"title":"La Fée aux Choux","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_F%C3%A9e_aux_Choux"},{"title":"The Great Train Robbery (1903 film)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Great_Train_Robbery_(1903_film)"},{"title":"Nickelodeon (movie theater)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nickelodeon_(movie_theater)"},{"title":"The Birth of a Nation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Birth_of_a_Nation"},{"title":"The Survival of American Silent Feature Films: 1912–1929 (David Pierce, 2013)","publisher":"Library of Congress / National Film Preservation Board","url":"https://www.loc.gov/static/programs/national-film-preservation-board/documents/pub158.final_version_sept_2013.pdf"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/12-1.jpg","alt":"The famous hand-colored frame from A Trip to the Moon (1902): the space capsule lands in the Moon's eye.","file":"Melies color Voyage dans la lune.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melies_color_Voyage_dans_la_lune.jpg","author":"Georges Méliès","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/12-2.jpg","alt":"Alice Guy-Blaché, one of the very first film directors, in a studio portrait from New York.","file":"Alice Guy.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alice_Guy.jpg","author":"Apeda Studio New York","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/12-3.jpg","alt":"The surprise final shot of The Great Train Robbery (1903): an outlaw aims his pistol straight at the audience.","file":"Great train robbery Barnes.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_train_robbery_Barnes.jpg","author":"Edwin Stanton Porter","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4025},{"n":13,"title":"Painting with Light: Color","when":"1861 – 1950s","summary":"For a long time, photos and movies were only black, white and gray. Clever people found that you can build every color out of just three, using filters, dyed potato starch, spinning color wheels and giant prism cameras. By the 1930s the movies burst into bright color, and Dorothy stepped out of gray Kansas into the rainbow land of Oz.","sections":[{"heading":"Three Colors Hiding Inside Your Eyes","paragraphs":["Look around the room you are in right now. Maybe there is a red book, a green plant, a blue sky outside the window. For about the first 70 years of photography, an ordinary camera could not catch any of those colors. Daguerreotypes were silvery gray. Calotypes were brown. Glass-plate portraits were black and white. Early movies flickered in shades of gray. People knew perfectly well that the world was colorful, and it drove inventors a little bit crazy that their marvelous new machines could not see it.","To understand how they finally solved the puzzle, we have to start not with a camera but with you, and the back of your eye. The inside of each eye is lined with a thin layer called the retina. It is packed with light-detecting cells of two kinds. The rods, about 92 million of them in each eye, are wonderful in dim light but cannot tell colors apart. The cones, about six or seven million per eye, need brighter light, and they are the ones that see color.","Here is the surprising part: there are only three kinds of cones. One kind responds most strongly to long wavelengths of light, which we see as reddish. One responds best to medium wavelengths, which we see as green. The third responds best to short wavelengths, which we see as blue or violet. Scientists call them L, M and S cones, for long, medium and short. Your brain never sees 'orange' or 'turquoise' directly. Instead, it compares how strongly each of the three kinds of cone is firing and works out the color from the mix. Because we have three cone types, our color vision is called trichromatic, which simply means 'three-colored'.","Imagine three flashlights in a dark room, one shining through red plastic, one through green and one through blue. Point the red and green beams at the same spot on a white wall and you will not see a muddy brownish mess, as you would with paint. You will see yellow! Add the blue beam and the spot turns white. Red plus blue makes a pinkish purple called magenta. Green plus blue makes a sky-like color called cyan. Turn the flashlights up and down and you can make almost any color you like.","This is called additive color, because you start with darkness and add colored light. It is exactly how the screen you might be reading this on works: look at it through a strong magnifying glass and you may see tiny red, green and blue dots or stripes, glowing in different amounts. The idea is much older than any screen, though. The first person to prove it could make a color photograph was a young Scottish physicist, standing in front of a London audience in 1861."]},{"heading":"Maxwell's Tartan Ribbon","paragraphs":["James Clerk Maxwell is one of the greatest scientists who ever lived. Today he is famous above all for his equations describing electricity, magnetism and light, which physicists still use every day. But as a young man he was also fascinated by color. He spun tops painted with colored sections and asked people to match the blurry colors they saw, carefully measuring how the eye mixes red, green and blue. In a paper of 1855 he suggested a way to make a color photograph: take three separate black-and-white pictures of the same scene, one through a red filter, one through a green filter and one through a blue filter. Each picture would then record how much of that one color was in each part of the scene.","On 17 May 1861, Maxwell gave a lecture on color vision at the Royal Institution in London. To prove his idea, he needed a photographer. He turned to Thomas Sutton, a skilled photographer who also edited a photography journal and invented a panoramic camera. The object they chose was a bow of ribbon with a tartan pattern, the kind of checked cloth worn in Scotland, full of different colored stripes.","Sutton made the photographs using glass plates and the wet collodion process you met in Part 7. Instead of modern colored plastic, the filters were glass containers filled with colored liquids. He exposed one plate through a red solution, one through a green solution and one through a blue-violet solution. Each plate came out as an ordinary black-and-white negative, and from each negative he made a positive on glass, a transparent picture called a lantern slide.","At the lecture, the three slides were placed in three separate projectors called magic lanterns. In front of each lantern was the same colored filter that had been used to take that picture. When the three images were lined up exactly on top of each other on the screen, the ribbon appeared, not perfectly, but recognizably, in color. It was the first color photograph made by the three-color method, and it proved Maxwell's theory in front of everyone in the room.","There was a mystery hidden in this triumph, though, and it took a hundred years to solve. The wet collodion plates of 1861 were almost completely blind to red light and only slightly sensitive to green. So how could the red picture have recorded anything at all? In 1961, researchers at Kodak finally worked out a likely answer. Many red dyes in cloth also reflect ultraviolet light, which is invisible to us but which the plates could see. Sutton's red filter let some ultraviolet through. So the 'red' picture was probably recorded partly with light nobody could see. Maxwell had the right idea, and also got a lucky break."]},{"heading":"Paintbrushes, Stencils and Potato Starch","paragraphs":["From the earliest days of film, moviemakers added color by hand, and it was astonishingly hard work. Remember that a film is a long strip of tiny pictures, often 16 or more for every second on screen. To hand-color a film, workers, very often young women, sat at benches with magnifiers and fine brushes, painting transparent dyes onto each frame, one color at a time. A short trick film of a few minutes could contain thousands of frames, and each print sold to a theater had to be painted separately.","The Pathé company in France found a faster way, called stencil coloring. For each color, a worker cut tiny holes in a copy of the film exactly where that color should go, perhaps around a dancer's skirt in every frame. Later this cutting was done with a machine guided by a pointer. The stencil film was then run together with a print, and dye was brushed or rolled through the holes. Repeat for red, for blue, for yellow, and you had a colorful film that could be copied many times. Pathé used these methods for years, including for big features in the 1920s.","Real color for ordinary photographers arrived in 1907, and it came from the same family that had helped launch cinema. Auguste and Louis Lumière, whose Cinématographe you met in Part 10, patented a process called the Autochrome in 1903 and began selling it in 1907. Its secret ingredient sounds like a joke: potatoes.","Workers at the Lumière factory in Lyon took potato starch and sorted out extremely tiny grains, only about 5 to 10 thousandths of a millimeter across. They split the grains into three batches and dyed one batch orange-red, one green and one blue-violet. Then they mixed the three batches together and spread them across a sticky glass plate in a single layer, about four million grains in every square inch. The little gaps between grains were filled with fine black soot called lampblack, so no white light could sneak through. The grains were flattened under huge pressure, varnished, and then coated with a black-and-white light-sensitive emulsion.","Here is how the magic worked. When you took a picture, light from a red rose passed easily through the red-orange grains but was blocked by the green and blue ones, so only the emulsion behind the red grains was exposed. After special developing that turned the image into a positive, the plate was clear behind the grains that had seen the rose's red and dark behind the others. Hold the finished plate up to the light and you saw red where the rose had been. Millions of microscopic colored windows added together into a full-color picture: additive color, just like Maxwell's, but all on one plate.","Autochromes look dreamy and soft, a little like a painting made of tiny dots. They needed much longer exposures than black-and-white plates, so photographers usually used a tripod and asked people to stay very still. Each one was a glass transparency, a one-of-a-kind picture you looked at by holding it up to a window or putting it in a folding viewer called a diascope. Projecting them was risky, because the strong heat and light of a projector could fade or damage the plate. Despite the cost and fuss, the Lumière factory made millions of Autochrome plates before film versions took over in the 1930s. Many museums now keep collections of them, including scenes of gardens, families and soldiers from the First World War."]},{"heading":"Kinemacolor: Two Colors, Flashing Fast","paragraphs":["A glass plate that sits still is one thing. A movie, racing past at many frames per second, is much harder. The first color film process that really worked as a business came from the English seaside town of Hove, near Brighton, which in the early 1900s was a busy little hub of filmmaking. George Albert Smith had been a stage hypnotist and lecturer before turning to film, and he was a clever experimenter. He was backed by Charles Urban, an energetic American-born film producer working in London, who had earlier paid for the color experiments of another inventor, Edward Raymond Turner.","Turner had tried to use three colors, but his system was too complicated to work well. Smith decided to simplify. He patented his process, which Urban named Kinemacolor, around 1906 to 1907, and it used only two colors: red-orange and blue-green. The camera shot ordinary black-and-white film, but in front of the lens spun a wheel with a red-orange filter and a blue-green filter. One frame was taken through the red, the next through the green, the next through the red again, and so on, at about 32 frames per second, roughly twice the usual speed of the time.","In the theater, the projector had a matching spinning filter wheel. Each frame shot through red was projected through red, and each frame shot through green was projected through green. The frames flashed by so fast that the viewer's eyes and brain blended them, and the audience saw a picture with reds, oranges, greens and many in-between shades. It could not show a strong, pure blue very well, but for the time it was dazzling.","Demonstrations began in 1908, and the general public in London first saw Kinemacolor on 26 February 1909, at the Palace Theatre. Later that year it reached New York, with a show at Madison Square Garden in December 1909. Historians generally call it the first successful commercial color motion-picture process. Urban's company sold licences around the world, and Kinemacolor theaters opened in several countries.","Its most famous production was a long documentary called With Our King and Queen Through India (1912), which showed the grand Delhi Durbar, a vast royal ceremony held for King George V and Queen Mary in 1911. Audiences saw glittering costumes, elephants and processions in color, and it ran for months. It is often described as the first feature-length film in natural color, meaning the colors came from the scene through filters rather than from paint.","Kinemacolor had a weakness you can probably guess. Because the red picture and the green picture were taken one after another, not at the same instant, anything that moved quickly had shifted slightly between the two frames. A galloping horse or a waving hand might show strange red and green fringes around its edges. The projectors also needed special equipment, and running the film so fast used a lot of expensive film stock.","Then came trouble in the courts. William Friese-Greene, another British inventor, challenged Smith's patent in 1913. After a back-and-forth battle, the patent was finally revoked in 1915, when Britain's highest court, the House of Lords, agreed it was not valid. Without its patent protection, and with the First World War disrupting business, Kinemacolor faded away. But it had shown the world that color movies could fill a theater and sell tickets."]},{"heading":"Taking Color Away: Subtractive Color and Technicolor","paragraphs":["Additive color, mixing beams of light, has a big problem for movies: you need special projectors with filters, and the filters waste a lot of light. The solution that finally won was the opposite idea, called subtractive color. It is the way paints, printer inks and colored glass work.","Start with white light, which contains all the colors. Now hold up a piece of cyan (blue-green) see-through plastic. Cyan dye absorbs red light and lets green and blue pass through. A magenta (pinkish-purple) dye absorbs green light. A yellow dye absorbs blue light. So each of these three dyes takes away, or subtracts, one of the three colors your cones detect. Stack yellow and magenta together and they remove blue and green, leaving only red. Stack cyan and yellow and you remove red and blue, leaving green. Use a little of each dye and you get gray; use a lot of all three and almost no light gets through, giving black. A film made this way can be shown in any ordinary projector, because all the color is already in the film itself.","The company that turned this into Hollywood magic was Technicolor. It was founded in Boston in 1914, and officially incorporated in 1915, by Herbert Kalmus, Daniel Comstock and W. Burton Wescott. Kalmus and Comstock had both studied at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, and the 'Tech' in Technicolor is a nod to MIT. Kalmus was not just a scientist but a determined businessman, and he led the company for decades.","Technicolor's road was long and bumpy. Its first system, in 1917, was additive and needed a special projector; only one film, The Gulf Between, was made with it, and just a few frames of it survive. Its second process, in 1922, was subtractive and two-colored. It made two thin prints, one tinted red-orange and one green, and glued them back to back. The Toll of the Sea (1922), starring Anna May Wong, was the first general-release film in Technicolor. Audiences liked the colors, but the cemented prints tended to bend into a cup shape in the heat of the projector and scratched easily. A third process, from 1928, solved that problem with a clever printing method called dye transfer, but it still used only two colors.","The real breakthrough was the three-strip camera, finished in 1932. Inside it, right behind the lens, sat a cube of glass made of two prisms with a special partly mirrored surface between them, called a beam splitter. About one third of the light was sent sideways through a green filter onto one strip of black-and-white film, which recorded the green parts of the scene. The rest of the light passed through a magenta filter onto two more strips pressed face to face. The front strip recorded blue light, and the back one recorded red. So every time the shutter opened, three separate black-and-white negatives were exposed at the very same instant: a red record, a green record and a blue record.","Those three negatives were then used to make printing films called matrices, a bit like rubber stamps. Each matrix soaked up dye: the red record took cyan dye, the green record took magenta and the blue record took yellow. One after another, the three matrices were pressed against a blank strip of film, transferring their dyes in perfect alignment. The result was a print with rich, glowing colors that were remarkably stable and did not fade easily. Many Technicolor prints from the 1930s and 1940s still look vivid today.","There was a price for all this beauty. The three-strip camera was a huge machine, and it had to be wrapped in a large soundproof cover so its noise did not ruin the sound recording. Because the beam splitter divided the light three ways, and the film was very slow, rated at only about ASA 5, the camera needed enormous amounts of light. Film sets were blasted with banks of powerful lamps, making them extremely hot and bright. Studios could not even buy the cameras: Technicolor rented them out, along with its own camera crew, and usually sent a color consultant too. For many years that consultant was Natalie Kalmus, Herbert's former wife, who had strong opinions about which colors could appear on screen, and directors did not always enjoy her advice."]},{"heading":"Cartoons First, Then Becky, Then Oz","paragraphs":["The first film made with the three-strip process was not a big live-action drama. It was a cartoon. Walt Disney's studio had already started a short film called Flowers and Trees in black and white, as part of its Silly Symphonies series of musical cartoons. When Disney saw what Technicolor's new process could do, he took a gamble: he had the film redone in full color. Animators did not have to worry about hot lights and giant cameras, because they were photographing paintings, so cartoons were a perfect place to test three-strip color.","Flowers and Trees was released on 30 July 1932. Trees danced, flowers bloomed and a fire raged in bright reds and oranges. It cost more than planned, but audiences loved it, and it won the very first Academy Award for Best Short Subject (Cartoons). Disney signed a deal giving his studio the exclusive right to use three-strip Technicolor for cartoons for a few years, which left rival animation studios stuck with older two-color systems. From then on, every Silly Symphony was made in color.","Live-action filmmakers soon followed. In 1934 a short musical film called La Cucaracha showed that three-strip color could work with real actors. Then came Becky Sharp (1935), based on William Makepeace Thackeray's novel Vanity Fair and directed by Rouben Mamoulian, who took over after the first director died during production. It starred Miriam Hopkins as a clever, ambitious young woman climbing her way into high society. Becky Sharp was the first feature-length film shot entirely in three-strip Technicolor. Mamoulian used color to tell the story: in one famous scene at a ball, as news of the Battle of Waterloo spreads, the colors of the soldiers' uniforms grow stronger and redder as the panic rises. Hopkins was nominated for an Oscar for Best Actress.","By the late 1930s, Technicolor features were appearing more often, though they were still expensive and special. The most famous of all came out in August 1939: The Wizard of Oz, from the studio MGM. Remember, it was not the first color film; you have just met many earlier ones. But it may be the most famous use of color in movie history, because it turned the switch from gray to color into part of the story itself.","The film's scenes in Kansas, along with the opening titles, were shot in black and white and printed in a warm brownish tint called sepia. When a tornado carries Dorothy's farmhouse to Oz, she walks to the front door, and suddenly the world bursts into Technicolor. The filmmakers achieved this without any computer tricks. MGM first thought about hand-tinting the frames to keep the sepia look but decided it was too costly. Instead, they painted the inside of the farmhouse set in sepia colors. A stand-in for Judy Garland named Bobbie Koshay, wearing a sepia-colored dress, opened the door with her back to the camera. Then the camera glided forward through the doorway, and Garland herself, in her blue-and-white checked dress, stepped into the dazzling color world of Munchkinland.","Making Oz was not comfortable. Technicolor's huge lights could heat the set to more than 38 degrees Celsius, or over 100 degrees Fahrenheit, and the actors, some in heavy costumes and makeup, had to perform under that glare day after day. The film's electricity bill was enormous. But the result was so vivid that, more than 80 years later, the yellow brick road and the ruby slippers still glow on screen."]},{"heading":"Color in Your Pocket: Kodachrome to Eastmancolor","paragraphs":["Technicolor's giant cameras were useless for a family on vacation, who needed a color film that fit an ordinary camera. Two musicians helped crack that problem. Leopold Mannes was a pianist, and Leopold Godowsky Jr. was a violinist. They became friends as teenagers in New York, both from musical families, and both were also trained in science. They began experimenting with color film in their spare time, doing chemistry in kitchens and bathrooms. According to a famous story, they timed their developing by whistling or humming pieces of music at a steady tempo, since they could not see a clock in the dark. Eventually Kodak noticed their work, gave them proper laboratories in Rochester, New York, and paired them with company chemists.","The result was Kodachrome, which Kodak introduced in 1935, first as 16mm film for home movies and then, in 1936, for 35mm still cameras and 8mm movie cameras. Kodachrome was a kind of film called a tripack, with three very thin layers of emulsion stacked on one base. The top layer recorded blue light, the middle green and the bottom red. When the film was processed, the colored dyes were added to each layer one by one in a complicated series of steps, so that the finished film became a positive transparency, a slide, with cyan, magenta and yellow dyes in the right places. Subtractive color, all in one strip of film!","Kodachrome's rich colors and sharpness made it a legend. Photographers for magazines like National Geographic used it for decades, including Steve McCurry's famous 1984 portrait known as Afghan Girl. Families projected their Kodachrome slides on living-room walls to show off their holidays. But in the digital age, demand collapsed. Kodak stopped making Kodachrome in June 2009, and by then only one lab in the world could still process it: Dwayne's Photo, in the small town of Parsons, Kansas. Photographers sent in their final rolls from around the world, and the very last Kodachrome processing was done there in December 2010. Steve McCurry was given one of the last rolls ever made.","Meanwhile, Hollywood was changing too. In 1950 Kodak introduced Eastmancolor, a color negative film for movies with its color-making chemicals built into the film itself. It could be loaded into an ordinary single-strip movie camera, needed far less light than three-strip Technicolor and was much cheaper. Within a few years, the giant three-strip cameras were retired; the last American feature shot with one came out in 1955. Technicolor survived by printing Eastmancolor films using its dye transfer method. Early Eastmancolor prints had one sad flaw, though: their dyes were not very stable, so many of them have faded over the decades to a pinkish color.","Even after color became cheaper, black-and-white did not vanish overnight. Color still cost more, and many directors liked the stark look of black and white for serious dramas. Through the 1950s and early 1960s, plenty of Hollywood films were still made in black and white, and the Academy Awards kept separate prizes for black-and-white and color cinematography into the mid-1960s. Only by the late 1960s, as television switched to color too, did color fully take over. For family snapshots, color prints became the normal choice by around 1970.","Yet the 1930s were the decade when color really arrived, and it arrived at the very same moment as an even bigger shock. The Flowers and Trees audience in 1932 was not only seeing color; it was listening to music that came from the film itself, perfectly in time with the pictures. Just a few years earlier, every movie had been 'silent', played with live music in the theater. Now the movies were about to start talking."]}],"deepDive":"Why three colors? The answer is in biology, not physics. Light comes in a smooth range of wavelengths, roughly 400 to 700 nanometers for the visible part, and a real object's color is a whole curve of how much it reflects at each wavelength. Our eyes throw most of that information away. Each cone type adds up the light it receives, weighted by its own sensitivity curve (peaking near 420, 530 and 560 nanometers for S, M and L cones), so the brain receives just three numbers per spot. Two physically different light mixtures that give the same three numbers look identical; scientists call them metamers. Every color process in this part, from Maxwell's lanterns to Technicolor to your phone screen, depends on metamers: it does not reproduce the real light from a scene, only a mixture that fools our three cone types. That is also why no three-color system can show every color perfectly. The filters and dyes overlap with each other and with the cone curves, so some very saturated colors, like the vivid cyan-green of a tropical sea, fall outside what a given set of primaries can make. Early processes had particular blind spots. Two-color systems like Kinemacolor and early Technicolor could not make a good pure blue, so filmmakers chose costumes and scenery that avoided it. Additive screen plates like the Autochrome absorbed most of the light (each grain passes only about a third of the spectrum, and the black filler blocks more), so they looked dim and needed long exposures. Subtractive systems avoid wasting light in projection, but their dyes are never perfect absorbers; real magenta dye, for example, also absorbs some blue, which is why color film and printers add corrections and, in printing, a separate black ink. Historians also debate how 'natural' early color really was. Technicolor's consultants deliberately controlled palettes, and the famous Technicolor look was partly an artistic style rather than a neutral record of reality. And as the 1861 ribbon showed, even the first color photograph worked partly by accident, thanks to ultraviolet light that no human could see.","funFact":"The inventors of Kodachrome, Leopold Mannes and Leopold Godowsky Jr., were professional musicians, and they are said to have timed their darkroom chemistry by singing or whistling music at a steady beat.","check":{"q":"In the Autochrome color plates sold by the Lumière brothers from 1907, what were the tiny colored filters made of?","a":"Dyed grains of potato starch","wrong":["Crushed colored glass","Drops of colored ink","Tiny pieces of butterfly wings"]},"sources":[{"title":"Color photography","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_photography"},{"title":"Cone cell","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cone_cell"},{"title":"Autochrome Lumière","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autochrome_Lumi%C3%A8re"},{"title":"Kinemacolor","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinemacolor"},{"title":"Technicolor","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technicolor"},{"title":"Flowers and Trees","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flowers_and_Trees"},{"title":"The Wizard of Oz (1939 film)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wizard_of_Oz_(1939_film)"},{"title":"Kodachrome","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kodachrome"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/13-1.jpg","alt":"The tartan ribbon of 1861, the first color photograph made with the three-color method Maxwell proposed.","file":"Tartan Ribbon.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tartan_Ribbon.jpg","author":"James Clerk Maxwell","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/13-2.jpg","alt":"The Eiffel Tower in 1914, an Autochrome color photograph on glass by Auguste Léon for the Albert Kahn collection.","file":"Tour Eiffel autochrome collection Albert Kahn museum.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_Eiffel_autochrome_collection_Albert_Kahn_museum.jpg","author":"Auguste Léon","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/13-3.jpg","alt":"A 1930s three-strip Technicolor camera with its side open, showing the film path inside.","file":"3-strip Technicolor camera.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3-strip_Technicolor_camera.jpg","author":"Marcin Wichary from San Francisco, U.S.A.","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4332},{"n":14,"title":"The Talkies Arrive","when":"1890s – 1931","summary":"For more than 30 years, movie actors could move and make faces but could not be heard. Inventors tried giant records, then learned to turn sound into a wiggly stripe of light right on the film. In 1927 Al Jolson sang and spoke in The Jazz Singer, and within a few years almost every movie in America could talk.","sections":[{"heading":"A Dream as Old as the Movies","paragraphs":["You may have read that the first movies were 'silent'. In Part 12 you learned that this is not really true: theaters filled the air with piano, organ or orchestra music, and sometimes with sound effects and live narrators. What early films could not do was carry their own sound. When an actor opened her mouth on screen, nobody in the audience could hear the actual words. For about 30 years, inventors chased the dream of fixing that.","The dream is almost as old as moving pictures themselves. Thomas Edison was already famous for inventing the phonograph in 1877, a machine that recorded sound as tiny wiggles on a cylinder. When his laboratory began working on moving pictures, the idea was to do for the eyes what the phonograph had done for the ears, and then put the two together. In about 1894 or 1895, William Kennedy Laurie Dickson, whom you met in Part 10, made a short test film at Edison's lab in New Jersey. In it, Dickson plays a violin into a huge recording horn while two men dance nearby. It is known today as the Dickson Experimental Sound Film, and it is one of the oldest films made with recorded sound meant to go with it.","Edison's company even sold a version of its peephole Kinetoscope with a phonograph inside, so a viewer could look through the eyepiece while listening through rubber ear tubes. But the sound and pictures were not truly linked, and it was not a big success. In 1913 Edison tried again with the Kinetophone, which connected a projector in the booth to a phonograph behind the screen using a long belt and pulleys. If the belt slipped, or the film had been cut and repaired, the voices drifted out of step with the lips. Operators often struggled to keep it in sync, the novelty soon wore off, and after a fire at Edison's works in December 1914 destroyed its masters, the system was dropped.","In France, Léon Gaumont showed his Chronophone, which linked a projector to a record player, in 1902, and he kept improving it for years. Other inventors in Germany, Britain and the United States tried similar ideas. All of them ran into the same two giant problems.","The first problem was synchronization, or 'sync': keeping sound and picture in perfect step. Our brains are very sensitive to this. If a voice is even a fraction of a second early or late, people notice at once. The second problem was loudness. Before the 1920s, sound was recorded and played back mechanically. A singer bellowed into a horn, the sound waves shook a needle, and on playback the needle shook a horn again. That might fill a parlor, but not a theater with a thousand seats. Nobody could make the sound big enough. Solving that would take electronics."]},{"heading":"Writing Sound with Light","paragraphs":["One clever idea was to put the sound right onto the film itself. Then the sound and the pictures could never drift apart, because they would be on the same strip, moving through the projector together. A French-born inventor working in England, Eugene Lauste, received a patent for recording sound on film in 1907 and made experimental recordings, but he never turned it into a working business.","The electronics that made sound film possible came partly from radio. In 1906 the American inventor Lee de Forest created the Audion, a vacuum tube that could make weak electrical signals stronger. Improved tubes like it became the heart of amplifiers, the devices that take a tiny signal from a microphone and boost it until it can drive a big loudspeaker. Electrical microphones, amplifiers and loudspeakers improved quickly in the late 1910s and early 1920s, thanks to the boom in telephones and radio broadcasting.","Think of an amplifier like a person controlling a big water tap. A tiny push from a microphone's weak signal tells the tap how far to open, and a powerful flow of electricity from the mains comes out the other side, rising and falling in exactly the same pattern as the small signal, only much stronger. That stronger signal can shake the paper cone of a loudspeaker hard enough to fill a theater with sound. Without amplifiers, no sound system could have reached the back row of a big cinema.","De Forest then turned to film. Working with an inventor named Theodore Case, who had developed sensitive light-detecting tubes, he created a sound-on-film system he called Phonofilm. Its first commercial screening took place at the Rivoli Theater in New York on 15 April 1923. Phonofilm shorts showed vaudeville performers, singers, comedians and even politicians giving speeches. People were impressed by the novelty, but the sound quality was poor, and Hollywood's big studios were not interested. De Forest and Case also quarrelled and split up. In Germany, three inventors developed a similar system called Tri-Ergon, which also struggled at first.","Phonofilm did not conquer the movies, but it proved the idea could work. The next big push came from a much larger organization: the research labs of the American telephone giant. And it came from an unexpected direction, a record."]},{"heading":"Vitaphone: Giant Records and Don Juan","paragraphs":["In the mid-1920s, engineers at Western Electric and its research arm, Bell Telephone Laboratories, had developed excellent electrical recording, amplifiers and loudspeakers. They built a system that recorded sound onto large discs, carefully linked to a film projector. Most Hollywood studios were not interested. But one smaller, ambitious studio was: Warner Bros., run by four brothers, Harry, Albert, Sam and Jack Warner. Sam Warner was especially excited by sound. In 1925 Warner Bros. took on the system and named it Vitaphone.","Vitaphone was a sound-on-disc system. For each reel of film, there was a matching record. The records were big, 16 inches across, about 40 centimeters, much larger than ordinary records of the time, and they spun at 33 1/3 revolutions per minute, slower than the usual 78. That combination was chosen so one disc could play for about 11 minutes, the length of one reel of film. Decades later, the same 33 1/3 speed was used for long-playing LP records, which many people still collect today.","Keeping the disc and film together took care. The turntable was mechanically connected to the projector motor, so both ran at the same speed. At the start of each reel, the projectionist lined up a marked start frame in the film with an arrow-marked start groove on the record, and then started them together. As long as nothing went wrong, the sound stayed in sync.","Recording for Vitaphone was nerve-racking. On the set, the sound was cut straight into a soft wax master disc while the camera filmed, so each scene had to be performed in one go. There was no easy way to snip out a cough or a fluffed line, as you can with a modern recording. If something went wrong, the whole take, picture and sound, was usually done again. Engineers listened in a separate room, watching dials and warning the director if the sound was too loud, too soft or spoiled by a noise from outside.","Things did go wrong, though. A disc could wear out after about 20 plays. It could crack or break. If a film broke and the projectionist cut out a few damaged frames to repair it, the picture would be slightly ahead of the record for the rest of the reel, and an actor's words would suddenly come from the wrong face. Discs also had to be shipped to every theater with every print.","Warner Bros. launched Vitaphone in New York in early August 1926 with a grand show. First came a program of short films: a filmed speech by Will Hays, the head of the film industry's trade association, welcoming the new invention, followed by famous musicians and opera singers performing. Then came the main feature, Don Juan, a swashbuckling adventure starring John Barrymore. Don Juan had no spoken dialogue. The actors still spoke through intertitles. But it had a full recorded orchestral score, played by the New York Philharmonic, and synchronized sound effects such as clashing swords and ringing bells.","Audiences were delighted, especially by the short musical films. For the first time, a small-town theater with no orchestra could offer the same music as a grand palace in New York. Even so, many people in Hollywood thought recorded music was a nice extra rather than a revolution. That changed a little over a year later."]},{"heading":"You Ain't Heard Nothin' Yet","paragraphs":["On the evening of 6 October 1927, crowds gathered outside Warner Bros.' theater on Broadway in New York for the premiere of The Jazz Singer. The story was about Jakie Rabinowitz, the son of a Jewish cantor, the singer who leads prayers in a synagogue. His father wants him to follow family tradition, but Jakie loves jazz and popular songs. He changes his name to Jack Robin and tries to become a star on the stage, torn between his family and his dreams.","The star was Al Jolson, then one of the most famous entertainers in America. Like Don Juan, most of the film was silent, with intertitles and a recorded music score. But in a few scenes, Jolson sang, and the audience heard his real voice coming from the screen, perfectly in time with his lips. He sang six songs, including 'Toot, Toot, Tootsie' and 'Blue Skies', and the film also included the Jewish prayer 'Kol Nidre'.","Then came the moments that made history. Before one song, Jolson spoke, apparently adding some lines of his own. 'Wait a minute, wait a minute, you ain't heard nothin' yet!' he says to the crowd in the scene. In another scene, he chats excitedly to his mother at the piano. In total, the film has only about two minutes of synchronized talking. It was not the first sound film and it was not a full 'talkie'. But hearing a star speak casually and naturally, as if he were really there, thrilled audiences in a way that recorded music alone had not.","There is an important and uncomfortable part of this story that we should be honest about. In several scenes, Jolson's character performs in blackface, wearing dark makeup to imitate and mock Black people, a custom from old minstrel shows. It was common in American entertainment at the time, and Jolson often performed this way. Today it is widely recognized as racist, because it turned Black Americans into crude stereotypes for white audiences' amusement while Black performers themselves were shut out of many opportunities. The Jazz Singer is a landmark in technology, and it is also a reminder that famous 'firsts' can carry the prejudices of their time.","The night also held a personal tragedy. Sam Warner, the brother who had pushed hardest for sound, died of pneumonia the day before the premiere, aged only 40. The other brothers were in California for his funeral and missed the triumph he had worked toward.","And it was a triumph. The Jazz Singer cost about 422,000 dollars to make and earned roughly 2.6 million dollars in rentals around the world. Every studio in Hollywood took notice. When the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences held its very first awards ceremony, a quick dinner of about 15 minutes at the Hollywood Roosevelt Hotel on 16 May 1929, Warner Bros. received a special honorary award for producing The Jazz Singer, which the Academy called the pioneer outstanding talking picture."]},{"heading":"Sound on Film: The Wiggly Stripe","paragraphs":["Vitaphone's big records had shown that people wanted sound. But the cleverer long-term answer was the one Lauste and de Forest had chased: put the sound on the film itself. Two systems in particular made it work. Fox's Movietone grew out of Theodore Case's work with his assistant Earl Sponable, after Case split from de Forest. RCA Photophone was developed from research at General Electric. Both stored sound as an optical soundtrack, a narrow strip running down the film beside the pictures.","Here is how an optical soundtrack works. Sound is made of vibrations, pressure waves in the air that rise and fall hundreds or thousands of times a second. A microphone turns those vibrations into an electrical signal that rises and falls in the same pattern. In the recording machine, that signal controls a beam of light shining onto the moving film. There were two main methods. In variable density recording, the beam got brighter and dimmer, so the soundtrack came out as a row of fine stripes, from pale gray to dark. In variable area recording, which RCA used, the beam stayed the same brightness but changed its width, so the soundtrack came out as a wavy, mountain-range shape of clear and black. If you look closely at old 35mm film, you can sometimes see that wavy line running beside the frames.","In the projector, the process runs backward. A small, steady lamp called the exciter lamp shines through the soundtrack as the film moves past. Where the track is clear, lots of light gets through. Where it is dark, less does. On the other side sits a photoelectric cell, a device that turns light into electricity: more light, more current. So the flickering light becomes a rising and falling electrical signal, exactly copying the original sound wave. Amplifiers make it stronger, and loudspeakers behind the screen turn it back into vibrations in the air. Sound becomes electricity, then light, then a pattern on film, then light, then electricity, and finally sound again.","There is one neat detail. The picture has to stop still for a split second in front of the lens for every frame, as you learned in Part 11, but sound has to be read while the film moves smoothly and steadily. So the sound reader sits a little way away from the picture gate, where the film runs smoothly, and the soundtrack is printed slightly ahead of the picture it belongs to.","Sound on film also fixed the speed of the movies. In the silent era, cameras and projectors were often cranked by hand at varying speeds. But sound has to play at exactly the speed it was recorded, or voices become squeaky or slow and growly. The coming of sound set 24 frames per second as the standard speed for sound films, and it has stayed the standard for most movies ever since.","Fox also used Movietone for feature films. Sunrise (1927), a beautiful drama directed by the German filmmaker F. W. Murnau, was released with a recorded Movietone music score and sound effects running on the film itself. Meanwhile, the other big studios had been waiting to see which system would win before spending money. In 1928, most of them agreed to adopt sound-on-film equipment from Western Electric, a decision that helped make optical soundtracks the industry standard.","Fox put Movietone to work in exciting ways. In 1927 its sound newsreels let audiences hear real events, including the roar of Charles Lindbergh's plane taking off on his famous flight across the Atlantic. Imagine sitting in a theater and hearing the engine of a plane you had only read about in newspapers. Sound on film was cheaper and more reliable than discs, and by 1931 even Warner Bros. had switched to it. The giant Vitaphone records became museum pieces, though some have since been rediscovered and matched again with their films."]},{"heading":"The Great Switch","paragraphs":["After The Jazz Singer, Hollywood raced to catch up. On 6 July 1928, Warner Bros. released Lights of New York, a gangster story directed by Bryan Foy. It was short, about 57 minutes, and cheap, made for about 23,000 dollars. Critics noticed the stiff acting, as performers seemed to huddle close to hidden microphones. But it had something no feature had had before: every scene had spoken dialogue. It was the first 'all-talking' feature film, and it earned more than a million dollars.","Studios now poured money into sound. They built soundproof stages, bought recording equipment and hired engineers from telephone and radio companies. Theaters had to be wired too, with new projectors, amplifiers and loudspeakers, which was expensive. In the United States, the number of theaters equipped for sound rose from about 100 to about 800 between 1928 and 1929, and by 1930 roughly half of American theaters could play sound films. Big city theaters converted first; small-town halls followed later.","Production changed even faster. By around 1930, almost every Hollywood film was being made with sound, and studios largely stopped making new silent features. In a few years, an art form that had developed for three decades without spoken words was transformed. Other countries followed at different speeds. Britain had more than 60 percent of its theaters equipped by the end of 1930, while many theaters in France and the Soviet Union stayed silent for some years longer.","Talking pictures also raised a new problem: language. A silent film could be shown anywhere by simply swapping the intertitle cards. A talkie spoke only one language. In the early sound years, some studios solved this by filming the same story several times, in English, German, Spanish and French, sometimes on the same sets at night with different actors. Later, dubbing, recording new voices in another language, and subtitles became the usual answers.","Many early sound films have had a strange afterlife. Because the Vitaphone discs were stored separately from the film reels, the two were often separated over the years. Sometimes a film survived without its records, and sometimes records survived without their film. Since 1991, a group of collectors and archivists called the Vitaphone Project has hunted for lost discs in attics, basements and collections, so that films silent for decades can be reunited with their sound and heard again."]},{"heading":"Growing Pains: Booths, Microphones and Missing Musicians","paragraphs":["The switch to sound was exciting, but it was also messy. The first problem was noise. Film cameras whirred and clattered, and the new sensitive microphones picked up every sound. So the cameras were locked inside soundproof booths, little rooms with a glass window, where the camera operator worked in the heat. Since the booth could not easily move, early talkies often look stiff, with long scenes filmed from one position. Soon engineers built soundproof covers called blimps that fit around the camera itself, and the camera could move again.","The second problem was microphones. Early microphones could not pick up sound from far away, and they were fixed in place. Sound engineers hid them in flower vases, lamps and other props, and actors had to stay near them, speaking toward them. Turn your head the wrong way and your line might vanish. Later, the boom microphone, a microphone on a long pole held above the actors just out of the picture, let performers move naturally again.","The third problem was voices. Silent stars had been chosen for their faces, their movements and their charm, not their voices. Now every accent, lisp or squeaky note could be heard. Some silent stars adapted easily, and stage-trained actors who knew how to speak clearly were suddenly in demand. Others found their careers fading, sometimes because their voices did not match the image audiences had imagined, and sometimes simply because studios wanted new faces. The movie Singin' in the Rain, made in 1952, is a famous comedy about exactly this moment, though it exaggerates for laughs.","The fourth problem was harder for many families. Thousands of musicians had earned their living playing in theater orchestras, or as organists and pianists accompanying silent films. When a film carried its own recorded music, theater owners no longer needed to pay them. Within a few years, many of those jobs disappeared. In Japan, the benshi, popular live narrators who explained and voiced silent films for audiences, lost their work too. New technology often brings both wonder and hardship, and the talkies brought plenty of both.","Not everyone rushed to talk. Charlie Chaplin, whose Little Tramp character was loved around the world, worried that giving the Tramp a voice would spoil him. Part of the Tramp's magic was that anyone, in any country, could understand him without words. Chaplin was so doubtful that he told a reporter he would give the talkies three years, that's all. Because he paid for and controlled his own films, he could choose for himself. His film City Lights, which premiered in January 1931, had a recorded soundtrack with music that Chaplin composed himself, plus sound effects, but no spoken dialogue. It was a huge success, and many critics today think it is his greatest film. Chaplin was a rare exception, though, and even he finally made a full talking picture, The Great Dictator, in 1940.","By the early 1930s, then, the movies had found their voices. Actors spoke, singers sang, guns banged and doors slammed. And one small studio in Los Angeles was thinking about something different. Why should only actors make sounds? What if drawings could whistle, sing and dance in perfect time with music? In that studio, a cheerful cartoon mouse was about to start whistling at the wheel of a steamboat."]}],"deepDive":"Why did sound-on-film beat sound-on-disc? Engineering, mostly. With a disc, synchronization depends on mechanics staying perfect for a whole reel: a single splice that removes damaged frames throws the lips off for everything after it, and a scratched or broken record ruins a show. An optical track is physically locked to its frames, survives splicing (losing only a blip of sound), is printed in the same lab step as the pictures, and costs almost nothing to copy. Its weaknesses were noise and range. Dust, scratches and film grain all add hiss and crackle, and the early tracks could reproduce only a limited range of frequencies, which is one reason 1930s films sound thin and boxy to modern ears. Engineers spent decades improving this: noise-reduction methods, stereo tracks squeezed into the same strip, and later digital soundtracks printed in other spaces on the film. The variable-density versus variable-area contest is a nice example of competing standards: Western Electric favored density, RCA favored area, and both companies licensed their systems widely, which kept prints playable on either type of equipment because readers simply measure the total light passing through. Historians also debate how sudden the 'coming of sound' really was. Sound experiments went back more than 30 years, Fox's Movietone newsreels and Warner's Vitaphone shorts had already familiarized audiences with recorded sound before The Jazz Singer, and the transition took several years and varied hugely by country. In Japan, silent films with benshi narrators remained common into the mid-1930s. Some film scholars argue that early talkies were a step backward artistically, because booths and fixed microphones froze camera movement that silent filmmakers had made fluid, while others point out that within about five years directors were moving cameras freely again and using sound creatively, with off-screen voices, sound effects that told part of the story, and musical numbers that could only exist in sound cinema.","funFact":"The very first Academy Awards ceremony, held on 16 May 1929 at the Hollywood Roosevelt Hotel, lasted only about 15 minutes, and one of its special awards went to Warner Bros. for The Jazz Singer.","check":{"q":"In an optical soundtrack, which device in the projector turns the flickering light coming through the soundtrack back into an electrical signal?","a":"A photoelectric cell","wrong":["A vacuum cleaner motor","A phonograph needle","A magnifying glass"]},"sources":[{"title":"Sound film","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sound_film"},{"title":"Vitaphone","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitaphone"},{"title":"The Jazz Singer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Jazz_Singer"},{"title":"Optical sound","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optical_sound"},{"title":"Lights of New York (1928 film)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lights_of_New_York_(1928_film)"},{"title":"City Lights","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_Lights"},{"title":"1st Academy Awards","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1st_Academy_Awards"},{"title":"Don Juan (1926 film)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Juan_(1926_film)"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/14-1.jpg","alt":"Poster for The Jazz Singer (1927), showing Al Jolson at the piano singing to his mother.","file":"The Jazz Singer 1927 Poster.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Jazz_Singer_1927_Poster.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/14-2.jpg","alt":"Close-up of 35mm film sound tracks: the wavy white stripes are the analog optical soundtrack, beside digital sound codes.","file":"35mm film audio macro.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:35mm_film_audio_macro.jpg","author":"Rotareneg","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/14-3.jpg","alt":"Engineer E. B. Craft (left) demonstrates the Vitaphone sound-on-disc system in 1926, next to the projector and its turntable.","file":"VitaphoneDemo.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VitaphoneDemo.jpg","author":"Unnamed photographer for Vitaphone / ATT","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3810},{"n":15,"title":"Drawings That Move: Animation","when":"1892 – 1940","summary":"Long before computers, artists made drawings come alive by filming them one picture at a time. A dinosaur named Gertie bowed to her audience, a mouse whistled in perfect time with music, and a giant gorilla made of rabbit fur climbed a skyscraper. Here is how they did it.","sections":[{"heading":"Luminous Pantomimes at the Wax Museum","paragraphs":["Imagine it is a chilly autumn evening in Paris on 28 October 1892. You push through the crowds at the Musée Grévin, a famous wax museum on the Boulevard Montmartre, and find your way into a small, dark room called the Cabinet Fantastique. A screen glows at the front. Then something astonishing happens: a painted clown named Pierrot strolls across the screen, sings a serenade under a window, and gets into a squabble with a rival. Nobody is wearing a costume. Nobody is on stage. The characters are paintings, and yet they move.","The man behind the screen was Émile Reynaud, the same French inventor you met earlier with his praxinoscope, the spinning toy with a ring of mirrors. Reynaud had kept tinkering. He wanted more than a ten-second loop that repeated forever. He wanted whole stories, with characters who walked in, did something funny, and walked off again. In 1888 he applied for a French patent on a machine he called the Théâtre Optique, the Optical Theatre, and the patent was granted in January 1889.","Here is how it worked. Reynaud painted every single picture by hand, in color, on small transparent squares of gelatin. He fastened hundreds of these squares one after another along a long flexible band, with holes along the edges so the band could be pulled smoothly over a wheel. Some bands were up to about 50 meters long. A lantern shone light through each little picture, and a clever arrangement of mirrors and lenses threw the moving characters onto the screen. A second lantern projected a painted background, such as a garden or a beach, so the characters seemed to move through a real place.","Reynaud did not use a motor. He cranked the band by hand, like a puppeteer, and he could speed up, slow down, or even run a gesture back and forth to stretch a moment for laughs. This meant a band of about 500 pictures could last around 12 to 15 minutes, far longer than you might expect. A musician played along, and at marked points on the band an electrical contact could set off a sound effect. Every show was a live performance, a little different each time.","Reynaud called his shows Pantomimes Lumineuses, or Luminous Pantomimes. The first program included Pauvre Pierrot (Poor Pierrot), made of about 500 images, Un bon bock (A Good Beer), with about 700, and Le Clown et ses chiens (The Clown and His Dogs). Later he added Autour d'une cabine (Around a Bathing Hut), a seaside comedy. The Grévin shows ran on and off until 1900, with more than 12,800 performances and over half a million visitors. That makes Reynaud's animated shows some of the very first projected moving pictures a paying audience ever saw, a few years before the Lumière brothers screened their photographic films just down the road.","Sadly, Reynaud's story has an unhappy ending. Hand-painting every frame was slow, and once photographic films arrived, audiences flocked to see real trains, real streets and real people. The Théâtre Optique could not compete. Around 1913, poor and discouraged, Reynaud is said to have smashed his last machine and thrown most of his picture bands into the River Seine. Only two of his bands survive today, including Pauvre Pierrot. In 2015 UNESCO added his Luminous Pantomimes to its Memory of the World Register, a list of the planet's most precious documents. Reynaud had shown that drawings could tell stories on a big screen. Others would now try to do it with a camera."]},{"heading":"Chalk Faces, Dancing Lines and a Shy Dinosaur","paragraphs":["A movie camera can be turned into an animation machine with a simple trick. Instead of filming a whole scene at once, you film just one frame, stop, change the drawing a tiny bit, then film one more frame. When the film is played back at normal speed, the tiny changes blur together into motion. Animators call this frame-by-frame or single-frame filming, and it is the same stop trick Georges Méliès used to make people vanish, applied one picture at a time.","One of the first people to use it for drawings was J. Stuart Blackton, a British-born American cartoonist who co-founded the Vitagraph film company in New York. In 1900 he had made The Enchanted Drawing, in which he sketched a face and objects that seemed to come alive. In 1906 Vitagraph released his Humorous Phases of Funny Faces, about three minutes long. Blackton draws faces in white chalk on a blackboard, and then the faces roll their eyes, puff smoke from a cigar, and change expression. Much of the film actually uses cutout paper pieces moved bit by bit, but in a few moments the drawings themselves change from frame to frame. Many historians call it the first animated cartoon recorded on standard movie film.","Across the Atlantic, a French caricaturist named Émile Cohl, then about 50 years old, was working for the Gaumont film company in Paris. On 17 August 1908 Gaumont released his Fantasmagorie. It lasts less than two minutes, but it took about 700 drawings. Cohl drew each one on paper with dark lines, filmed them, and printed the film as a negative, so the lines show up white on black, like chalk on a blackboard. In it, a little stick-figure clown is born from the artist's hand, and everything around him keeps transforming: a flower turns into a bottle, an elephant turns into a house, a man turns into a cannon. There is no real plot, just a joyful dream where anything can become anything else. That freedom, where a line can turn into whatever the artist imagines, is something only animation can do.","Then came Winsor McCay, an American newspaper cartoonist famous for his gorgeous comic strip Little Nemo in Slumberland. McCay was a speed-drawing star in vaudeville, the live variety shows of the time. In 1911 he animated his Little Nemo characters, and in 1912 he made How a Mosquito Operates, about a mosquito who drinks so much blood from a sleeping man that it pops. But his most famous creation premiered in February 1914: Gertie the Dinosaur.","McCay performed with Gertie live on stage. He stood beside the screen in a smart suit, holding a whip like a circus trainer, and called out commands. On the screen, an enormous, friendly brontosaurus peeked out from behind rocks, bowed, ate a tree, cried when she was scolded, and swallowed a whole lake. At the end, McCay walked behind the screen and appeared, drawn, riding away on Gertie's back. Audiences adored her because she had a personality. She was not just moving; she seemed to feel things, to be shy, playful and a bit naughty.","Making Gertie took enormous work. McCay and his assistant drew on thin, see-through mulberry paper so they could trace the background rocks and lake onto every sheet. McCay later boasted of 10,000 drawings, though historians think that number is probably exaggerated, because surviving copies contain only about seven minutes of animation. He also invented smart shortcuts. He drew the most important poses first, the key moments of an action, and then filled in the pictures between them. He used registration marks so each sheet lined up exactly, and he reused loops of drawings for repeated movements like Gertie's breathing. About 400 of the original Gertie drawings still survive, and in 1991 the US Library of Congress added the film to its National Film Registry.","McCay did not patent his methods. He thought of animation as an art. But not everyone saw it that way, and a new idea was about to turn cartoons into an industry."]},{"heading":"The Clear-Sheet Sandwich: How Cel Animation Works","paragraphs":["Think about the problem McCay faced. If Gertie stands in front of a rocky lake, and you need 24 pictures for every second she moves, then drawing the whole picture again and again means drawing those same rocks and that same lake thousands of times. It is exhausting, and the background wobbles a little in every copy, so it seems to shiver on screen.","The solution came from a young American animator named Earl Hurd, who in 1914 patented the idea of drawing the moving characters on clear sheets of celluloid, the same kind of transparent plastic used for film. These sheets became known as cels, short for celluloid. You paint the background just once, on paper or board. Then you lay a cel with the character on top. Wherever the cel is clear, the background shows through. Wherever the character is painted, it covers the background. For the next frame, you lift off that cel and lay down the next one, with the character in a slightly different pose. The background never has to be redrawn.","Cel animation turned cartoons into a team sport. Here is roughly how a big studio did it by the 1930s. A lead animator drew the key poses, the most important moments, such as a character's foot leaving the ground and landing again. Assistants called inbetweeners drew the pictures in between, so the motion flowed smoothly. Every sheet of paper had holes punched at the bottom that fit onto pegs on the drawing desk and under the camera, which kept every picture lined up perfectly. Then the drawings went to the ink and paint department. Inkers traced each outline carefully onto the front of a cel with pen or brush, and painters filled in the colors on the back of the cel, so the brushstrokes did not show from the front. At Disney, this delicate work was done mostly by women, many of whom were highly skilled artists whose names were rarely in the credits.","How many pictures are needed? Sound films ran at 24 frames per second. Animating on ones means drawing a new picture for every single frame, 24 drawings per second, which looks extra smooth and is used for fast or delicate action. Most of the time, animators worked on twos: each drawing is photographed for two frames in a row, so only 12 drawings are needed for each second. Your eye still sees smooth motion, and the studio saves half the work. A feature-length cartoon could still need well over 100,000 painted cels."]},{"heading":"A Cat, a Rabbit and a Whistling Mouse","paragraphs":["By the 1920s, cartoons were a regular part of a trip to the movies. Before the main film, audiences watched newsreels and short cartoons. The biggest cartoon star of the silent era was Felix the Cat, a black cat created at Pat Sullivan's studio in New York, with much of the animation by Otto Messmer, starting in 1919. Felix paced back and forth when he was thinking, and he could pull his tail off and use it as a cane or a question mark.","Meanwhile, in Kansas City and then in Los Angeles, a young cartoonist named Walt Disney was trying to build a studio. His closest partner was Ub Iwerks, a superb and very fast animator Disney had met in 1919, when both were young artists at a commercial art studio in Kansas City. In 1927 they created Oswald the Lucky Rabbit for Universal Pictures. Oswald was a hit, but in early 1928 Disney discovered that his distributor, Charles Mintz, and Universal owned the rights to Oswald, and that Mintz had hired away most of his animators. Disney lost his character and most of his team in one meeting.","Disney and Iwerks needed a new star, fast. Together they came up with a cheerful mouse. Iwerks did most of the drawing of the first films, and Walt Disney gave the character his personality and, soon, his voice. Mickey Mouse first appeared in a silent short called Plane Crazy, tested in front of an audience in May 1928, and then in another silent film, The Gallopin' Gaucho. Neither one found a distributor.","Then Disney had a bold idea. The Jazz Singer had just shown that audiences went wild for synchronized sound. What if a cartoon could have music and sound effects that matched the action exactly, so every footstep, whistle and honk landed on the beat? The third Mickey film, Steamboat Willie, was designed from the start for sound. The animators planned the action around a steady musical tempo, so they knew exactly which frame each beat would fall on. In a test screening in July 1928, Iwerks and others stood behind a bedsheet screen, banging pots and pans and blowing whistles in time with the picture, while Disney's family and staff watched to see if the sound felt real. It did.","Recording the real soundtrack was hard. The first recording session in New York, in September 1928, failed because the orchestra could not stay in time with the film. For the second attempt, the team used a click track, a steady clicking beat in the musicians' ears, and a filmed bouncing ball to help them follow the tempo. Disney, short of money, sold his car to help pay for it. The sound was recorded with Cinephone, a system belonging to the businessman Pat Powers that was based on Lee de Forest's sound-on-film technology.","Steamboat Willie opened on 18 November 1928 at the Colony Theatre in New York. Mickey steers a riverboat, whistling a tune, and later plays music on everything in sight: he cranks a goat's tail to make its mouth play like a music box, and uses a cow's teeth like a xylophone. Walt Disney himself provided Mickey's squeaks and laughs. Audiences loved it, and Mickey became a star almost overnight.","It is important to be fair about the history here. Steamboat Willie was not the first sound cartoon. The Fleischer brothers had made sing-along cartoons with a bouncing ball, the Song Car-Tunes, and some of these had sound from the mid-1920s using de Forest's Phonofilm. Another studio released a sound cartoon called Dinner Time a couple of months before Steamboat Willie. It was also not the first Mickey cartoon made; it was the third. What made it special was how tightly the music and action were tied together, and how much fun the result was. Steamboat Willie entered the public domain in the United States on 1 January 2024, meaning anyone can now share that first version of Mickey freely, although later versions of the character are still protected."]},{"heading":"Shadow Puppets and the First Feature-Length Cartoons","paragraphs":["Who made the first full-length animated movie? Many people would guess Disney, but the answer takes us to Argentina and Germany.","On 9 November 1917, a cinema in Buenos Aires showed El Apóstol (The Apostle), a political comedy made by the Italian-born cartoonist Quirino Cristiani for the producer Federico Valle. In the film, Argentina's president, Hipólito Yrigoyen, dreams of flying to Mount Olympus and borrowing thunderbolts from the god Zeus to blast corruption out of Buenos Aires. It ran about an hour and ten minutes and used tens of thousands of frames, many made with cutout figures moved by hand under the camera. El Apóstol is usually counted as the world's first animated feature film. Sadly, no one can watch it today: the only known copy was destroyed in a fire at Valle's studio in 1926. Cristiani made another feature, and later a sound feature, but those are lost too.","That makes our next film the oldest animated feature that still survives. In Berlin, a young woman named Lotte Reiniger had loved cutting paper silhouettes since childhood. She could snip a delicate figure, with lacy hair and curling fingers, out of black card in minutes. Reiniger built her characters from cardboard and thin sheets of lead, cutting each arm, leg and head separately and joining them with tiny wire hinges so the joints could bend. She laid the figures on a glass table lit from below, so they appeared as pure black shadows against glowing backgrounds, and moved them a fraction for each frame.","From 1923 to 1926, Reiniger worked on The Adventures of Prince Achmed, a tale inspired by the Arabian Nights, with a flying horse, an evil sorcerer, a witch, demons, and Aladdin with his magic lamp. Her husband, Carl Koch, ran the camera, and the experimental artists Walter Ruttmann and Berthold Bartosch helped create magical backgrounds and effects. Koch and Reiniger set up their camera above several layers of glass, so different parts of the scene could sit at different levels, an early relative of the multiplane camera that Disney would later make famous. The film premiered in Germany in September 1926 and runs a little over an hour.","The original prints were tinted in rich colors, so a scene might glow amber, deep blue or green. For many years only black-and-white copies were easy to see, but archivists in Germany and Britain restored the tinting in the late 1990s using surviving nitrate prints. Reiniger kept making silhouette films for the rest of her long life, and she is remembered as one of the great pioneers of animation, a woman director at a time when there were very few."]},{"heading":"Giants One Frame at a Time: Stop-Motion","paragraphs":["Animation does not have to be drawn. If you take a toy, photograph it, move it a tiny bit, and photograph it again, then play the pictures back quickly, the toy seems to come alive on its own. This is stop-motion animation, and it works with models, clay, puppets, even everyday objects. Early filmmakers such as the Russian-Polish animator Władysław Starewicz made remarkable stop-motion films in the 1910s using real beetles and other insects fitted with wire legs.","The great American pioneer was Willis H. O'Brien. He started by building small clay dinosaurs, and in 1915 he made The Dinosaur and the Missing Link, a short comedy, after an exhibitor saw about 90 seconds of his test footage and paid him to finish it. O'Brien's big break came with the 1925 film The Lost World, based on Arthur Conan Doyle's novel about explorers who find dinosaurs living on a remote plateau. Its dinosaurs had metal skeletons called armatures, with joints that could be bent and would stay put, covered in rubber skin. Some even had air bladders inside so their chests seemed to breathe.","Then came King Kong, which premiered in New York on 2 March 1933 at two huge theaters, Radio City Music Hall and the RKO Roxy. It was directed by Merian C. Cooper and Ernest B. Schoedsack, and O'Brien led the effects team. On screen, Kong looks like a monster tall enough to climb the Empire State Building. In reality, the main Kong models were only about 14 to 18 inches (36 to 46 centimeters) tall, built by the model-maker Marcel Delgado on metal armatures padded with cotton and latex and covered with rabbit fur.","Every time an animator touched Kong to move him for the next frame, the fur got pressed in slightly different directions. On screen, this makes Kong's fur ripple and shimmer, as if wind were blowing through it. It was an accident, but many viewers thought it made him seem more alive. For close-ups, the team also built a giant mechanical head and shoulders, operated from inside by several people, and a huge hand that could hold the actress Fay Wray.","The real magic was in combining things. O'Brien's team placed tiny model jungles in front of layered glass paintings to create depth. They used rear projection, shining a film onto a screen behind the actors, so Fay Wray could react to a dinosaur fight that was really happening on a screen behind her. They even built miniature rear-projection screens into the model sets, so a tiny moving image of a real actor appeared among the models. The stop-motion animation alone took about 55 weeks, and the animators had to keep the lighting exactly the same from one frame to the next, because even a small change would show up as flicker on screen."]},{"heading":"Disney's Folly: Depth, Color and Snow White","paragraphs":["After Mickey's success, the Disney studio kept experimenting. Its Silly Symphonies series let animators try new ideas set to music. In 1932 Flowers and Trees became the first cartoon made in full three-color Technicolor, and it won the very first Academy Award for an animated short. The Three Little Pigs in 1933 showed that cartoon characters could have truly different personalities, even when they looked alike. But Walt Disney dreamed of something much bigger: a cartoon as long as a regular feature film, one that could make people laugh, feel scared and even cry.","Many people in Hollywood thought this was a terrible idea. Who would sit through an 80-minute cartoon? Some called the project Disney's Folly. Disney chose the fairy tale Snow White, and work on it lasted several years, from the mid-1930s until the end of 1937. Hundreds of artists worked on it. Live-action reference films were shot of a young dancer, Marge Champion (then Marjorie Belcher), performing Snow White's movements, so animators could study how a real person walks, turns and dances. A young trained singer, Adriana Caselotti, gave Snow White her high, sweet voice.","One big problem was depth. When a cartoon camera moves toward a flat drawing, everything gets bigger at the same rate, like zooming into a photo. That looks flat and fake. In real life, when you walk through a forest, nearby trees slide past quickly while faraway mountains barely move. To capture this, Disney's engineer William Garity built the multiplane camera, completed in early 1937. It was a tall machine with a camera at the top pointing down through as many as seven layers of artwork painted on glass, each layer at a different distance and each able to move separately. Foreground branches could slide past fast, the middle of the scene a little slower, and the distant background slowest of all, just as in real vision.","Disney tested the multiplane camera on a short called The Old Mill (1937), in which a windmill and its animals ride out a thunderstorm. It won an Academy Award. Then the camera was put to work on Snow White, giving its forests and cottage a feeling of real depth. Ub Iwerks, who had left Disney for a while, had built his own multiplane device in 1933, and Lotte Reiniger had used layered glass years earlier, but Disney's version was the most advanced.","The cost kept climbing until it reached about 1.5 million dollars, a huge amount for the time. Disney had to borrow money from the Bank of America, and he had to show the bankers an unfinished version of the film to persuade them. Finally, on 21 December 1937, Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs premiered at the Carthay Circle Theatre in Los Angeles, with many Hollywood stars in the audience. Reports from the night say some of them cried when Snow White seemed to have died.","Snow White was the first full-length cel-animated feature in color with synchronized sound made in the United States, but remember that it was not the first animated feature ever. It became the highest-earning film of 1938, and Disney received a special Academy Award: one full-sized Oscar statuette and seven little ones, one for each dwarf, presented by the child star Shirley Temple. Songs like Heigh-Ho and Someday My Prince Will Come became classics. The Folly paid for Disney's new studio in Burbank.","Other studios followed. The Fleischers released their own feature, Gulliver's Travels, in December 1939, and in 1940 Disney released Pinocchio and Fantasia, which paired animation with classical music played by an orchestra conducted by Leopold Stokowski. In less than 50 years, moving drawings had gone from Reynaud hand-cranking painted squares in a wax museum to hundreds of artists building whole worlds of color, sound and depth.","Yet all this magic still belonged to big studios with giant cameras, painted glass and armies of artists. Outside the cinema, something just as important was happening in ordinary homes, schools and streets. Cameras were getting smaller, cheaper and easier. Soon a schoolteacher, a reporter, a farmer or a child would be able to carry a camera everywhere and catch real life as it happened. The 20th century was about to put a camera into almost everyone's hands."]}],"deepDive":"Who deserves the title of first animated film? It depends on how you define animation and film. Émile Reynaud's Théâtre Optique (1892) projected hand-painted moving images to paying audiences, but it used no camera and no photographic film, so some historians put it in a separate category of pre-cinema projection. J. Stuart Blackton's Humorous Phases of Funny Faces (1906) is often called the first animated cartoon on standard film, yet much of it is cutout animation rather than new drawings for each frame. Émile Cohl's Fantasmagorie (1908) is frequently named the first fully hand-drawn animated film, because nearly every frame is a new drawing. Even earlier, a few short experiments, such as some made for toy projectors in Germany, may qualify, and their exact dates are debated. The honest answer is that animation grew from many people's experiments at almost the same time. The arithmetic of animation explains why studios became factories. At 24 frames per second, one minute of film needs 1,440 frames. Animated on twos, that is still 720 drawings per minute, and an 80-minute feature needs over 57,000 drawings for a single character, before counting extra layers for other characters, effects like rain, and the many rough sketches that were thrown away. That is why the cel system, with backgrounds painted once and characters split across separate sheets, was such a big economic invention, and why the cel patents of Earl Hurd and his business partner, the studio owner John Randolph Bray, mattered so much in the 1910s and 1920s. Historians also discuss the hidden workforce. Disney's Ink and Paint department employed many women who mixed custom paints and traced animators' lines with great precision, yet animators, almost all men at the time, received most of the credit. Similarly, Ub Iwerks is now recognized as doing much of the drawing that created Mickey's first films, even though Walt Disney became the public face. Finally, there is the question of firsts in sound: the Fleischers' Song Car-Tunes and Dinner Time came before Steamboat Willie, but Steamboat Willie's careful planning of music and action on a frame-by-frame beat set the model for the cartoon soundtracks that followed.","funFact":"King Kong, the giant ape who climbs the Empire State Building, was mostly played by models only about 14 to 18 inches (36 to 46 centimeters) tall, covered in rabbit fur.","check":{"q":"Why was Steamboat Willie (1928) so famous, even though it was not the first sound cartoon?","a":"Its music and sound effects were tightly synchronized with the action","wrong":["It was the first cartoon ever made","It was the first cartoon in full color","It was the first feature-length cartoon"]},"sources":[{"title":"Théâtre Optique","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Théâtre_Optique"},{"title":"Fantasmagorie (1908 film)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantasmagorie_(1908_film)"},{"title":"Gertie the Dinosaur","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gertie_the_Dinosaur"},{"title":"Steamboat Willie","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steamboat_Willie"},{"title":"The Adventures of Prince Achmed","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Prince_Achmed"},{"title":"El Apóstol","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Apóstol"},{"title":"King Kong (1933 film)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_Kong_(1933_film)"},{"title":"Multiplane camera","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplane_camera"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/15-1.jpg","alt":"Jules Chéret's 1892 poster for Émile Reynaud's Pantomimes Lumineuses at the Musée Grévin in Paris.","file":"Musée Grévin, Pantomimes Lumineuses, Théâtre optique de E. Reynaud, musique de Gaston Paulin MET DP358281.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mus%C3%A9e_Gr%C3%A9vin,_Pantomimes_Lumineuses,_Th%C3%A9%C3%A2tre_optique_de_E._Reynaud,_musique_de_Gaston_Paulin_MET_DP358281.jpg","author":"Jules Chéret","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/15-2.jpg","alt":"A hand-drawn frame from Winsor McCay's Gertie the Dinosaur (1914): Gertie bends down toward a boulder.","file":"Gertie the Dinosaur and a boulder.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gertie_the_Dinosaur_and_a_boulder.jpg","author":"Winsor McCay","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/15-3.jpg","alt":"Disney's tall multiplane camera, built in 1937 for Snow White, with the camera on top and artwork levels below.","file":"Disney 1937 multiplane camera.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Disney_1937_multiplane_camera.jpg","author":"HarshLight from San Jose, CA, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4342},{"n":16,"title":"Cameras for Everyone, Everywhere","when":"1913 – 1990s","summary":"In the 1900s, cameras shrank until they fit in a pocket, flashes lit up dark rooms, and a new camera could print a photo in about a minute. Photographers used pictures to help stop children working in factories, and astronauts photographed the whole Earth from space.","sections":[{"heading":"A Camera Small Enough for a Coat Pocket","paragraphs":["At the start of the 1900s, serious cameras were still big. Many photographers used wooden or metal cameras with glass plates or large sheets of film, often on a tripod. Even folding cameras for amateurs were bulky. If you wanted to photograph a street scene quietly, without everyone turning to stare, you had a problem.","In the small German town of Wetzlar, an engineer named Oskar Barnack worked for Ernst Leitz, a company famous for microscopes. Barnack loved hiking and photography, and the story often told is that he wanted a camera light enough to carry on long walks. Around 1913 to 1914 he built a small metal prototype, now called the Ur-Leica, meaning the original Leica. His clever idea was to borrow film from the movie industry. Cinema film was 35 millimeters wide, with sprocket holes along both edges, and it was made in huge quantities, so it was cheap and easy to get.","In a movie camera, each frame sits across the film strip. Barnack turned it sideways, running the film horizontally and making each picture twice as big as a movie frame: 24 by 36 millimeters. That size, with a shape of 2 by 3, is still called full frame by photographers today, even on digital cameras. A single roll could take dozens of pictures before reloading. The tiny negative was then enlarged onto paper to make a bigger print.","World War I and hard economic times delayed things, but in 1924 the company boss, Ernst Leitz II, decided to manufacture Barnack's camera anyway. It was shown to the public at the Leipzig Spring Fair in 1925 as the Leica, a name made from the first letters of LEItz and CAmera. Its sharp little lens and quiet shutter changed what photography could be.","With a Leica in hand, a photographer could move through a crowd, catch a child jumping over a puddle or a worker laughing at a café table, and never be noticed. This is called candid photography, meaning natural pictures of people who are not posing. The French photographer Henri Cartier-Bresson became famous for using a small Leica to catch what he called the decisive moment, the split second when everything in a scene comes together. War photographers like Robert Capa carried small 35mm cameras into battle. Soon other companies built 35mm cameras too, and by the 1960s and 1970s Japanese brands such as Nikon and Canon made 35mm the most popular film size in the world.","There is also a brave story connected to the Leica company. After the Nazis came to power in Germany in 1933, Ernst Leitz II quietly helped many Jewish employees and others leave the country by sending them to work in Leitz sales offices abroad. This effort, later called the Leica Freedom Train, only became widely known after his death."]},{"heading":"Lighting Up the Dark: From Flash Powder to Flashbulbs","paragraphs":["Film needs light. Outdoors on a sunny day, that is easy. But what about inside a dark room, at night, or in a gloomy alley? In the 1860s, some photographers burned ribbons of magnesium metal, which glows with a brilliant white light, but it was smoky and slow. Then, in 1887, two German scientists, Adolf Miethe and Johannes Gaedicke, introduced flash powder, a mixture of powdered magnesium and a chemical called potassium chlorate that burns in an instant.","Using flash powder was dramatic and dangerous. A photographer poured the powder into a metal tray held high above the head, opened the camera shutter, and then lit the powder. There was a blinding bang of light, a cloud of white smoke, and often a smell of burning. Too much powder could burn hands, singe eyebrows or even start fires, and there were serious accidents. But for the first time, photographers could take pictures in places where the sun never reached.","One of the first people to use flash powder to change the world was Jacob Riis, a Danish immigrant who had arrived in New York in 1870 and become a police reporter. Riis knew the city's crowded tenements, cheap apartment buildings where poor families, many of them immigrants, were packed into dark, airless rooms. Words alone did not seem to move wealthy readers. After reading about flash powder in 1887, Riis and some friends began going into the slums at night to photograph what they found. People were sometimes startled awake by the flash. In 1890 he published a book, How the Other Half Lives, which combined his writing with pictures of the slums.","Riis's work helped push New York to clear one of its worst slums, Mulberry Bend, and turn it into a park in 1897, and it impressed Theodore Roosevelt, then the city's police commissioner, who became his friend. Historians today also point out that Riis's writing included unfair stereotypes about some immigrant groups, a reminder that even reformers can carry the prejudices of their time.","Flash powder was finally replaced by something much safer: the flashbulb. Commercial flashbulbs went on sale in Germany in 1929 and spread around the world in the early 1930s. A flashbulb was a glass bulb filled with oxygen and thin metal foil or wire, first magnesium or aluminum, later zirconium. A small electric current set off the metal inside, which burned in a brilliant flash within the sealed glass. No smoke, no loose powder. The bulb could be used only once, and photographers swapped hot used bulbs between shots. In 1965 Kodak introduced the Flashcube, a little cube with four bulbs that rotated after each picture, so family photographers could take four flash shots in a row."]},{"heading":"Doc Edgerton Freezes Time","paragraphs":["A flashbulb's light lasted a fraction of a second, short enough for a portrait but far too long to freeze a hummingbird's wings or a bullet in flight. To stop really fast motion, you need light that switches on and off almost instantly. That was the specialty of Harold Edgerton, an engineer at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) in Cambridge, Massachusetts, whom students nicknamed Doc and later Papa Flash.","Edgerton grew up in Nebraska and arrived at MIT as a graduate student in the 1920s. He was studying electric motors and wanted to see how their spinning parts behaved. In 1931 he developed a new kind of electronic flash: a glass tube filled with gas that, when a big jolt of stored electricity passed through it, lit up in an extremely short, bright flash. Unlike a flashbulb, it could flash again and again, very quickly and very precisely. A device that flashes rapidly like this is called a stroboscope, or strobe.","With his strobe, Edgerton could make a flash lasting only a tiny fraction of a thousandth of a second, and for some special pictures less than a millionth of a second. Suddenly, things too fast for the human eye could be seen frozen in a photograph. He photographed a hummingbird in flight in 1936, its wings sharp instead of blurred. He photographed a golf club smashing into a ball, flattening it for an instant. His famous picture Milk Drop Coronet (1957) shows a drop of milk splashing into a thin layer of milk and bouncing up in a perfect crown with little beads around the rim. In 1964 he photographed a bullet tearing through an apple, with the fruit exploding on both sides.","Edgerton's strobes were not just for beautiful pictures. They helped engineers study machines, and during World War II his giant flashes helped with nighttime aerial photography. He also helped build special high-speed cameras for studying nuclear tests, worked on underwater cameras and sonar with the ocean explorer Jacques Cousteau, and won an Academy Award for a short film called Quicker'n a Wink (1940). The electronic flash units that clip onto cameras today, and the strobe lights at concerts and on emergency vehicles, all owe a debt to Edgerton's work."]},{"heading":"Pictures That Changed Laws","paragraphs":["Imagine a girl about ten years old, standing barefoot beside a row of enormous spinning machines in a cotton mill, her hair tied back so it will not get caught. She works long hours, and she is so small she can barely reach the threads. In the early 1900s, scenes like this were common in the United States. Children worked in mills, coal mines, canneries, glass factories and fields, and on city streets selling newspapers. Many people knew about it, but few had really looked.","Lewis Hine made them look. Born in Oshkosh, Wisconsin, in 1874, Hine became a teacher at the Ethical Culture School in New York, where he started using a camera with his students. Between 1904 and 1909 he photographed immigrants arriving at Ellis Island, and he came to believe that a photograph could be a powerful tool for making society fairer. He called his kind of work social photography.","From 1908 Hine worked for the National Child Labor Committee, a group campaigning to end child labor. Factory owners did not want photographers inside, so Hine sometimes pretended to be a fire inspector, a postcard seller or a Bible salesman to get through the gate. He was threatened by factory guards and foremen more than once. Hine was careful to collect facts as well as pictures. He wrote down names, ages and working hours, and he is said to have estimated children's heights by noting which button of his own coat or vest they came up to. That way, no one could claim his pictures were exaggerated.","Over the years he traveled tens of thousands of miles and made thousands of photographs: young boys called breaker boys sorting coal in dusty mines, girls in textile mills, children shucking oysters before dawn. His pictures appeared in pamphlets, posters, magazines and exhibitions. They helped build public support for child labor laws. A national law in 1916 was struck down by the Supreme Court, and the fight went on for years, but in 1938 the Fair Labor Standards Act finally set nationwide limits on child labor in the United States. Hine himself did not grow rich. He struggled for work in the Great Depression and died in poverty in 1940, but his photographs are now treasured collections in museums and the Library of Congress."]},{"heading":"Life Magazine and the Migrant Mother","paragraphs":["In the 1930s, printing presses could finally reproduce photographs cheaply and beautifully on large pages, and small 35mm cameras let photographers capture real life as it happened. Together, these made a new kind of journalism possible: photojournalism, telling news stories mostly through pictures.","On 23 November 1936, the American publisher Henry Luce launched Life, a weekly magazine built around photographs. It cost ten cents. The first cover showed a huge concrete dam at Fort Peck, Montana, photographed by Margaret Bourke-White, one of the first women photojournalists to become famous. The first issue sold about 380,000 copies, and within four months Life was selling over a million copies a week. In the years that followed, Life photographers such as Robert Capa, Alfred Eisenstaedt and Gordon Parks brought war, science, sports, famous people and ordinary lives into millions of living rooms. Before television news, Life was how many families saw the world.","The Great Depression, which began in 1929, threw millions of Americans out of work. On top of that, a terrible drought in the 1930s turned parts of the Great Plains into the Dust Bowl. Many farming families packed their belongings and drove to California, hoping to find work picking crops. The US government's Resettlement Administration, later called the Farm Security Administration (FSA), hired photographers to record how these families were living, partly to build support for programs to help them.","One of those photographers was Dorothea Lange, who had run a portrait studio in San Francisco before turning her camera on the streets. In March 1936, driving home after a long trip, she passed a sign for a pea pickers' camp near Nipomo, California. She later wrote that she drove on for miles, then turned around and went back. The pea crop had frozen, so there was little work. In the camp, she found a mother with her children sheltering in a tent. Lange later said she stayed only about ten minutes and made a handful of exposures, moving closer each time.","The best-known picture shows the mother gazing into the distance with a worried, tired face, her hand raised to her chin, two children leaning their heads on her shoulders with their faces turned away, and a baby on her lap. It became known as Migrant Mother, and it is one of the most famous photographs ever taken, a symbol of the hardship of the Great Depression. Lange even retouched the negative to remove the mother's thumb, which had appeared in a corner of the frame.","For decades the woman's name was not widely known. In the 1970s a journalist traced her: she was Florence Owens Thompson, a mother of several children with Cherokee roots, who had worked hard all her life. Her feelings about the picture were complicated. For years she was unhappy that her image had been printed so many times while her family stayed poor, and she said Lange had not asked her name. Later, her children came to speak more warmly about the photograph. Her story is a reminder that every famous photo of a person is also a picture of a real human being, with her own life and feelings."]},{"heading":"Why Can't I See It Now? The Polaroid","paragraphs":["In the 1940s, taking a photograph meant waiting. You finished the roll, took it to a shop, and days later you got your prints back, sometimes discovering that someone's eyes were closed or the picture was blurry. Edwin Land thought this could be different, and the idea came from a small child.","Land was a brilliant American inventor. Born in 1909 in Bridgeport, Connecticut, he left Harvard University after his first year to work on his own experiments, and he invented cheap plastic sheets that polarize light, filtering out glare. These were used in sunglasses, camera filters and scientific instruments, and in 1937 he named his company Polaroid. The story he often told goes like this: during a family vacation in Santa Fe, New Mexico, in the 1940s, he took a picture of his three-year-old daughter Jennifer, and she asked why she could not see the photograph right away. Land later said that by the end of that day, he had worked out much of how an instant camera could work, though it took years of research to make it real.","On 21 February 1947, Land demonstrated his instant camera at a meeting of the Optical Society of America in New York, making a portrait of himself that appeared in about a minute. Then, in November 1948, the first Polaroid Land Camera, the Model 95, went on sale at Jordan Marsh, a department store in Boston, for 89.75 dollars. All 57 cameras the store had were sold on the first day.","How did it work? The camera held two rolls: a light-sensitive negative and a positive sheet of paper, plus a small sealed pod of thick, gooey chemicals. After you took a picture, you pulled the film out between two rollers. The rollers squeezed the pod, spreading the jelly evenly between the negative and the paper, like toothpaste squeezed into a sandwich. The chemicals developed the negative and, at the same time, moved silver to the paper to make a positive image. After about a minute, you opened a little door and peeled the finished picture away. The first prints were brownish, a color called sepia. Black-and-white prints followed, then Polacolor color film in 1963.","In 1972 Polaroid launched the SX-70, a folding camera that pushed out a print which developed by itself in front of your eyes, with no peeling at all. Instant cameras became party favorites, and many people loved watching a picture slowly appear. Land, who held 535 patents by the time he retired as Polaroid's chairman in 1982, called it his crowning achievement. Polaroid struggled in the digital age and stopped making film in 2008, but a group of fans called the Impossible Project rented part of an old Polaroid factory in the Netherlands and, from 2010, started making instant film again, and today instant cameras from Polaroid and Fujifilm's Instax line are popular once more."]},{"heading":"Home Movies and Easy-Load Cameras","paragraphs":["Ordinary families did not only want still photos. They wanted moving pictures of birthday parties, first steps and summer holidays. The problem was that professional 35mm movie film was expensive, and it was made of nitrate, which could catch fire easily. That was not something to keep in a family cupboard.","In 1923 Kodak introduced 16mm film for amateurs, along with the Ciné-Kodak camera and the Kodascope projector. The first outfit cost 335 dollars, a lot of money then, but 16mm film used a much safer acetate base that did not burn like nitrate. It also used reversal film, which turns directly into a positive picture after processing, so no separate print was needed. Wealthy families, schools, scientists and explorers loved it, and later TV news crews and even armies used 16mm for its light, portable cameras.","To make home movies cheaper, Kodak introduced 8mm film in 1932. It was a clever trick: the film was really 16mm wide, but the camera exposed pictures down only one half of the strip. Then you opened the camera, flipped the spool over, and filmed down the other half. After processing, the lab slit the film down the middle and joined the two halves into one long 8mm strip. Each frame was tiny, but it was good enough for a family screen, and it used much less film.","Threading film into a camera in the right way was fiddly, and mistakes could ruin a reel. So in 1965 Kodak launched Super 8, which came in a plastic cartridge you simply dropped into the camera. Its sprocket holes were smaller, leaving more room for a bigger picture. A cartridge lasted about three minutes of filming. From 1973, some Super 8 cameras could record sound too. Millions of families filmed their lives in Super 8 until video camcorders replaced it in the 1980s, and several famous film directors made their first movies on 8mm or Super 8 as children.","Still cameras got the same easy treatment. In 1963 Kodak introduced the Instamatic, which used a drop-in film cartridge called 126. The first models cost about 16 dollars, and more than 50 million Instamatics were made by 1970. In 1972 the smaller Pocket Instamatic, using 110 film, followed. Later came automatic point-and-shoot 35mm cameras that focused and set the exposure for you, and from the mid-1980s single-use cameras, cardboard boxes with film inside, that you could buy at a gas station and send back whole for developing. By the 1990s, taking pictures was something almost every family did without thinking twice."]},{"heading":"Earth in the Viewfinder","paragraphs":["The most important home photograph of all may have been taken about 380,000 kilometers from home. On 24 December 1968, three NASA astronauts, Frank Borman, Jim Lovell and Bill Anders, were orbiting the Moon in Apollo 8, the first time humans had ever traveled there. Their job included photographing the Moon's surface to help plan future landings. Then, as their spacecraft came around the Moon, Anders saw something nobody had seen with their own eyes before: Earth, blue and white, rising over the gray lunar horizon.","The crew scrambled for cameras. Anders first took a black-and-white picture, then asked for a color film magazine and took more. He used a modified Hasselblad camera, a Swedish medium-format camera, loaded with special Kodak Ektachrome color film. One of those color photographs, catalogued by NASA as AS08-14-2383, became known as Earthrise. In the original frame, Earth is to the side of the Moon, because in space there is no real up or down; the published version was rotated so that Earth seems to rise above the horizon, just as the Moon rises at home.","Earthrise showed our whole planet as small, beautiful and fragile, floating alone in the darkness of space. Anders later said the crew had gone to explore the Moon, and the most important thing they discovered was Earth. The nature photographer Galen Rowell called it the most influential environmental photograph ever taken. It appeared on a US postage stamp in 1969, and it is often credited with helping inspire the environmental movement, including the first Earth Day on 22 April 1970.","Four years later came another famous view. On 7 December 1972, the crew of Apollo 17, the last crewed mission to the Moon so far, photographed Earth from about 29,000 kilometers away as they headed out. Because the Sun was shining from behind them, almost the whole planet was lit up, like a full moon. The photograph, called The Blue Marble, shows Africa, the Arabian Peninsula, Madagascar and the ice of Antarctica, with swirling white clouds. NASA credits the whole crew, Eugene Cernan, Ronald Evans and Harrison Schmitt, though evidence suggests Schmitt probably pressed the shutter. It became one of the most widely shared images in history.","Think how far photography had come. A century earlier, photographers hauled glass plates and darkroom wagons across battlefields. Now a camera only a little larger than a lunchbox had shown all of humanity what our home looks like from space.","By the end of the 20th century, cameras were everywhere: in handbags, on spacecraft, at birthday parties and in newsrooms. People trusted photographs. A picture in a newspaper or a family album felt like proof that something had really happened, just as it looked. But that trust had a weak spot. Ever since the 1800s, a few clever people had been discovering how easily a photograph could be staged, cut, painted over or combined with another, and how readily people would believe it. The next part asks a tricky question: can a picture lie?"]}],"deepDive":"Many of the most influential photographs of the 20th century raise hard questions historians still discuss. Take Migrant Mother. Dorothea Lange's field notes and later accounts described the family as having sold their car tires to buy food and living on frozen vegetables and birds the children caught. Florence Owens Thompson's family later disputed parts of this; one of her sons said they had no tires to sell. Historians also note that the photo was carefully chosen and retouched (the thumb in the corner was removed), and that the FSA's photography project, led by Roy Stryker, selected images partly to win public support for government aid. None of this makes the picture fake, but it shows that documentary photographs are made by people with goals, and that the subjects often had little say in how their faces were used. Something similar applies to reformers like Jacob Riis, whose flash photos exposed real suffering but whose captions sometimes stereotyped the very people he photographed. On the technical side, the shift to 35mm involved a real trade-off. A 24 by 36 millimeter negative has far less area than a 4 by 5 inch sheet of film (about 864 square millimeters versus about 12,900), so enlargements show more grain and less fine detail. Photographers accepted that loss in exchange for speed, quietness and the ability to shoot dozens of frames quickly, and lens and film improvements steadily narrowed the gap. Flash technology also illustrates how speed is measured. A typical flashbulb burns for very roughly a fiftieth of a second, while Edgerton's electronic flashes could be shorter than a microsecond. Because motion blur depends on how far an object moves while the light is on, a bullet travelling hundreds of meters per second moves less than a millimeter during a sub-microsecond flash, which is why it appears frozen. Finally, the space photos remind us that crediting a photograph can be complicated: Frank Borman at first said he had taken the black-and-white Earthrise frame before agreeing that Anders took all of them, and NASA still credits The Blue Marble to the whole Apollo 17 crew.","funFact":"All 57 Polaroid Land Cameras that the Jordan Marsh department store in Boston had for the first day of sale in November 1948 sold out that same day.","check":{"q":"What simple idea let Oskar Barnack make the Leica camera so small?","a":"He used 35mm movie film, running it sideways to make 24 x 36 mm pictures","wrong":["He used tiny glass plates","He used a digital sensor","He removed the lens completely"]},"sources":[{"title":"Leica Camera","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leica_Camera"},{"title":"Flash (photography)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flash_(photography)"},{"title":"Harold Eugene Edgerton","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harold_Eugene_Edgerton"},{"title":"Lewis Hine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewis_Hine"},{"title":"Migrant Mother","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Migrant_Mother"},{"title":"Edwin H. Land","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edwin_H._Land"},{"title":"Super 8 film","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_8_film"},{"title":"Earthrise","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earthrise"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/16-1.jpg","alt":"A Leica I from 1927 with its Elmar 50 mm lens: a small camera that used 35 mm movie film.","file":"LEI0060 186 Leica I Sn.5193 1927 Originalzustand Front-2 FS-15.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LEI0060_186_Leica_I_Sn.5193_1927_Originalzustand_Front-2_FS-15.jpg","author":"Kameraprojekt Graz 2015","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/16-2.jpg","alt":"\"Migrant Mother\" (1936): Dorothea Lange's photo of Florence Owens Thompson and her children in Nipomo, California.","file":"Lange-MigrantMother02.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lange-MigrantMother02.jpg","author":"Dorothea Lange","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/16-3.jpg","alt":"Earthrise: Bill Anders photographed Earth rising over the Moon from Apollo 8 on 24 December 1968.","file":"NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg","author":"NASA/Bill Anders","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3967},{"n":17,"title":"Truth, Tricks and Trouble","when":"1840 – today","summary":"People used to say the camera never lies, but people behind cameras sometimes do. In this part you will meet ghost photos, paper fairies and leaders who made enemies vanish from pictures. You will also see how cameras raised hard questions about privacy, safety and fairness, and how honest photos helped make the world better.","sections":[{"heading":"Ghosts in the Darkroom","paragraphs":["Imagine walking into a photography studio in Boston in the early 1860s. The country is at war, and many families have lost someone. A photographer named William H. Mumler makes you an offer: sit for your portrait, and the camera may also capture the spirit of a loved one who has died. A few days later you collect your print. There you are, sitting stiffly in a chair, and floating behind you is a pale, misty face. For a grieving person, that picture could feel like a miracle.","Mumler (1832 to 1884) was the most famous 'spirit photographer' of his time. According to his own story, his first ghost picture happened by accident. He took a self-portrait and, when he developed it, he said a faint figure appeared beside him, which he claimed was a cousin who had died 12 years earlier. Whether that first image was a mistake or a plan, Mumler soon realized that people would pay well for portraits with ghosts in them.","Today we know how such pictures could be made. Remember from earlier parts that a glass negative records whatever light reaches it. If a photographer exposes the same plate twice, once with a person dressed up as a pale 'spirit' and once with the paying customer, both images end up layered on top of each other. This is called a double exposure. A photographer could also slip in a prepared plate that already carried a faint image. Because most customers had no idea how photography worked, the results looked mysterious.","Mumler's most famous picture appears to show Mary Todd Lincoln, the widow of President Abraham Lincoln, with the shadowy figure of her husband standing behind her, his hands resting on her shoulders. Historians give different dates for it, from about 1869 to the early 1870s. Most experts consider it a double exposure, but it became one of the best-known spirit photographs ever made.","Not everyone was fooled. Some customers recognized the 'ghosts' in their pictures as people who were still very much alive. In April 1869, Mumler was put on trial in New York for fraud. One of the witnesses against him was the famous showman P. T. Barnum, a man who knew a lot about entertaining tricks. Barnum argued that Mumler was taking advantage of grieving people. To prove how easy it was, Barnum had another photographer, Abraham Bogardus, make a picture of Barnum himself with the 'ghost' of Abraham Lincoln. The fake was shown in court.","Surprisingly, Mumler was found not guilty. The prosecution could not prove beyond doubt exactly how he had faked his pictures. Even so, his story taught an early lesson that still matters: a photograph can look completely real and still be a trick. Spirit photography stayed popular for decades, especially after the huge losses of the First World War, when many families again longed for comfort."]},{"heading":"Fairies at the Bottom of the Garden","paragraphs":["In 1917, in the village of Cottingley near Bradford in the north of England, two cousins spent their days playing by a small stream, which locals call a beck, at the bottom of the garden. Elsie Wright was 16, and Frances Griffiths was 9. Frances kept coming home with wet feet, and when the grown-ups scolded her, the girls said they had been playing with fairies.","Elsie borrowed her father Arthur's camera, a small plate camera called a Midg, and the girls went down to the beck. When Arthur, an amateur photographer, developed the plate, he saw Frances leaning on her hand while a group of tiny winged fairies danced in front of her. A later photo showed Elsie holding out her hand to a small gnome. Arthur suspected the fairies were paper cut-outs and would not lend the camera again. Elsie's mother, Polly, was more curious.","In 1919, Polly took the photos to a meeting of the Theosophical Society, a group interested in spiritual ideas. A member named Edward Gardner became excited and began showing the pictures at his lectures. Experts gave mixed opinions. A photographer named Harold Snelling said he could find no sign the negatives had been tampered with. Kodak declined to say the pictures were genuine, and the Ilford company reported some evidence of faking.","Then the story reached one of the most famous writers in the world: Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, the creator of Sherlock Holmes. Isn't it strange that the man who invented the world's most logical detective was fooled? Conan Doyle believed strongly in spiritualism, the idea that the living can contact spirits, and he hoped that if people accepted fairies, they might be more open to other unseen things. In December 1920, he published the photos in The Strand Magazine. To protect the girls, the article called them 'Alice' and 'Iris'.","Earlier, in the summer of 1920, Gardner gave the cousins two cameras and 24 plates that he had secretly marked, so that no one could swap them. The girls produced three more fairy photos that summer, bringing the total to five. The pictures caused a sensation, and arguments about them carried on for more than 60 years.","Finally, in 1983, the cousins, now elderly women, admitted the truth in a magazine called The Unexplained. Elsie had copied fairy drawings from a popular children's book, Princess Mary's Gift Book, cut them out of cardboard, and held them up with hatpins. One mystery remained: Frances always insisted that the fifth photo was real, while Elsie said it was faked, and both claimed to have taken it. Both women also said, to the end, that they really had seen fairies by the beck.","Why did so many adults believe two girls with paper cut-outs? Partly because they wanted to. After years of war, the idea of a magical world felt hopeful. And partly because many people trusted photographs more than their own common sense. The prints and the cameras are kept today at the National Science and Media Museum in Bradford, a reminder of how a simple trick can fool even a very clever person."]},{"heading":"Scissors, Paint and Vanishing People","paragraphs":["Some people think photo editing began with computers. In fact, it is nearly as old as photography itself. Back in 1840, Hippolyte Bayard staged his own fake death in a photo to complain about not getting credit for his invention, as you read in Part 5. In 1857, the Swedish-born photographer Oscar Rejlander created a large artwork called The Two Ways of Life by combining about 30 separate negatives into one print, like a jigsaw puzzle. A well-known portrait of Abraham Lincoln from the 1860s turns out to be Lincoln's head attached to the body of another politician, John C. Calhoun.","Even war photos were sometimes arranged. In 1863, after the Battle of Gettysburg, Alexander Gardner's team photographed a fallen soldier lying among rocks, in a scene titled Home of a Rebel Sharpshooter. More than a hundred years later, historian William Frassanito studied the landscape closely and argued that the same body appears in another photo taken some distance away, so it seems the photographers moved it to make a more dramatic picture. Early photographers often did not think of this as lying. They thought of themselves as making a powerful picture, not a scientific record.","The most chilling photo editing happened in the Soviet Union under Joseph Stalin, who ruled from the 1920s until 1953. Stalin had many former allies arrested and killed. It was not enough for them to disappear from life; they also had to disappear from history. Workers in photo departments used airbrushes, tiny paint sprayers, along with retouching paint, scalpels and careful rephotography to remove people from pictures.","The best-known example involves Nikolai Yezhov. He was the head of the Soviet secret police during the worst years of Stalin's purges, a time when huge numbers of people were arrested. In one photo, Yezhov walks beside Stalin along the bank of the Moscow-Volga Canal. Then Yezhov himself fell from power. He was arrested in 1939 and executed in 1940. In later versions of the photograph, he is simply gone. Where he once stood, there is only water and stone, as if Stalin had been strolling alone with his other companions.","Yezhov was not the only one. A famous photo shows Vladimir Lenin speaking to soldiers in Moscow on 5 May 1920, with Leon Trotsky and Lev Kamenev standing on the steps beside the platform. After Trotsky and Kamenev fell out of favor, they were painted out of the picture. Even a cosmonaut trainee named Valentin Bondarenko, who died in a training accident in 1961, was removed from group photos, and the public did not learn he had existed until 1986. The British writer and designer David King collected many before-and-after examples in a book called The Commissar Vanishes (1997).","Moving pictures could be used to shape people's beliefs, too. Films made to persuade people, often by governments, are called propaganda films. In the 1930s, Nazi Germany used carefully staged films such as Leni Riefenstahl's Triumph of the Will (1935) to make its leader look all-powerful and its huge rallies look magnificent. Many countries, including Britain and the United States, made propaganda films during the Second World War as well. The lesson for viewers was the same as for photos: a film shows what its makers chose to film, frame and keep."]},{"heading":"Whose Picture Is It, Anyway?","paragraphs":["As cameras became smaller and faster, a new worry appeared: being photographed without saying yes. You may remember from Part 9 that in 1890 two American lawyers, Samuel Warren and Louis Brandeis, wrote an article called The Right to Privacy, partly because snapshot cameras and gossip newspapers made it easy to publish pictures of people's private lives. That worry has only grown since.","In 1960, the Italian director Federico Fellini made a film called La Dolce Vita, which includes a pushy news photographer named Paparazzo who chases celebrities around Rome. His name became a word. Today, 'paparazzi' (the plural) means photographers who follow famous people to snap candid pictures and sell them to magazines and websites. Some celebrities fought back in court. In the United States, Jacqueline Kennedy Onassis, the widow of President John F. Kennedy, won a court order in the 1970s that kept the photographer Ron Galella a set distance away from her and her children.","The most painful example came in 1997, when Diana, Princess of Wales, died in a car crash in a tunnel in Paris while her driver was speeding away from photographers. Official investigations blamed mainly the driver's speed and his drinking, but the tragedy made many people around the world ask whether chasing someone for a photo had gone too far. Later, some places passed laws to protect people, especially children. In 2013, for example, California passed a law against harassing children because of who their parents are, including to photograph them.","Cameras also began to watch everyone, not only the famous. Closed-circuit television, or CCTV, means cameras that send pictures to a limited set of screens rather than broadcasting to the public. One early system was installed in Germany in 1942 to watch rocket launches from a safe distance. In 1968, the town of Olean, New York, became the first city in the United States to put video cameras along its main shopping street to fight crime. The United Kingdom became famous for its many cameras; estimates of the number range from under 2 million to more than 4 million, depending on how they are counted.","Supporters say such cameras help solve crimes and keep people safe. Critics worry that being watched all the time, especially when cameras can be linked to computers that recognize faces, takes away people's freedom to go about their lives without being tracked. Many countries now have rules about where cameras can point, how long recordings may be kept and who may look at them.","There is one more puzzle: if someone takes a photo of you, who owns it? In many countries, the copyright, meaning the legal right to copy and sell a creative work, usually belongs to the person who pressed the button, not the person in the picture. But laws differ. In some places, using a person's photo in an advertisement without permission is not allowed, and many schools and websites ask parents before sharing pictures of children. A good rule for everyone, at any age: ask before you post a picture of someone else."]},{"heading":"Smoke at the Hogmanay Matinee","paragraphs":["Some of photography's troubles were not about truth at all, but about safety. For decades, movie film was made from cellulose nitrate, the clear, bright plastic you met in Part 11. Nitrate gave sharp, beautiful pictures, but it had a terrible secret. It burns extremely fast, and because the material contains oxygen in its own chemical makeup, a nitrate fire can keep burning even when it is covered up, and can produce thick, poisonous smoke.","On the afternoon of 31 December 1929, the last day of the year, which Scots call Hogmanay, hundreds of children packed into the Glen Cinema in Paisley, a town near Glasgow in Scotland, for a special matinee. Estimates say there were between 700 and 1,000 children, from toddlers to about 14 years old. For many of them, a cheap holiday picture show was a special treat.","During the show, a canister of film that had been placed in a small room near the projection area began to give off thick smoke. An official inquiry later concluded that the canister had been set down on a battery and that an electrical short circuit started it smoldering. A young assistant tried to smother it, but the canister came open and smoke drifted into the hall where the children sat. There were no big flames in the auditorium, but someone shouted 'Fire!' and the children rushed for the exits.","What happened next was a crush, not a fire. One of the escape doors at the bottom of a stairway opened inward and had been padlocked shut. As frightened children pushed forward, those at the front could not get out, and more children piled up behind them. Seventy-one children died, mostly from being crushed or unable to breathe, and dozens more were hurt. The cinema's manager was put on trial but was found not guilty. Since 2009, the town has held a memorial service every year.","The disaster changed the rules for cinemas across Britain. The law was strengthened to require more exits, doors that open outward, push bars that can be opened easily in a hurry, and limits on how many people a theater can hold. Next time you see a long metal bar across a cinema or school exit door, you are looking at a safety idea shaped by tragedies like this one. Nitrate film itself was gradually replaced by much safer 'safety film', and by the early 1950s new film stock was no longer made of nitrate.","Photography caused another quieter kind of harm: pollution. Making and developing film used large amounts of chemicals, including silver compounds, acids and strong solvents. For many years, some factories and photo labs released waste into rivers, sewers and the air. Silver can harm fish and other water life, and some solvents are bad for people to breathe. Over the late 20th century, governments tightened environmental laws, and labs learned to capture and recycle silver from their used chemicals. Digital cameras removed most darkroom chemicals, though they bring their own problem: mountains of electronic waste."]},{"heading":"Whose Skin Did the Film See?","paragraphs":["Here is a kind of unfairness that is hard to spot because it is built right into the machines. When you get photos printed from film, the printing lab has to decide how to balance the colors. Too much red, and everyone looks sunburned; too much blue, and everyone looks chilly. To check their machines, labs in North America used reference cards showing a photo of a woman next to colored patches and gray scales. These cards became known as Shirley cards, reportedly after one of the first models to pose for them.","For decades, the woman on the standard card was a light-skinned white woman. Labs adjusted their equipment so that her skin looked 'right'. The film chemistry itself had also been developed with light skin in mind. The result was that people with darker skin were often photographed poorly. Their faces could come out too dark, lacking detail, or with a strange color, especially when they stood next to lighter-skinned friends in the same picture. Many families of color have old school photos where faces look like dark shadows.","The Canadian researcher Lorna Roth, who studied this history, found that some of the first pressure to improve film came from an unexpected direction. Companies that sold chocolate and dark wooden furniture complained that the film could not show the rich differences between dark browns in their advertisements. During the 1970s and 1980s, film makers including Kodak improved their films so they could record a wider range of dark tones. In the mid-1990s, Kodak introduced a new reference card showing three women with different skin colors.","The story of the Shirley card matters because it shows that technology is not automatically neutral. A machine is tested on whatever examples its designers choose. If those examples include only some kinds of people, the machine may work worse for everyone else. The same question comes up today with digital cameras, face-recognition software and AI programs. Engineers now often test camera sensors and software on a wide range of skin tones, and some phone makers advertise cameras designed to show all skin tones accurately. Asking 'who was this tested on?' is one of the smartest questions a young inventor can learn."]},{"heading":"Rules for the Silver Screen","paragraphs":["Movies faced their own battles over what could be shown. In the 1920s, a string of Hollywood scandals and pressure from local censor boards worried the big studios. Rather than have dozens of governments cutting their films, the studios decided to police themselves. Their trade group was led by Will H. Hays, and in 1930 it adopted a set of rules called the Motion Picture Production Code, often known as the Hays Code.","At first many filmmakers ignored it. But starting in 1934, a new office called the Production Code Administration, led by Joseph Breen, enforced it strictly. A film needed the office's seal of approval to be shown in most major theaters. The rules said criminals must always be punished by the end, that authority figures like police and clergy must be treated with respect, and that bad language and many adult subjects were not allowed. Some rules were openly unjust: the code banned showing a romance between a Black person and a white person.","Hollywood also spread hurtful stereotypes, meaning unfair, oversimplified pictures of whole groups of people. Black actors were mostly offered roles as servants or comic characters. When Hattie McDaniel became the first African American to win an Academy Award, in 1940 for Gone with the Wind, she had to sit at a separate table at the ceremony. The Chinese American star Anna May Wong hoped to play the lead in The Good Earth (1937), a story set in China, but the role went to a white actress, Luise Rainer, wearing makeup to look Asian. One biographer believed the code's rules about romance were part of the reason, though other historians are not sure. Native American characters were often shown as nothing but villains in westerns.","These things were challenged from the start. Groups such as the NAACP, the oldest civil rights organization in the United States, protested racist films. Independent Black filmmakers such as Oscar Micheaux made dozens of films from 1919 onward with Black casts telling their own stories. Over time, actors, writers and audiences pushed for fairer and more truthful portrayals, and the work continues today.","The code itself slowly lost its grip. Television, foreign films and bold directors pushed against it. A 1952 Supreme Court decision said films deserved free speech protection, and the comedy Some Like It Hot was released without a seal in 1959 and was a hit anyway. On 1 November 1968, the code was replaced by a rating system with letters that told audiences, especially parents, what to expect, rather than banning subjects altogether. Today's movie ratings come from that idea."]},{"heading":"When Pictures Tell the Truth","paragraphs":["After all these tricks and troubles, you might wonder whether we should trust photos at all. The answer is yes, but wisely. Honest photographs have changed the world for the better again and again.","You met Lewis Hine in Part 16. His pictures of children working in mills and mines gave Americans evidence they could not ignore and helped support laws against child labor. In 1955, after a 14-year-old Black boy named Emmett Till was murdered in Mississippi, his mother, Mamie Till-Mobley, chose to let the world see what had been done to her son. Photographs published in Jet magazine shocked many Americans and helped energize the civil rights movement. Photos of polluted rivers, starving families and damaged forests have led to laws, rescues and donations.","Photos are also the backbone of science. In 1952, a photograph made using X-rays in the lab of Rosalind Franklin, known as Photo 51, helped scientists work out the double-helix shape of DNA. Astronomers use photos to discover planets and measure the universe. Doctors use images to find broken bones and diseases. In all these cases, scientists record exactly how a picture was made, so others can check it.","And photos are memory. A family album lets you see your great-grandparents' faces; a class photo freezes a year of your life. Museums and archives collect photos carefully, recording who took them, when and where. That information, called provenance, is how historians tell a real record from a fake.","So how can you be a good photo detective? Ask who took the picture, and why. Look for the original source. Check whether other photos or witnesses show the same thing. Notice what might be outside the frame. A photo is not truth or lies by itself; it is a piece of evidence, and like all evidence it has to be checked.","Meanwhile, while people argued about fake fairies and studio censors, the movie theater was facing a brand-new rival. In 1948, only about 1 out of every 100 American homes had a television set. By 1955, about three out of four did. A glowing box in the living room was about to change how everyone watched moving pictures."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of photography argue less about whether old photos were manipulated (many were) than about what counts as manipulation. In the 1800s, combination printing, retouching negatives with pencil, and adding clouds from a separate negative were normal studio practice, because early plates could not record bright skies and dark land at the same time. Was a photographer lying by adding clouds, or correcting a technical limitation? Today, news agencies usually draw a line: adjusting brightness and contrast is acceptable, but adding, removing or moving things is not. The debate over staging is just as lively. William Frassanito's work on Gettysburg photos in the 1970s showed that at least one body was moved for Home of a Rebel Sharpshooter, while the authenticity of Robert Capa's 1936 photo The Falling Soldier, from the Spanish Civil War, is still argued over, with some researchers suggesting it was taken away from the actual fighting. Soviet retouching is a different category: it was systematic, ordered by the state and meant to rewrite the record. Its tools, from airbrushes to rephotographing pasted-up prints, were slow, so investigators can often detect them by looking for blurred edges, mismatched grain and impossible shadows. The Cottingley case shows a third problem: experts at the time tested whether the negatives had been altered, and they had not. The fakery happened in front of the lens, not in the darkroom, so a 'genuine' negative could still record a lie. Bias in film, such as the Shirley card standard, is subtler still, because no one has to intend to deceive for a picture to misrepresent someone. Researchers like Lorna Roth argue that technical standards carry the assumptions of the people who set them. Modern answers include forensic software that checks for copied pixels, metadata that records camera settings, and newer 'content credentials' that try to log every edit. None is perfect, which is why historians still rely on the oldest method of all: comparing many independent sources.","funFact":"In the 1860s, the showman P. T. Barnum had a fake 'spirit photograph' made of himself with the ghost of Abraham Lincoln, just to prove in court how easy such pictures were to fake.","check":{"q":"How did the Cottingley cousins make their famous fairy photographs?","a":"With cardboard cut-outs of fairy drawings held up by hatpins","wrong":["By painting fairies onto the glass negatives","By photographing real butterflies","By using a computer to add fairies"]},"sources":[{"title":"Cottingley Fairies","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottingley_Fairies"},{"title":"William H. Mumler","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_H._Mumler"},{"title":"Censorship of images in the Soviet Union","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Censorship_of_images_in_the_Soviet_Union"},{"title":"Glen Cinema disaster","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glen_Cinema_disaster"},{"title":"Hays Code (Motion Picture Production Code)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hays_Code"},{"title":"Shirley card (Color chart)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shirley_card"},{"title":"Paparazzi","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paparazzi"},{"title":"Closed-circuit television","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Closed-circuit_television"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/17-1.jpg","alt":"The third Cottingley Fairies photo (1920): Frances Griffiths with a 'leaping fairy', really a paper cut-out.","file":"Cottingley Fairies 3.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cottingley_Fairies_3.jpg","author":"Elsie Wright","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/17-2.jpg","alt":"William Mumler's faked 'spirit photograph' of Mary Todd Lincoln with a 'ghost' of Abraham Lincoln behind her.","file":"Mumler (Lincoln).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mumler_(Lincoln).jpg","author":"William H. Mumler","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/17-3.jpg","alt":"The original photo of Stalin walking by a canal with Nikolai Yezhov (right), who was later erased from it.","file":"Nikolai Yezhov with Stalin and Molotov along the Volga–Don Canal, original.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikolai_Yezhov_with_Stalin_and_Molotov_along_the_Volga%E2%80%93Don_Canal,_original.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4051},{"n":18,"title":"Screens at Home and Spectacles in Theaters","when":"1950 – 2000","summary":"When television arrived, movie theaters had to fight to keep their audiences. They tried giant curved screens, 3D glasses and films so big they ran sideways through the projector. Meanwhile, tape let people record TV at home, and computers learned to create dinosaurs and talking toys.","sections":[{"heading":"The Box That Emptied the Cinemas","paragraphs":["Picture an American family in 1946. On a Saturday night, going to the movies is what almost everyone does. There is a newsreel, a cartoon, maybe two feature films, all for the price of a ticket. Movies are the biggest entertainment in the country.","Now jump forward ten years. The same family is sitting at home in the living room, watching a small, flickering black-and-white screen set inside a wooden cabinet. In 1948, only about 1 in every 100 American homes had a television. By 1955, about three out of every four did. That is one of the fastest changes in daily life the world had ever seen.","For Hollywood, it felt like an earthquake. By one widely quoted count, movie attendance in the United States fell from about 90 million in 1948 to about 46 million in 1951. Why pay for a ticket, find parking and hire a babysitter, when comedy shows, sports and news came into your house for free? At the same time, a 1948 court case known as the Paramount decision forced the big studios to sell their chains of theaters, which shook up the business even more.","The studios asked themselves a simple question: what can a cinema do that a small TV screen cannot? Their answer was spectacle. Theaters would offer pictures that were bigger, wider, brighter, louder and even three-dimensional. The 1950s became a decade of wild experiments, some brilliant, some short-lived and a few just plain silly. One 1960 film, Scent of Mystery, even pumped smells into the theater in a system called Smell-O-Vision. It did not catch on."]},{"heading":"Wider, Wider, Widest","paragraphs":["On 30 September 1952, an audience at the Broadway Theatre in New York sat down in front of a deeply curved screen. The film, This Is Cinerama, began with an ordinary-sized black-and-white picture. Then the curtains swept open, the screen grew enormous, and the audience found themselves riding at the front of a roller coaster. People gripped their seats as if they were really falling down the drops.","Cinerama was invented by Fred Waller, who had earlier worked on special effects and on a multi-projector system to train aircraft gunners. Cinerama used three cameras filming side by side. In the theater, three projectors, each in its own booth, threw three pictures onto a giant screen curved around the audience so that it filled about 146 degrees of their view, close to the edge of what your eyes can see without turning your head. A seven-track sound system with speakers behind the screen and around the hall, developed by Hazard E. Reeves, made sounds move from one side to the other.","Cinerama was thrilling, but it was difficult. The joins between the three pictures could be seen as faint lines, and things close to the camera could look bent where the pictures met. Only special theaters could afford the equipment. Most Cinerama films were travel adventures that flew over oceans and mountains. Only two told regular stories with actors, including How the West Was Won (1962), after which the three-camera system was dropped.","The 20th Century Fox studio wanted something cheaper that ordinary theaters could install. They found it in an invention from the 1920s by a French scientist, Henri Chrétien, who had designed a special lens called the Hypergonar. Fox named the system CinemaScope. The trick is called anamorphic, from Greek words meaning 'formed again'. When filming, a special lens squeezes a wide scene sideways so it fits onto a normal 35mm frame. On the film itself, everything looks tall and skinny, like a reflection in a funhouse mirror. In the theater, a matching lens on the projector stretches the picture back out to its proper width.","The first CinemaScope film, The Robe, a story set in ancient Rome starring Richard Burton, premiered at the Roxy Theatre in New York on 16 September 1953. Its picture was about two and a half times as wide as it was tall, compared with the nearly square shape of older films and TV screens. The Robe was a huge hit, and soon many studios were making 'Scope' films. Early CinemaScope lenses had a funny flaw: in close-ups, faces could look puffed out sideways, a problem nicknamed 'the mumps'. Later lenses from a company called Panavision fixed this, and Panavision lenses became the standard.","Another widescreen method simply used wider film. Todd-AO, first used for the musical Oklahoma! (1955), ran film 70mm wide, giving a sharper and brighter picture. The shape of today's movie screens, and even of the TV in your living room, comes from this 1950s battle to look wider than television."]},{"heading":"Glasses On: The First 3D Craze","paragraphs":["If wider was good, what about deeper? On 26 November 1952, a film called Bwana Devil opened in Los Angeles. Its makers promised that lions would seem to leap right off the screen. Audiences put on gray cardboard glasses and gasped. The film was not considered very good, but it made a lot of money and started the first big 3D craze in American movies.","How does 3D work? Your two eyes are a few centimeters apart, so each sees the world from a slightly different angle. Your brain combines the two views and works out how far away things are. A 3D film is shot with two cameras placed side by side, like two eyes. In the theater, the two films are projected together through filters that send the light waves vibrating in different directions. This is called polarization. The lenses of your glasses are polarizing filters too, each letting through only one of the pictures, so your left eye sees the left camera's view and your right eye sees the right camera's view. Your brain does the rest.","For a few years, 3D was everywhere. Warner Bros.' horror film House of Wax (1953), starring Vincent Price, was a big success, and many westerns, monster movies and even musicals were made in 3D. But the craze faded quickly. The two projectors had to stay perfectly in step, or the picture became blurry and gave people headaches. The glasses made the picture dimmer. And CinemaScope offered a sense of size without any glasses at all. By the mid-1950s, most 3D films were being shown flat. 3D would return in waves over the following decades, with its biggest comeback arriving in the digital age."]},{"heading":"Pictures on a Ribbon of Magnetic Tape","paragraphs":["Early television had a big problem. Most shows were broadcast live, as they happened. That was fine in New York, but the United States stretches across several time zones. A news program shown at 6:30 in the evening on the East Coast would reach California in the middle of the afternoon. To replay shows later, networks filmed a TV screen with a movie camera, rushed the film through developing, and broadcast it a few hours later. These blurry copies were called kinescopes.","Engineers knew that sound could already be recorded on magnetic tape, a plastic ribbon coated with tiny magnetic particles. Video was much harder, because a TV picture contains vastly more information each second than sound. Pulling tape past a recording head fast enough would need miles of tape. At the Ampex company in California, a small team including Charles Ginsburg and a young engineer named Ray Dolby, who later became famous for Dolby sound, found a clever solution. Instead of moving the tape incredibly fast, they spun the recording heads. Four heads sat on a wheel spinning more than 14,000 times a minute, sweeping across tape 2 inches wide. Each sweep wrote a thin stripe of picture information.","Ampex showed its machine at a broadcasters' convention in Chicago on 14 April 1956. The audience saw a recording played back moments after it was made, and it looked almost as good as live TV. They burst into applause. The machine was huge and cost about $45,000 at the time, yet TV networks lined up to buy it. On 30 November 1956, CBS used videotape to delay its evening news program, Douglas Edwards and the News, for viewers in the West. Within a few years, videotape had changed television, allowing shows to be recorded, edited and repeated.","Home video came next. In 1975, Sony launched Betamax, a video cassette recorder (VCR) for the home. In 1976, the Japanese company JVC launched a rival called VHS. The two kinds of tape did not fit in each other's machines, so shoppers had to pick one. This contest became known as the videotape format war. Betamax often had a slightly sharper picture, but early VHS tapes could record for two hours instead of one, long enough for a whole movie or a football game. Many companies made VHS machines, which helped bring prices down. By 1987, VHS held about 90 percent of the American VCR market, and in 1988 Sony itself started selling VHS machines.","Hollywood studios were at first alarmed that people could copy TV shows and films at home. Universal Studios and Disney sued Sony. In 1984, in a case often called the Betamax case, the US Supreme Court ruled that recording a program at home to watch later was allowed. Soon studios discovered they could make huge sums by selling and renting tapes. Video rental shops appeared in towns everywhere, and a Friday night trip to pick a movie became a family ritual.","Then came the camcorder, a camera and video recorder combined into one machine you could hold. Sony's Betamovie, released in 1983, was one of the first for consumers; it could record but not play back. JVC released smaller VHS-C cameras the same year, and in 1985 Sony introduced the compact Video8 format. For the first time, ordinary families could record long stretches of moving pictures with sound and play them instantly on the TV, with no film to develop. Birthday parties, school plays and baby's first steps were suddenly captured on tape."]},{"heading":"IMAX: A Frame Ten Times Bigger","paragraphs":["While some inventors made screens smaller and more personal, a group of Canadians went in the opposite direction. In 1967, filmmakers Graeme Ferguson, Roman Kroitor and Robert Kerr, together with engineer William C. Shaw, founded a company to build the biggest, sharpest movie system in the world. At first it was called Multiscreen Corporation. They later chose a catchier name: IMAX, short for 'Image Maximum'.","Their first film, Tiger Child, was shown at Expo '70, the world's fair in Osaka, Japan, in 1970. The first permanent IMAX theater opened in May 1971 at the Cinesphere, a dome-shaped building at Ontario Place in Toronto.","The secret of IMAX is the size of each frame. Normal 35mm film runs vertically through the projector, and each frame is a little larger than a postage stamp. IMAX uses film 70mm wide, but turns it sideways. The film runs horizontally, and each frame stretches across 15 of the little sprocket holes along the edge, rather than 4 or 5. The result is a frame about ten times the area of a standard 35mm movie frame. More area means more detail, so the picture stays sharp even on a screen several stories tall.","Moving such big frames smoothly was a serious engineering challenge. Normal projectors yank the film forward with a claw, as you read in Part 11, but that would tear heavy IMAX film. William Shaw adapted an idea from an Australian inventor called the rolling loop, in which the film moves in a gentle wave, and added puffs of compressed air to help each frame settle into place. Each frame is held perfectly flat against the lens by suction for its moment on the screen.","In 1973, a version for dome-shaped theaters, first called OMNIMAX, opened in San Diego, wrapping the picture overhead like a planetarium sky. IMAX became famous for nature and science films: flights through the Grand Canyon, journeys under the sea and even footage filmed by astronauts aboard the Space Shuttle, using IMAX cameras carried into orbit."]},{"heading":"Spaceships on a Robot Arm","paragraphs":["In 1975, a young director named George Lucas had a problem. He was planning a space adventure called Star Wars, full of space battles, and no special effects company could make the shots he imagined. So, on 26 May 1975, he started his own. A team led by John Dykstra set up in a warehouse in Van Nuys, California. They named themselves Industrial Light & Magic, or ILM.","Their most important tool was a camera rig nicknamed the Dykstraflex, built in 1976. It is often called the first digital motion-control camera system. A camera was mounted on a long, motorized arm and track, controlled by a computer. It could move in seven different ways, sliding along, rising, tilting, turning and rolling, while the computer also controlled the focus and the shutter.","Why did that matter? A space battle shot is made by filming many separate things: an X-wing model, a TIE fighter model, glowing engines, a background of stars. Each piece is filmed separately, usually in front of a blue screen so it can be cut out later, and then all the pieces are combined into one picture, a process called compositing. For the pieces to fit together, the camera has to make exactly the same movement every time. A human operator can never repeat a move perfectly. The Dykstraflex could, again and again, as many times as needed. The models themselves barely moved; it was the camera that swooped past them, creating the feeling of high-speed flight.","Star Wars opened on 25 May 1977 and became one of the most successful films ever made. Audiences had never seen spaceships fly like that. Dykstra and two colleagues, Alvah Miller and Jerry Jeffress, who built the electronics, received Academy Awards for the system. ILM went on to create effects for hundreds of films and became one of the most important effects companies in the world.","Notice something about this story: Star Wars mostly still used real models, real cameras and real film. The computer was the camera's brain, not the artist. That was about to change."]},{"heading":"Computers Learn to Draw","paragraphs":["Computer graphics, often called CGI (computer-generated imagery), means pictures created by a computer from mathematical descriptions of shapes, colors and light, rather than photographed from real objects. In the 1970s, computers were slow and huge, and making even a simple image took enormous effort. Early movies used CGI for small things, like screens in the background.","Then, on 9 July 1982, Disney released Tron, directed by Steven Lisberger, about a programmer who is pulled inside a computer world. Four computer graphics companies made about 15 to 20 minutes of computer animation for it, including racing light cycles that zoomed at sharp right angles. Much of the glowing look, however, was made by hand, by filming actors in black-and-white and then coloring the light through the film frame by frame in a technique called backlit animation. Tron was not nominated for a visual effects Oscar. Later, the scientist Ken Perlin, who had worked on it, won a technical Academy Award in 1997 for 'Perlin noise', a method for making computer surfaces look naturally rough, like clouds, marble or wood, which became used all over the film industry.","The same summer, Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan (1982) included a sequence of about a minute showing a dead, rocky planet bursting into life, with fire sweeping across its surface and oceans and forests spreading behind it. It was made by the young computer graphics team at Lucasfilm, the company George Lucas had set up alongside ILM. To make the flames, the team invented a way of drawing thousands of tiny moving points, called a particle system, an idea still used today for fire, smoke, rain, sparks and magic spells in films and video games. Each new trick added a tool to the computer artist's toolbox, and the people who invented those tools would soon become famous in their own right.","In 1985, the film Young Sherlock Holmes included a scene in which a knight made of pieces of a stained-glass window steps out of the window and walks toward a priest. It is often called the first fully computer-generated, realistic character in a feature film. It was created by the computer graphics group at Lucasfilm, George Lucas's company, with animator John Lasseter. The shot lasts only a few seconds, but it showed that a computer could make a believable character move. In 1989, ILM created a watery tentacle that imitated people's faces in The Abyss, and in 1991 a liquid-metal villain called the T-1000 in Terminator 2: Judgment Day.","The biggest leap came with dinosaurs. Steven Spielberg planned Jurassic Park using giant robot dinosaurs made by Stan Winston's team, plus stop-motion style puppets by the animator Phil Tippett for full-body shots. Winston's life-size Tyrannosaurus rex was about 20 feet (6 meters) tall and weighed about 9,000 pounds (4 tonnes). But at ILM, animators Steve Williams and Mark Dippé secretly experimented with computer dinosaurs. When Spielberg saw a test of a computer T. rex walking in daylight, he was so impressed that he switched the full-body shots to CGI.","Tippett, a stop-motion master, reportedly joked, 'I think I'm extinct.' Spielberg liked the line so much he put it into the film's script. Tippett did not lose his job, though. His team helped build the Dinosaur Input Device, a dinosaur-shaped puppet skeleton covered in sensors. When animators posed the puppet, the computer copied the pose into the digital dinosaur, combining old-fashioned animation skill with new technology.","Jurassic Park was released on 11 June 1993. Of its about 15 minutes of dinosaur screen time, roughly 9 minutes were Stan Winston's animatronics and only about 6 minutes were CGI. Yet those 6 minutes changed filmmaking. The film earned well over $900 million in its first run and won three Academy Awards, including for visual effects. After Jurassic Park, filmmakers knew they could put almost anything they could imagine on the screen."]},{"heading":"To Infinity and Beyond: Toy Story","paragraphs":["Remember the Lucasfilm computer group that made the stained-glass knight? In 1986, it was sold to Steve Jobs, the co-founder of Apple, and became an independent company called Pixar. At first Pixar mainly sold an expensive image computer and made TV commercials. But John Lasseter kept making short films to show what computer animation could do. His 1988 short Tin Toy, about a wind-up toy band fleeing a drooling baby, won the 1989 Academy Award for Best Animated Short Film, the first computer-animated film to do so. That success convinced Disney to make a deal with Pixar for a full-length film.","The result was Toy Story, directed by Lasseter, about a cowboy doll named Woody and a space-ranger toy named Buzz Lightyear, voiced by Tom Hanks and Tim Allen. Making it was enormous work. The crew grew to about 110 people, including 27 animators. Every character and set had to be built as a 3D model inside a computer, with controls for every eyebrow, finger and wobble.","Then each frame had to be rendered. Rendering means the computer calculates how light falls on every surface and what color each pixel should be. The finished film has 114,240 frames in 1,561 shots, and rendering them took about 800,000 machine hours on racks of Sun Microsystems computers running day and night. That works out to roughly seven hours of computer time per frame, on average. On a good day, the studio could finish less than 30 seconds of film.","Toy Story opened in the United States on 22 November 1995. It was the first feature-length film made entirely with computer animation, and it became the biggest box-office film of that year in the US. Lasseter received a Special Achievement Academy Award for leading the team. Within a decade, most big animated features were made with computers rather than hand-drawn cels, the method you read about in Part 15.","Look back at the half-century in this part. In 1950, a movie was light shining through a strip of film in a dark room, the same basic idea as in the 1890s. By 2000, people could record their own videos at home, sit in front of screens ten times bigger, and watch worlds that existed only as numbers in a computer. But one thing had not changed: most cameras, from family camcorders to IMAX rigs, still recorded the final picture on film or tape.","Yet the computer revolution was already reaching inside the camera itself. Back in 1975, in a laboratory at Kodak, the company that had put film into everyone's hands, a young engineer had quietly built a strange, toaster-sized camera that used no film at all."]}],"deepDive":"The widescreen wars of the 1950s left a lasting vocabulary: the aspect ratio, which is a picture's width divided by its height. Older films used the Academy ratio of about 1.37 to 1, close to the 4 to 3 shape of old TVs. Most American films then settled on 1.85 to 1, while anamorphic 'Scope' films use about 2.39 to 1. The math of anamorphic lenses is neat: a 2x squeeze lets a frame that is physically about 1.2 times as wide as tall carry an image about 2.4 times as wide as tall. The cost is that each horizontal detail is spread over half the film width, which is why early lenses needed careful design to avoid distortion like 'the mumps'. IMAX takes the opposite approach, adding area instead of squeezing: its 15-perforation 70mm frame, roughly 70 by 48 millimeters, has about ten times the area of a standard 35mm frame, which reduces visible grain when enlarged onto screens over 20 meters wide. Videotape required a different trick. The quantity that matters for recording is 'writing speed', the speed of the head relative to the tape. Quadruplex spun its heads so fast that the effective writing speed was around 1,500 inches per second, even though the tape itself crawled along at 15 inches per second. Home VCRs later used helical scanning, tilting the drum so each head wrote long diagonal stripes, which allowed smaller cassettes. Historians still debate why VHS beat Betamax. The usual explanations are longer recording time, lower prices and JVC's willingness to license VHS to many manufacturers, though some argue Sony's insistence on picture quality simply did not match what buyers wanted. For CGI, the debate is about credit and labels: Tron, Young Sherlock Holmes, The Abyss and Jurassic Park each claim a 'first' with a different definition, and Jurassic Park's success depended as much on animatronics as on computers, a balance many filmmakers still argue was the right one.","funFact":"When stop-motion animator Phil Tippett saw ILM's computer dinosaurs for Jurassic Park, he joked 'I think I'm extinct', and Steven Spielberg liked the line so much he put it in the movie.","check":{"q":"What was the first fully computer-animated feature film, released in November 1995?","a":"Toy Story","wrong":["Tron","Jurassic Park","Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs"]},"sources":[{"title":"Cinerama","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinerama"},{"title":"CinemaScope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CinemaScope"},{"title":"2-inch quadruplex videotape","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2-inch_quadruplex_videotape"},{"title":"Videotape format war","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Videotape_format_war"},{"title":"IMAX","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IMAX"},{"title":"Dykstraflex","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dykstraflex"},{"title":"Jurassic Park (film)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurassic_Park_(film)"},{"title":"Toy Story","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toy_Story"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/18-1.jpg","alt":"An Ampex VR-1000A videotape recorder from the 1950s, one of the first practical video recorders for TV stations.","file":"Ampex VR1000A (serial 329).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ampex_VR1000A_(serial_329).jpg","author":"Karl Baron from Lund, Sweden","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/18-2.jpg","alt":"Huge IMAX 70 mm film running sideways through a projector at the China National Film Museum.","file":"IMAX Film in China National Film Museum 20230407.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IMAX_Film_in_China_National_Film_Museum_20230407.jpg","author":"Steven Sun","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/18-3.jpg","alt":"Rival home video formats: a Betamax cassette (top) next to a bigger VHS cassette (bottom).","file":"VHS vs Betamax size.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VHS_vs_Betamax_size.jpg","author":"Ya, saya inBaliTimur","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3730},{"n":19,"title":"Pictures Made of Numbers","when":"1957 – 2012","summary":"What if a picture was not made of silver at all, but of numbers? In this part, scientists teach computers to see by chopping pictures into tiny squares called pixels. A camera with no film is born, and the mighty Kodak company faces the biggest change in its history.","sections":[{"heading":"A Baby Photo Inside a Giant Computer","paragraphs":["Imagine walking into a laboratory in Washington, D.C., in 1957. The room hums and clicks. Rows of cabinets as tall as grown-ups hold glowing vacuum tubes and tangles of wire. This is SEAC, the Standards Eastern Automatic Computer at the National Bureau of Standards, the United States government lab that is known today as NIST. SEAC was one of the first computers in America that could store its own program, and it filled a whole room. Yet a scientist named Russell Kirsch had a wonderful, slightly crazy idea: he wanted to show this machine a photograph.","The photo Kirsch picked was a small black-and-white snapshot of his son Walden, who was about three months old. It was only about 5 centimeters on each side, roughly the size of a large postage stamp. Kirsch and his team built a scanner around a rotating drum. The photo was wrapped around the drum, and as the drum turned, a light sensor looked at one tiny spot at a time and measured whether that spot was light or dark. Step by step, line by line, the scanner swept across the whole picture.","Each spot became a number that the computer could store. The scan was a grid 176 spots wide and 176 spots tall, which adds up to 30,976 little squares. Today we call those squares pixels, short for 'picture elements'. Each pixel in Kirsch's first scan could only be pure black or pure white, nothing in between, because SEAC stored one bit, a single 0 or 1, for each square. To get shades of gray, the team scanned the picture several times using different settings for what counted as 'dark', and then combined the results.","The finished image looks rough to our eyes. Baby Walden appears as a blocky pattern of squares, a little like a picture made out of square tiles in a bathroom. But it was a giant step. For the first time, a photograph had been turned into pure numbers that a machine could store, copy, change and send. Kirsch's group wanted to know whether computers could someday read and recognize pictures, the way people do. That question led, decades later, to everything from medical scans to the face-unlock on a phone.","In 2003, Life magazine put the little scan of Walden on its list of 100 photographs that changed the world. Late in life, Kirsch said he wished he had not made pixels square, and he experimented with pixels of different shapes that could follow curves more smoothly. Whether or not square was the best choice, the idea stuck. Almost every digital picture you have ever seen is built from a grid of tiny squares, all descended from a baby photo spinning on a drum in 1957."]},{"heading":"Two Scientists, One Blackboard and the CCD","paragraphs":["Kirsch's scanner could turn an existing photo into numbers, but it could not take a new picture of the world by itself. For that, you needed a chip that could catch light directly, the way film does, and turn it into electricity. The chip that did this came out of Bell Labs, the famous research center of the American telephone company, in New Jersey.","In 1969, two physicists there, Willard Boyle and George E. Smith, were thinking about a new kind of computer memory. Other researchers at Bell Labs were excited about 'magnetic bubble' memory, which stored information as tiny magnetic spots that could be pushed along a material. Boyle and Smith wondered whether they could do the same thing with little packets of electric charge on a silicon chip. According to the story they later told, they sketched the basic idea on a blackboard in a short meeting, in about an hour. Their notebook called it 'Charge Bubble Devices'. Soon it got the name it still has today: the charge-coupled device, or CCD.","Here is the clever trick. A CCD is a row, or a grid, of tiny metal squares sitting on a thin insulating layer on top of silicon. Each square acts like a little bucket that can hold electric charge. By changing voltages in a careful rhythm, the chip can make each bucket pass its charge to the next bucket, like a line of firefighters handing buckets of water along a line. At the end of the line, the charge in each bucket is measured, one after another.","The physicists soon realized something important. Silicon is sensitive to light. When light hits it, the energy knocks electrons loose. If you let light fall on a grid of CCD buckets, the brightest spots fill their buckets with the most electrons, and dark spots collect only a few. Then you shift the charges out, measure them, and you have a picture. Their first paper, published in 1970, already listed imaging as one possible use, alongside memory.","Within months, Bell Labs researchers including Gil Amelio and Michael Tompsett were building working devices, and by 1971 they were capturing simple images. The electronics company Fairchild produced the first commercial CCD chips, and by 1974 it made a sensor 100 pixels by 100 pixels. Astronomers fell in love with CCDs almost immediately. A photographic plate turns only a small fraction of the light that hits it into an image, but a good CCD catches a far larger share, so faint stars and galaxies suddenly became easier to record. Within about twenty years, CCDs had replaced photographic plates at most big telescopes.","In 2009, Willard Boyle and George E. Smith shared half of the Nobel Prize in Physics for inventing the CCD. The other half went to Charles Kao for his work on sending light through glass fibers, the technology behind fast internet cables. In a way, that prize celebrated two halves of modern photography: catching light as numbers, and sending those numbers around the world."]},{"heading":"How a Sensor Turns Light into Numbers","paragraphs":["Let's open up a digital camera and see what happens in the tiny fraction of a second when you take a picture. Some parts will sound familiar from the earlier science chapters. Light still enters through a lens, which bends it to make a sharp image. An aperture still controls how wide the opening is, and a shutter, which may be a real moving curtain or simply an electronic timer, still decides how long the light is collected. The big change is what sits where the film used to be: an image sensor.","An image sensor is a flat chip covered in millions of tiny light-catching spots called photosites. Each one becomes one pixel. While the shutter is open, light knocks electrons loose inside each photosite, and those electrons are collected and stored. A photosite in a bright part of the scene, such as a sunny sky, gathers lots of electrons. One in a deep shadow gathers very few. When the exposure ends, the camera measures the charge in every photosite and turns each measurement into a number. A common camera might store each pixel's brightness as a number from 0 to 4,095, or even larger, before simplifying it into the 0 to 255 range used in many picture files.","Notice how similar this is to film. In film, light knocks loose tiny specks of silver inside silver halide crystals to make a hidden latent image. In a sensor, light knocks loose electrons inside silicon. Both are about light carrying energy that changes a material. The difference is that the sensor's 'latent image' is read out as electricity right away, with no developer, fixer or darkroom needed.","Photographers measure sensors in megapixels. One megapixel means one million pixels. A camera with a grid of 4,000 pixels across and 3,000 pixels down has 12 million pixels, or 12 megapixels. More pixels can capture finer detail, but they are not the only thing that matters. Bigger photosites catch more light, which means less 'noise', the grainy speckles you see in dim photos. That is why a large professional camera with fewer, bigger pixels can sometimes beat a phone with more, smaller ones.","There are two main families of sensor chips. The CCD, which you just met, passes charges along like buckets. The other family, called CMOS, was developed into a practical camera sensor in the early 1990s by a team led by engineer Eric Fossum at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California, which wanted small, low-power cameras for spacecraft. In a CMOS 'active-pixel' sensor, every pixel has its own tiny amplifier, so its signal can be read directly instead of being handed down a long line. CMOS chips can be made in the same factories as ordinary computer chips, use much less battery power, and can carry image-processing circuits on the same piece of silicon. Those advantages made them perfect for small gadgets, and today nearly every phone camera uses a CMOS sensor.","There is a puzzle hiding here. A photosite measures how much light hits it, but not what color the light is. On its own, a sensor would only make black-and-white pictures, just like Kirsch's scan. So how do digital cameras see color? The answer came from a quiet scientist at the very company that would later struggle most with the digital age: Kodak."]},{"heading":"Bryce Bayer's Checkerboard of Color","paragraphs":["Bryce Bayer worked at Eastman Kodak's research labs in Rochester, New York, the same city where George Eastman had once coated glass plates in his mother's kitchen. In the 1970s, Bayer was thinking about how a single electronic chip could record color. One idea was to use three separate sensors, one each for red, green and blue light, with a prism to split the light, a little like Technicolor's three-strip movie camera. But that would be big, heavy and expensive. Bayer wanted something simpler.","His solution was to lay a tiny mosaic of colored filters directly on top of the sensor, one filter over each photosite. Some photosites would sit under a red filter, so they only measured red light. Others sat under blue filters. And the rest sat under green. Bayer's patent, called simply 'Color imaging array', was issued on 20 July 1976. The pattern he chose repeats in little blocks of four: one red, one blue and two green. Half of all the pixels on the chip are green.","Why so much green? Because of your eyes. As you learned in the chapter on color, your retina has three kinds of cone cells, roughly tuned to red, green and blue light. Our sense of brightness and fine detail depends most heavily on the green-sensitive part of our vision, so in daylight we notice sharpness in green far more than in blue. By giving green twice as many pixels, Bayer made sure the camera captured detail where our eyes care about it most. It was engineering designed around human biology.","But wait. If each pixel only measures one color, where do the other two colors come from? The camera works them out by looking at the neighbors. A green pixel knows its own green value, but it borrows red and blue values from the red and blue pixels beside it and makes a smart estimate. This guessing game is called demosaicing, because it turns the mosaic of single colors back into a full-color picture. Early methods sometimes produced odd colored fringes along sharp edges, like the edge of a black letter on white paper. Better methods learned to estimate along edges rather than across them, which reduced those errors.","Today, the Bayer pattern, or something closely based on it, sits on top of the sensors in most digital cameras and phones on Earth. Billions of devices use the checkerboard that one Kodak scientist designed. Bryce Bayer died in 2012, the same year his old company filed for bankruptcy, a sad coincidence that shows how strangely the story of Kodak and digital photography is tangled together."]},{"heading":"The Toaster-Sized Camera of 1975","paragraphs":["In 1975, a young engineer in his mid-twenties named Steven Sasson had recently joined Kodak. His supervisor, Gareth Lloyd, gave him what seemed like a small side project: see whether you can build a camera using one of those new CCD chips from Fairchild. Nobody had a design to copy. Sasson had to gather parts and figure it out himself.","He built a strange-looking machine. It used a lens taken from a Super 8 home-movie camera, the 100-by-100-pixel Fairchild CCD, a bundle of circuit boards, a battery pack, and a portable digital cassette recorder. Altogether it weighed about 3.6 kilograms, roughly 8 pounds, about as much as a house cat. Its pictures were black and white and had 10,000 pixels in total. That is 0.01 megapixels. A modern phone camera might have more than a thousand times as many.","Taking a picture was slow. When you pressed the button, the CCD captured the image almost instantly, and the camera held it briefly in electronic memory. Then it took about 23 seconds to write that one picture onto the cassette tape as a long string of numbers. To see the picture, Sasson and his colleague took the tape out and put it into a special playback machine they had built, which read the numbers and turned them back into an image on a television screen. In tests, they photographed people in the lab. The first results were blurry, blocky and gray, but they were real photographs with no film and no chemicals.","Sasson later described showing the camera to Kodak managers in meeting after meeting. As he remembered it, they were interested but puzzled. Who would want to look at their photos on a television? How would people store them? When would the pictures be good enough to compete with film? Sasson himself estimated that it might take 15 to 20 years before digital cameras could compete with film for ordinary customers. That guess turned out to be roughly right.","Kodak filed a patent for the 'electronic still camera' in 1977, naming Sasson and Lloyd as inventors, and it was granted as US patent 4,131,919. One clever part of the design was that the image was read off the sensor quickly into temporary memory, then saved more slowly to storage, an approach that many cameras still use. Kodak did not show the prototype to the public until 2000. In 2009, President Barack Obama awarded Sasson the National Medal of Technology and Innovation.","It is worth being careful about the word 'first'. Sasson's camera is often called the first digital camera, and it was an early self-contained camera that saved digital pictures. But Kirsch had made digital images back in 1957, scientists had been capturing CCD images since the early 1970s, and engineers at other companies and labs were exploring similar ideas. Like almost every invention in this Deep Dive, the digital camera grew from many people's work."]},{"heading":"Floppy Disks, Fotoman and a Format Called JPEG","paragraphs":["In August 1981, Sony surprised the photo world by unveiling the Mavica, short for Magnetic Video Camera. It used a CCD sensor and recorded pictures on a small 2-inch floppy disk that held about 50 frames, which you could view on a television through a playback unit. But the Mavica was not truly digital. It stored each picture as an analog video signal, the same kind of wavy signal a TV used, rather than as numbers. Engineers call this 'still video'. The original prototype was never sold, though still video cameras from Sony, Canon and others went on sale in the late 1980s, and news photographers sometimes used them to send pictures quickly over telephone lines.","The race to a camera that saved real numbers was on. At the Photokina trade fair in Germany in 1988, Fujifilm showed the DS-1P, which saved pictures as digital data on a removable memory card holding about ten photos, though it was not sold in shops. Around 1990, a small American company called Dycam made one of the first digital cameras you could actually buy and connect to a personal computer. It was sold as the Dycam Model 1 and also under the name Logitech Fotoman. It took grainy black-and-white pictures, stored a few dozen of them in its built-in memory, and cost about a thousand dollars. It was not a big hit, partly because of its grainy, colorless pictures and high price, but it showed that a digital camera could talk straight to a computer.","Professionals needed much better quality. In 1991 Kodak released the DCS 100, which looked like a normal Nikon F3 film camera, a favorite of photojournalists, but with a 1.3-megapixel digital sensor fitted where the film would go. The camera was connected by a cable to a separate Digital Storage Unit carried over the shoulder, which held the batteries, a small screen and a 200-megabyte hard drive. That drive could hold about 156 uncompressed images. The whole kit cost about $20,000, and only 987 were sold. But for a newspaper photographer racing a deadline, sending a picture straight from a laptop instead of waiting to develop film was a huge advantage.","Digital pictures had a size problem, though. A full-color image contains three numbers for every pixel, so a one-megapixel photo is about three million numbers. In the early 1990s that was a lot for a computer's memory and far too much to send quickly over a phone line. Experts from many countries had formed the Joint Photographic Experts Group in 1986 to solve this. In 1992 they released their standard, named after the group: JPEG.","JPEG works by throwing away details your eyes are unlikely to miss. First it separates brightness from color, and it often stores color at lower detail, because our eyes notice fine changes in brightness far more than fine changes in color. Then it chops the picture into blocks of 8 by 8 pixels and uses a mathematical tool to describe each block as a mix of smooth patterns and fine patterns. The finest, least noticeable patterns are rounded off or dropped. A typical JPEG is about ten times smaller than the original, and most people cannot spot the difference. Squeeze it too hard, though, and you see blocky squares and smudges, the telltale 'JPEG artifacts'.","Through the 1990s, digital cameras got cheaper and better. Apple's QuickTake 100 of 1994, designed for Apple by Kodak, was one of the first color digital cameras aimed at ordinary consumers. Casio's QV-10 of 1995 had a small color screen on the back so you could see your photo the moment you took it, which became one of the most loved features of digital cameras. Sony's digital Mavica of 1997 saved JPEG pictures straight onto ordinary computer floppy disks. And the big advantage of digital became clear: a digital photo can be copied endlessly with no loss. The ten-thousandth copy of a JPEG is exactly the same as the first, while every copy of a film print loses a little sharpness."]},{"heading":"Movies Without Film","paragraphs":["Hollywood was watching. On 18 June 1999, two theaters, one in Los Angeles and one in New York, showed Star Wars: Episode I, The Phantom Menace using digital projectors built with technology from Texas Instruments. Inside these projectors were chips covered with hundreds of thousands of microscopic mirrors, each able to tilt back and forth thousands of times a second to send light toward the screen or away from it. Each mirror controlled one pixel of light on the screen.","The director George Lucas wanted to go further and shoot an entire big-budget movie digitally. For Star Wars: Episode II, Attack of the Clones, filmed in 2000 mostly in Sydney, Australia, his team used the Sony HDW-F900, a high-definition video camera developed with the camera company Panavision. It recorded 24 frames per second, the same rate as film, so the footage would feel like cinema rather than television. When the film was released in May 2002, it was one of the first major movies shot entirely with a high-definition digital 24-frame system. Lucas hoped theaters would buy digital projectors for it, but at the time only a few did.","Why did theaters eventually change? Money, mostly. A single 35mm film print of a feature cost roughly $1,500 to make and ship. A big release on 4,000 screens needed 4,000 heavy reels of film, costing millions of dollars, and the prints got scratched and dusty as they were used. A digital movie, by contrast, could be delivered on a hard drive or over a network and copied with no loss. In 2005 the big studios published a shared technical standard for digital cinema, so any approved movie file would play on any approved projector.","The switch happened fast. By early 2011, there were about 36,000 digital screens around the world. By March 2015, about 38,700 of roughly 39,800 movie screens in the United States had gone digital. Projectionists who had once spliced reels and watched for film breaks found their jobs changed or gone. For the first time in more than a century, most cinemas no longer had any film running through them at all. The strip of perforated 35mm film that Dickson's team had standardized in the 1890s was now, in most theaters, a museum piece."]},{"heading":"What Really Happened to Kodak","paragraphs":["For most of the 20th century, Kodak was one of the most famous companies on Earth. It sold cameras cheaply and made much of its money on film, chemicals and processing, the things people had to keep buying. In 1996, Kodak's yearly revenue reached about $16 billion. Then, on 19 January 2012, the company filed for bankruptcy protection in the United States. How did the company that built an early digital camera end up there?","A popular version of the story says Kodak invented digital photography, hid it in a drawer and ignored it until it was too late. That version is too simple, and in important ways it is wrong. Kodak spent large amounts on digital research. It invented the Bayer filter, made professional digital cameras like the DCS series, built cameras for Apple, and sold its own EasyShare digital cameras. In 2005, Kodak was the number one seller of digital cameras in the United States.","The real trouble was that the money worked differently in the digital world. With film, every photo a family took meant buying film and paying for prints. With digital, once you owned the camera, taking a thousand pictures cost almost nothing. Digital cameras themselves earned only thin profits, because many companies, especially in Japan, Korea and China, made good ones and kept cutting prices. Kodak's share of the digital camera market fell to about 7 percent by 2010. Meanwhile, film sales, which had kept the company rich, began falling in 2001 and then collapsed.","Kodak had other problems, too. Its rival Fujifilm had been winning film customers since the 1990s, which started a price war. Earlier, Kodak had tried to grow by buying a drug company, which lost money and was sold. In the 2000s it closed 13 film factories and about 130 photo-processing labs, and around 50,000 workers lost their jobs. Then, just as Kodak was fighting for the digital camera market, a new rival arrived that made many stand-alone cameras less necessary: the mobile phone.","During bankruptcy, Kodak sold many of its digital imaging patents for about $525 million. It came out of bankruptcy in September 2013 as a smaller company focused on business printing and imaging, while its film and consumer photo business became a separate company called Kodak Alaris. Kodak still makes motion picture film today. Historians and business experts still argue about what Kodak should have done differently. The lesson most agree on is that even an excellent invention cannot save a company if the way it earns money disappears.","And that brings us to a scene on the other side of the world. In November 2000, in Japan, shoppers began buying a new mobile phone from the company J-Phone. It looked like any other phone of its time, with buttons and a small screen. It rang like any other phone. But on its back was a tiny lens, smaller than a pea, sitting in front of a CMOS sensor. This phone could take a picture and send it to a friend in seconds. Nobody could have guessed it then, but that little lens would change photography more than anything since George Eastman's first Kodak."]}],"deepDive":"Why did digital imaging take nearly 50 years to beat film, when the key ideas existed by the early 1970s? Part of the answer is raw numbers. A good 35mm color negative records detail roughly comparable to somewhere between about 10 and 20 megapixels, depending on the film and how you measure it. Sasson's 1975 sensor had 0.01 megapixels, about a thousand times too few. Moore's law, the observation that the number of transistors on a chip doubled roughly every two years, slowly closed the gap, along with cheaper memory and storage. A second part of the answer is the sensor itself. CCDs produce very clean images, but they need special manufacturing and fairly high voltages, and they read out charges one bucket at a time. In the early 1990s, Eric Fossum's team at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory developed the CMOS active-pixel sensor, where every pixel has its own tiny amplifier and the sensor can be built with the same factories used for ordinary computer chips. Fossum's 1993 paper was provocatively titled 'Active Pixel Sensors: Are CCD's Dinosaurs?' CMOS sensors used less power, cost less and could hold image-processing circuits on the same chip. They were first adopted for webcams through Fossum's spin-off company Photobit, and by around 2007 CMOS sales passed CCD sales; by 2017 CMOS had close to 90 percent of the market. That low-power, single-chip design is exactly what made phone cameras practical. Historians of technology also debate Kodak's choices. The 'Kodak ignored digital' story is a myth, but scholars point out that a company's existing business can make it cautious: in the 1990s, film produced enormous profits with little competition, while digital promised thin margins and fierce rivals. Some business researchers call this the innovator's dilemma. Others argue Kodak's timing was simply unlucky, because just as it reached the top of the US digital camera market, smartphones began to swallow that market whole.","funFact":"The first digital scan of a photo, made in 1957, showed a three-month-old baby, and it was so small that its 30,976 pixels would fit nearly 390 times into a single 12-megapixel phone photo.","check":{"q":"Why does the Bayer color filter pattern have twice as many green filters as red or blue?","a":"Because human eyes are most sensitive to green light and detail","wrong":["Because green filters were the cheapest to make","Because Kodak's company color was green","Because green light passes through lenses faster"]},"sources":[{"title":"Fiftieth Anniversary of First Digital Image Marked","publisher":"National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST)","url":"https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2007/05/fiftieth-anniversary-first-digital-image-marked"},{"title":"Charge-coupled device","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charge-coupled_device"},{"title":"Bayer filter","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayer_filter"},{"title":"Steven Sasson","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steven_Sasson"},{"title":"Active-pixel sensor","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Active-pixel_sensor"},{"title":"Kodak DCS 100","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kodak_DCS_100"},{"title":"Digital cinema","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_cinema"},{"title":"Kodak","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kodak"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/19-1.jpg","alt":"One of the first scanned digital images (1957): Russell Kirsch's baby son Walden, made of blocky pixels.","file":"NBSFirstScanImage.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NBSFirstScanImage.jpg","author":"Russell A. Kirsch","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/19-2.jpg","alt":"Diagram of a Bayer filter: tiny red, green and blue squares sit over the light sensors of a digital camera chip.","file":"Bayer pattern on sensor.svg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayer_pattern_on_sensor.svg","author":"en:User:Cburnett","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/19-3.jpg","alt":"The 1991 Kodak DCS: a Nikon F3 camera body (right) linked to a separate Digital Storage Unit box (left).","file":"Kodak DCS system and Nikon F3 2014 CP+.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kodak_DCS_system_and_Nikon_F3_2014_CP%2B.jpg","author":"Morio","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4330},{"n":20,"title":"Every Pocket a Studio, and Tomorrow's Pictures","when":"1997 – today and the future","summary":"Today the camera lives in your pocket, inside your phone. You can snap a photo, film a movie and share it with the whole world in seconds. Giant telescopes take pictures of things no eye has ever seen, and new tools may change pictures again, so we must use them honestly and kindly.","sections":[{"heading":"A Newborn Baby and a Tangle of Wires","paragraphs":["On 11 June 1997, in a hospital in California, a software inventor named Philippe Kahn was waiting for his daughter to be born. Kahn was not just a nervous dad. For almost a year he had been building a system he called Picture Mail, a computer server at his home that could receive pictures and share them. He had a plan, and he had brought a bag of gadgets.","When his daughter Sophie arrived, Kahn took her picture with a small digital camera. Then he did something nobody had done in quite this way before. Using a laptop, some homemade connections and his mobile phone, he rigged the camera and the phone together and sent the photos through the phone network to his server. From there, the pictures of baby Sophie went out to more than 2,000 friends, family members and colleagues, almost as soon as she was born. Kahn later said he had always wanted to have it working in time to share his daughter's birth photo. In 2016, Time magazine included that picture in its list of the 100 most influential photos of all time.","It is a lovely bookend to our story. The first digital scan, back in 1957, was a picture of a baby. One of the first instantly shared phone photos was a picture of a baby too. People have always wanted to show the ones they love to others, and every new technology in this Deep Dive, from the daguerreotype portrait studio to the Brownie camera, has been used for exactly that.","Kahn's setup was a clever hack, not a product. Companies soon raced to put the camera right inside the phone. In May 1999, the Japanese company Kyocera released the Visual Phone VP-210, with a small color camera on the front, meant for video calls and pictures. In June 2000, Samsung's SCH-V200 in South Korea could take and store photos, though not send them over the network. Then, in November 2000, J-Phone in Japan launched the Sharp J-SH04. Its camera had about 110,000 pixels, roughly 0.11 megapixels, and its screen could show only 256 colors. That sounds tiny now, but this phone could snap a picture and send it straight to a friend's phone. J-Phone called the service 'sha-mail', from the Japanese word for photo. It is often described as the first mass-market camera phone.","The idea spread across Japan and South Korea and then the world. By 2003, camera phones were outselling stand-alone digital cameras worldwide. By 2010, there were more than a billion camera phones on the planet. Picture this: in less than ten years, a camera went from being something you remembered to pack for a holiday to something nearly everyone carried every minute of the day.","In January 2007, Apple's Steve Jobs announced the first iPhone, which went on sale in the United States on 29 June 2007. Its camera, honestly, was not impressive even then. It had 2 megapixels, no flash, no zoom, and it could not record video. What made the iPhone and the smartphones that followed so important for photography was everything around the camera: a big touchscreen to view pictures, a fast internet connection, and soon, apps. The camera was suddenly connected to the whole world."]},{"heading":"Nineteen Seconds at the Zoo","paragraphs":["At 8:31 in the evening, Pacific Time, on 23 April 2005, a young man named Jawed Karim uploaded a video to a brand-new website he had helped start. The video is 19 seconds long. Karim stands in front of the elephants at the San Diego Zoo, and his high school friend Yakov Lapitsky holds the camera. Karim says, more or less, that the cool thing about the elephants is that they have really, really long trunks. That's it. That's the whole film. Its title is 'Me at the zoo', and it was the first video on YouTube.","Compare that with the first public film screening in this Deep Dive, the Lumières' show at the Grand Café in Paris in 1895. That needed a special camera-projector, a rented room and paying customers. 'Me at the zoo' needed a small camera and an internet connection, and it has now been watched hundreds of millions of times. Anyone, anywhere, could now be a filmmaker and a broadcaster at the same time.","Photos joined the party. On 6 October 2010, Kevin Systrom and Mike Krieger launched an iPhone app called Instagram. At first every picture had to be square, 640 pixels wide, which oddly echoed the square-ish pictures of old Kodak Instamatic and Polaroid cameras. Users could add filters that made photos look warm, faded or old-fashioned, as if they had been taken on film decades ago. Instagram gained a million users in about two months. In 2012 Facebook bought it for about a billion dollars, and by 2018 it had a billion users.","Phones also made it easy to turn the camera around. The front-facing camera turned the self-portrait into a daily habit. Robert Cornelius had to sit still for minutes in Philadelphia in 1839 to make his early self-portrait. Now people could do it in a fraction of a second. The word 'selfie' became so popular that Oxford Dictionaries named it the word of the year for 2013.","Serious filmmakers noticed too. In 2015, director Sean Baker premiered a feature film called Tangerine at the Sundance Film Festival. He and cinematographer Radium Cheung shot the whole movie on three iPhone 5s phones, using an app called FiLMiC Pro and an add-on lens adapter that gave the pictures a wide, cinematic shape. The film cost about $100,000 to make, very little for a feature film, and critics praised it. Its success showed that the gear no longer decided who could make a movie. What mattered most was the story and the people telling it.","All of this created an ocean of images. Researchers and industry analysts estimate that people now take more than a trillion photos every year, and most of them are taken on phones. Billions of short videos are uploaded too. For better and for worse, our age is the most photographed time in human history."]},{"heading":"The Camera That Thinks","paragraphs":["Here is a puzzle. A phone camera's lens is tiny, often only a few millimeters across, and its sensor is much smaller than a fingernail. By the old rules of photography, small lenses and small sensors should mean dim, grainy, blurry pictures in poor light. So how can a modern phone take a clear picture of a dark street at night? The answer is that the phone is not just a camera anymore. It is a camera joined to a powerful computer. This combination is called computational photography.","Start with HDR, which stands for high dynamic range. Imagine taking a picture of a friend standing in front of a bright window. If the camera sets the exposure for the window, your friend becomes a dark silhouette. If it sets the exposure for your friend, the window turns into a blank white blob. Your eyes handle this scene easily, but a single exposure cannot. An HDR camera takes several pictures very quickly, some darker and some brighter, then combines them, keeping the best-exposed parts of each. Photographers in the 1850s did something similar by hand. Gustave Le Gray printed seascapes from two glass negatives, one for the bright sky and one for the darker sea. Your phone does it automatically, in a blink.","Night modes go further. In 2018 Google introduced a feature called Night Sight for its Pixel phones. When you press the button in a dark place, the phone captures a burst of up to 15 frames while you hold it. Each frame on its own would be dim and speckled with noise, the random grainy dots that appear when very little light reaches the sensor. The phone's software lines the frames up, carefully skipping parts that moved, and averages them together. Because the noise is random but the real scene stays the same, averaging cancels much of the noise and leaves a cleaner picture. The software even measures how much your hands are shaking and how much the scene is moving, and chooses longer exposures if the phone is steady, such as on a tripod.","Portrait mode creates the soft, blurry background you see in professional portraits. With a big camera, that blur comes naturally from a large lens with a wide aperture, which can only keep a thin slice of distance in sharp focus. A phone lens is too small to do this. So the phone fakes it intelligently. It works out how far away each part of the scene is, using two cameras side by side, special pixels that see from slightly different angles, or software trained to recognize people. Then it keeps the person sharp and blurs everything behind them on purpose.","Modern phones use machine learning, a kind of artificial intelligence, for many other jobs too: adjusting colors, recognizing faces, brightening skies, and sharpening zoomed images by combining several frames. This raises an interesting question, one that connects back to the chapter on photographic truth. When the phone combines fifteen frames, brightens shadows and blurs a background, is the result still a 'photograph' of what was in front of it? Most people would say yes, because it is built from real light that entered the lens. But the line between capturing a picture and creating one is getting harder to see."]},{"heading":"Pictures No Eye Could Ever See","paragraphs":["Cameras have also traveled where no person can go. On 24 April 1990, the Space Shuttle Discovery carried the Hubble Space Telescope into orbit, a few hundred kilometers above Earth. Hubble's main mirror is 2.4 meters across. Above the blurring and twinkling of Earth's air, it was supposed to take the sharpest pictures of the universe ever made. Then came an embarrassing surprise: the mirror had been ground to slightly the wrong shape. Its outer edge was too flat by about 2.2 micrometers, a tiny fraction of the width of a human hair, but enough to make the first pictures fuzzy.","In December 1993, astronauts on the shuttle Endeavour flew up and installed corrective optics, a set of small mirrors that worked like a pair of glasses for the telescope's instruments, and a new main camera with its own correction built in. The fix worked brilliantly. Hubble went on to take some of the most famous pictures in history, from towering clouds of gas where stars are born to deep views showing thousands of faraway galaxies in a patch of sky so small you could cover it with a speck held at arm's length. And here is the link to the previous chapter: Hubble's cameras use the same kind of CCD sensors that Boyle and Smith dreamed up in 1969.","In 2019, scientists did something many people thought was impossible: they took a picture of a black hole. A black hole's gravity is so strong that not even light can escape it, so you cannot photograph the hole itself. But you can photograph its shadow against the glowing gas swirling around it. The target was a giant black hole at the center of a galaxy called Messier 87, or M87, in the constellation Virgo, about 55 million light-years away. It is roughly 6.5 billion times as massive as our Sun.","The problem was size. Even though the black hole is enormous, it is so far away that it looks unimaginably small from Earth, like trying to see a doughnut on the surface of the Moon. To see detail that fine, you would need a telescope as big as the whole planet. So the Event Horizon Telescope team created one. In April 2017, radio telescopes in several places around the world, from Hawaii and Arizona to Chile, Mexico, Spain and the South Pole, all observed M87 at the same time, recording radio waves about 1.3 millimeters long. Each site kept extremely precise time with atomic clocks.","The amount of data was so huge that it was faster to fly hard drives to computing centers than to send the data over the internet. At MIT's Haystack Observatory in the United States and the Max Planck Institute for Radio Astronomy in Germany, supercomputers combined the recordings as if they had come from one Earth-sized telescope. Then teams of scientists, working separately so they would not influence each other, used different computer methods to turn the data into a picture. One of the people who helped develop the imaging methods was a young researcher named Katie Bouman, but she was one of more than 200 scientists in the collaboration. On 10 April 2019, they revealed a blurry orange ring around a dark center. It was the first image of a black hole. In May 2022, the same team released an image of Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the center of our own Milky Way.","On Christmas Day 2021, the James Webb Space Telescope launched on an Ariane 5 rocket from French Guiana. Webb's mirror is 6.5 meters across and made of 18 six-sided segments of beryllium coated with a thin layer of gold, which reflects infrared light very well. It had to fold up to fit inside the rocket and unfold in space. Webb orbits around a point about 1.5 million kilometers from Earth, shaded by a five-layer sunshield about the size of a tennis court that keeps its instruments extremely cold.","Webb sees mainly infrared light, which our eyes cannot detect. Infrared can pass through dust clouds that block visible light, and light from the most distant galaxies has been stretched into infrared on its long journey across the expanding universe. On 11 July 2022, the first Webb image was shown to the public: a deep field crowded with thousands of galaxies, some seen as they were more than 13 billion years ago. More images followed the next day. Because the light is infrared, scientists translate it into colors we can see, usually giving the shortest wavelengths blue and the longest red. So these pictures are real data, but their colors are a careful translation. That is a good reminder that every photograph, even a scientific one, involves choices."]},{"heading":"Tomorrow's Pictures: What Might Happen Next","paragraphs":["Predicting the future of photography is risky. In 1975, few people guessed that film would almost disappear from cinemas within 40 years. So everything in this section is a possibility, not a promise. But some changes have already begun.","The biggest is artificial intelligence. Computer programs can now create realistic-looking pictures and videos from a typed description, with no camera involved at all. They learn by studying enormous numbers of existing images. This can be wonderful for art, games, design and imagination. It also raises hard questions. Who should get credit when a program learns from the work of millions of photographers and artists? And how can anyone trust that a picture shows something that really happened?","That second question is serious because of deepfakes. The word, a blend of 'deep learning' and 'fake', appeared in 2017 to describe videos where software swapped one person's face onto another's. Today, AI tools can make fake videos and voices of real people that are very hard to spot. They have been used for scams, bullying and spreading false news. The chapter on truth and tricks showed that faked photos are as old as photography, from spirit photographs to the Cottingley Fairies. What is new is how fast, cheap and convincing the fakes can be.","People are working on answers. In 2019, the software company Adobe, together with The New York Times and Twitter, started the Content Authenticity Initiative. In 2021, a wider group of technology and news organizations, including Microsoft and the BBC, formed a standards body called the C2PA. Their idea is called content credentials: a kind of digital label, sealed with cryptography, that can record which camera or program made an image and how it was edited. If someone changes the file, the seal shows it. Some newer cameras and phones can add these labels when you take a photo. But a label can only tell you where a picture came from, not whether its message is true, and it only helps if people check it. Learning to ask 'Where did this picture come from?' may become one of the most important skills of the century.","Moviemaking is changing too. For the TV series The Mandalorian, first shown in 2019, Industrial Light and Magic built a stage called the Volume, surrounded by giant walls of LED screens. Instead of acting in front of a green screen and adding the background months later, the actors performed in front of moving computer-generated landscapes, rendered in real time by a video game engine. When the camera moved, the background shifted to match, as if the scene were real. The glowing screens even lit the actors with the colors of the alien sky. Many films and shows now use similar 'virtual production' stages.","Other possibilities are on the horizon. Virtual reality headsets can place you inside a 360-degree video. Phones and special cameras can capture objects and rooms in 3D, so you could one day walk around a memory instead of just looking at it. Some researchers are working on cameras that can see around corners by timing how light bounces, or capture images using tiny flat lenses thinner than a sheet of paper. Which of these will become everyday tools, and which will stay experiments, nobody knows yet.","And yet, some people are choosing the old ways. Director Christopher Nolan filmed Oppenheimer (2023) on large-format film, including IMAX film cameras. Kodak created a special black-and-white IMAX film stock for the movie, the first time black-and-white IMAX film had been used in this way. Many young photographers love film cameras, instant cameras and even pinhole cameras, because the slow, careful process feels special. New technology rarely wipes out the old completely. More often, it gives people more choices."]},{"heading":"From a Pinhole to a Pixel","paragraphs":["Let's take one last walk through the whole story. More than 2,000 years ago, thinkers in China and Greece noticed that light passing through a small hole paints an upside-down picture. Around the year 1000, Ibn al-Haytham used dark rooms to study how light travels in straight lines. Renaissance scholars added lenses and smaller openings to make the image brighter and sharper. Artists traced it. But for centuries nobody could keep it.","Then came the chemists. Schulze showed light darkens silver salts. Wedgwood and Davy made shadow pictures that faded. Niépce let the sun harden tar on a pewter plate for hours, possibly days, and saved the view from his window. Daguerre made his mirror with a memory, Talbot invented the negative so pictures could be copied, and Herschel found the fixer that made them last. Glass plates, dry plates and Eastman's roll film made cameras faster and easier, until anyone could press the button.","Next, pictures began to move. Muybridge froze a galloping horse, Le Prince, Dickson, the Skladanowsky brothers and the Lumières turned strips of film into moving pictures, and Méliès and Alice Guy-Blaché used them to tell stories. Color arrived, then sound, then cartoons, then television, widescreen, videotape and computer effects. Finally, pictures turned into numbers: Kirsch's pixels, Boyle and Smith's CCD, Fossum's CMOS sensor, Bayer's checkerboard and Sasson's toaster-sized camera. Today the whole journey fits inside a phone.","Look closely, though, and the same basic ideas are still there. Every phone camera has a lens that bends light, just like Cardano's lens in a camera obscura around 1550. It has an aperture that controls how much light comes in, and a light-sensitive surface that records the image, today a silicon sensor instead of silver or tar. Even the upside-down picture is still there: the lens projects an inverted image onto the sensor, and the phone's software simply flips it the right way up. The camera obscura never really went away. It just shrank to the size of a pea.","The story also shows how pictures carry power. Photographs helped end child labor, protect Yosemite, reveal the horrors of war and show us our fragile planet from space. They have also been faked, used to spread prejudice and taken without permission. That power now sits in almost everyone's hands. Using it well means asking simple questions. Is this true? Did this person agree to be photographed? Would I want this picture of me shared? A camera can be used to show the world as it is, to remember the people we love, and to help others see what we see. That is worth doing honestly and kindly.","So here is your final challenge. Put down this story, pick up a camera, or a phone, or even a box with a pinhole in it, and go take a picture. Find something that interests you: a sunbeam on the floor, a friend laughing, a bug on a leaf, the sky at sunset. And when you press that button, remember what you are holding. Inside it are the dark rooms of ancient thinkers, the silver of Schulze's bottle, Niépce's patient sunlight, Daguerre's mirror, Talbot's negative, Eastman's roll of film, the Lumières' flickering screen and the buckets of electrons on a silicon chip. You are holding more than 2,000 years of human curiosity about light. Now go and catch some."]}],"deepDive":"Is a computational photograph still a photograph? Philosophers and photography historians have argued about this question for decades, but smartphones have made it urgent. A traditional photograph has what some theorists call an indexical link to reality: light from the scene physically changed the film, the way a footprint is made by a foot. A phone image is still built from real photons hitting real pixels, but it is often assembled from many frames, with noise averaged away, shadows lifted, skies recolored and backgrounds blurred by software that learned from millions of other pictures. Some phones have been criticized for 'enhancing' zoomed shots of the Moon with detail drawn partly from learned patterns rather than from the frame itself, which shows how blurry the line can get. Defenders point out that darkroom printers always dodged, burned and retouched, and that the human eye and brain also edit what we see. The science side is just as interesting. Burst averaging works because random noise shrinks roughly with the square root of the number of frames combined, so merging 15 frames reduces random noise to about a quarter of a single frame's. The Event Horizon Telescope used a technique called very-long-baseline interferometry: by combining signals from dishes thousands of kilometers apart, its sharpness depends on the distance between the dishes rather than the size of any single one, reaching an angular resolution of about 25 microarcseconds. But such an array only samples parts of the incoming waves, so turning the data into an image requires filling the gaps with careful assumptions, which is why the EHT used independent teams and several algorithms and checked that they agreed. Finally, the estimate that more than a trillion photos are taken each year comes from industry analysts rather than an exact count, so it should be treated as an order-of-magnitude figure. Even so, it suggests that more pictures are now taken every few days than in the first several decades of photography combined.","funFact":"The very first video ever uploaded to YouTube, 'Me at the zoo', is only 19 seconds long and shows a man talking about elephants' long trunks.","check":{"q":"How did the Event Horizon Telescope get enough detail to photograph a black hole's shadow?","a":"It linked radio telescopes around the world to act like one Earth-sized telescope","wrong":["It flew a camera close to the black hole","It used a single giant mirror on the Moon","It took a very long exposure with a phone camera"]},"sources":[{"title":"Camera phone","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camera_phone"},{"title":"Philippe Kahn","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippe_Kahn"},{"title":"Me at the zoo","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Me_at_the_zoo"},{"title":"Night Sight: Seeing in the Dark on Pixel Phones","publisher":"Google Research","url":"https://research.google/blog/night-sight-seeing-in-the-dark-on-pixel-phones/"},{"title":"Event Horizon Telescope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Event_Horizon_Telescope"},{"title":"James Webb Space Telescope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Webb_Space_Telescope"},{"title":"Hubble Space Telescope","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hubble_Space_Telescope"},{"title":"Content Authenticity Initiative","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Content_Authenticity_Initiative"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/photography/20-1.jpg","alt":"The Sharp J-SH04 (2000), an early camera phone sold by J-Phone in Japan, with its little lens on the back.","file":"Sharp J-SH04 CP+ 2011.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sharp_J-SH04_CP%2B_2011.jpg","author":"Morio","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/20-2.jpg","alt":"The first picture of a black hole (2019): the Event Horizon Telescope's glowing ring around the dark center of M87*.","file":"Black hole - Messier 87 crop max res.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg","author":"Event Horizon Telescope, uploader cropped and converted TIF to JPG","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/photography/20-3.jpg","alt":"The James Webb Space Telescope's 18 gold hexagonal mirror segments, with two engineers in clean-room suits below.","file":"James Webb Space Telescope Mirrors Will Piece Together Cosmic Puzzles (30108124923).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Webb_Space_Telescope_Mirrors_Will_Piece_Together_Cosmic_Puzzles_(30108124923).jpg","author":"Chris Gunn","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":3861}],"words":81262};
